1293
01:42:06.118 --> 01:42:12.091
Both take Boolean values. Setting error bad lines to false makes pandas skip bad

1294
01:42:12.091 --> 01:42:18.140
lines and load the rest of the data, instead of throwing an error. Warn bad lines

1295
01:42:18.140 --> 01:42:24.188
tells pandas whether to display messages when unparsable lines are skipped. Let's

1296
01:42:24.188 --> 01:42:27.100
try importing the corrupted file again.

1297
01:42:27.100 --> 01:42:32.464
This time with error bad lines set to false and warn bad lines equal to true.

1298
01:42:32.464 --> 01:42:38.316
Success! A word of caution. If lines were skipped, it's worth investigating what was

1299
01:42:38.316 --> 01:42:43.889
left out to see if there are underlying issues that should be addressed. In this

1300
01:42:43.889 --> 01:42:49.740
video, you've learned about common issues encountered importing data from flat files

1301
01:42:49.740 --> 01:42:55.383
and how to handle those issues. Now, it's your turn to practice. Welcome! In this

1302
01:42:55.383 --> 01:42:55.940
chapter,

1303
01:42:55.940 --> 01:43:00.918
you will learn how to build pipelines to data stored in spreadsheets, plus

1304
01:43:00.918 --> 01:43:06.300
additional data wrangling techniques. Spreadsheets, also called Excel files, are

1305
01:43:06.300 --> 01:43:11.749
ubiquitous today. If you ever worked with them, the data frame structure probably

1306
01:43:11.749 --> 01:43:17.333
looked familiar. Like data frames, spreadsheets organize information in tables with

1307
01:43:17.333 --> 01:43:19.620
cells of data in rows and columns.

1308
01:43:19.620 --> 01:43:25.179
Unlike data frames and flat files, spreadsheets can have formulas with automatically

1309
01:43:25.179 --> 01:43:30.739
updating results. Spreadsheets also support formatting that flat files do not. While

1310
01:43:30.739 --> 01:43:36.165
data frames can be formatted for presentation purposes, like in Jupyter Notebooks,

1311
01:43:36.165 --> 01:43:41.592
Pandas does not import spreadsheet formatting, so check that spreadsheets you want

1312
01:43:41.592 --> 01:43:47.152
to load do not use formatting to convey important information. Or better yet, export

1313
01:43:47.152 --> 01:43:48.740
them without formatting.

1314
01:43:48.740 --> 01:43:54.738
Finally, an Excel file or workbook can have multiple spreadsheets. At its simplest,

1315
01:43:54.738 --> 01:44:00.519
reading data from a spreadsheet resembles reading a flat file, just with its own

1316
01:44:00.519 --> 01:44:06.012
function. Read Excel. Let's demonstrate with an Excel file of responses from

1317
01:44:06.012 --> 01:44:11.359
FreeCodeCamp's New Developer Survey, which asks new developers about their

1318
01:44:11.359 --> 01:44:15.840
employment, education, job goals, and learning resources used.

1319
01:44:15.840 --> 01:44:21.814
This file has been modified to make it easier to practice with, but you can grab the

1320
01:44:21.814 --> 01:44:27.148
full dataset from Kaggle or FreeCodeCamp's GitHub. Previewing the file in a

1321
01:44:27.148 --> 01:44:32.980
spreadsheet program, we see that it's pretty straightforward. Tabular data with no

1322
01:44:32.980 --> 01:44:38.883
formatting. As always, we first import pandas as pd, then pass the spreadsheet path

1323
01:44:38.883 --> 01:44:44.714
as a string to pd.readExcel and assign the result to a variable. When we check the

1324
01:44:44.714 --> 01:44:45.070
head,

1325
01:44:45.070 --> 01:44:51.053
the data frame looks as expected. Of course, not all spreadsheets are so simple, and

1326
01:44:51.053 --> 01:44:57.035
formatting features that help human readers can make it trickier to read by machine.

1327
01:44:57.035 --> 01:45:02.946
Spreadsheets commonly contain non-tabular information, such as metadata headers, or

1328
01:45:02.946 --> 01:45:08.929
have smaller tables of information, like this invoice. Fortunately, read Excel, like

1329
01:45:08.929 --> 01:45:09.570
read CSV.

1330
01:45:09.570 --> 01:45:15.604
Has keyword arguments to select columns and skip or limit rows to get only the cells

1331
01:45:15.604 --> 01:45:21.279
you want. ReadExcel shares many parameters with ReadCSV, but some work slightly

1332
01:45:21.279 --> 01:45:27.026
differently, so check Panda's documentation if you're unsure. NRows is the same,

1333
01:45:27.026 --> 01:45:33.060
taking an integer of rows to load. ReadExcel's skipRows argument is also similar. It

1334
01:45:33.060 --> 01:45:38.951
takes either a number of rows to skip at the beginning or a list of row numbers to

1335
01:45:38.951 --> 01:45:39.310
skip.

1336
01:45:39.310 --> 01:45:45.473
Finally, ReadExcel's useCalls argument accepts a list of either column numbers or

1337
01:45:45.473 --> 01:45:51.788
column names, or a function to decide whether to load a column, just like ReadCSV's

1338
01:45:51.788 --> 01:45:57.874
useCalls. However, it also accepts an integer of columns to load, or a string of

1339
01:45:57.874 --> 01:46:04.265
Excel column letters or ranges, like A through P. Let's use these arguments to get a

1340
01:46:04.265 --> 01:46:08.450
subset of survey response data, focusing on employment.

1341
01:46:08.450 --> 01:46:14.217
The columns are organized alphabetically with job-related variables in columns W

1342
01:46:14.217 --> 01:46:20.127
through AB and income in AR. Also note that this version of the data has a two-row

1343
01:46:20.127 --> 01:46:25.966
header providing links to the source that we'll want to exclude. We load the data

1344
01:46:25.966 --> 01:46:31.949
with Read Excel as before, but set skip rows to 2 to exclude the metadata. Since we

1345
01:46:31.949 --> 01:46:34.400
know which column letters we want,

1346
01:46:34.400 --> 01:46:40.056
we can pass the range and standalone income column as a single string to use calls.

1347
01:46:40.056 --> 01:46:45.780
Checking the data frame, we see there are only a few columns loaded and no metadata.

1348
01:46:45.780 --> 01:46:51.299
In this lesson, you learned about creating data frames from Excel data. Now, it's

1349
01:46:51.299 --> 01:46:56.887
your turn to practice. You can now create data frames from a single spreadsheet or

1350
01:46:56.887 --> 01:47:02.542
from parts of one. It's fairly common for Excel files to contain multiple sheets of

1351
01:47:02.542 --> 01:47:03.360
data though.

1352
01:47:03.360 --> 01:47:09.095
In this lesson, you'll learn how to specify sheets to get data from, as well as a

1353
01:47:09.095 --> 01:47:14.760
workflow to combine multiple sheets into a single data frame. Although the terms

1354
01:47:14.760 --> 01:47:20.496
spreadsheet and Excel file are often used interchangeably, an Excel file can be a

1355
01:47:20.496 --> 01:47:26.373
workbook with multiple sheets containing data as well as charts and metadata. We'll

1356
01:47:26.373 --> 01:47:29.630
focus on sheets with tabular data. By default,

1357
01:47:29.630 --> 01:47:35.515
Read Excel pulls data from the first sheet in a workbook only. This behavior can be

1358
01:47:35.515 --> 01:47:41.187
changed with the sheet name keyword argument. Sheet name accepts the name of the

1359
01:47:41.187 --> 01:47:47.143
desired sheet as a string, an integer indicating the desired sheet's position in the

1360
01:47:47.143 --> 01:47:52.957
workbook, or a list containing any combination of sheet names and position numbers

1361
01:47:52.957 --> 01:47:57.140
to read. Two things to note are that sheets are zero index,

1362
01:47:57.140 --> 01:48:03.216
and that if you load multiple sheets at once, any other arguments passed to ReadXL

1363
01:48:03.216 --> 01:48:09.365
apply to all sheets. For example, if you set nRows to 50, the first 50 rows of each

1364
01:48:09.365 --> 01:48:15.589
sheet listed in sheet name will be loaded. If sheets need different parameters, load

1365
01:48:15.589 --> 01:48:21.813
them with separate ReadXL calls. The new developer survey file actually contains two

1366
01:48:21.813 --> 01:48:24.480
sheets, one with responses from 2016

1367
01:48:24.480 --> 01:48:31.257
another with responses from 2017. We already got 2016's data since it's the first

1368
01:48:31.257 --> 01:48:38.117
sheet. Let's get 2017. To get the second sheet containing 2017's responses, we can

1369
01:48:38.117 --> 01:48:44.893
either pass its position index, 1, to sheet name, or the actual sheet name, 2017.

1370
01:48:44.893 --> 01:48:51.753
Either method gets the same results, as shown when we check if the two data frames

1371
01:48:51.753 --> 01:48:52.590
are equal.

1372
01:48:52.590 --> 01:48:58.136
What if we wanted to get all sheets in a workbook at once? We could list them all,

1373
01:48:58.136 --> 01:49:03.750
but the easier way is to pass none to sheet name. Let's do this for the survey data

1374
01:49:03.750 --> 01:49:09.228
and check the data type of the result. Instead of getting a data frame, we got an

1375
01:49:09.228 --> 01:49:14.909
ordered dictionary. Iterating through the items reveals that the keys are sheet name

1376
01:49:14.909 --> 01:49:20.320
and the values are data frames for each sheet. We have one data frame per sheet.

1377
01:49:20.320 --> 01:49:26.118
Which is great if sheets have different columns or describe different subjects. For

1378
01:49:26.118 --> 01:49:31.776
the survey data, each sheet has the same columns, just for different years. Let's

1379
01:49:31.776 --> 01:49:37.015
combine them into one data frame. First, we create an empty data frame, all

1380
01:49:37.015 --> 01:49:42.464
responses. Then, we loop through the items in the survey responses dictionary.

1381
01:49:42.464 --> 01:49:48.332
Remember, each value is a data frame corresponding to a worksheet, and each key is a

1382
01:49:48.332 --> 01:49:49.100
sheet name.

1383
01:49:49.100 --> 01:49:54.651
For each data frame, we add a column, year, containing the sheet name, so we know

1384
01:49:54.651 --> 01:49:59.859
which data set a record came from. Finally, we append the data frames to all

1385
01:49:59.859 --> 01:50:05.068
responses. We can check unique values in the year column to confirm that all

1386
01:50:05.068 --> 01:50:10.550
responses has both years. In this case, we only combined two sheets, so we could

1387
01:50:10.550 --> 01:50:16.032
have appended one to the other, but looping has the advantage of working for any

1388
01:50:16.032 --> 01:50:17.540
number of sheets. Now,

1389
01:50:17.540 --> 01:50:22.909
it's your turn to practice working with multiple sheets. In this course, you've

1390
01:50:22.909 --> 01:50:28.414
mostly handled string and numeric data. This lesson focuses on another data type,

1391
01:50:28.414 --> 01:50:33.919
boolean, and special considerations for working with them. A boolean variable has

1392
01:50:33.919 --> 01:50:39.560
only two possible values, true or false, which makes them convenient for tasks like

1393
01:50:39.560 --> 01:50:43.570
filtering. Despite this simplicity, booleans can be tricky.

1394
01:50:43.570 --> 01:50:49.156
We'll use a subset of the new developer survey data to focus on them. This version

1395
01:50:49.156 --> 01:50:54.879
only has an ID column, and columns for whether the respondent attended a programming

1396
01:50:54.879 --> 01:51:00.465
boot camp, and if they took out a loan for it. True and false are represented in a

1397
01:51:00.465 --> 01:51:06.052
few ways for demonstration purposes. 0s and 1s, which are common among people with

1398
01:51:06.052 --> 01:51:11.434
coding experience, trues and falses, and yeses and nos, which tend to appear in

1399
01:51:11.434 --> 01:51:12.660
surveys and forms.

1400
01:51:12.660 --> 01:51:18.138
Let's load this data with no additional arguments and check D types. Pandas

1401
01:51:18.138 --> 01:51:24.055
interpreted no columns as Boolean. Even true-false columns were loaded as floats.

1402
01:51:24.055 --> 01:51:30.044
Let's investigate. First, let's sum the data frame's columns to see how many trues

1403
01:51:30.044 --> 01:51:36.034
each float column has. Recall that these columns code true as 1 and false as 0. In

1404
01:51:36.034 --> 01:51:41.220
our data subset, 38 attended a boot camp and 14 took out a loan for it.

1405
01:51:41.220 --> 01:51:46.824
Let's also check how many values in each column are missing by summing the results

1406
01:51:46.824 --> 01:51:52.222
of isNA. Every record has a value for bootcamp attendance, but most of the loan

1407
01:51:52.222 --> 01:51:57.758
values are blank, even for some students who attended a bootcamp. Now, let's cast

1408
01:51:57.758 --> 01:52:03.361
these columns as booleans with the dtype argument. ReadExcel's dtype works exactly

1409
01:52:03.361 --> 01:52:08.692
like ReadCSV's, so we can pass a dictionary specifying which columns should be

1410
01:52:08.692 --> 01:52:10.400
boolean. Checking dtypes,

1411
01:52:10.400 --> 01:52:16.136
It looks like it worked. Checking counts of true values reveals issues. The lone

1412
01:52:16.136 --> 01:52:22.159
columns have too many trues, and the yes-no ones are all true. Checking NA values by

1413
01:52:22.159 --> 01:52:27.465
column, we see there aren't any. What happened? Pandas automatically loads

1414
01:52:27.465 --> 01:52:33.201
true-false columns as floats, but that can be changed with dtype. Boolean values

1415
01:52:33.201 --> 01:52:37.360
must be either true or false, so NAs were recoded as true.

1416
01:52:37.360 --> 01:52:43.055
While pandas recognized that 0s and 1s are false and true respectively, it did not

1417
01:52:43.055 --> 01:52:48.680
know what to do with yes and no, so they were all coded as true. We can solve the

1418
01:52:48.680 --> 01:52:54.514
issue of yes-no columns being misinterpreted with read Excel's true values and false

1419
01:52:54.514 --> 01:53:00.070
values argument. Each takes a list of values that pandas should treat as true or

1420
01:53:00.070 --> 01:53:05.626
false when they appear in Boolean columns. Values in non-Boolean columns are not

1421
01:53:05.626 --> 01:53:06.320
converted.

1422
01:53:06.320 --> 01:53:12.248
Let's pass yes and no as single-item lists to true values and false values. Then, we

1423
01:53:12.248 --> 01:53:17.400
check true counts with the sum method. Now the yes-no columns match their

1424
01:53:17.400 --> 01:53:23.045
counterparts, but there's still the issue of NAs being coded as true. What to do

1425
01:53:23.045 --> 01:53:28.762
depends on the data. In our case, we don't want fake trues, so we might decide to

1426
01:53:28.762 --> 01:53:31.020
keep the lone columns as floats.

1427
01:53:31.020 --> 01:53:36.127
Things to consider when casting a column as Boolean include the presence of NA

1428
01:53:36.127 --> 01:53:41.495
values, how the column will be used in the analysis, the consequences of fake true

1429
01:53:41.495 --> 01:53:46.602
values, and whether alternative representations like floats would do. You just

1430
01:53:46.602 --> 01:53:51.578
learned how to cast columns as Boolean and set custom true and false values.

1431
01:53:51.578 --> 01:53:56.881
Importantly, you also learned what to consider before doing so. Now, it's time to

1432
01:53:56.881 --> 01:53:57.470
practice.

1433
01:53:57.470 --> 01:54:03.066
You've now worked with numeric, string, and boolean data. This lesson will focus on

1434
01:54:03.066 --> 01:54:08.393
one last data type you're likely to encounter, date times. How computers handle

1435
01:54:08.393 --> 01:54:13.855
dates and times is a rich topic, but what you need to know for now is that Python

1436
01:54:13.855 --> 01:54:19.249
stores them as a special data type. Date time. Date times can be translated into

1437
01:54:19.249 --> 01:54:24.913
myriad text representations, and there is a common set of codes used to describe how

1438
01:54:24.913 --> 01:54:27.340
date times are formatted as strings.

1439
01:54:27.340 --> 01:54:33.115
By default, pandas loads datetime data as objects. If you want to arrange records by

1440
01:54:33.115 --> 01:54:38.341
time, select within a time span, or calculate intervals, though, you'll need

1441
01:54:38.341 --> 01:54:43.773
datetime columns. We use the parseDates keyword argument, not dtype, to specify

1442
01:54:43.773 --> 01:54:49.479
datetime columns. ParseDates accepts a list of column names or numbers to parse. It

1443
01:54:49.479 --> 01:54:55.117
also accepts a list of lists, where each sublist is a group of columns that should

1444
01:54:55.117 --> 01:54:57.180
be combined and parsed as one.

1445
01:54:57.180 --> 01:55:02.896
Such as separate day, month, and year columns. Finally, to combine columns, parse

1446
01:55:02.896 --> 01:55:08.682
them, and store the result as a new column, you can supply a dictionary where each

1447
01:55:08.682 --> 01:55:14.327
key is a new column name and each value is a list of columns to parse. Let's see

1448
01:55:14.327 --> 01:55:20.184
this with the new developer survey data, whose date-time columns have been modified

1449
01:55:20.184 --> 01:55:25.688
for demonstration purposes. Part 1 start time and Part 1 end time have data in

1450
01:55:25.688 --> 01:55:27.170
standard year, month,

1451
01:55:27.170 --> 01:55:33.207
day, hour, second format. Part 2 start times data has been split into date and time

1452
01:55:33.207 --> 01:55:39.244
column. Part 2 end time is in a non-standard format. To parse the dates in standard

1453
01:55:39.244 --> 01:55:45.063
format, we pass the column names in a list to read Excel's parse dates argument.

1454
01:55:45.063 --> 01:55:50.736
When we check the D types of the timestamp column, we see the two columns were

1455
01:55:50.736 --> 01:55:55.100
parsed successfully. To parse the split up timestamp column,

1456
01:55:55.100 --> 01:56:00.939
we can add a list within the list containing Part 2 Start Date and Part 2 Start

1457
01:56:00.939 --> 01:56:06.999
Time, and pass that to Parse Dates. Pandas creates a new Combine Date Time column,

1458
01:56:06.999 --> 01:56:12.912
Part 2 Start Date underscore Part 2 Start Time. But to control the column names,

1459
01:56:12.912 --> 01:56:18.529
let's create a dictionary, pass that instead, and view the resulting column.

1460
01:56:18.529 --> 01:56:19.120
However,

1461
01:56:19.120 --> 01:56:25.755
ParseDates only works if the data is in a format that Pandas understands. If you try

1462
01:56:25.755 --> 01:56:32.390
to parse unusually formatted dates with ParseDates, like 12-31-99 for December 31st,

1463
01:56:32.390 --> 01:56:38.788
1999, you'll get the columns back as strings. Instead, convert non-standard dates

1464
01:56:38.788 --> 01:56:45.028
after import with Pandas ToDateTime method. ToDateTime takes the data frame and

1465
01:56:45.028 --> 01:56:46.450
column to convert.

1466
01:56:46.450 --> 01:56:52.791
Plus a format argument containing a string that describes how the data is formatted.

1467
01:56:52.791 --> 01:56:58.906
DateTime formatting is described with a set of codes. Sturftime.org is a valuable

1468
01:56:58.906 --> 01:57:05.172
reference for them all. Some important codes are the ones for four-digit year, zero

1469
01:57:05.172 --> 01:57:11.287
padded month, zero padded day, hour on the 24-hour clock, zero padded minute, and

1470
01:57:11.287 --> 01:57:16.420
zero padded second. Let's use toDateTime to parse part two end time.

1471
01:57:16.420 --> 01:57:25.614
We review the data to build the string format description, %m, %d, %y, %h, colon,

1472
01:57:25.614 --> 01:57:34.808
%m, colon, %s. We pass the data frame and column to pd to datetime and supply the

1473
01:57:34.808 --> 01:57:44.228
format string to the format keyword argument. We reassign the result back to part 2

1474
01:57:44.228 --> 01:57:45.250
end time.

1475
01:57:45.250 --> 01:57:50.649
When we check the column, we see the dates were parsed correctly. That's a lot of

1476
01:57:50.649 --> 01:57:56.249
ways to parse a date. Now, it's your turn to practice. In this chapter, you'll learn

1477
01:57:56.249 --> 01:58:01.715
to build pipelines to relational databases, which underpin the information systems

1478
01:58:01.715 --> 01:58:07.247
of many organizations. Relational databases organize data about entities in tables,

1479
01:58:07.247 --> 01:58:12.314
with rows representing instances of entities and columns of attributes. This

1480
01:58:12.314 --> 01:58:13.980
probably sounds familiar.

1481
01:58:13.980 --> 01:58:20.080
Data frames, flat files, and many Excel sheets arrange data similarly. Relational

1482
01:58:20.080 --> 01:58:25.652
databases differ in that tables can be linked or related via unique record

1483
01:58:25.652 --> 01:58:31.902
identifiers or keys. Databases handle more data and support more simultaneous users

1484
01:58:31.902 --> 01:58:38.002
than spreadsheets or flat files. They also offer more data quality controls, like

1485
01:58:38.002 --> 01:58:40.110
enforcing column data types.

1486
01:58:40.110 --> 01:58:46.661
And we interface with databases via a specific language, Structured Query Language,

1487
01:58:46.661 --> 01:58:52.423
or SQL. Common relational databases include Microsoft SQL Server, Oracle,

1488
01:58:52.423 --> 01:58:58.974
PostgreSQL, and SQLite, which this course uses. Unlike the others, SQLite databases

1489
01:58:58.974 --> 01:59:00.000
are stored as

1490
01:59:00.000 --> 01:59:05.940
regular, self-contained computer files, just as CSVs and Excel files are, making

1491
01:59:05.940 --> 01:59:12.103
them great for sharing data. Reading data from a database is a two-step process. We

1492
01:59:12.103 --> 01:59:18.192
first make a way to connect to the database, then query it with SQL and Pandas. To

1493
01:59:18.192 --> 01:59:24.281
do this, we'll use the SQLAlchemy library, which has tools to work with many major

1494
01:59:24.281 --> 01:59:26.880
relational databases. Specifically,

1495
01:59:26.880 --> 01:59:32.905
we'll use SQLAlchemy's CreateEngine function. CreateEngine takes a string URL to a

1496
01:59:32.905 --> 01:59:39.004
database and makes an engine object that manages database connection. URLs follow a

1497
01:59:39.004 --> 01:59:45.029
pattern that varies slightly depending on the database. For SQLite, the pattern is

1498
01:59:45.029 --> 01:59:50.980
SQLite, colon, three forward slashes, then the database file name. Once we create

1499
01:59:50.980 --> 01:59:56.050
the database engine, we can pull data with Panda's read SQL function.

1500
01:59:56.050 --> 02:00:01.676
Read SQL needs two arguments. The first is a string of either a SQL query or, to

1501
02:00:01.676 --> 02:00:07.512
load a whole table, just the table name. The second argument is a way to connect to

1502
02:00:07.512 --> 02:00:13.208
the database. We'll supply the engine we made here. Let's take a minute to review

1503
02:00:13.208 --> 02:00:18.693
SQL SELECT statements, which are used to query databases. The basic syntax is,

1504
02:00:18.693 --> 02:00:24.107
select column names from table name. This will get all rows for the specified

1505
02:00:24.107 --> 02:00:24.670
columns.

1506
02:00:24.670 --> 02:00:30.492
To get all rows and all columns, use SELECT star from table name. A note about code

1507
02:00:30.492 --> 02:00:36.244
style. Keywords like SELECT and FROM are not case sensitive, but it's conventional

1508
02:00:36.244 --> 02:00:41.925
to type them in all capital letters. It's also best practice to mark the end of a

1509
02:00:41.925 --> 02:00:47.537
SQL statement with a semicolon. Now let's put all this together to fetch weather

1510
02:00:47.537 --> 02:00:53.218
data from a SQLite database containing information about New York City. We import

1511
02:00:53.218 --> 02:00:54.130
pandas as pd.

1512
02:00:54.130 --> 02:01:00.208
Plus the createEngine function from SQLAlchemy. Then, we make the engine object,

1513
02:01:00.208 --> 02:01:06.058
passing the database URL string SQLite colon slash slash slash data.db to the

1514
02:01:06.058 --> 02:01:12.211
createEngine function. Since we want everything in the weather table, we can make

1515
02:01:12.211 --> 02:01:18.365
the first argument to read SQL the table name as a string. Or, we can use the SQL

1516
02:01:18.365 --> 02:01:21.100
statement. Select star from weather.

1517
02:01:21.100 --> 02:01:26.562
As the first argument. We also supply the engine object as the second argument. Then

1518
02:01:26.562 --> 02:01:31.959
we can check out our new data frame. And that's the basic workflow to get data from

1519
02:01:31.959 --> 02:01:37.421
a relational database with pandas. Now it's your turn to practice. Now that you know

1520
02:01:37.421 --> 02:01:42.493
how to connect to a database and query it with pandas, let's focus on refining

1521
02:01:42.493 --> 02:01:47.759
imports with different SQL queries. Panda's read SQL function has fewer arguments

1522
02:01:47.759 --> 02:01:49.580
than read Excel or read CSV.

1523
02:01:49.580 --> 02:01:55.825
But SQL lets you customize data imports in even more ways. The last lesson mentioned

1524
02:01:55.825 --> 02:02:01.996
one such way. Specifying columns in a SELECT statement lets you load only variables

1525
02:02:01.996 --> 02:02:07.869
you are interested in studying, akin to use calls in ReadExcel and ReadCSV. The

1526
02:02:07.869 --> 02:02:13.742
statement SELECT DATE FROM WEATHER, for example, gets only the date and average

1527
02:02:13.742 --> 02:02:16.270
temperatures in the weather table.

1528
02:02:16.270 --> 02:02:22.606
The other common way to selectively get data with SQL is by using a WHERE clause to

1529
02:02:22.606 --> 02:02:28.332
filter rows. The syntax looks like, select columns from table where a given

1530
02:02:28.332 --> 02:02:34.592
condition or conditions are met. To import records based on numeric values, we can

1531
02:02:34.592 --> 02:02:40.928
use standard operators to compare numbers equals, greater than, and greater than or

1532
02:02:40.928 --> 02:02:45.890
equal to, less than, and less than or equal to, and not equal to.

1533
02:02:45.890 --> 02:02:51.749
Note that SQL's equality and inequality operators differ from Python. The weather

1534
02:02:51.749 --> 02:02:57.752
data contains Fahrenheit temperatures, so the SQL query to get all days with a high

1535
02:02:57.752 --> 02:03:03.828
temperature above freezing would be, select star from weather, where Tmax is greater

1536
02:03:03.828 --> 02:03:09.542
than 32. WHERE clauses can also filter text. To match a string exactly, use the

1537
02:03:09.542 --> 02:03:13.810
equal sign, followed by the text to match in single quotes.

1538
02:03:13.810 --> 02:03:20.122
Note that string matching is case-sensitive. To get call records about incidents in

1539
02:03:20.122 --> 02:03:26.358
the borough of Brooklyn, for example, the query would be, select star from HPD 311

1540
02:03:26.358 --> 02:03:32.593
calls where borough equals Brooklyn, with Brooklyn in single quotes. Let's combine

1541
02:03:32.593 --> 02:03:38.829
SQL and Python to get Brooklyn calls. As before, we import pandas and SQLAlchemy's

1542
02:03:38.829 --> 02:03:43.620
createengine function. We pass createengine the URL to data.db.

1543
02:03:43.620 --> 02:03:49.050
And assign the result to engine. Since our SQL queries are getting more complicated,

1544
02:03:49.050 --> 02:03:54.286
we'll write out the query first and assign it to the variable query. Wrapping the

1545
02:03:54.286 --> 02:03:59.522
string in triple quotes lets us split it between multiple lines so it's easier to

1546
02:03:59.522 --> 02:04:04.500
read. Then we pass the query and engine to read SQL. When we check the unique

1547
02:04:04.500 --> 02:04:09.930
borough values in the resulting data frame, we see there are only Brooklyn calls. We

1548
02:04:09.930 --> 02:04:12.580
can even combine conditions with SQLs and

1549
02:04:12.580 --> 02:04:18.702
and OR operators, and returns only records where all conditions are true. For

1550
02:04:18.702 --> 02:04:25.381
example, if we create the query, select star from HPG 311 calls where borough equals

1551
02:04:25.381 --> 02:04:31.742
Bronx and complaint type equals plumbing, then pass the query and engine to read

1552
02:04:31.742 --> 02:04:38.103
SQL, we get the 2016 call records about plumbing from the Bronx. The OR operator

1553
02:04:38.103 --> 02:04:41.920
returns records with at least one MET condition.

1554
02:04:41.920 --> 02:04:47.946
If we change our query to get records that are about plumbing or water leak, we get

1555
02:04:47.946 --> 02:04:53.754
10,684 records that meet one or both conditions. As you probably noticed, SQL is

1556
02:04:53.754 --> 02:04:59.562
integral to building pipelines to databases, even when you're working in Python.

1557
02:04:59.562 --> 02:05:05.588
Now, it's your turn to practice. The SQL we've used so far mimics the functionality

1558
02:05:05.588 --> 02:05:10.960
of Panda's keyword arguments like use calls and skip rows. In this lesson,

1559
02:05:10.960 --> 02:05:17.099
will take advantage of SQL's features to wrangle data in ways that cannot be done at

1560
02:05:17.099 --> 02:05:23.237
the import stage using pandas alone. In an analysis, you might need unique values in

1561
02:05:23.237 --> 02:05:29.230
a column or unique combinations of values across several columns. Examples include

1562
02:05:29.230 --> 02:05:35.222
getting unique values to check data quality and creating crosswalks between values

1563
02:05:35.222 --> 02:05:37.780
to combine datasets. Alternatively,

1564
02:05:37.780 --> 02:05:43.564
data might have duplicate records that should be excluded. Pandas has tools to do

1565
02:05:43.564 --> 02:05:49.134
this wrangling in data frames, but it can be done with SQL during import using

1566
02:05:49.134 --> 02:05:54.918
select distinct. The syntax is select distinct column names from table. To remove

1567
02:05:54.918 --> 02:06:00.702
duplicate records, select distinct on all columns with the query, select distinct

1568
02:06:00.702 --> 02:06:06.415
star from table. For another example, if we wanted to map buildings with housing

1569
02:06:06.415 --> 02:06:07.200
complaints,

1570
02:06:07.200 --> 02:06:13.366
we could use the query, select distinct incident address, borough, from HPD 311

1571
02:06:13.366 --> 02:06:19.610
calls to get unique street addresses and boroughs. Other times, you might not be

1572
02:06:19.610 --> 02:06:26.166
interested in the details of individual records, particularly when visualizing data.

1573
02:06:26.166 --> 02:06:32.176
You can query the database directly for descriptive statistics with aggregate

1574
02:06:32.176 --> 02:06:36.000
functions like sum, average, max, min, and count.

1575
02:06:36.000 --> 02:06:41.808
The first four functions all take a single column name in parentheses. For example,

1576
02:06:41.808 --> 02:06:47.546
the query, select average tmax from weather, with tmax in parentheses, returns the

1577
02:06:47.546 --> 02:06:53.284
average daily high temperature. Count is a little different. While it can accept a

1578
02:06:53.284 --> 02:06:59.022
single column name, you can do things like get the number of rows that fit a query

1579
02:06:59.022 --> 02:07:04.550
with count star, or even get the number of unique values in a column with count

1580
02:07:04.550 --> 02:07:05.180
distinct.

1581
02:07:05.180 --> 02:07:10.545
And the column name. Aggregate functions return a single number on their own. More

1582
02:07:10.545 --> 02:07:15.975
likely, you want data summarized by categories such as average high temperatures by

1583
02:07:15.975 --> 02:07:21.143
month or counts of plumbing complaints by borough. In that case, add a group by

1584
02:07:21.143 --> 02:07:26.508
clause after the select statement and where clauses. Remember to select the column

1585
02:07:26.508 --> 02:07:31.938
you're grouping by as well as the aggregate function. Otherwise, you'll end up with

1586
02:07:31.938 --> 02:07:34.620
unlabeled summary figures for each group.

1587
02:07:34.620 --> 02:07:40.423
The query to get counts of plumbing complaints by burrow, for example, would be

1588
02:07:40.423 --> 02:07:46.373
select burrow count star from HPD 311 calls where complaint type equals plumbing,

1589
02:07:46.373 --> 02:07:52.543
group by burrow. Let's see what the results of that query look like in a data frame.

1590
02:07:52.543 --> 02:07:58.493
With the necessary libraries imported, we create the engine, write out the query,

1591
02:07:58.493 --> 02:08:02.900
then pass the query and engine to Panda's read SQL function.

1592
02:08:02.900 --> 02:08:08.314
When we check the results, we see we have a neat summary data frame ready for

1593
02:08:08.314 --> 02:08:14.079
plotting. This has been a SQL-heavy lesson, with Python as the glue connecting SQL

1594
02:08:14.079 --> 02:08:19.775
queries to data visualization and analysis libraries. Working in two languages at

1595
02:08:19.775 --> 02:08:25.681
once is tricky at first, but it's a useful skill, and it gets easier. One feature of

1596
02:08:25.681 --> 02:08:31.446
relational databases is that tables can be linked to one another via unique record

1597
02:08:31.446 --> 02:08:32.290
identifiers.

1598
02:08:32.290 --> 02:08:37.624
Or keys. This lets users combine tables to custom datasets with SQL join, which

1599
02:08:37.624 --> 02:08:42.756
we'll explore in this lesson. As mentioned, records typically have keys that

1600
02:08:42.756 --> 02:08:48.226
uniquely identify them. At their simplest, keys can be automatically assigned row

1601
02:08:48.226 --> 02:08:53.290
numbers, like in the 3-1-1 call data, but they can also carry meaning, like

1602
02:08:53.290 --> 02:08:58.760
university course numbers. When records include other tables' keys, you can bring

1603
02:08:58.760 --> 02:08:59.570
in, or join,

1604
02:08:59.570 --> 02:09:05.282
data from the reference table. For example, given a column of instructor ID numbers

1605
02:09:05.282 --> 02:09:10.788
and a professor table, we can then join in professors' names. When building data

1606
02:09:10.788 --> 02:09:16.363
pipelines, this means you're not limited to working with a single table's column.

1607
02:09:16.363 --> 02:09:21.594
Let's join weather data to 311 call records to study if certain problems are

1608
02:09:21.594 --> 02:09:27.100
exacerbated by weather conditions. Both tables contain date columns as text with

1609
02:09:27.100 --> 02:09:29.440
unique dates in the weather table.

1610
02:09:29.440 --> 02:09:35.620
So they'll be the join key. First, we select star from HPD 311 calls to get all

1611
02:09:35.620 --> 02:09:42.191
columns there. Star will also get all columns from weather when we join it. Then, we

1612
02:09:42.191 --> 02:09:48.527
join weather on HPD 311 calls dot created date equals weather dot date, which are

1613
02:09:48.527 --> 02:09:54.863
the key column. We use dot notation to specify the table and column when querying

1614
02:09:54.863 --> 02:09:58.540
multiple tables. Two things to note here. Join.

1615
02:09:58.540 --> 02:10:04.407
By default, only returns records with key values that appear in both tables, and key

1616
02:10:04.407 --> 02:10:10.274
columns must be the same data type or nothing will match. We can incorporate a WHERE

1617
02:10:10.274 --> 02:10:15.931
clause after the join to refine the dataset. Here, we filter the data to focus on

1618
02:10:15.931 --> 02:10:21.658
heat and hot water calls, which probably spike in cold weather. It's even possible

1619
02:10:21.658 --> 02:10:25.220
to summarize data and then join additional columns.

1620
02:10:25.220 --> 02:10:31.333
Imagine we wanted to compare call counts by borough against census data about

1621
02:10:31.333 --> 02:10:37.763
population and housing in another table, borough census. Let's build the query in

1622
02:10:37.763 --> 02:10:44.034
parts. We first get call counts by borough by selecting hpd311calls.borough and

1623
02:10:44.034 --> 02:10:50.464
count star from hpd311calls, grouping by hpd311calls.borough. Because both tables

1624
02:10:50.464 --> 02:10:54.910
have borough columns, we have to specify the table here.

1625
02:10:54.910 --> 02:11:00.410
The borough census table has more columns than we need, so we also select its total

1626
02:11:00.410 --> 02:11:05.645
population and housing units columns. Notice that we don't have to list borough

1627
02:11:05.645 --> 02:11:10.880
census in the from clause. Finally, we add the join clause between the from and

1628
02:11:10.880 --> 02:11:16.048
group by clauses, joining on the borough column. Let's pass this query and the

1629
02:11:16.048 --> 02:11:21.217
database engine to read SQL and check out the results. Looks like the Bronx is

1630
02:11:21.217 --> 02:11:24.530
overrepresented in our call data. As you can tell,

1631
02:11:24.530 --> 02:11:30.129
SQL queries can get complicated. Let's review the order of keywords. First comes the

1632
02:11:30.129 --> 02:11:35.394
SELECT statement, including aggregate functions, and the table you're selecting

1633
02:11:35.394 --> 02:11:40.993
from. Then a JOIN clause, if there is one. Then the WHERE clause, if any. Last comes

1634
02:11:40.993 --> 02:11:46.591
GROUP BY. This is just the beginning when it comes to using SQL plus Pandas to build

1635
02:11:46.591 --> 02:11:51.790
data pipelines. But enough of me talking about SQL. It's your turn. Until now.

1636
02:11:51.790 --> 02:11:57.141
You've built pipelines to data in tabular formats. In this chapter, we'll shift our

1637
02:11:57.141 --> 02:12:02.557
focus to data in JavaScript Object Notation, or JSON data. As the name implies, JSON

1638
02:12:02.557 --> 02:12:07.909
is based on JavaScript, but popular programming languages have versions of the data

1639
02:12:07.909 --> 02:12:13.002
structures JSONs use, making them easy for programs to generate and parse while

1640
02:12:13.002 --> 02:12:18.160
still being human-readable. And because of JavaScript's role in web development,

1641
02:12:18.160 --> 02:12:23.809
JSON is a common format for transmitting data through the web. Unlike data frames,

1642
02:12:23.809 --> 02:12:29.320
JSON data is not tabular. This makes for more efficient data storage. If a value

1643
02:12:29.320 --> 02:12:35.038
doesn't exist for a record, the attribute can be omitted instead of storing it with

1644
02:12:35.038 --> 02:12:40.273
a null value. In other words, records don't all have to have the same set of

1645
02:12:40.273 --> 02:12:45.922
attributes. Instead, data is organized to collections of objects. Objects resemble

1646
02:12:45.922 --> 02:12:47.300
Python dictionaries.

1647
02:12:47.300 --> 02:12:53.575
They're enclosed in curly braces and contain attribute value pairs. One last feature

1648
02:12:53.575 --> 02:12:59.626
of JSONs is that they can be nested. Values themselves can be objects or lists of

1649
02:12:59.626 --> 02:13:05.751
objects. You can guess the panda's function to load a JSON into a data frame. It's

1650
02:13:05.751 --> 02:13:11.951
readJSON. ReadJSON takes a string of the path to the JSON to load. This path can be

1651
02:13:11.951 --> 02:13:17.180
to a file saved on a computer or a URL ending in .JSON. Alternatively,

1652
02:13:17.180 --> 02:13:22.970
You can supply JSON data directly as a string. As with flat files and spreadsheets,

1653
02:13:22.970 --> 02:13:28.690
Pandas guesses attribute data types, but you can specify them with a dictionary of

1654
02:13:28.690 --> 02:13:34.549
column names and values and the dtype argument. JSON data can be laid out in various

1655
02:13:34.549 --> 02:13:40.130
ways, so there is an orient keyword argument to flag uncommon layout. Since JSON

1656
02:13:40.130 --> 02:13:45.780
data isn't tabular, Pandas makes assumptions about how it's arranged or oriented.

1657
02:13:45.780 --> 02:13:50.790
To load it into a data frame, Pandas automatically detects record and column

1658
02:13:50.790 --> 02:13:56.064
orientation, which you'll encounter most often. Let's see what they look like. A

1659
02:13:56.064 --> 02:14:01.602
record-oriented JSON consists of a list of dictionaries, each translating to a table

1660
02:14:01.602 --> 02:14:06.876
record. For example, this JSON of causes of death from New York City's open data

1661
02:14:06.876 --> 02:14:12.414
site is record-oriented. To reduce the file size by not repeating attribute names, a

1662
02:14:12.414 --> 02:14:14.260
JSON may be column-oriented.

1663
02:14:14.260 --> 02:14:19.972
The entire JSON is a dictionary. Keys are column names. Values are lists of values

1664
02:14:19.972 --> 02:14:25.476
for that column, or dictionaries of row indices and column values, like in this

1665
02:14:25.476 --> 02:14:30.770
rearranged version of the death causes data. However, other orientations are

1666
02:14:30.770 --> 02:14:36.622
possible. This version of the death data is split-oriented, with different lists for

1667
02:14:36.622 --> 02:14:41.150
column names, indices, and values. Let's load it to a data frame.

1668
02:14:41.150 --> 02:14:46.499
We import pandas, then load the data with readjson, specifying orientation by

1669
02:14:46.499 --> 02:14:52.057
passing one of a set of documented string values to the orient keyword argument.

1670
02:14:52.057 --> 02:14:57.407
When we print the data, it looks like it loaded correctly. And that's a quick

1671
02:14:57.407 --> 02:15:03.243
introduction to JSON. We'll dive deeper in later lessons. But now, it's your turn to

1672
02:15:03.243 --> 02:15:08.870
practice working. You now know how to load JSON data into pandas, given a path or

1673
02:15:08.870 --> 02:15:10.190
URL to a JSON file.

1674
02:15:10.190 --> 02:15:15.914
In this lesson, we'll turn our attention to working with Web Application Programming

1675
02:15:15.914 --> 02:15:21.639
Interfaces, or APIs, the most common source of JSON data. An application programming

1676
02:15:21.639 --> 02:15:27.159
interface is a defined way for an application to communicate with other programs,

1677
02:15:27.159 --> 02:15:32.815
and vice versa. They let programmers get data from an application without having to

1678
02:15:32.815 --> 02:15:38.267
know about that application's database architecture. One caveat, APIs are shared

1679
02:15:38.267 --> 02:15:38.880
resources

1680
02:15:38.880 --> 02:15:44.424
and often limit how much data you can get in a specified time frame. Using an API to

1681
02:15:44.424 --> 02:15:49.441
get data is like using a catalog to order products. The catalog shows what's

1682
02:15:49.441 --> 02:15:54.853
available and provides order instructions. You send a properly formed order to the

1683
02:15:54.853 --> 02:15:59.869
right address and get back what you asked for. Similarly, an API provides an

1684
02:15:59.869 --> 02:16:05.414
endpoint to send requests to, and documentation describes what a request should look

1685
02:16:05.414 --> 02:16:07.790
like, such as parameters to include.

1686
02:16:07.790 --> 02:16:14.010
While there are Python libraries geared towards popular APIs, we'll use the requests

1687
02:16:14.010 --> 02:16:20.155
library in this course. Requests lets users send and get data from any URL, so it's

1688
02:16:20.155 --> 02:16:26.375
not tied to any particular API. The function to retrieve data from a URL, logically,

1689
02:16:26.375 --> 02:16:31.780
is requests get. Requests get takes a string of the URL to get data from.

1690
02:16:31.780 --> 02:16:37.404
And has optional keyword arguments that are useful for working with APIs. The params

1691
02:16:37.404 --> 02:16:42.827
keyword lets you pass a dictionary of parameter names and values to customize API

1692
02:16:42.827 --> 02:16:48.251
requests. The headers keyword also takes a dictionary of names and values. If the

1693
02:16:48.251 --> 02:16:53.540
API you're using requires a user authentication key, it should be passed in the

1694
02:16:53.540 --> 02:16:58.160
header. The result is a response object containing data and metadata.

1695
02:16:58.160 --> 02:17:03.978
We need to use the response's JSON method to get just the data. Importantly, the

1696
02:17:03.978 --> 02:17:09.942
JSON method returns a dictionary, which readjson can't parse. It expects a string.

1697
02:17:09.942 --> 02:17:15.833
To load the data to a data frame, we need to use pd data frame instead. We'll use

1698
02:17:15.833 --> 02:17:21.942
requests to work with data from Yelp. Yelp lets users submit ratings and reviews for

1699
02:17:21.942 --> 02:17:27.760
businesses and makes that data available via its APIs. In the API documentation,

1700
02:17:27.760 --> 02:17:33.610
We see the endpoint URL, optional and required parameters to use in a request, and

1701
02:17:33.610 --> 02:17:39.461
even a sample response. Note that if we want business details, the data we need is

1702
02:17:39.461 --> 02:17:45.453
nested under the businesses key. Let's get data from the API about bookstores in San

1703
02:17:45.453 --> 02:17:51.090
Francisco, California. We import pandas and the requests library, then create a

1704
02:17:51.090 --> 02:17:53.230
variable for the API endpoint.

1705
02:17:53.230 --> 02:17:59.023
The API documentation indicated required parameters and authorization, so we also

1706
02:17:59.023 --> 02:18:04.316
set up a dictionary of request parameter fields and values, plus a headers

1707
02:18:04.316 --> 02:18:10.253
dictionary of authorization info. API keys are strings used to identify the program

1708
02:18:10.253 --> 02:18:16.189
calling the API and confirm it can make the call. They're sensitive information, so

1709
02:18:16.189 --> 02:18:20.910
it's hidden behind the API key variable here. We pass the API URL.

1710
02:18:20.910 --> 02:18:26.447
Params, and headers to requests get and store the result as response. Since

1711
02:18:26.447 --> 02:18:32.354
responses contain data and metadata, we use the JSON method to isolate the data.

1712
02:18:32.354 --> 02:18:38.187
Let's check it before loading it to a data frame. We have a dictionary with the

