2553
03:30:12.506 --> 03:30:14.860
finally if you ever need to update your code

2554
03:30:14.860 --> 03:30:19.277
Functions that each have a single responsibility make it easier to predict how

2555
03:30:19.277 --> 03:30:23.921
changes in one place will affect the rest of the code. Repeated code and functions

2556
03:30:23.921 --> 03:30:28.621
that do more than one thing are examples of code smells, which are indications that

2557
03:30:28.621 --> 03:30:33.265
you may need to refactor. Refactoring is the process of improving code by changing

2558
03:30:33.265 --> 03:30:37.852
it a little bit at a time. This process is well described in Martin Fowler's book

2559
03:30:37.852 --> 03:30:41.760
Refactoring, which is a good read for any aspiring software engineer.

2560
03:30:41.760 --> 03:30:47.021
Now you can do some refactoring of your own in the exercise. The way that Python

2561
03:30:47.021 --> 03:30:52.480
passes information to function is different from any other language. It is referred

2562
03:30:52.480 --> 03:30:57.873
to as pass by assignment, which I will explain in this lesson. Let's say we have a

2563
03:30:57.873 --> 03:31:03.266
function foo that takes a list and sets the first value of the list to 99. Then we

2564
03:31:03.266 --> 03:31:08.461
set myList to the value 123 and pass it to foo. What do you expect the value of

2565
03:31:08.461 --> 03:31:10.500
myList to be after calling foo?

2566
03:31:10.500 --> 03:31:16.091
If you said 99, 2, 3, then you are right. Lists in Python are mutable objects,

2567
03:31:16.091 --> 03:31:22.041
meaning that they can be changed. Now let's say we have another function, bar, that

2568
03:31:22.041 --> 03:31:27.704
takes an argument and adds 90 to it. Then we assign the value 3 to the variable

2569
03:31:27.704 --> 03:31:33.725
myvar and call bar with myvar as the argument. What do you expect the value of myvar

2570
03:31:33.725 --> 03:31:39.603
to be after we've called bar? If you said 3, you're right. In Python, integers are

2571
03:31:39.603 --> 03:31:40.320
immutable.

2572
03:31:40.320 --> 03:31:46.109
Meaning they can't be changed. Let's look at another example to understand what's

2573
03:31:46.109 --> 03:31:51.755
going on. Imagine that this gray bar is your computer's memory. When we set the

2574
03:31:51.755 --> 03:31:57.758
variable a equal to the list 123, the Python interpreter says, okay, now a points to

2575
03:31:57.758 --> 03:32:03.761
this location in memory. Then if we type b equals a, the interpreter says, okay, now

2576
03:32:03.761 --> 03:32:09.550
b points to whatever a is pointing to. So if we were to append 4 to the end of a,

2577
03:32:09.550 --> 03:32:14.796
both variables get it, because there is only one list. Likewise, if we append 5 to

2578
03:32:14.796 --> 03:32:20.042
b, both variables get it. However, if we assign a to a different object in memory,

2579
03:32:20.042 --> 03:32:25.416
that does not change where b is pointing. Now, things that happen to a are no longer

2580
03:32:25.416 --> 03:32:30.790
happening to b, and vice versa. How does this relate to the example functions we saw

2581
03:32:30.790 --> 03:32:35.844
earlier? When we assign a list to the variable myList, it sets up a location in

2582
03:32:35.844 --> 03:32:36.740
memory for it.

2583
03:32:36.740 --> 03:32:42.202
Then, when we pass myList to the function foo, the parameter x gets assigned to that

2584
03:32:42.202 --> 03:32:47.664
same location. So, when the function modifies the thing that x points to, it is also

2585
03:32:47.664 --> 03:32:52.670
modifying the thing that myList points to. In the other example, we created a

2586
03:32:52.670 --> 03:32:58.067
variable myvar and assigned it the value 3. Then, we passed it to the function bar,

2587
03:32:58.067 --> 03:33:03.399
which caused the argument x to point to the same place myvar is pointing. But, the

2588
03:33:03.399 --> 03:33:05.870
bar function assigns x to a new value.

2589
03:33:05.870 --> 03:33:10.348
So the myvar variable isn't touched. In fact, there is no way in Python to have

2590
03:33:10.348 --> 03:33:15.110
changed x or myvar directly because integers are immutable variables. There are only

2591
03:33:15.110 --> 03:33:19.815
a few immutable data types in Python because almost everything is represented as an

2592
03:33:19.815 --> 03:33:24.123
object. The only way to tell if something is mutable is to see if there is a

2593
03:33:24.123 --> 03:33:28.431
function or method that will change the object without assigning it to a new

2594
03:33:28.431 --> 03:33:33.022
variable. Finally, here is a thing that can get you in trouble. Foo is a function

2595
03:33:33.022 --> 03:33:35.630
that appends the value 1 to the end of a list.

2596
03:33:35.630 --> 03:33:40.456
But whoever wrote this function gave the argument an empty list as a default value.

2597
03:33:40.456 --> 03:33:44.992
When we call foo the first time, we get what you would expect, a list with one

2598
03:33:44.992 --> 03:33:49.760
entry. But when we call foo again, the default value has already been modified. If

2599
03:33:49.760 --> 03:33:54.528
you really want a mutable variable as a default value, consider defaulting to none

2600
03:33:54.528 --> 03:33:59.354
and setting the argument in the function. You can check your understanding with the

2601
03:33:59.354 --> 03:34:04.180
following exercise. In this lesson, I'll introduce the concept of context managers.

2602
03:34:04.180 --> 03:34:08.423
And show you how to use these special kinds of functions. A context manager is a

2603
03:34:08.423 --> 03:34:12.878
type of function that sets up a context for your code to run in, runs your code, and

2604
03:34:12.878 --> 03:34:17.121
then removes the context. That's not a very helpful definition though, so let me

2605
03:34:17.121 --> 03:34:21.523
explain with an analogy. Imagine that you are throwing a fancy party and have hired

2606
03:34:21.523 --> 03:34:25.925
some caterers to provide refreshments for your guests. Before the party starts, the

2607
03:34:25.925 --> 03:34:30.273
caterers set up tables with food and drinks. Then you and your friends dance, eat,

2608
03:34:30.273 --> 03:34:32.660
and have a good time. When the party is done,

2609
03:34:32.660 --> 03:34:37.213
the caterers clean up the food and remove the tables in this analogy the caterers

2610
03:34:37.213 --> 03:34:41.765
are like a context manager first they set up a context for your party which was a

2611
03:34:41.765 --> 03:34:46.487
room full of food and drinks then they let you and your friends do whatever you want

2612
03:34:46.487 --> 03:34:51.039
this is like you being able to run your code inside the context manager's context

2613
03:34:51.039 --> 03:34:55.760
finally when the party is over the caterers clean up and remove the context that the

2614
03:34:55.760 --> 03:35:00.257
party happened in you may have used code like this before the open function is a

2615
03:35:00.257 --> 03:35:01.100
context manager

2616
03:35:01.100 --> 03:35:05.710
When you write with open, it opens a file that you can read from or write to. Then

2617
03:35:05.710 --> 03:35:10.264
it gives control back to your code so that you can perform operations on the file

2618
03:35:10.264 --> 03:35:14.762
object. In this example, we read the text of the file, store the contents of the

2619
03:35:14.762 --> 03:35:19.204
file in the variable text, and store the length of the contents in the variable

2620
03:35:19.204 --> 03:35:23.702
length. When the code inside the indented block is done, the open function makes

2621
03:35:23.702 --> 03:35:27.300
sure that the file is closed before continuing on in the script.

2622
03:35:27.300 --> 03:35:31.588
The print statement is outside of the context, so by the time it runs, the file is

2623
03:35:31.588 --> 03:35:35.981
closed. Any time you use a context manager, it will look like this. The keyword with

2624
03:35:35.981 --> 03:35:40.269
lets Python know that you are trying to enter a context. Then you call a function.

2625
03:35:40.269 --> 03:35:44.505
You can call any function that is built to work as a context manager. In the next

2626
03:35:44.505 --> 03:35:48.845
lesson, I'll show you how to write your own functions that work this way. A context

2627
03:35:48.845 --> 03:35:53.238
manager can take arguments like any normal function. You end the with statement with

2628
03:35:53.238 --> 03:35:56.480
a colon, as if you were writing a for loop or an if statement.

2629
03:35:56.480 --> 03:36:01.121
Statements in Python that have an indented block after them, like for loops, if-else

2630
03:36:01.121 --> 03:36:05.540
statements, function definitions, etc., are called compound statements. The with

2631
03:36:05.540 --> 03:36:09.849
statement is another type of compound statement. Any code that you want to run

2632
03:36:09.849 --> 03:36:14.379
inside the context that the context manager created needs to be indented. When the

2633
03:36:14.379 --> 03:36:18.964
indented block is done, the context manager gets a chance to clean up anything that

2634
03:36:18.964 --> 03:36:22.500
it needs to, like when the open context manager closed the file.

2635
03:36:22.500 --> 03:36:27.402
Some context managers want to return a value that you can use inside the context by

2636
03:36:27.402 --> 03:36:32.185
adding as and a variable name at the end of the with statement you can assign the

2637
03:36:32.185 --> 03:36:36.792
returned value to the variable name we used this ability when calling the open

2638
03:36:36.792 --> 03:36:41.694
context manager which returns a file that we can read from or write to by adding as

2639
03:36:41.694 --> 03:36:46.359
my file to the with statement we assign the file to the variable my file you'll

2640
03:36:46.359 --> 03:36:50.080
learn how to write your own context managers in the next lesson

2641
03:36:50.080 --> 03:36:54.341
For now, you can practice using them in order to understand. Now that you know how

2642
03:36:54.341 --> 03:36:58.707
to use context managers, I want to show you how to write a context manager for other

2643
03:36:58.707 --> 03:37:03.020
people to use. There are two ways to define a context manager in Python. By using a

2644
03:37:03.020 --> 03:37:07.281
class that has special double underscore enter and double underscore exit methods,

2645
03:37:07.281 --> 03:37:11.335
or by decorating a certain kind of function. This course is focused on writing

2646
03:37:11.335 --> 03:37:15.544
functions, and some of you may not have been introduced to the concept of classes

2647
03:37:15.544 --> 03:37:18.610
yet, so I will only present the function-based method here.

2648
03:37:18.610 --> 03:37:22.898
There are five parts to creating a context manager. First, you need to define a

2649
03:37:22.898 --> 03:37:27.404
function. Next, you can add any setup code your context needs. This is not required

2650
03:37:27.404 --> 03:37:31.746
though. Third, you must use the yield keyword to signal to Python that this is a

2651
03:37:31.746 --> 03:37:36.252
special kind of function. I will explain what this keyword means in a moment. After

2652
03:37:36.252 --> 03:37:40.595
the yield statement, you can add any teardown code that you need to clean up the

2653
03:37:40.595 --> 03:37:45.100
context. Finally, you must decorate the function with the context manager decorator

2654
03:37:45.100 --> 03:37:46.620
from the context lib module.

2655
03:37:46.620 --> 03:37:50.629
You might not know what a decorator is, and that's okay. We will discuss decorators

2656
03:37:50.629 --> 03:37:54.687
in depth in the next chapter of this course. For now, the important thing to know is

2657
03:37:54.687 --> 03:37:58.455
that you write the at symbol followed by contextlib.contextManager on the line

2658
03:37:58.455 --> 03:38:02.513
immediately above your contextManager function. The yield keyword may also be new to

2659
03:38:02.513 --> 03:38:06.425
you. When you write this word, it means that you are going to return a value, but

2660
03:38:06.425 --> 03:38:10.000
you expect to finish the rest of the function at some point in the future.

2661
03:38:10.000 --> 03:38:14.946
The function at some point in the future. The value that your context manager yields

2662
03:38:14.946 --> 03:38:19.893
can be assigned to a variable in the with statement by adding as variable name. Here

2663
03:38:19.893 --> 03:38:24.721
we've assigned the value 42 that my context yields to the variable foo. By running

2664
03:38:24.721 --> 03:38:29.667
this code you can see that after the context block is done executing the rest of the

2665
03:38:29.667 --> 03:38:34.496
my context function gets run printing goodbye. Some of you may recognize the yield

2666
03:38:34.496 --> 03:38:37.970
keyword as a thing that gets used when creating generators.

2667
03:38:37.970 --> 03:38:42.595
In fact, a context manager function is technically a generator that yields a single

2668
03:38:42.595 --> 03:38:46.997
value. The ability for a function to yield control and know that it will get to

2669
03:38:46.997 --> 03:38:51.622
finish running later is what makes context managers so useful. This context manager

2670
03:38:51.622 --> 03:38:56.192
is an example of code that accesses a database. Like most context managers, it has

2671
03:38:56.192 --> 03:39:00.872
some setup code that runs before the function yields. This context manager uses that

2672
03:39:00.872 --> 03:39:02.990
setup code to connect to the database.

2673
03:39:02.990 --> 03:39:07.490
Most context managers also have some teardown or cleanup code when they get control

2674
03:39:07.490 --> 03:39:11.719
back after yielding. This one uses the teardown section to disconnect from the

2675
03:39:11.719 --> 03:39:16.218
database. This setup teardown behavior allows a context manager to hide things like

2676
03:39:16.218 --> 03:39:20.718
connecting and disconnecting from a database so that a programmer using the context

2677
03:39:20.718 --> 03:39:24.947
manager can just perform operations on the database without worrying about the

2678
03:39:24.947 --> 03:39:29.447
underlying detail. The database context manager that we've been looking at yields a

2679
03:39:29.447 --> 03:39:30.260
specific value.

2680
03:39:30.260 --> 03:39:34.296
The database connection, that can be used in the context block. Some context

2681
03:39:34.296 --> 03:39:38.704
managers don't yield an explicit value. Indir is a context manager that changes the

2682
03:39:38.704 --> 03:39:42.900
current working directory to a specific path and then changes it back after the

2683
03:39:42.900 --> 03:39:47.361
context block is done. It does not need to return anything with its yield statement.

2684
03:39:47.361 --> 03:39:51.822
Now it's your turn to practice writing. You've learned a lot about using and writing

2685
03:39:51.822 --> 03:39:56.230
context managers. In this lesson, we'll cover nested contexts, handling errors, and

2686
03:39:56.230 --> 03:39:58.620
how to know when to create a context manager.

2687
03:39:58.620 --> 03:40:03.228
Imagine you are implementing this copy function that copies the contents of one file

2688
03:40:03.228 --> 03:40:07.725
to another file. One way you could write this function would be to open the source

2689
03:40:07.725 --> 03:40:11.894
file, store the contents of the file in the contents variable, then open the

2690
03:40:11.894 --> 03:40:16.337
destination file and write the contents to it. This approach works fine until you

2691
03:40:16.337 --> 03:40:20.890
try to copy a file that is too large to fit in memory. What would be ideal is if we

2692
03:40:20.890 --> 03:40:25.443
could open both files at once and copy over one line at a time. Fortunately for us,

2693
03:40:25.443 --> 03:40:28.240
the file object that the OpenContextManager returns

2694
03:40:28.240 --> 03:40:33.322
can be iterated over in a for loop the statement for line in my file here will read

2695
03:40:33.322 --> 03:40:38.220
in the contents of my file one line at a time until the end of the file so going

2696
03:40:38.220 --> 03:40:43.240
back to our copy function if we could open both files at once we could read in the

2697
03:40:43.240 --> 03:40:48.200
source file line by line and write each line out to the destination as we go this

2698
03:40:48.200 --> 03:40:53.098
would let us copy the file without worrying about how big it is in Python nested

2699
03:40:53.098 --> 03:40:57.200
with statements are perfectly legal this code opens the source file

2700
03:40:57.200 --> 03:41:02.077
and then opens the destination file inside the source file's context. That means

2701
03:41:02.077 --> 03:41:07.197
code that runs inside the context created by opening the destination file has access

2702
03:41:07.197 --> 03:41:12.135
to both the f underscore source and the f underscore dest file objects. So we are

2703
03:41:12.135 --> 03:41:17.195
able to copy the file over one line at a time like we wanted to. One thing you will

2704
03:41:17.195 --> 03:41:21.889
want to think about when writing your context managers is what happens if the

2705
03:41:21.889 --> 03:41:26.400
programmer who uses your context manager writes code that causes an error?

2706
03:41:26.400 --> 03:41:31.269
Imagine you've written this function that lets someone connect to the printer. The

2707
03:41:31.269 --> 03:41:36.198
printer only allows one connection at a time, so it is imperative that p.disconnect

2708
03:41:36.198 --> 03:41:41.127
gets called, or else no one else will be able to print. Someone decides to use your

2709
03:41:41.127 --> 03:41:45.818
git printer function to print the text of their document. However, they weren't

2710
03:41:45.818 --> 03:41:50.688
paying attention and accidentally typed txt instead of text. This will raise a key

2711
03:41:50.688 --> 03:41:55.617
error because txt is not in the doc dictionary, and that means p.disconnect doesn't

2712
03:41:55.617 --> 03:41:56.270
get called.

2713
03:41:56.270 --> 03:42:00.917
So what can we do? You may be familiar with the try statement. It allows you to

2714
03:42:00.917 --> 03:42:05.858
write code that might raise an error inside the try block and catch the error inside

2715
03:42:05.858 --> 03:42:10.328
the accept block. You can choose to ignore the error or re-raise it. The try

2716
03:42:10.328 --> 03:42:14.975
statement also allows you to add a finally block. This is code that gets run no

2717
03:42:14.975 --> 03:42:19.916
matter what, whether an exception occurred or not. The solution then is to put a try

2718
03:42:19.916 --> 03:42:24.445
statement before the yield statement in our getPrinter function and a finally

2719
03:42:24.445 --> 03:42:26.210
statement before p.disconnect.

2720
03:42:26.210 --> 03:42:30.612
When the sloppy programmer runs their code, they still get the key error, but

2721
03:42:30.612 --> 03:42:35.072
finally ensures that p.disconnect is called before the error is raised. If you

2722
03:42:35.072 --> 03:42:39.874
notice that your code is following any of these patterns, you might consider using a

2723
03:42:39.874 --> 03:42:44.562
context manager. For instance, in this lesson, we've talked about open, which uses

2724
03:42:44.562 --> 03:42:49.308
the open close pattern, and git printer, which uses the connect disconnect pattern.

2725
03:42:49.308 --> 03:42:54.110
See if you can find other instances of these patterns in code you are familiar with.

2726
03:42:54.110 --> 03:42:58.751
Test your understanding with the next few actions. In this chapter, you are going to

2727
03:42:58.751 --> 03:43:03.282
learn about decorators, a powerful way of modifying the behavior of functions. But

2728
03:43:03.282 --> 03:43:07.647
first, we need to build up some foundational concepts that will make decorators

2729
03:43:07.647 --> 03:43:12.177
easier to understand. The main thing you should take away from this lesson is that

2730
03:43:12.177 --> 03:43:16.487
functions are just like any other object in Python. They are not fundamentally

2731
03:43:16.487 --> 03:43:21.128
different from lists, dictionaries, data frames, strings, integers, floats, modules,

2732
03:43:21.128 --> 03:43:22.620
or anything else in Python.

2733
03:43:22.620 --> 03:43:27.291
And because functions are just another type of object, you can do anything to or

2734
03:43:27.291 --> 03:43:32.079
with them that you would do with any other kind of object. You can take a function

2735
03:43:32.079 --> 03:43:36.575
and assign it to a variable, like x. Then, if you wanted to, you could call x

2736
03:43:36.575 --> 03:43:41.479
instead of my function. It doesn't have to be a function you defined, either. If you

2737
03:43:41.479 --> 03:43:46.267
felt so inclined, you could assign the print function to printymcprintface and use

2738
03:43:46.267 --> 03:43:50.588
it instead of your print function. You can also add functions to a list or

2739
03:43:50.588 --> 03:43:51.230
dictionary.

2740
03:43:51.230 --> 03:43:55.393
Here, we've added the functions myFunction, open, and print to the list

2741
03:43:55.393 --> 03:44:00.259
listoffunction. We can call an element of the list and pass it arguments. Since the

2742
03:44:00.259 --> 03:44:05.008
third element of the list is the print function, it prints the string argument to

2743
03:44:05.008 --> 03:44:09.875
the console. Below that, we've added the same three functions to a dictionary under

2744
03:44:09.875 --> 03:44:14.682
the keys func1, func2, and func3. Since the print function is stored under the key

2745
03:44:14.682 --> 03:44:19.490
func3, we can reference it and use it as if we were calling the function directly.

2746
03:44:19.490 --> 03:44:23.481
Notice that when you assign a function to a variable, you do not include the

2747
03:44:23.481 --> 03:44:27.262
parentheses after the function name. This is a subtle but very important

2748
03:44:27.262 --> 03:44:31.516
distinction. When you type my function with the parentheses, you are calling that

2749
03:44:31.516 --> 03:44:35.717
function. It evaluates to the value that the function returns. However, when you

2750
03:44:35.717 --> 03:44:40.023
type my function without the parentheses, you are referencing the function itself.

2751
03:44:40.023 --> 03:44:44.119
It evaluates to a function object. Here's where things get really fun. Since a

2752
03:44:44.119 --> 03:44:47.060
function is just an object like anything else in Python,

2753
03:44:47.060 --> 03:44:51.835
you can pass one as an argument to another function. The hasDocString function

2754
03:44:51.835 --> 03:44:56.855
checks to see whether the function that is passed to it has a docstring or not. We

2755
03:44:56.855 --> 03:45:01.691
could define these two functions, no and yes, and pass them as arguments to the

2756
03:45:01.691 --> 03:45:06.221
hasDocString function. Since the no function doesn't have a docstring, the

2757
03:45:06.221 --> 03:45:11.363
hasDocString function returns false. Likewise, hasDocString returns true for the yes

2758
03:45:11.363 --> 03:45:15.220
function. Functions can also be defined inside other functions.

2759
03:45:15.220 --> 03:45:20.182
These kinds of functions are called nested functions, although you may also hear

2760
03:45:20.182 --> 03:45:25.392
them called inner functions, helper functions, or child functions. A nested function

2761
03:45:25.392 --> 03:45:30.230
can make your code easier to read. In this example, if x and y are within some

2762
03:45:30.230 --> 03:45:35.006
bounds, foo prints x times y. We can make that if statement easier to read by

2763
03:45:35.006 --> 03:45:39.906
defining an inrange function. There's also nothing stopping us from returning a

2764
03:45:39.906 --> 03:45:44.930
function. For instance, the function getFunction creates a new function, printMe,

2765
03:45:44.930 --> 03:45:49.628
and then returns it. If we assign the result of calling git function to the variable

2766
03:45:49.628 --> 03:45:54.213
new func, we are assigning the return value print me to new func. We can then call

2767
03:45:54.213 --> 03:45:58.855
new func as if it were the print me function. The way that Python treats everything

2768
03:45:58.855 --> 03:46:03.441
as an object is one of my favorite things about the language, and it gives you the

2769
03:46:03.441 --> 03:46:08.082
ability to do a lot of really complex things. In the coming lessons, we'll dig into

2770
03:46:08.082 --> 03:46:12.780
what this ability allows us to do. For now, you can check your understanding. Before

2771
03:46:12.780 --> 03:46:14.290
we can dig into decorators,

2772
03:46:14.290 --> 03:46:18.691
We need to understand how scope works in Python. Scope determines which variables

2773
03:46:18.691 --> 03:46:23.254
can be accessed at different points in your code. Names are very useful things, both

2774
03:46:23.254 --> 03:46:27.600
in Python and in the real world. For instance, this is Tom, and this is Janelle.

2775
03:46:27.600 --> 03:46:32.001
When we say Tom, we know we are talking about the person on the left, and when we

2776
03:46:32.001 --> 03:46:36.510
say Janelle, we know we are talking about the person on the right. If Janelle says,

2777
03:46:36.510 --> 03:46:41.019
Tom didn't go to work yesterday, we can be fairly sure she is talking about the Tom

2778
03:46:41.019 --> 03:46:42.160
standing next to her.

2779
03:46:42.160 --> 03:46:47.063
And not some Tom in a different country. This is sort of how scope works in

2780
03:46:47.063 --> 03:46:52.555
programming languages like Python. Python has names too, variable names. When we say

2781
03:46:52.555 --> 03:46:57.851
print x here, Python knows we mean the x that we just defined. What happens if we

2782
03:46:57.851 --> 03:47:03.277
redefine x inside the function foo though? In foo's print statement, do we mean the

2783
03:47:03.277 --> 03:47:08.573
x that equals 42 or the x that equals 7? Python applies the same logic we applied

2784
03:47:08.573 --> 03:47:11.580
with Tom and Janelle and assumes we mean the x

2785
03:47:11.580 --> 03:47:16.608
that was defined right there in the function. However, there is no y defined in the

2786
03:47:16.608 --> 03:47:21.635
function foo, so it looks outside the function for a definition when asked to print

2787
03:47:21.635 --> 03:47:26.663
y. Note that setting x equal to 42 inside the function foo doesn't change the value

2788
03:47:26.663 --> 03:47:31.448
of x that we said earlier outside the function. Python has to have strict rules

2789
03:47:31.448 --> 03:47:36.476
about which variable you are referring to when using a particular variable name. So

2790
03:47:36.476 --> 03:47:39.020
when we typed print x in the function foo,

2791
03:47:39.020 --> 03:47:43.259
the interpreter had to follow those rules to determine which X we met. First, the

2792
03:47:43.259 --> 03:47:47.394
interpreter looks in the local scope. When you are inside a function, the local

2793
03:47:47.394 --> 03:47:51.738
scope is made up of the arguments and any variables defined inside the function. If

2794
03:47:51.738 --> 03:47:56.134
the interpreter can't find the variable in the local scope, it expands its search to

2795
03:47:56.134 --> 03:48:00.478
the global scope. These are the things defined outside the function. Finally, if it

2796
03:48:00.478 --> 03:48:04.769
can't find the thing it is looking for in the global scope, the interpreter checks

2797
03:48:04.769 --> 03:48:08.590
the built-in scope. These are things that are always available in Python.

2798
03:48:08.590 --> 03:48:13.085
For instance, the print function is in the built-in scope, which is why we were able

2799
03:48:13.085 --> 03:48:17.419
to use it in our foo function. I actually skipped a level on that diagram. In the

2800
03:48:17.419 --> 03:48:21.700
case of nested functions, where one function is defined inside another function,

2801
03:48:21.700 --> 03:48:26.195
Python will check the scope of the parent function before checking the global scope.

2802
03:48:26.195 --> 03:48:30.476
This is called the non-local scope to show that it is not the local scope of the

2803
03:48:30.476 --> 03:48:34.971
child function and not the global scope. Note that Python only gives you read access

2804
03:48:34.971 --> 03:48:37.700
to variables defined outside of your current scope.

2805
03:48:37.700 --> 03:48:42.783
In foo, when we set x equal to 42, Python assumed we wanted a new variable in the

2806
03:48:42.783 --> 03:48:47.740
local scope, not the x in the global scope. If what we had really wanted was to

2807
03:48:47.740 --> 03:48:52.948
change the value of x in the global scope, then we have to declare that we mean the

2808
03:48:52.948 --> 03:48:58.156
global x by using the global keyword. Notice that when we print x after calling foo

2809
03:48:58.156 --> 03:49:03.176
now, it prints 42 instead of 7 like it used to. However, you should try to avoid

2810
03:49:03.176 --> 03:49:06.000
using global variables like this if possible.

2811
03:49:06.000 --> 03:49:10.410
Because it can make testing and debugging harder. And if we ever want to modify a

2812
03:49:10.410 --> 03:49:14.603
variable that is defined in the non-local scope, we have to use the non-local

2813
03:49:14.603 --> 03:49:19.013
keyword. It works exactly the same way as the global keyword, but it is used when

2814
03:49:19.013 --> 03:49:23.477
you are inside a nested function and you want to update a variable that is defined

2815
03:49:23.477 --> 03:49:27.833
inside your parent function. Try a few exercises to make sure you understand how

2816
03:49:27.833 --> 03:49:32.189
scope works before moving on. The last topic I need to explain before discussing

2817
03:49:32.189 --> 03:49:35.020
decorators is how closures work in Python. A closure

2818
03:49:35.020 --> 03:49:40.290
In Python is a tuple of variables that are no longer in scope, but that a function

2819
03:49:40.290 --> 03:49:45.623
needs in order to run. Let's explain this with an example. The function foo defines

2820
03:49:45.623 --> 03:49:50.893
a nested function, bar, that prints the value of a. Foo returns this new function.

2821
03:49:50.893 --> 03:49:56.098
So when we say func equals foo, we are assigning the bar function to the variable

2822
03:49:56.098 --> 03:50:01.111
func. Now, what happens when we call func? As expected, it prints the value of

2823
03:50:01.111 --> 03:50:03.810
variable a, which is 5. But wait a minute.

2824
03:50:03.810 --> 03:50:08.933
How does function func know anything about variable a? A is defined in foo's scope,

2825
03:50:08.933 --> 03:50:14.056
not bar's. You would think that a would not be observable outside the scope of foo.

2826
03:50:14.056 --> 03:50:18.809
That's where closures come in. When foo returned the new bar function, Python

2827
03:50:18.809 --> 03:50:23.994
helpfully attached any non-local variable that bar was going to need to the function

2828
03:50:23.994 --> 03:50:28.808
object. Those variables get stored in a tuple in the double underscore closure

2829
03:50:28.808 --> 03:50:32.820
attribute of the function. The closure for func has one variable.

2830
03:50:32.820 --> 03:50:38.102
And you can view the value of that variable by accessing the cell contents of the

2831
03:50:38.102 --> 03:50:43.383
item. Let's examine this bit of code. Here, x is defined in the global scope. Foo

2832
03:50:43.383 --> 03:50:48.861
creates a function bar that prints whatever argument was passed to foo. When we call

2833
03:50:48.861 --> 03:50:54.338
foo and assign the result to my func, we pass in x. So, as expected, calling my func

2834
03:50:54.338 --> 03:50:59.815
prints the value of x. Now, let's delete x and call my func again. What do you think

2835
03:50:59.815 --> 03:51:01.250
will happen this time?

2836
03:51:01.250 --> 03:51:06.439
If you guessed that we would still print 25, then you are correct. That's because

2837
03:51:06.439 --> 03:51:11.821
foo's value argument gets added to the closure attached to the new my func function.

2838
03:51:11.821 --> 03:51:17.010
So even though x doesn't exist anymore, the value persists in its closure. Notice

2839
03:51:17.010 --> 03:51:22.263
that nothing changes if we overwrite x instead of deleting it. Here we've passed x

2840
03:51:22.263 --> 03:51:27.517
into foo and then assigned the new function to the variable x. The old value of x,

2841
03:51:27.517 --> 03:51:30.720
25, is still stored in the new function's closure.

2842
03:51:30.720 --> 03:51:35.170
Even though the new function is now stored in the x variable. This is going to be

2843
03:51:35.170 --> 03:51:39.564
important to remember when we talk about decorators in the next lesson. Let's go

2844
03:51:39.564 --> 03:51:44.123
over some of the key concepts again to be sure you understand. A nested function is

2845
03:51:44.123 --> 03:51:48.408
a function defined inside another function. We'll sometimes refer to the outer

2846
03:51:48.408 --> 03:51:53.023
function as the parent and the nested function as the child. A non-local variable is

2847
03:51:53.023 --> 03:51:57.582
any variable that gets defined in the parent function's scope that gets used by the

2848
03:51:57.582 --> 03:51:59.120
child function. And finally,

2849
03:51:59.120 --> 03:52:03.857
A closure is Python's way of attaching non-local variables to a returned function so

2850
03:52:03.857 --> 03:52:08.537
that the function can operate even when it is called outside of its parent's scope.

2851
03:52:08.537 --> 03:52:13.049
We've gotten pretty deep into the internals of how Python works, and you must be

2852
03:52:13.049 --> 03:52:17.729
wondering, why does all of this matter? Well, in the next lesson, we'll finally get

2853
03:52:17.729 --> 03:52:22.297
to talk about decorators. In order to work, decorators have to make use of all of

2854
03:52:22.297 --> 03:52:26.582
these concepts, functions as objects, nested functions, non-local scope, and

2855
03:52:26.582 --> 03:52:27.090
closures.

2856
03:52:27.090 --> 03:52:31.480
Now that you have a firm foundation to build on, understanding how decorators work

2857
03:52:31.480 --> 03:52:35.817
should be easy. Before we move on though, try out a few practice problems to make

2858
03:52:35.817 --> 03:52:40.100
sure you understand how closures work. Now that you know functions can be passed

2859
03:52:40.100 --> 03:52:44.223
around as variables, and you understand scope and closures, we can talk about

2860
03:52:44.223 --> 03:52:48.613
decorators. So, what is a decorator? Let's say you have a function that takes some

2861
03:52:48.613 --> 03:52:53.057
inputs and returns some outputs. A decorator is a wrapper that you can place around

2862
03:52:53.057 --> 03:52:55.680
a function that changes that function's behavior.

2863
03:52:55.680 --> 03:53:00.587
You can modify the inputs, modify the outputs, or even change the behavior of the

2864
03:53:00.587 --> 03:53:05.555
function itself. You may have seen decorators in Python before. When you use them,

2865
03:53:05.555 --> 03:53:10.583
you type the at symbol, followed by the decorator's name on the line directly above

2866
03:53:10.583 --> 03:53:15.248
the function you are decorating. Here, the double args decorator modifies the

2867
03:53:15.248 --> 03:53:20.276
behavior of the multiply function. Double args is a decorator that multiplies every

2868
03:53:20.276 --> 03:53:24.820
argument by 2 before passing them to the decorated function. So, 1 times 5.

2869
03:53:24.820 --> 03:53:29.317
Becomes 2 times 10, which equals 20. That seems kind of magical that we can alter

2870
03:53:29.317 --> 03:53:33.759
the behavior of function. So let's peel back the layers and see how it works. We

2871
03:53:33.759 --> 03:53:38.367
will build the double args decorator together in this lesson. Let's continue to use

2872
03:53:38.367 --> 03:53:42.865
the multiply function as the function we are decorating. Now, decorators are just

2873
03:53:42.865 --> 03:53:47.473
functions that take a function as an argument and return a modified version of that

2874
03:53:47.473 --> 03:53:52.081
function. To start off, let's not have double args modify anything. It just takes a

2875
03:53:52.081 --> 03:53:54.080
function and immediately returns it.

2876
03:53:54.080 --> 03:53:59.126
If we call this version of double args that does nothing and pass it the multiply

2877
03:53:59.126 --> 03:54:04.234
function and then assign the result to the variable new multiply, then we call new

2878
03:54:04.234 --> 03:54:09.467
multiply 1 5 and get the same value we would have gotten from multiply 1 5. In order

2879
03:54:09.467 --> 03:54:14.513
for your decorator to return a modified function, it is usually helpful for it to

2880
03:54:14.513 --> 03:54:19.310
define a new function to return. We'll call that nested function wrapper. All

2881
03:54:19.310 --> 03:54:21.490
wrapper does is take two arguments.

2882
03:54:21.490 --> 03:54:25.687
And passes them on to whatever function was passed to double args in the first

2883
03:54:25.687 --> 03:54:30.100
place. If double args then returns the new wrapper function, the return value acts

2884
03:54:30.100 --> 03:54:34.513
exactly the same as whatever function was passed to double args, assuming that the

2885
03:54:34.513 --> 03:54:38.925
function passed to double args also takes exactly two arguments. So double args is

2886
03:54:38.925 --> 03:54:43.177
still not doing anything to actually modify the function it is decorating. Once

2887
03:54:43.177 --> 03:54:47.643
again, we'll pass multiply to double args and assign the result to new multiply. If

2888
03:54:47.643 --> 03:54:51.410
we then call new multiply, which is now equal to the wrapper function,

2889
03:54:51.410 --> 03:54:56.504
Wrapper calls multiply because it is the function that was passed to double args. So

2890
03:54:56.504 --> 03:55:01.174
wrapper calls multiply with the arguments 1 and 5, which returns 5. Now let's

2891
03:55:01.174 --> 03:55:06.087
actually modify the function our decorator is decorating. This time, wrapper will

2892
03:55:06.087 --> 03:55:10.878
still call whatever function is passed to double args, but it will double every

2893
03:55:10.878 --> 03:55:15.851
argument when it calls the original function. See how it calls func with a times 2

2894
03:55:15.851 --> 03:55:20.400
and b times 2? As usual, we will call double args on the multiply function.

2895
03:55:20.400 --> 03:55:25.841
And assign the result to new multiply. Now what happens when we call new multiply

2896
03:55:25.841 --> 03:55:31.080
with 1 and 5 as arguments? Well, new multiply is equal to wrapper, which calls

2897
03:55:31.080 --> 03:55:36.655
multiply after doubling each argument. So 1 becomes 2 and 5 becomes 10, giving us 2

2898
03:55:36.655 --> 03:55:42.163
times 10, which equals 20. We're almost there. This time, instead of assigning the

2899
03:55:42.163 --> 03:55:47.603
new function to new multiply, we're going to overwrite the multiply variable. Now

2900
03:55:47.603 --> 03:55:50.290
calling multiply with arguments 1 and 5,

2901
03:55:50.290 --> 03:55:54.914
gives us 20 instead of 5. Remember that we can do this because Python stores the

2902
03:55:54.914 --> 03:55:59.595
original multiply function in the new function's closure. When I first showed you

2903
03:55:59.595 --> 03:56:04.450
the double args decorator at the beginning of this lecture, I used at double args on

2904
03:56:04.450 --> 03:56:09.132
the line before the definition of multiply. This is just a Python convenience for

2905
03:56:09.132 --> 03:56:13.814
saying multiply equals the value returned by calling double args with multiply as

2906
03:56:13.814 --> 03:56:18.669
the only argument. The code shown here on the left is exactly equivalent to the code

2907
03:56:18.669 --> 03:56:19.420
on the right.

2908
03:56:19.420 --> 03:56:23.501
And that's how decorators work. Let's practice. You've learned a lot about how

2909
03:56:23.501 --> 03:56:27.686
decorators work. This lesson will walk you through some real-world decorators so

2910
03:56:27.686 --> 03:56:32.028
that you can start to recognize common decorator patterns. The timer decorator runs

2911
03:56:32.028 --> 03:56:36.318
the decorated function and then prints how long it took for the function to run. I

2912
03:56:36.318 --> 03:56:40.556
usually wind up adding some version of this to all of my projects because it is a

2913
03:56:40.556 --> 03:56:44.270
pretty easy way to figure out where your computational bottlenecks are.

2914
03:56:44.270 --> 03:56:48.448
All decorators have fairly similar looking doc strings because they all take and

2915
03:56:48.448 --> 03:56:52.678
return a single function. For brevity, I will only include the description of the

2916
03:56:52.678 --> 03:56:57.064
function in the doc strings of the examples that follow. Like most decorators, we'll

2917
03:56:57.064 --> 03:57:01.294
start off by defining a wrapper function. This is the function that the decorator

2918
03:57:01.294 --> 03:57:05.681
will return. Wrapper takes any number of positional and keyword arguments so that it

2919
03:57:05.681 --> 03:57:09.911
can be used to decorate any function. The first thing the new function will do is

2920
03:57:09.911 --> 03:57:12.940
record the time that it was called with the time function.

2921
03:57:12.940 --> 03:57:17.336
Then, wrapper gets the result of calling the decorated function. We don't return

2922
03:57:17.336 --> 03:57:21.952
that value yet though. After calling the decorated function, wrapper checks the time

2923
03:57:21.952 --> 03:57:26.403
again, and prints a message about how long it took to run the decorated function.

2924
03:57:26.403 --> 03:57:30.634
Once we've done that, we need to return the value that the decorated function

2925
03:57:30.634 --> 03:57:35.030
calculated. So, if we decorate this simple sleep n seconds function, you can see

2926
03:57:35.030 --> 03:57:39.646
that sleeping for 5 seconds takes about 5 seconds, and sleeping for 10 seconds takes

2927
03:57:39.646 --> 03:57:40.580
about 10 seconds.

2928
03:57:40.580 --> 03:57:44.660
This is a trivial use of the decorator to show how it works, but it can be very

2929
03:57:44.660 --> 03:57:48.947
useful for finding the slow parts of your code. Memoizing is the process of storing

2930
03:57:48.947 --> 03:57:53.286
the results of a function so that the next time the function is called with the same

2931
03:57:53.286 --> 03:57:57.624
arguments, you can just look up the answer. We start by setting up a dictionary that

2932
03:57:57.624 --> 03:58:00.000
will map arguments to results. Then, as usual,

2933
03:58:00.000 --> 03:58:04.398
we create wrapper to be the new decorated function that this decorator returns. When

2934
03:58:04.398 --> 03:58:08.324
the new function gets called, we check to see whether we've ever seen these

2935
03:58:08.324 --> 03:58:12.565
arguments before. If we haven't, we send them to the decorated function and store

2936
03:58:12.565 --> 03:58:16.963
the result in the cache dictionary. Now we can look up the return value quickly in a

2937
03:58:16.963 --> 03:58:20.837
dictionary of results. The next time we call this function with those same

2938
03:58:20.837 --> 03:58:24.764
arguments, the return value will already be in the dictionary. Here, we are

2939
03:58:24.764 --> 03:58:28.900
memoizing slow function. Slow function simply returns the sum of its arguments.

2940
03:58:28.900 --> 03:58:33.323
In order to simulate a slow function, we have it sleep for 5 seconds before

2941
03:58:33.323 --> 03:58:38.218
returning. If we call slow function with the arguments 3 and 4, it will sleep for 5

2942
03:58:38.218 --> 03:58:43.055
seconds and then return 7. But if we call slow function with the arguments 3 and 4

2943
03:58:43.055 --> 03:58:47.891
again, it will immediately return 7. Because we've stored the answer in the cache,

2944
03:58:47.891 --> 03:58:52.314
the decorated function doesn't even have to call the original slow function

2945
03:58:52.314 --> 03:58:57.209
function. So when is it appropriate to use a decorator? You should consider using a

2946
03:58:57.209 --> 03:58:57.740
decorator

2947
03:58:57.740 --> 03:59:01.773
when you want to add some common bit of code to multiple functions. We could have

2948
03:59:01.773 --> 03:59:05.607
added timing code in the body of all three of these functions, but that would

2949
03:59:05.607 --> 03:59:09.490
violate the principle of don't repeat yourself. Adding a decorator is a better

2950
03:59:09.490 --> 03:59:13.622
choice. Now that you've seen a few more decorators in action, you can try your hand

2951
03:59:13.622 --> 03:59:17.655
at creating some in the following example. One of the problems with decorators is

2952
03:59:17.655 --> 03:59:21.738
that they obscure the decorated function's metadata. In this lesson, I'll show you

2953
03:59:21.738 --> 03:59:25.572
why it's a problem and how to fix it. Here we have a nice function, sleep and

2954
03:59:25.572 --> 03:59:25.970
seconds.

2955
03:59:25.970 --> 03:59:30.502
With a doc string that explains exactly what it does. If we look at the doc string

2956
03:59:30.502 --> 03:59:35.089
attribute, we can see the text of the doc string. We can also access other metadata

2957
03:59:35.089 --> 03:59:39.621
for the function, like its name and default arguments. But watch what happens when

2958
03:59:39.621 --> 03:59:44.098
we decorate sleep n seconds with the timer decorator, as we've done here. When we

2959
03:59:44.098 --> 03:59:48.740
try to print the doc string, we get nothing back. Even stranger, when we try to look

2960
03:59:48.740 --> 03:59:53.328
up the function's name, Python tells us the name of the sleep n seconds function is

2961
03:59:53.328 --> 03:59:54.820
wrapper. To understand why,

2962
03:59:54.820 --> 03:59:59.283
we have to examine the timer decorator. Remember that when we write decorators, we

2963
03:59:59.283 --> 04:00:03.747
almost always define a nested function to return. Because the decorator overwrites

2964
04:00:03.747 --> 04:00:08.156
the sleepNSeconds function, when you ask for the sleepNSeconds docstring or name,

2965
04:00:08.156 --> 04:00:12.728
you are actually referencing the nested function that was returned by the decorator.

2966
04:00:12.728 --> 04:00:17.301
In this case, the nested function was called wrapper and it didn't have a docstring.

2967
04:00:17.301 --> 04:00:21.655
Fortunately, Python provides us with an easy way to fix this. The wraps function

2968
04:00:21.655 --> 04:00:24.540
from the functools module is a decorator that you use

2969
04:00:24.540 --> 04:00:28.924
when defining a decorator. If you use it to decorate the wrapper function that your

2970
04:00:28.924 --> 04:00:33.256
decorator returns, it will modify wrapper's metadata to look like the function you

2971
04:00:33.256 --> 04:00:37.165
are decorating. Notice that the wraps decorator takes the function you are

2972
04:00:37.165 --> 04:00:41.443
decorating as an argument. We haven't talked about decorators that take arguments

2973
04:00:41.443 --> 04:00:45.722
yet, but we will cover that in the next lesson. If we use this updated version of

2974
04:00:45.722 --> 04:00:49.472
the timer decorator to decorate sleepnseconds and then try to print the

2975
04:00:49.472 --> 04:00:52.800
sleepnseconds docstring, we get the result we expect. Likewise.

2976
04:00:52.800 --> 04:00:57.622
Printing the name or any other metadata now gives you the metadata from the function

2977
04:00:57.622 --> 04:01:02.445
being decorated rather than the metadata of the wrapper function. As an added bonus,

2978
04:01:02.445 --> 04:01:07.209
using wraps when creating your decorator also gives you easy access to the original

2979
04:01:07.209 --> 04:01:11.802
undecorated function via the double underscore wrapped attribute. Of course, you

2980
04:01:11.802 --> 04:01:16.510
always had access to this function via the closure, but this is an easy way to get

2981
04:01:16.510 --> 04:01:20.815
to it if you need it. Now that you know how to preserve the metadata of the

2982
04:01:20.815 --> 04:01:22.480
functions you are decorating,

2983
04:01:22.480 --> 04:01:26.624
Take a moment to practice. Sometimes it would be nice to add arguments to our

2984
04:01:26.624 --> 04:01:30.876
decorators. To do that, we need to add another level of function nesting. Let's

2985
04:01:30.876 --> 04:01:35.397
consider this silly run three times decorator. If you use it to decorate a function,

2986
04:01:35.397 --> 04:01:39.542
it will run that function three times. If we use it to decorate the print sum

2987
04:01:39.542 --> 04:01:44.009
function, and then run print sum with arguments three and five, it will print eight

2988
04:01:44.009 --> 04:01:48.476
three times. Let's think about what we would need to change if we wanted to write a

2989
04:01:48.476 --> 04:01:51.490
run n times decorator. We want to pass n as an argument.

2990
04:01:51.490 --> 04:01:56.389
Instead of hard coding it in the decorator. If we had some way to pass n into the

2991
04:01:56.389 --> 04:02:01.409
decorator, we could decorate print sum so that it gets run three times and decorate

2992
04:02:01.409 --> 04:02:05.945
print hello to run five times. But a decorator is only supposed to take one

2993
04:02:05.945 --> 04:02:11.025
argument, the function it is decorating. Also, when you use decorator syntax, you're

2994
04:02:11.025 --> 04:02:15.863
not supposed to use the parentheses. So what gives? To make run n times work, we

2995
04:02:15.863 --> 04:02:19.250
have to turn it into a function that returns a decorator

2996
04:02:19.250 --> 04:02:23.851
rather than a function that is a decorator. So let's start by redefining run end

2997
04:02:23.851 --> 04:02:28.395
times so that it takes n as an argument instead of func. Then inside of run end

2998
04:02:28.395 --> 04:02:33.226
times we'll define a new decorator function. This function takes func as an argument

2999
04:02:33.226 --> 04:02:37.885
because it is the function that will be acting as our decorator. We start our new

3000
04:02:37.885 --> 04:02:42.716
decorator with a nested wrapper function as usual. Now since we are still inside the

3001
04:02:42.716 --> 04:02:47.375
run end times function we have access to the end parameter that was passed to run

3002
04:02:47.375 --> 04:02:47.950
end times.

3003
04:02:47.950 --> 04:02:52.555
We can use that to control how many times we repeat the loop that calls our

3004
04:02:52.555 --> 04:02:57.651
decorated function. As usual for any decorator, we return the new wrapper function.

3005
04:02:57.651 --> 04:03:02.564
And if runNTimes returns the decorator function we just defined, then we can use

3006
04:03:02.564 --> 04:03:07.169
that return value as a decorator. Notice how when we decorate printSum with

3007
04:03:07.169 --> 04:03:11.897
runNTimes, we use the parentheses after runNTimes. This indicates that we are

3008
04:03:11.897 --> 04:03:16.993
actually calling runNTimes and decorating printSum with the result of that function

3009
04:03:16.993 --> 04:03:17.300
call.

3010
04:03:17.300 --> 04:03:22.096
Since the return value from run ntimes is a decorator function, we can use it to

3011
04:03:22.096 --> 04:03:27.133
decorate printSum. This is a little bit confusing, so let me show you how this works

3012
04:03:27.133 --> 04:03:31.989
without using decorator syntax. Like before, we have a function, run ntimes, that

3013
04:03:31.989 --> 04:03:36.606
returns a decorator function when you call it. If we call run ntimes with the

3014
04:03:36.606 --> 04:03:41.462
argument 3, it will return a decorator. In fact, it returns the decorator that we

3015
04:03:41.462 --> 04:03:44.640
defined at the beginning of this lesson, run 3 times.

3016
04:03:44.640 --> 04:03:49.388
We could decorate print sum with this new decorator using decorator syntax. Python

3017
04:03:49.388 --> 04:03:53.847
makes it convenient to do both of those in a single step, though. When we use

3018
04:03:53.847 --> 04:03:58.654
decorator syntax, the thing that comes after the at symbol must be a reference to a

3019
04:03:58.654 --> 04:04:03.344
decorator function. We can use the name of a specific decorator, or we can call a

3020
04:04:03.344 --> 04:04:08.151
function that returns a decorator. To prove to you that it works the way we expect,

3021
04:04:08.151 --> 04:04:12.668
here is print sum decorated with run n times 3 when we call print sum with the

3022
04:04:12.668 --> 04:04:13.710
arguments 3 and 5.

3023
04:04:13.710 --> 04:04:18.240
It prints eight three times. And we can just as easily decorate print hello, which

3024
04:04:18.240 --> 04:04:22.826
prints a hello message with run n times five. When we call print hello, we get five

3025
04:04:22.826 --> 04:04:27.467
hello messages as expected. I try to keep this example simple to explain the concept

3026
04:04:27.467 --> 04:04:31.997
of decorator. In the next lesson, I'll show you some useful things you can do with

3027
04:04:31.997 --> 04:04:36.252
decorators that take arguments like this. In the meantime, try these practice

3028
04:04:36.252 --> 04:04:38.130
exercises to get more comfortable.

3029
04:04:38.130 --> 04:04:42.138
We're going to finish up by looking at an example of a real-world decorator that

3030
04:04:42.138 --> 04:04:46.347
takes an argument to give you a better sense of how they work. Let's imagine that we

3031
04:04:46.347 --> 04:04:50.556
have some functions that occasionally either run for longer than we want them to, or

3032
04:04:50.556 --> 04:04:54.614
just hang and never return. It would be nice if we could add some kind of timeout

3033
04:04:54.614 --> 04:04:58.522
decorator to those functions that will raise an error if the function runs for

3034
04:04:58.522 --> 04:05:02.481
longer than expected. To create the timeout decorator, we are going to use some

3035
04:05:02.481 --> 04:05:06.389
functions from Python's signal module. These functions have nothing to do with

3036
04:05:06.389 --> 04:05:06.940
decorators.

3037
04:05:06.940 --> 04:05:11.799
But understanding them will help you understand the timeout decorator I am about to

3038
04:05:11.799 --> 04:05:16.717
show you. The raiseTimeout function simply raises a timeout error when it is called.

3039
04:05:16.717 --> 04:05:21.635
The signal function tells Python, when you see the signal whose number is signalNum,

3040
04:05:21.635 --> 04:05:26.084
call the handler function. In this case, we tell Python to call raiseTimeout

3041
04:05:26.084 --> 04:05:30.944
whenever it sees the alarm signal. The alarm function lets us set an alarm for some

3042
04:05:30.944 --> 04:05:35.569
number of seconds in the future. Passing zero to the alarm function cancels the

3043
04:05:35.569 --> 04:05:35.920
alarm.

3044
04:05:35.920 --> 04:05:40.292
We'll start by creating a decorator that times out in exactly 5 seconds, and then

3045
04:05:40.292 --> 04:05:44.826
build from there to create a decorator that takes the timeout length as an argument.

3046
04:05:44.826 --> 04:05:49.091
Our timeout in 5 seconds decorator starts off by defining a wrapper function to

3047
04:05:49.091 --> 04:05:53.571
return as the new decorated function. Returning this function is what makes timeout

3048
04:05:53.571 --> 04:05:58.051
in 5 seconds a decorator. First, wrapper sets an alarm for 5 seconds in the future.

3049
04:05:58.051 --> 04:06:02.369
Then it calls the function being decorated. It wraps that call in a try block so

3050
04:06:02.369 --> 04:06:04.960
that in a finally block we can cancel the alarm.

3051
04:06:04.960 --> 04:06:09.872
This ensures that the alarm either rings or gets cancelled. Remember, when the alarm

3052
04:06:09.872 --> 04:06:14.550
rings, Python calls the raiseTimeout function. Let's use timeout in 5 seconds to

3053
04:06:14.550 --> 04:06:19.462
decorate a function that will definitely timeout. Foo sleeps for 10 seconds and then

3054
04:06:19.462 --> 04:06:23.848
prints foo. If we call foo, the 5-second alarm will ring before it finishes

3055
04:06:23.848 --> 04:06:28.468
sleeping, and Python will raise a timeout error. Now let's create a more useful

3056
04:06:28.468 --> 04:06:33.380
version of the timeout decorator. This decorator takes an argument. To decorate foo,

3057
04:06:33.380 --> 04:06:37.943
we'll set the timeout to 5 seconds like we did previously. But when decorating bar,

3058
04:06:37.943 --> 04:06:42.175
we can set the timeout to 20 seconds. This allows us to set a timeout that is

3059
04:06:42.175 --> 04:06:46.573
appropriate for each function. Timeout is a function that returns a decorator. I

3060
04:06:46.573 --> 04:06:51.080
like to think of it as a decorator factory. When you call timeout, it cranks out a

3061
04:06:51.080 --> 04:06:55.588
brand new decorator that times out in 5 seconds or 20 seconds or whatever value we

3062
04:06:55.588 --> 04:07:00.041
pass in as n seconds. The first thing we need to do is to find this new decorator

3063
04:07:00.041 --> 04:07:01.140
that it will return.

3064
04:07:01.140 --> 04:07:05.907
That decorator begins, like all of our decorators, by defining a wrapper function to

3065
04:07:05.907 --> 04:07:10.560
return. Now, because n seconds is available to the wrapper function, we can set an

3066
04:07:10.560 --> 04:07:15.156
alarm for n seconds in the future. The rest of the wrapper function looks exactly

3067
04:07:15.156 --> 04:07:19.526
like the wrapper function from the timeout in 5 seconds function. Notice that

3068
04:07:19.526 --> 04:07:24.179
wrapper returns the result of calling func, decorator returns wrapper, and timeout

3069
04:07:24.179 --> 04:07:28.605
returns decorator. So, when we call foo, which has a 5 second timeout, it will

3070
04:07:28.605 --> 04:07:29.740
timeout like before.

3071
04:07:29.740 --> 04:07:34.034
But bar, which has a 20 second timeout, prints its message in 10 seconds, so the

3072
04:07:34.034 --> 04:07:38.328
alarm gets cancelled. The following exercises will give you a few chances to try

3073
04:07:38.328 --> 04:07:42.729
your hand at creating decorators that take great job. You've covered a lot in this

3074
04:07:42.729 --> 04:07:46.969
course. In the first chapter, you learned how to make high quality functions by

3075
04:07:46.969 --> 04:07:51.424
giving them doc strings and by making sure that they only do one thing. Remembering

3076
04:07:51.424 --> 04:07:55.932
the acronym DRY, or don't repeat yourself, helped you notice when you needed to pull

3077
04:07:55.932 --> 04:07:58.240
part of your code into a reusable function.

3078
04:07:58.240 --> 04:08:02.783
You also learned how Python passes arguments to functions and the difference between

3079
04:08:02.783 --> 04:08:07.326
mutable and immutable variables. In the chapter on context managers, you learned how

3080
04:08:07.326 --> 04:08:11.707
to use the keyword with to enter and then exit a context. You also learned how to

3081
04:08:11.707 --> 04:08:16.034
write your own context managers by using the context manager decorator. You also

3082
04:08:16.034 --> 04:08:20.469
spent a lot of time in this course understanding decorators, how they work, how to

3083
04:08:20.469 --> 04:08:24.580
use them, and how to write decorators of your own. Finally, in chapter four,

3084
04:08:24.580 --> 04:08:28.655
You learned how to use functools.wraps to make sure your decorated functions

3085
04:08:28.655 --> 04:08:32.623
maintain their metadata. And you learned how to write decorators that take

3086
04:08:32.623 --> 04:08:36.913
arguments. It has been an honor to spend this time with you. I wanted to let you

