04:08:36.913 --> 04:08:41.309
know that I'll be donating a portion of the proceeds from this course to WIMLDS, a

3088
04:08:41.309 --> 04:08:45.814
nonprofit that helps support and promote women in the fields of machine learning and

3089
04:08:45.814 --> 04:08:50.318
data science. I'll be looking forward to hearing about all of the amazing things you

3090
04:08:50.318 --> 04:08:54.500
do with Python. Hello, and welcome to unit testing for data science in Python.

3091
04:08:54.500 --> 04:08:59.294
My name is Dibbo. I'm a test automation engineer, and I will be your instructor for

3092
04:08:59.294 --> 04:09:03.799
this course. Consider this question. Suppose we have just implemented a Python

3093
04:09:03.799 --> 04:09:08.593
function. How can we test whether our implementation is correct? The easiest way is

3094
04:09:08.593 --> 04:09:13.387
to open an interpreter, test the function on a few arguments, and check whether the

3095
04:09:13.387 --> 04:09:18.123
return value is correct. If correct, we can accept the implementation and move on,

3096
04:09:18.123 --> 04:09:22.802
right? While testing on the interpreter is easy, it is actually very inefficient.

3097
04:09:22.802 --> 04:09:24.130
This will become clear.

3098
04:09:24.130 --> 04:09:28.795
If we think about the big picture of a function's lifecycle in a data science

3099
04:09:28.795 --> 04:09:33.642
project. The lifecycle of a function typically looks like this. We implement the

3100
04:09:33.642 --> 04:09:38.611
function and then test it. If the tests pass, we accept the implementation. If the

3101
04:09:38.611 --> 04:09:43.639
tests fail, we fix any bugs that we found and test again. Later, we might get a new

3102
04:09:43.639 --> 04:09:48.547
feature request or we may be asked to refactor the function. So, we implement the

3103
04:09:48.547 --> 04:09:51.940
new feature or refactor the code and then test it again.

3104
04:09:51.940 --> 04:09:56.890
Alternatively, someone might discover a previously unseen bug. In that case, we fix

3105
04:09:56.890 --> 04:10:01.840
that bug and test again. Notice how many times a function needs to be tested during

3106
04:10:01.840 --> 04:10:06.850
the lifecycle. Every time we modify the function, either to fix bugs or to implement

3107
04:10:06.850 --> 04:10:11.740
new features, we have to test it. If a project continues for a few years, we might

3108
04:10:11.740 --> 04:10:16.691
be testing the function about a hundred times, maybe more. Let's look at an example

3109
04:10:16.691 --> 04:10:21.343
function. It's called rowToList. It takes a single argument, which is a Python

3110
04:10:21.343 --> 04:10:21.760
string.

3111
04:10:21.760 --> 04:10:26.823
The string is a single row in a data file which contains data on housing area and

3112
04:10:26.823 --> 04:10:31.760
market price of the house. The string contains the housing area in square feet,

3113
04:10:31.760 --> 04:10:36.948
followed by a single tab, followed by the housing price in dollars, and ending with

3114
04:10:36.948 --> 04:10:42.135
a newline character. If the string follows this format, the function returns a list

3115
04:10:42.135 --> 04:10:47.260
of length 2, containing the housing area and the housing price. But this data file

3116
04:10:47.260 --> 04:10:51.760
is not clean, and some rows in this data file do not follow this format.

3117
04:10:51.760 --> 04:10:56.589
The third row has missing area, while the penultimate row is missing the tab between

3118
04:10:56.589 --> 04:11:01.418
the area and price. For these invalid rows, the function should return none. To test

3119
04:11:01.418 --> 04:11:06.247
this function, we will have to try all the arguments that we listed and check if the

3120
04:11:06.247 --> 04:11:10.962
function returns the correct value. Remembering that a function needs to be tested

3121
04:11:10.962 --> 04:11:15.791
about 100 times in its lifecycle, and assuming it takes 5 minutes to test each time,

3122
04:11:15.791 --> 04:11:19.700
we will spend 8 hours just testing this function on the interpreter.

3123
04:11:19.700 --> 04:11:24.484
Unit tests automate this repetitive and tedious testing process. Unit tests will

3124
04:11:24.484 --> 04:11:29.447
reduce the testing time over the lifecycle of a single function to one hour instead

3125
04:11:29.447 --> 04:11:33.932
of eight. Now imagine how much time we would save when we unit test all the

3126
04:11:33.932 --> 04:11:38.717
functions in a project. This makes unit testing a must-have skill for productive

3127
04:11:38.717 --> 04:11:43.740
data scientists. This course will teach you how to write unit tests. We have created

3128
04:11:43.740 --> 04:11:48.584
an example data science project which predicts prices from the housing area using

3129
04:11:48.584 --> 04:11:49.660
linear regression.

3130
04:11:49.660 --> 04:11:54.104
As we can see in the plot. The complete code for this project, including actual

3131
04:11:54.104 --> 04:11:58.548
implementations for functions like row to list, is available in a public GitHub

3132
04:11:58.548 --> 04:12:03.217
repository. We will share the link to the repository at the end of the course. Here

3133
04:12:03.217 --> 04:12:07.943
is the folder structure of this project. During this course, we are going to write a

3134
04:12:07.943 --> 04:12:12.162
complete unit test suite for this example project. It's going to be fun and

3135
04:12:12.162 --> 04:12:16.719
exciting. This will prepare you to write unit tests for your own projects. Sounds

3136
04:12:16.719 --> 04:12:18.350
good? Then let's get started.

3137
04:12:18.350 --> 04:12:23.695
By practicing the concepts. In the last exercise, you went through a function's

3138
04:12:23.695 --> 04:12:29.310
lifecycle. At every step, you tested it by calling rowToList on different arguments

3139
04:12:29.310 --> 04:12:34.655
and checked if the return values are correct. This was repetitive, tedious, and

3140
04:12:34.655 --> 04:12:40.203
time-consuming. In this lesson, we will learn to write unit tests and improve this

3141
04:12:40.203 --> 04:12:45.615
process. There are many Python libraries for writing unit tests, such as PyTest,

3142
04:12:45.615 --> 04:12:47.780
UnitTest, NoseTest, and DocTest.

3143
04:12:47.780 --> 04:12:53.151
We will use PyTest for this course because PyTest has all essential features, is

3144
04:12:53.151 --> 04:12:58.522
easiest to use, and is the most popular testing library in Python. To start unit

3145
04:12:58.522 --> 04:13:03.759
testing with PyTest, we will first create a file called testRowToList.py. When

3146
04:13:03.759 --> 04:13:09.265
PyTest sees a file name starting with test underscore, it understands that this is

3147
04:13:09.265 --> 04:13:14.904
not an usual Python file, but a special one containing unit tests. We must make sure

3148
04:13:14.904 --> 04:13:17.120
to follow this naming convention.

3149
04:13:17.120 --> 04:13:22.481
Files holding unit tests are also called test modules, and we just created our first

3150
04:13:22.481 --> 04:13:27.842
test module. In the test module, we first import PyTest. Then we import the function

3151
04:13:27.842 --> 04:13:32.948
under test. A unit test is written as a Python function whose name starts with a

3152
04:13:32.948 --> 04:13:38.181
test underscore, just like the test module. This way, PyTest can tell that it is a

3153
04:13:38.181 --> 04:13:40.670
unit test and not an ordinary function.

3154
04:13:40.670 --> 04:13:45.759
The unit test usually corresponds to exactly one entry in the argument and return

3155
04:13:45.759 --> 04:13:50.596
value table for row to list. The unit test checks whether row to list has the

3156
04:13:50.596 --> 04:13:55.496
expected return value when called on this particular argument. This particular

3157
04:13:55.496 --> 04:14:00.521
argument is a clean row, so we call the unit test test for clean row. The actual

3158
04:14:00.521 --> 04:14:05.673
check is done via an assert statement, and every test must contain one. The assert

3159
04:14:05.673 --> 04:14:10.510
statement has a required first argument, which can be any Boolean expression.

3160
04:14:10.510 --> 04:14:15.611
If the expression is true, the assert statement passes, giving us a blank output. If

3161
04:14:15.611 --> 04:14:20.409
the expression is false, it raises an assertion error. In this case, we want to

3162
04:14:20.409 --> 04:14:25.085
check if rowToList returns the correct list when called on the clean row. The

3163
04:14:25.085 --> 04:14:30.187
expression we use is rowToList called on the argument equal equals the correct list.

3164
04:14:30.187 --> 04:14:34.985
If the function works, this will evaluate to true and the assert statement will

3165
04:14:34.985 --> 04:14:38.750
pass. This will make the test pass. If the function has a bug,

3166
04:14:38.750 --> 04:14:43.934
it will evaluate to false, the assert statement will raise an assertion error, and

3167
04:14:43.934 --> 04:14:49.054
the test will fail. For the second row in the table, we create a unit test called

3168
04:14:49.054 --> 04:14:54.175
testOnMissingArea because the argument has missing area data. Then we assert that

3169
04:14:54.175 --> 04:14:59.422
the return value for this argument is none. Note that the correct way to check if a

3170
04:14:59.422 --> 04:15:04.542
variable's value is none is to use the boolean expression var is none and not var

3171
04:15:04.542 --> 04:15:05.680
equal equals none.

3172
04:15:05.680 --> 04:15:10.473
For the third row in the table, we create a unit test called test for missing tab

3173
04:15:10.473 --> 04:15:15.029
because the argument is missing the tab separating area and price. The assert

