07:24:08.400 --> 07:24:12.914
It shows us the schedule for the DAG in date or cron format. We can see the owner of

5599
07:24:12.914 --> 07:24:17.428
the DAG, which of the most recent tasks have run, when the last run started, and the

5600
07:24:17.428 --> 07:24:21.781
last three DAG runs. The links area on the right gives us quick access to many of

5601
07:24:21.781 --> 07:24:26.295
the DAG-specific views. Don't worry about those for now. Instead, we'll click on the

5602
07:24:26.295 --> 07:24:30.701
example underscore DAG link, which takes us to our DAG detail page. The DAG detail

5603
07:24:30.701 --> 07:24:35.215
view gives us specific access to information about the DAG itself, including several

5604
07:24:35.215 --> 07:24:36.720
views of information, graph.

5605
07:24:36.720 --> 07:24:41.385
Tree, and code, illustrating the tasks and dependencies in the code. We also get

5606
07:24:41.385 --> 07:24:46.166
access to the task duration, task tries, timings, a Gantt chart view, and specific

5607
07:24:46.166 --> 07:24:51.006
details about the DAG. We have the ability to trigger the DAG to start, refresh our

5608
07:24:51.006 --> 07:24:55.729
view, and delete the DAG if we desire. The detail view defaults to the tree view,

5609
07:24:55.729 --> 07:25:00.452
showing the specific name tasks, which operators are in use, and any dependencies

5610
07:25:00.452 --> 07:25:05.117
between tasks. The circles in front of the words represent the state of the task

5611
07:25:05.117 --> 07:25:05.700
slash DAG.

5612
07:25:05.700 --> 07:25:09.602
In the case of our specific DAG, we see that we have one task called generate

5613
07:25:09.602 --> 07:25:13.555
underscore random underscore number. The DAG graph view arranges the tasks and

5614
07:25:13.555 --> 07:25:17.812
dependencies in a chart format. This provides another view into the flow of the DAG.

5615
07:25:17.812 --> 07:25:21.916
You can see the operators in use and the state of tasks at any point in time. The

5616
07:25:21.916 --> 07:25:26.021
tree and graph view provide different information depending on what you'd like to

5617
07:25:26.021 --> 07:25:30.126
know. Try moving between them when examining a DAG to obtain further details. For

5618
07:25:30.126 --> 07:25:34.028
this view, we again see that we have a task called generate underscore random

5619
07:25:34.028 --> 07:25:34.940
underscore number.

5620
07:25:34.940 --> 07:25:38.645
We can also see that it is of the type bash operator in the middle left of the

5621
07:25:38.645 --> 07:25:42.634
image. The DAG code view does exactly as it sounds. It provides a copy of the Python

5622
07:25:42.634 --> 07:25:46.339
code that makes up the DAG. The code view provides easy access to exactly what

5623
07:25:46.339 --> 07:25:50.281
defines the DAG without clicking in various portions of the UI. As you use Airflow,

5624
07:25:50.281 --> 07:25:54.081
you'll determine which tools work best for you. It is worth noting that the code

5625
07:25:54.081 --> 07:25:57.975
view is read-only. Any DAG code changes must be done via the actual DAG script. In

5626
07:25:57.975 --> 07:26:01.728
this view, we can finally see the code making up the generate underscore random

5627
07:26:01.728 --> 07:26:02.820
underscore number task.

5628
07:26:02.820 --> 07:26:06.939
And that it runs the bash command echo $random. The logs page under the browse menu

5629
07:26:06.939 --> 07:26:11.107
option provides troubleshooting and audit ability while using Airflow. This includes

5630
07:26:11.107 --> 07:26:15.027
items such as starting the Airflow web server, viewing the graph or tree nodes,

5631
07:26:15.027 --> 07:26:19.046
creating users, starting DAGs, etc. When using Airflow, look at the logs often to

5632
07:26:19.046 --> 07:26:23.115
become more familiar with the types of information included, and also what happens

5633
07:26:23.115 --> 07:26:25.100
behind the scenes of an Airflow install.

5634
07:26:25.100 --> 07:26:28.761
Note that you'll often refer to the event type present on the logs view when

5635
07:26:28.761 --> 07:26:32.808
searching, such as graph, tree, CLI scheduler. In most circumstances, you can choose

5636
07:26:32.808 --> 07:26:36.807
between using the Airflow web UI or the command line tool based on your preference.

5637
07:26:36.807 --> 07:26:40.757
The web UI is often easier to use overall. The command line tool may be simpler to

5638
07:26:40.757 --> 07:26:44.756
access depending on settings, via SSH, etc. Now that we've covered some of the most

5639
07:26:44.756 --> 07:26:48.754
important pages of the Airflow UI, let's practice examining some workflows. Welcome

5640
07:26:48.754 --> 07:26:52.801
back. We've discussed the basics of tasks in Airflow without covering exactly what a

5641
07:26:52.801 --> 07:26:53.620
task consists of.

5642
07:26:53.620 --> 07:26:57.686
The most common task in Airflow is the operator. Let's take a look at operators now.

5643
07:26:57.686 --> 07:27:01.558
Airflow operators represent a single task in a workflow. This can be any type of

5644
07:27:01.558 --> 07:27:05.575
task, from running a command, sending an email, running a Python script, and so on.

5645
07:27:05.575 --> 07:27:09.640
Typically, Airflow operators run independently, meaning that all resources needed to

5646
07:27:09.640 --> 07:27:13.706
complete the task are contained within the operator. Generally, Airflow operators do

5647
07:27:13.706 --> 07:27:17.626
not share information between each other. This is to simplify workflows and allow

5648
07:27:17.626 --> 07:27:20.240
Airflow to run the tasks in the most efficient manner.

5649
07:27:20.240 --> 07:27:24.027
It is possible to share information between operators, but the details of how are

5650
07:27:24.027 --> 07:27:27.768
beyond this course. Airflow contains many various operators to perform different

5651
07:27:27.768 --> 07:27:31.275
tasks. For example, the Domini operator can be used to represent a task for

5652
07:27:31.275 --> 07:27:35.203
troubleshooting, or a task that has not yet been implemented. We are focusing on the

5653
07:27:35.203 --> 07:27:38.943
bash operator for this lesson, but we'll look at the Python operator and several

5654
07:27:38.943 --> 07:27:42.684
others later on. The bash operator executes a given bash command or script. This

5655
07:27:42.684 --> 07:27:46.471
command can be pretty much anything bash is capable of that would make sense in a

5656
07:27:46.471 --> 07:27:49.230
given workflow. The bash operator requires three arguments.

5657
07:27:49.230 --> 07:27:53.174
The task ID, which is the name that shows up in the UI, the bash command, the raw

5658
07:27:53.174 --> 07:27:57.214
command or script, and the DAG it belongs to. The bash operator runs the command in

5659
07:27:57.214 --> 07:28:01.255
a temporary directory that gets automatically cleaned up afterwards. It is possible

5660
07:28:01.255 --> 07:28:05.199
to specify environment variables for the bash command to try to replicate running

5661
07:28:05.199 --> 07:28:08.996
the task as you would on a local system. If you're unfamiliar with environment

5662
07:28:08.996 --> 07:28:12.648
variables, these are runtime settings interpreted by the shell. It provides

5663
07:28:12.648 --> 07:28:15.520
flexibility while running the scripts in a generalized way.

5664
07:28:15.520 --> 07:28:19.658
The first example runs a bash command to echo example exclamation mark to the

5665
07:28:19.658 --> 07:28:24.118
standard alt. The second example uses a predefined bash script for its command, run

5666
07:28:24.118 --> 07:28:27.772
cleanup.sh. Before using the bash operator, it must be imported from

5667
07:28:27.772 --> 07:28:31.856
Airflow.operators.bash underscore operator. The first example creates a bash

5668
07:28:31.856 --> 07:28:35.994
operator that takes a task underscore ID, runs the bash command echo one, and

5669
07:28:35.994 --> 07:28:40.346
assigns the operator to the DAG. Note that we've previously defined the DAG in an

5670
07:28:40.346 --> 07:28:41.260
earlier exercise.

5671
07:28:41.260 --> 07:28:45.118
The second example is a bash operator to run a quick data cleaning operation using

5672
07:28:45.118 --> 07:28:49.070
cat and awk. Don't worry if you don't understand exactly what this is doing. This is

5673
07:28:49.070 --> 07:28:52.880
a common scenario when running workflows. You may not know exactly what a command

5674
07:28:52.880 --> 07:28:56.644
does, but you can still run it in a reliable way. There are some general gotchas

5675
07:28:56.644 --> 07:29:00.596
when using operators. The biggest is that individual operators are not guaranteed to

5676
07:29:00.596 --> 07:29:04.454
run in the same location or environment. This means that just because one operator

5677
07:29:04.454 --> 07:29:08.405
ran in a given directory with a certain setup, it does not necessarily mean that the

5678
07:29:08.405 --> 07:29:11.040
next operator will have access to that same information.

5679
07:29:11.040 --> 07:29:14.623
If this is required, you must explicitly set it up. You may need to set up

5680
07:29:14.623 --> 07:29:18.690
environment variables, especially for the bash operator. For example, it's common in

5681
07:29:18.690 --> 07:29:22.660
bash to use the tilde character to represent a home directory. This is not defined

5682
07:29:22.660 --> 07:29:26.437
by default in Airflow. Another example of an environment variable could be AWS

5683
07:29:26.437 --> 07:29:30.504
credentials, database connectivity details, or other information specific to running

5684
07:29:30.504 --> 07:29:30.940
a script.

5685
07:29:30.940 --> 07:29:34.705
Finally, it can also be tricky to run tasks with any form of elevated privilege.

5686
07:29:34.705 --> 07:29:38.659
This means that any access to resources must be set up for the specific user running

5687
07:29:38.659 --> 07:29:42.377
the tasks. If you're uncertain what elevated privileges are, think of running a

5688
07:29:42.377 --> 07:29:46.095
command as root or the administrator on a system. We've discussed the basics of

5689
07:29:46.095 --> 07:29:49.860
Airflow Operator. Let's practice using them in some workflows. Welcome back. Now

5690
07:29:49.860 --> 07:29:53.626
that you've worked with the operators a bit, let's take a look at the concept of

5691
07:29:53.626 --> 07:29:57.579
tasks within Airflow. Within Airflow, tasks are instantiated operators. It basically

5692
07:29:57.579 --> 07:30:00.450
is a shortcut to refer to a given operator within a workflow.

5693
07:30:00.450 --> 07:30:04.729
Tasks are usually assigned to a variable within Python code. Using a previous

5694
07:30:04.729 --> 07:30:09.286
example, we assign the bash operator to the variable example underscore task. Note

5695
07:30:09.286 --> 07:30:13.509
that within the Airflow tools, this task is referred by its task ID, not the

5696
07:30:13.509 --> 07:30:17.900
variable name. Task dependencies in Airflow define an order of task completion.

5697
07:30:17.900 --> 07:30:22.512
While not required, task dependencies are usually present. If task dependencies are

5698
07:30:22.512 --> 07:30:26.847
not defined, task execution is handled by Airflow itself with no guarantees of

5699
07:30:26.847 --> 07:30:27.180
order.

5700
07:30:27.180 --> 07:30:31.761
Task dependencies are referred to as upstream or downstream tasks. An upstream task

5701
07:30:31.761 --> 07:30:36.286
means that it must complete prior to any downstream tasks. Since Airflow 1.8, task

5702
07:30:36.286 --> 07:30:40.922
dependencies are defined using the bit shift operators. The upstream operator is two

5703
07:30:40.922 --> 07:30:45.558
greater than symbols. The downstream operator is two less than symbols. It's easy to

5704
07:30:45.558 --> 07:30:50.194
get confused on when to use an upstream or downstream operator. The simplest analogy

5705
07:30:50.194 --> 07:30:53.340
is that upstream means before and downstream means after.

5706
07:30:53.340 --> 07:30:57.679
This means that any upstream tasks would need to complete prior to any downstream

5707
07:30:57.679 --> 07:31:01.964
ones. Let's look at a simple example involving two bash operators. We define our

5708
07:31:01.964 --> 07:31:06.357
first task and assign it to the variable task1. We then create our second task and

5709
07:31:06.357 --> 07:31:10.643
assign it to the variable task2. Once each operator is defined and assigned to a

5710
07:31:10.643 --> 07:31:15.089
variable, we can define the task order using the bit shift operators. In this case,

5711
07:31:15.089 --> 07:31:16.910
we want to run task1 before task2.

5712
07:31:16.910 --> 07:31:21.090
The most readable method for this is using the upstream operator, two greater than

5713
07:31:21.090 --> 07:31:25.270
symbols, as task one, upstream operator, task two. Note that you could also define

5714
07:31:25.270 --> 07:31:29.501
this in reverse using the downstream operator to accomplish the same thing. In this

5715
07:31:29.501 --> 07:31:33.680
case, it'd be task two, two less than symbols, task one. Let's take a look at what

5716
07:31:33.680 --> 07:31:37.860
the Airflow UI shows for tasks and their dependencies. In this case, we're looking

5717
07:31:37.860 --> 07:31:40.460
at the graph view within the Airflow web interface.

5718
07:31:40.460 --> 07:31:44.220
Note that in the task area, our two tasks, first underscore task and second

5719
07:31:44.220 --> 07:31:48.431
underscore task, are both present, but there is no order to the task execution. This

5720
07:31:48.431 --> 07:31:52.541
is the DAG prior to setting the task dependency using the bit shift operator. Now,

5721
07:31:52.541 --> 07:31:56.652
let's look again at the view with a defined order via the bit shift operators. The

5722
07:31:56.652 --> 07:32:00.762
view is similar, but we can see the order of tasks indicated by the directed arrow

5723
07:32:00.762 --> 07:32:04.773
between first underscore task and second underscore task. Dependencies can be as

5724
07:32:04.773 --> 07:32:07.630
complex as required to define the workflow to your needs.

5725
07:32:07.630 --> 07:32:12.087
We can chain a dependency, in this case setting task 1 upstream of task 2, upstream

5726
07:32:12.087 --> 07:32:16.221
of task 3, upstream of task 4. The Airflow graph view shows a dependency view

5727
07:32:16.221 --> 07:32:20.678
indicating this order. You can also mix upstream and downstream bit shift operators

5728
07:32:20.678 --> 07:32:24.974
in the same workflow. If we define task 1 upstream of task 2, then downstream of

5729
07:32:24.974 --> 07:32:29.377
task 3, we get a configuration different than what we might expect. This creates a

5730
07:32:29.377 --> 07:32:33.565
DAG where first underscore task and third underscore task must finish prior to

5731
07:32:33.565 --> 07:32:34.800
second underscore task.

5732
07:32:34.800 --> 07:32:38.846
This means we could define the same dependency graph on two lines in a possibly

5733
07:32:38.846 --> 07:32:43.047
clearer form. Task one upstream of task two, task three upstream of task two. Note

5734
07:32:43.047 --> 07:32:47.093
that because we don't require it, either task one or task three could run first

5735
07:32:47.093 --> 07:32:51.242
depending on Airflow scheduling. There are many intricacies to defining tasks and

5736
07:32:51.242 --> 07:32:55.288
using the bit shift operators. The best way to solidify these is practicing the

5737
07:32:55.288 --> 07:32:59.591
exercise. Welcome back. Now that we've used the bash operator and worked with tasks,

5738
07:32:59.591 --> 07:33:03.330
let's take a look at some more common operators available within Airflow.

5739
07:33:03.330 --> 07:33:07.607
The Python operator is similar to the bash operator, except that it runs a Python

5740
07:33:07.607 --> 07:33:11.937
function or callable method. Much like the bash operator, it requires a task ID, a

5741
07:33:11.937 --> 07:33:16.214
DAG entry, and most importantly, a Python underscore callable argument set to the

5742
07:33:16.214 --> 07:33:20.333
name of the function in question. You can also pass arguments or keyword style

5743
07:33:20.333 --> 07:33:24.768
arguments into the Python callable as needed. Our first example shows a simple print

5744
07:33:24.768 --> 07:33:29.151
me function that writes a message to the task logs. We must first import the Python

5745
07:33:29.151 --> 07:33:32.900
operator from the Airflow.operators.Python underscore operator library.

5746
07:33:32.900 --> 07:33:37.007
Afterwards, we create our function print me, which will write a quick log message

5747
07:33:37.007 --> 07:33:40.860
when run. Once defined, we create the Python operator instance called Python

5748
07:33:40.860 --> 07:33:45.119
underscore task and add the necessary arguments. The Python operator supports adding

5749
07:33:45.119 --> 07:33:49.378
arguments to a given task. This allows you to pass arguments that can then be passed

5750
07:33:49.378 --> 07:33:53.434
to the Python function assigned to Python callable. The Python operator supports

5751
07:33:53.434 --> 07:33:57.692
both positional and keyword style arguments as options to the task. For this course,

5752
07:33:57.692 --> 07:34:01.140
we'll focus on using keyword arguments only for the sake of clarity.

5753
07:34:01.140 --> 07:34:05.499
To implement keyword arguments with the Python operator, we define an argument on

5754
07:34:05.499 --> 07:34:09.912
the task called op underscore kwargs. This is a dictionary consisting of the named

5755
07:34:09.912 --> 07:34:14.109
arguments for the intended Python function. Let's create a new function called

5756
07:34:14.109 --> 07:34:18.360
sleep, which takes a length of time argument. It uses this argument to call the

5757
07:34:18.360 --> 07:34:22.827
time.sleep method. Once defined, we create a new task called sleep underscore task,

5758
07:34:22.827 --> 07:34:26.540
with the task ID, DAG, and Python callable arguments added as before.

5759
07:34:26.540 --> 07:34:30.534
This time we'll add our op underscore kwrgs dictionary with the length of time

5760
07:34:30.534 --> 07:34:34.836
variable and the value of 5. Note that the dictionary key must match the name of the

5761
07:34:34.836 --> 07:34:39.035
function argument. If the dictionary contains an unexpected key, it will be passed

5762
07:34:39.035 --> 07:34:43.131
to the Python function and typically cause an unexpected keyword argument error.

5763
07:34:43.131 --> 07:34:47.330
There are many other operators available within the Airflow ecosystem. The primary

5764
07:34:47.330 --> 07:34:51.324
operators are in the Airflow.operators or Airflow.contrib.operators libraries.

5765
07:34:51.324 --> 07:34:53.680
Another useful operator is the email operator.

5766
07:34:53.680 --> 07:34:58.079
Which as expected sends an email from within an Airflow task. It can contain the

5767
07:34:58.079 --> 07:35:02.533
typical components of an email, including HTML content and attachments. Note that

5768
07:35:02.533 --> 07:35:07.098
the Airflow system must be configured with the email server details to successfully

5769
07:35:07.098 --> 07:35:11.552
send a message. A quick example for sending an email would be sending a generated

5770
07:35:11.552 --> 07:35:16.116
sales report upon completion of a workflow. We must first import the email operator

5771
07:35:16.116 --> 07:35:20.460
object from Airflow.operators.email underscore operator. We can then create our

5772
07:35:20.460 --> 07:35:22.660
email operator instance with the task ID

5773
07:35:22.660 --> 07:35:27.097
the to, subject, and content fields, and a list of any files to attach. Note that in

5774
07:35:27.097 --> 07:35:31.164
this case, we assume the file latest underscore sales dot xlsx was previously

5775
07:35:31.164 --> 07:35:35.548
generated. Later in the course, we'll see how to verify that first. Finally, we add

5776
07:35:35.548 --> 07:35:39.773
it to our DAG as usual. We've looked at a couple of new Airflow operators. Let's

5777
07:35:39.773 --> 07:35:44.210
practice using them in the exercises. Welcome back. We've spent most of this chapter

5778
07:35:44.210 --> 07:35:48.436
implementing Airflow tasks within our workflows. Now let's take a look at what's

5779
07:35:48.436 --> 07:35:52.080
required to schedule those workflows and have them run automatically.

5780
07:35:52.080 --> 07:35:56.669
When referring to scheduling an Airflow, we must first discuss a DAG run. This is an

5781
07:35:56.669 --> 07:36:00.876
instance of a workflow at a given point in time. For example, it could be the

5782
07:36:00.876 --> 07:36:05.411
currently running instance, or it could be one run last Tuesday at 3 p.m. A DAG can

5783
07:36:05.411 --> 07:36:10.000
be run manually or via the schedule interval parameter past when the DAG is defined.

5784
07:36:10.000 --> 07:36:14.330
Each DAG run maintains a state for itself and the tasks within. The DAGs can have a

5785
07:36:14.330 --> 07:36:18.504
running, failed, or success state. The individual tasks can have these states or

5786
07:36:18.504 --> 07:36:22.834
others as well, I.e. Queued or skipped. Within the Airflow UI, you can view all DAG

5787
07:36:22.834 --> 07:36:27.217
runs under the Browse DAG runs menu option. This provides the assorted details about

5788
07:36:27.217 --> 07:36:31.443
any DAGs that have run within the current Airflow instance. As mentioned, you can

5789
07:36:31.443 --> 07:36:35.721
view the state of a DAG run within this page, illustrating whether the DAG run was

5790
07:36:35.721 --> 07:36:36.660
successful or not.

5791
07:36:36.660 --> 07:36:40.575
When scheduling a DAG, there are many attributes to consider depending on your

5792
07:36:40.575 --> 07:36:44.590
scheduling needs. The Start Date value specifies the first time the DAG could be

5793
07:36:44.590 --> 07:36:48.605
scheduled. This is typically defined with a Python DateTime object. The End Date

5794
07:36:48.605 --> 07:36:52.821
represents the last possible time to schedule the DAG. Max Tries represents how many

5795
07:36:52.821 --> 07:36:56.887
times to retry before fully failing the DAG run. The Schedule Interval represents

5796
07:36:56.887 --> 07:37:00.952
how often to schedule the DAG for execution. There are many nuances to this which

5797
07:37:00.952 --> 07:37:05.118
we'll cover in a moment. The Schedule Interval represents how often to schedule the

5798
07:37:05.118 --> 07:37:05.570
DAG runs.

5799
07:37:05.570 --> 07:37:10.254
The scheduling occurs between the start date and the potential end date. Note that

5800
07:37:10.254 --> 07:37:14.766
this is not when the DAGs will absolutely run, but rather a minimum and maximum

5801
07:37:14.766 --> 07:37:19.335
value of when they could be scheduled. The schedule interval can be defined by a

5802
07:37:19.335 --> 07:37:24.133
couple methods, with a cron-style syntax or via built-in presets. The cron syntax is

5803
07:37:24.133 --> 07:37:28.874
the same as the format for scheduling jobs using the Unix cron tool. It consists of

5804
07:37:28.874 --> 07:37:33.615
five fields separated by a space, starting with the minute value, 0 through 59, the

5805
07:37:33.615 --> 07:37:34.700
hour, 0 through 23,

5806
07:37:34.700 --> 07:37:39.630
the day of the month, 1 through 31, the month, 1 through 12, and the day of the

5807
07:37:39.630 --> 07:37:44.622
week, 0 through 6. An asterisk in any of the fields represents running for every

5808
07:37:44.622 --> 07:37:49.614
interval. For example, an asterisk in the minute field means run every minute. A

5809
07:37:49.614 --> 07:37:54.856
list of values can be given on a field via comma-separated values. The cron entry 0,

5810
07:37:54.856 --> 07:37:59.848
12, asterisk, asterisk, asterisk, means run daily at noon, 12 o'clock. Asterisk,

5811
07:37:59.848 --> 07:38:01.470
asterisk, 25, 2, asterisk.

5812
07:38:01.470 --> 07:38:06.955
Represents running once per minute but only on February 25th. Zero comma 15 comma 30

5813
07:38:06.955 --> 07:38:12.439
comma 45 asterisk asterisk asterisk asterisk asterisk means to run every 15 minutes.

5814
07:38:12.439 --> 07:38:17.859
Airflow has several presets or shortcut syntax options representing often used time

5815
07:38:17.859 --> 07:38:23.344
intervals. The at hourly preset means run once an hour at the beginning of the hour.

5816
07:38:23.344 --> 07:38:28.828
It's equivalent to zero asterisk asterisk asterisk asterisk in cron. The at daily at

5817
07:38:28.828 --> 07:38:29.220
weekly

5818
07:38:29.220 --> 07:38:33.726
at monthly, and at yearly presets behave similarly. Airflow also has two special

5819
07:38:33.726 --> 07:38:38.346
presets for schedule intervals. None means don't ever schedule the DAG and is used

5820
07:38:38.346 --> 07:38:42.627
for manually triggered workflows. At once means to only schedule a DAG once.

5821
07:38:42.627 --> 07:38:47.021
Scheduling DAGs has an important nuance to consider. When scheduling DAG runs,

5822
07:38:47.021 --> 07:38:51.527
Airflow will use the start date as the earliest possible value, but not actually

5823
07:38:51.527 --> 07:38:56.146
schedule anything until at least one schedule interval has passed beyond the start

5824
07:38:56.146 --> 07:38:58.850
date. Given a start date of February 25th, 2020,

5825
07:38:58.850 --> 07:39:02.949
and an at daily schedule interval, Airflow would then use the date of February 26th,

5826
07:39:02.949 --> 07:39:06.902
2020 for the first run of the DAG. This can be tricky to consider when adding new

5827
07:39:06.902 --> 07:39:10.855
DAG schedules, especially if they have longer schedule intervals. We've covered a

5828
07:39:10.855 --> 07:39:14.808
lot of ground in this lesson and chapter. Let's practice scheduling our workflows

5829
07:39:14.808 --> 07:39:18.810
and we'll see each other back in chapter. Welcome back. Now that we've gotten some

5830
07:39:18.810 --> 07:39:22.812
practice using operators and tasks within Airflow, let's look at a special kind of

5831
07:39:22.812 --> 07:39:26.765
operator called a sensor. A sensor is a special kind of operator that waits for a

5832
07:39:26.765 --> 07:39:28.180
certain condition to be true.

5833
07:39:28.180 --> 07:39:32.671
Some examples of conditions include waiting for the creation of a file, uploading a

5834
07:39:32.671 --> 07:39:37.055
database record, or a specific response from a web request. With sensors, you can

5835
07:39:37.055 --> 07:39:41.600
define how often to check for the conditions to be true. Since sensors are a type of

5836
07:39:41.600 --> 07:39:46.091
operator, they are assigned to tasks just like normal operators. This means you can

5837
07:39:46.091 --> 07:39:50.529
apply the bit shift dependencies to them as well. All sensors are derived from the

5838
07:39:50.529 --> 07:39:54.641
Airflow.sensors.base-sensor-operator class. There are some default arguments

5839
07:39:54.641 --> 07:39:56.860
available to all sensors, including mode,

5840
07:39:56.860 --> 07:40:00.893
poke underscore interval, and timeout. The mode tells the sensor how to check for

5841
07:40:00.893 --> 07:40:05.076
the condition and has two options, poke or reschedule. The default is poke and means

5842
07:40:05.076 --> 07:40:09.010
to continue checking until complete without giving up a worker slot. Reschedule

5843
07:40:09.010 --> 07:40:12.944
means to give up the worker slot and wait for another slot to become available.

5844
07:40:12.944 --> 07:40:17.127
We'll discuss worker slots in the next lesson, but for now consider a worker slot to

5845
07:40:17.127 --> 07:40:21.060
be the capability to run a task. The poke interval is used in the poke mode and

5846
07:40:21.060 --> 07:40:23.600
tells Airflow how often to check for the condition.

5847
07:40:23.600 --> 07:40:27.540
This should be at least one minute to keep from overloading the Airflow scheduler.

5848
07:40:27.540 --> 07:40:31.528
The timeout field is how long to wait in seconds before marking the sensor task has

5849
07:40:31.528 --> 07:40:35.467
failed. To avoid issues, make sure your timeout is significantly shorter than your

5850
07:40:35.467 --> 07:40:39.215
schedule interval. Note that as sensors or operators, they also include normal

5851
07:40:39.215 --> 07:40:43.203
operator attributes such as task underscore ID and DAG. A useful sensor is the file

5852
07:40:43.203 --> 07:40:47.191
sensor found in the Airflow.contrib.sensors library. The file sensor checks for the

5853
07:40:47.191 --> 07:40:51.178
existence of a file at a certain location in the file system. It can also check for

5854
07:40:51.178 --> 07:40:52.860
any files within a given directory.

5855
07:40:52.860 --> 07:40:57.058
A quick example is importing the file sensor object, then defining a task called

5856
07:40:57.058 --> 07:41:01.309
file underscore sensor underscore task. We set the task ID and the DAG entries as

5857
07:41:01.309 --> 07:41:05.664
usual. The file path argument is set to sales data dot CSV, looking for a file with

5858
07:41:05.664 --> 07:41:10.020
this file name to exist before continuing. We set the poke interval to 300 seconds,

5859
07:41:10.020 --> 07:41:14.376
or to repeat the check every five minutes until true. Finally, we use the bit shift

5860
07:41:14.376 --> 07:41:18.731
operators to define the sensor's dependencies within our DAG. In this case, we must

5861
07:41:18.731 --> 07:41:21.250
run the init underscore sales underscore cleanup

5862
07:41:21.250 --> 07:41:25.304
then wait for the file underscore sensor underscore task to finish. Then we run

5863
07:41:25.304 --> 07:41:29.204
generate underscore report. There are many types of sensors available within

5864
07:41:29.204 --> 07:41:33.361
Airflow. The external task sensor waits for a task in a separate DAG to complete.

5865
07:41:33.361 --> 07:41:37.313
This allows a loose connection to other workflow tasks without making any one

5866
07:41:37.313 --> 07:41:41.623
workflow too complex. The HTTP sensor will request a web URL and allow you to define

5867
07:41:41.623 --> 07:41:45.934
the content to check for. The SQL sensor runs a SQL query to check for content. Many

5868
07:41:45.934 --> 07:41:49.937
other sensors are available in the Airflow.sensors and Airflow.contrib.sensors

5869
07:41:49.937 --> 07:41:50.450
libraries.

5870
07:41:50.450 --> 07:41:54.159
You may be wondering when to use a sensor versus an operator. For the most part,

5871
07:41:54.159 --> 07:41:57.543
you'll want to use a normal operator unless you have any of the following

5872
07:41:57.543 --> 07:42:01.391
requirements. You're uncertain when a condition will be true. If you know something

5873
07:42:01.391 --> 07:42:05.239
will complete that day, but it might vary by an hour or so, you can use a sensor to

5874
07:42:05.239 --> 07:42:08.762
check until it is. If you want to continue to check for a condition, but not

5875
07:42:08.762 --> 07:42:12.379
necessarily fail the entire DAG immediately, this provides some flexibility in

5876
07:42:12.379 --> 07:42:16.087
defining your DAG. Finally, if you want to repeatedly run a check without adding

5877
07:42:16.087 --> 07:42:18.220
cycles to your DAG, sensors are a good choice.

5878
07:42:18.220 --> 07:42:21.956
We've looked at a lot about sensors. Let's practice using them. Now that we've

5879
07:42:21.956 --> 07:42:25.931
implemented some Airflow sensors, let's discuss the execution model within Airflow.

5880
07:42:25.931 --> 07:42:29.954
In Airflow, an executor is the component that actually runs the tasks defined within

5881
07:42:29.954 --> 07:42:33.881
your workflows. Each executor has different capabilities and behaviors for running

5882
07:42:33.881 --> 07:42:37.665
the set of tasks. Some may run a single task at a time on a local system, while

5883
07:42:37.665 --> 07:42:41.688
others might split individual tasks among all the systems in a cluster. As mentioned

5884
07:42:41.688 --> 07:42:45.471
in the previous lesson, this is often referred to as the number of worker slots

5885
07:42:45.471 --> 07:42:45.950
available.

5886
07:42:45.950 --> 07:42:50.033
We'll discuss some of these in more detail soon, but a few examples of executors are

5887
07:42:50.033 --> 07:42:54.116
the sequential executor, the local executor, and the celery executor. This is not an

5888
07:42:54.116 --> 07:42:58.054
exhaustive list, and you can also create your own executor if required, though we

5889
07:42:58.054 --> 07:43:01.991
won't cover that in this course. The sequential executor is the default execution

5890
07:43:01.991 --> 07:43:06.074
engine for Airflow. It runs only a single task at a time. This means having multiple

5891
07:43:06.074 --> 07:43:10.109
workflows scheduled around the same time frame may cause things to take longer than

5892
07:43:10.109 --> 07:43:14.095
expected. The sequential executor is useful for debugging as it's fairly simple to

5893
07:43:14.095 --> 07:43:15.310
follow the flow of tasks.

5894
07:43:15.310 --> 07:43:19.092
And it can also be used with some integrated development environments, though we

5895
07:43:19.092 --> 07:43:23.016
won't cover that here. The most important aspect of the sequential executor is that

5896
07:43:23.016 --> 07:43:26.987
while it's very functional for learning and testing, it's not really recommended for

5897
07:43:26.987 --> 07:43:30.864
production due to the limitations of task resources. The local executor is another

5898
07:43:30.864 --> 07:43:34.741
option for Airflow that runs entirely on a single system. It basically treats each

5899
07:43:34.741 --> 07:43:38.665
task as a process on the local system and is able to start as many concurrent tasks

5900
07:43:38.665 --> 07:43:42.542
as desired, requested, and permitted by the system resources, for example, the CPU

5901
07:43:42.542 --> 07:43:43.440
cores, memory, etc.

5902
07:43:43.440 --> 07:43:47.519
This concurrency is the parallelism of the system, and it is defined by the user in

5903
07:43:47.519 --> 07:43:51.549
one of two ways, either unlimited or unlimited to a certain number of simultaneous

5904
07:43:51.549 --> 07:43:55.383
tasks. Defined intelligently, the local executor is a good choice for a single

5905
07:43:55.383 --> 07:43:59.462
production Airflow system and can utilize all the resources of a given host system.

5906
07:43:59.462 --> 07:44:03.541
The last executor we'll look at is the Celery executor. If you're not familiar with

5907
07:44:03.541 --> 07:44:07.669
Celery, it's a general queuing system written in Python that allows multiple systems

5908
07:44:07.669 --> 07:44:09.340
to communicate as a basic cluster.

5909
07:44:09.340 --> 07:44:13.574
Using a Celery Executor, multiple Airflow systems can be configured as workers for a

5910
07:44:13.574 --> 07:44:17.606
given set of workflows or tasks. You can add extra systems at any time to better

5911
07:44:17.606 --> 07:44:21.790
balance workflows. The power of the Celery Executor is significantly more difficult

5912
07:44:21.790 --> 07:44:25.620
to set up and configure. It requires a working Celery configuration prior to

5913
07:44:25.620 --> 07:44:29.703
configuring Airflow, not to mention some method to share DAGs between the system,

5914
07:44:29.703 --> 07:44:33.584
such as a Git server, network file system, etc. While it is more difficult to

5915
07:44:33.584 --> 07:44:37.767
configure, the Celery Executor is a powerful choice for anyone working with a large

5916
07:44:37.767 --> 07:44:39.330
number of DAGs or expects their

5917
07:44:39.330 --> 07:44:43.431
processing needs to grow. Sometimes when developing Airflow workflows, you may want

5918
07:44:43.431 --> 07:44:47.385
to know the executor being used. If you have access to the command line, you can

5919
07:44:47.385 --> 07:44:51.338
determine this by looking at the appropriate Airflow.config file, search for the

5920
07:44:51.338 --> 07:44:55.390
executor colon line, and it will specify the executor in use. Note that we haven't

5921
07:44:55.390 --> 07:44:59.195
discussed the Airflow.config file in depth, as we assume a configured Airflow

5922
07:44:59.195 --> 07:45:03.346
instance in this course. The Airflow.cfg file is where most of the configuration and

5923
07:45:03.346 --> 07:45:07.200
settings for the Airflow instance are defined, including the type of executor.

5924
07:45:07.200 --> 07:45:11.166
You can also determine the executor by running Airflow list underscore DAGs from the

5925
07:45:11.166 --> 07:45:15.131
command line. Within the first few lines, you should see an entry for which executor

5926
07:45:15.131 --> 07:45:19.049
is in use. In this case, it's the sequential executor. We've just discussed some of

5927
07:45:19.049 --> 07:45:22.873
the various Airflow executors. Let's practice what we've learned in the exercises

5928
07:45:22.873 --> 07:45:26.697
ahead. Welcome back. Let's take a look at one of the biggest aspects of running a

5929
07:45:26.697 --> 07:45:30.332
production system with Airflow and data engineering in general, debugging and

5930
07:45:30.332 --> 07:45:34.156
troubleshooting. There are several common issues you may run across while working

5931
07:45:34.156 --> 07:45:34.770
with Airflow.

5932
07:45:34.770 --> 07:45:38.845
It helps to have an idea of what these might be and how best to handle them. The

5933
07:45:38.845 --> 07:45:42.971
first common issue is a DAG or DAGs that won't run on schedule. The next is a DAG

5934
07:45:42.971 --> 07:45:47.047
that simply won't load into the system. The last common scenario involves syntax

5935
07:45:47.047 --> 07:45:51.326
errors. Let's look at these more closely. The most common reason why a DAG won't run

5936
07:45:51.326 --> 07:45:55.401
on schedule is the scheduler is not running. Airflow contains several components

5937
07:45:55.401 --> 07:45:59.680
that accomplish various aspects of the system. The Airflow scheduler handles DAG run

5938
07:45:59.680 --> 07:46:02.940
and task scheduling. If it is not running, no new tasks can run.

5939
07:46:02.940 --> 07:46:06.819
You'll often see this error within the web UI if the scheduler component is not

5940
07:46:06.819 --> 07:46:10.944
running. You can easily fix this issue by running Airflow Scheduler from the command

5941
07:46:10.944 --> 07:46:15.020
line. As we've covered before, another common issue with scheduling is the scenario

5942
07:46:15.020 --> 07:46:18.998
where at least one schedule interval period has not passed since either the start

5943
07:46:18.998 --> 07:46:23.024
date or the last DAG run. There isn't a specifics fix for this, but you might want

5944
07:46:23.024 --> 07:46:26.560
to modify the start date or schedule interval to meet your requirements.

5945
07:46:26.560 --> 07:46:30.882
The last scheduling issue you'll often see is related to what we've covered in the

5946
07:46:30.882 --> 07:46:35.152
previous lesson. The executor does not have enough free slots to run tasks. There

5947
07:46:35.152 --> 07:46:39.580
are basically three ways to alleviate this problem. By changing the executor type to

5948
07:46:39.580 --> 07:46:43.850
something capable of more tasks, the local executor or seller executor, by adding

5949
07:46:43.850 --> 07:46:48.067
systems or system resources, RAM, CPUs, or finally by changing the scheduling of

5950
07:46:48.067 --> 07:46:52.284
your DAGs. You'll often see an issue where a new DAG will not appear in your DAG

5951
07:46:52.284 --> 07:46:55.710
view of the web UI or in the Airflow list underscore DAGs output.

5952
07:46:55.710 --> 07:46:59.920
The first thing to check is that the Python file is in the expected DAGs folder or

5953
07:46:59.920 --> 07:47:03.873
directory. You can determine the current DAGs folder setting by examining the

5954
07:47:03.873 --> 07:47:07.827
Airflow.cfg file. The line DAGs underscore folder will indicate where Airflow

5955
07:47:07.827 --> 07:47:12.139
expects to find your Python DAG files. Note that the folder path must be an absolute

5956
07:47:12.139 --> 07:47:16.349
path. Probably the most common reason a DAG workflow won't appear in your DAG list

5957
07:47:16.349 --> 07:47:20.559
is one or more syntax errors in your Python code. These are sometimes difficult to

5958
07:47:20.559 --> 07:47:24.769
find, especially in an editor not set up for Python or Airflow, such as a base vim

5959
07:47:24.769 --> 07:47:25.180
install.

5960
07:47:25.180 --> 07:47:29.118
I tend to prefer using Vim with some Python tools loaded, or VS Code, but it's

5961
07:47:29.118 --> 07:47:33.358
really up to your preference. There are two quick methods to check for these issues,

5962
07:47:33.358 --> 07:47:37.548
Airflow list underscore DAGs, and running your DAG script with Python. The first is

5963
07:47:37.548 --> 07:47:41.788
to run Airflow space list underscore DAGs. As we've seen before, Airflow will output

5964
07:47:41.788 --> 07:47:45.776
some debugging information in the list of DAGs it's processed. If there are any

5965
07:47:45.776 --> 07:47:49.663
errors, those will appear in the output, helping you to troubleshoot further.

5966
07:47:49.663 --> 07:47:53.702
Another method to verify Python syntax is to run the actual Python 3 interpreter

5967
07:47:53.702 --> 07:47:54.560
against the file.

5968
07:47:54.560 --> 07:47:58.783
You won't see any output normally, as there's nothing for the interpreter to do, but

5969
07:47:58.783 --> 07:48:02.956
it can check for any syntax errors in your code. If there are errors, you'll get an

5970
07:48:02.956 --> 07:48:06.978
appropriate error message. If there are no errors, you'll be returned to command

5971
07:48:06.978 --> 07:48:11.150
prompt. Let's practice handling some of these common issues in the exercises ahead.

5972
07:48:11.150 --> 07:48:14.971
Welcome back. Let's talk now about SLAs and reporting in Airflow. You may be

5973
07:48:14.971 --> 07:48:18.842
wondering, what is an SLA? SLA stands for Service Level Agreement. Within the

5974
07:48:18.842 --> 07:48:23.065
business world, this is often an uptime or availability guarantee. Airflow treats it

5975
07:48:23.065 --> 07:48:23.970
a bit differently.

5976
07:48:23.970 --> 07:48:28.677
It's considered the amount of time a task or a DAG should require to run. An SLA

5977
07:48:28.677 --> 07:48:33.560
miss is any situation where a task or DAG does not meet the expected timing for the

5978
07:48:33.560 --> 07:48:38.443
SLA. If an SLA is missed, an email alert is sent out per system configuration and a

5979
07:48:38.443 --> 07:48:43.208
note is made in the log. Any SLA miss can be viewed in the Browse SLA Misses menu

5980
07:48:43.208 --> 07:48:48.092
item of the web UI. To view any given SLA miss, you can access it in the web UI via

5981
07:48:48.092 --> 07:48:49.680
the Browse SLA Misses link.

5982
07:48:49.680 --> 07:48:53.837
It provides you general information about what task missed the SLA and when it

5983
07:48:53.837 --> 07:48:58.207
failed. It also indicates if an email has been sent and when the SLA failed. There

5984
07:48:58.207 --> 07:49:02.577
are several ways to define an SLA, but we'll only look at two for this course. The

5985
07:49:02.577 --> 07:49:07.053
first is via an SLA argument on the task itself. This takes a time delta object with

5986
07:49:07.053 --> 07:49:11.263
the amount of time to pass. The second way is using the default underscore args

5987
07:49:11.263 --> 07:49:15.633
dictionary and defining an SLA key. The dictionary is then passed into the default

5988
07:49:15.633 --> 07:49:19.470
underscore args argument of the DAG and applies to any tasks internally.

5989
07:49:19.470 --> 07:49:23.771
We haven't covered the TimeDelta object yet, so let's look at some of the details.

5990
07:49:23.771 --> 07:49:27.862
It's found in the DateTime library along with the DateTime object, most easily

5991
07:49:27.862 --> 07:49:31.796
accessed with an import statement of fromDateTime importTimeDelta. It takes

5992
07:49:31.796 --> 07:49:36.149
arguments of days, seconds, minutes, hours, and weeks. It also has milliseconds and

5993
07:49:36.149 --> 07:49:40.450
microseconds available, but those wouldn't apply to Airflow. To create the object,

5994
07:49:40.450 --> 07:49:44.751
you simply call TimeDelta with the argument or arguments you wish to reference. To

5995
07:49:44.751 --> 07:49:47.950
create a 30-second TimeDelta, call it with seconds equals 30.

5996
07:49:47.950 --> 07:49:52.101
Or weeks equals two, or you could combine it into a longer mix of any of the

5997
07:49:52.101 --> 07:49:56.580
arguments you wish. In this case, four days, 10 hours, 20 minutes, and 30 seconds.

5998
07:49:56.580 --> 07:50:01.059
For reporting purposes, you can use email alerting built into Airflow. There are a

5999
07:50:01.059 --> 07:50:05.375
couple of ways to do this. Airflow has built-in options for sending messages on

6000
07:50:05.375 --> 07:50:09.908
success, failure, or error or retry. These are handled via keys in the default args

6001
07:50:09.908 --> 07:50:14.387
dictionary that gets passed on DAG creation. The required component is the list of

6002
07:50:14.387 --> 07:50:16.190
emails assigned to the email key.

6003
07:50:16.190 --> 07:50:20.259
Then there are boolean options for email underscore on underscore failure email

6004
07:50:20.259 --> 07:50:24.173
underscore on underscore retry and email underscore on underscore success in

6005
07:50:24.173 --> 07:50:28.396
addition we've already looked at the email operator earlier but this is useful for

6006
07:50:28.396 --> 07:50:32.567
sending emails outside of one of the defined Airflow options note that sending an

6007
07:50:32.567 --> 07:50:36.842
email does require a configuration within Airflow that is outside the scope of this

6008
07:50:36.842 --> 07:50:41.013
course the Airflow documentation provides information on how to set up the global

6009
07:50:41.013 --> 07:50:44.670
email configuration let's finish up this chapter by practicing what you

6010
07:50:44.670 --> 07:50:48.532
Great work getting to the last chapter of this course. You've learned a lot, but

6011
07:50:48.532 --> 07:50:52.443
let's take a look at some of the more advanced features of Airflow, starting with

6012
07:50:52.443 --> 07:50:56.401
templates. You may be wondering what templates are and what they do in the case of

6013
07:50:56.401 --> 07:51:00.215
Airflow. Templates allow substitution of information during a DAG run. In other

6014
07:51:00.215 --> 07:51:03.981
words, every time a DAG with templated information is executed, information is

6015
07:51:03.981 --> 07:51:07.988
interpreted and included with the DAG run. Templates provide added flexibility when

6016
07:51:07.988 --> 07:51:11.995
defining tasks. We'll see examples of this shortly. Templates are created using the

6017
07:51:11.995 --> 07:51:13.250
Jinja templating language.

6018
07:51:13.250 --> 07:51:17.506
A full explanation of Jinja is out of scope for this course, but we'll cover some

6019
07:51:17.506 --> 07:51:21.762
basics in the coming slides. Before we get specifically into a templated example,

6020
07:51:21.762 --> 07:51:25.912
let's consider what we would do for the following requirement. Your manager has

6021
07:51:25.912 --> 07:51:30.326
asked you to simply echo the word reading and a list of files to a log or output. If

6022
07:51:30.326 --> 07:51:34.739
we were to do this with what we currently know about Airflow, we would likely create

6023
07:51:34.739 --> 07:51:38.995
multiple tasks using the bash operator. Our first task would set up the task with

6024
07:51:38.995 --> 07:51:41.780
the intended bash command. In this case, echo reading

6025
07:51:41.780 --> 07:51:45.967
file1.txt as an argument to the bash operator. If we had a second file, we would

6026
07:51:45.967 --> 07:51:50.155
create a second task using the bash command echo reading file2.txt. This type of

6027
07:51:50.155 --> 07:51:54.551
code would continue for the entire list of files. Consider what this would look like

6028
07:51:54.551 --> 07:51:58.843
if we had 5, 10, or even 100 plus files we needed to process. There would be a lot

6029
07:51:58.843 --> 07:52:03.083
of repetitive code, not to mention what if you needed to change the command being

6030
07:52:03.083 --> 07:52:07.480
used. Let's take a look at how we would accomplish the same behavior with templates.

6031
07:52:07.480 --> 07:52:10.620
First, we need to create a variable containing our template.

6032
07:52:10.620 --> 07:52:14.758
Which is really just a string with some specialized formatting. Our string is the

6033
07:52:14.758 --> 07:52:18.947
actual bash command echo, and instead of the file name, we're using two open curly

6034
07:52:18.947 --> 07:52:22.932
braces, the term params.filename, and then two closing curly braces. The curly

6035
07:52:22.932 --> 07:52:27.223
braces, when used in this manner, represent information to be substituted. This will

6036
07:52:27.223 --> 07:52:31.412
make more sense in a moment. If you've done any web development or worked with any

6037
07:52:31.412 --> 07:52:35.499
reporting tool, you've likely worked with something similar. Next, we create our

6038
07:52:35.499 --> 07:52:39.790
Airflow task as we have previously. We assign a task ID and a DAG argument as usual.

6039
07:52:39.790 --> 07:52:43.989
But our bash command looks a little different. We set the bash command to use the

6040
07:52:43.989 --> 07:52:48.135
templated command string we defined earlier. We also have an additional argument

6041
07:52:48.135 --> 07:52:52.282
called params. In this case, params is a dictionary containing a single key file

6042
07:52:52.282 --> 07:52:56.325
name with the value file1.txt. Now, if you look back at the templated command,

6043
07:52:56.325 --> 07:53:00.420
you'll notice that the term in the curly braces is params.filename. At runtime,

6044
07:53:00.420 --> 07:53:04.308
Airflow will execute the bash operator by reading the templated command and

6045
07:53:04.308 --> 07:53:08.454
replacing params.filename with the value stored in the params dictionary for the

6046
07:53:08.454 --> 07:53:09.180
file name key.

6047
07:53:09.180 --> 07:53:14.115
In other words, it would pass the bash operator echo reading file1.txt. The actual

6048
07:53:14.115 --> 07:53:19.109
log output would be reading file1.txt after the bash operator executed the command.

6049
07:53:19.109 --> 07:53:23.803
Now let's consider one way to use templates for our earlier task of outputting

6050
07:53:23.803 --> 07:53:28.738
reading file1.txt and reading file2.txt. First, we create our templated command as

6051
07:53:28.738 --> 07:53:33.792
before. Next, create the first task and pass the params dict with a filename key and

6052
07:53:33.792 --> 07:53:36.380
the value file1.txt. To pass another entry,

6053
07:53:36.380 --> 07:53:40.019
we can create a second task and modify the params dict accordingly. This time, the

6054
07:53:40.019 --> 07:53:43.703
file name would contain file2.txt, and Airflow would substitute that value instead.

6055
07:53:43.703 --> 07:53:47.432
The resulting output would be as expected. Note, you may be wondering what templates

6056
07:53:47.432 --> 07:53:50.982
do for you here. You'll see more in the coming exercises and lessons. We'll have

6057
07:53:50.982 --> 07:53:54.577
more to discuss about templates in the next lesson, but let's practice what we've

6058
07:53:54.577 --> 07:53:58.261
learned, and I'll see you later. Welcome back. Hopefully you've cemented the basics

6059
07:53:58.261 --> 07:54:01.945
of templates in your mind. Now let's look at some of the more powerful options they

6060
07:54:01.945 --> 07:54:02.300
provide.

6061
07:54:02.300 --> 07:54:06.194
In our last lesson, we were given the task of taking a list of filenames and

6062
07:54:06.194 --> 07:54:10.497
printing reading filename to the log or output. In our templated version, we created

6063
07:54:10.497 --> 07:54:14.134
a templated command that substituted the filename value in place of the

6064
07:54:14.134 --> 07:54:17.874
params.filename. We also used two tasks with different filename values to

6065
07:54:17.874 --> 07:54:22.075
demonstrate one way to use templates without changing the actual bash command. Now

6066
07:54:22.075 --> 07:54:25.968
let's consider another way to perform a substitution. Jinja templates can be

6067
07:54:25.968 --> 07:54:28.530
considerably more powerful than we've used so far.

6068
07:54:28.530 --> 07:54:33.419
It is possible to use a for construct to allow us to iterate over a list and output

6069
07:54:33.419 --> 07:54:38.248
content accordingly. Let's change our templated command to the following. We start

6070
07:54:38.248 --> 07:54:43.196
with an open curly brace and the percent symbol. Then we use a normal Python command

6071
07:54:43.196 --> 07:54:47.907
of for filename and params.filenames and then percent close curly brace. Then we

6072
07:54:47.907 --> 07:54:52.678
modify our output line to be echo reading open curly braces file name close curly

6073
07:54:52.678 --> 07:54:56.860
braces. We then use a jinja entry to represent the end of the for loop.

6074
07:54:56.860 --> 07:55:01.550
Open curly brace percent, end for, percent close curly brace. Note that this is

6075
07:55:01.550 --> 07:55:06.358
required to define the end of the loop, as opposed to Python's typical whitespace

6076
07:55:06.358 --> 07:55:11.285
indention. Now let's look at our bash operator task. It looks similar, except we've

6077
07:55:11.285 --> 07:55:15.915
modified the params key to be file names, and the value is now a list with two

6078
07:55:15.915 --> 07:55:20.783
strings, file1.txt and file2.txt. When Airflow executes the bash operator, it will

6079
07:55:20.783 --> 07:55:25.710
iterate over the entries in the file names list and substitute them in accordingly.

6080
07:55:25.710 --> 07:55:30.391
Our output is the same as before, with a single task instead of two. Consider, too,

6081
07:55:30.391 --> 07:55:35.016
the difference in code if you had 100 files in the list. As part of the templating

6082
07:55:35.016 --> 07:55:39.753
system, Airflow provides a set of built-in runtime variables. These provide assorted

6083
07:55:39.753 --> 07:55:44.265
information about DAG runs, individual tasks, and even the system configuration.

6084
07:55:44.265 --> 07:55:49.003
Template examples include the execution date, which is DS and the double curly brace

6085
07:55:49.003 --> 07:55:53.345
pairs. It returns the date in a four-digit year-two-digit month-two-digit day

6086
07:55:53.345 --> 07:55:53.740
format.

6087
07:55:53.740 --> 07:55:58.435
There's also a ds underscore no dash variety to get the same info without dashes.

6088
07:55:58.435 --> 07:56:00.000
Note that this is a string,

6089
07:56:00.000 --> 07:56:04.530
not a Python datetime object. Another variable available is the prev underscore ds,

6090
07:56:04.530 --> 07:56:08.786
which gives us the date of the previous DAG runs in the same format as ds. The

6091
07:56:08.786 --> 07:56:13.261
no-dash variety is here as well. You can also access the full DAG object using the

6092
07:56:13.261 --> 07:56:17.300
DAG variable, or you can use the conf object to access the current Airflow

6093
07:56:17.300 --> 07:56:21.884
configuration within code. There are many more variables available. You can refer to

6094
07:56:21.884 --> 07:56:26.414
the documentation for more information. In addition to the other Airflow variables,

6095
07:56:26.414 --> 07:56:28.160
there is also a macros variable.

6096
07:56:28.160 --> 07:56:33.147
The macros package provides a reference to various useful objects or methods for the

6097
07:56:33.147 --> 07:56:38.074
Airflow templates. Some examples of these include the macros.datetime, which is the

6098
07:56:38.074 --> 07:56:42.645
Python datetime.datetime object. The macros.timedelta template references the

6099
07:56:42.645 --> 07:56:47.573
timedelta object. A macros.uuid is the same as Python's uuid object. Finally, there

6100
07:56:47.573 --> 07:56:52.144
are also some added functions available, such as macros.ds underscore add. It

6101
07:56:52.144 --> 07:56:57.071
provides an easy way to perform datemath within a template. It takes two arguments,

6102
07:56:57.071 --> 07:56:58.140
a four-digit year,

6103
07:56:58.140 --> 07:57:02.795
two-digit month, two-digit day, date string, and an integer representing the number

6104
07:57:02.795 --> 07:57:07.451
of days to add or subtract if the number is negative. Our example here would return

6105
07:57:07.451 --> 07:57:11.826
April 20th, 2020. These are not all the available macros objects. Refer to the

6106
07:57:11.826 --> 07:57:16.201
Airflow documentation for more info. We've seen several interesting aspects of

6107
07:57:16.201 --> 07:57:20.744
Airflow templates. Let's practice using them. Nice work so far. We're nearing the

6108
07:57:20.744 --> 07:57:25.119
end of our introduction to Airflow. Let's take a look at our last new concept,

6109
07:57:25.119 --> 07:57:25.680
branching.

6110
07:57:25.680 --> 07:57:29.982
Branching provides the ability for conditional logic within Airflow. Basically, this

6111
07:57:29.982 --> 07:57:34.182
means that tasks can be selectively executed or skipped depending on the result of

6112
07:57:34.182 --> 07:57:38.279
an operator. By default, we're using the branch Python operator. There are other

