08:30:49.478 --> 08:30:54.227
they start a Spark session, so either one could be given as the main Python file.

6564
08:30:54.227 --> 08:30:58.977
Alright, that's it. Let's practice packaging and running a Spark job to make sure

6565
08:30:58.977 --> 08:31:03.550
our pipeline works as expected. Ready for the next step in building pipelines?

6566
08:31:03.550 --> 08:31:08.602
We already know how to ingest data and how to deal with big datasets. Now let's make

6567
08:31:08.602 --> 08:31:13.653
our code more robust and reusable. A software product, like a browser app, is rarely

6568
08:31:13.653 --> 08:31:18.344
ever finished. It is natural for a product to change because different or more

6569
08:31:18.344 --> 08:31:23.275
functionality is requested. Also, problems get found and need to be resolved, like

6570
08:31:23.275 --> 08:31:28.086
incorrect calculations or poor responsiveness. However, key aspects of a product

6571
08:31:28.086 --> 08:31:29.710
also need to stay constant.

6572
08:31:29.710 --> 08:31:34.586
Code that calculates people's wages based on the hours they worked had better be

6573
08:31:34.586 --> 08:31:39.646
constant and well tested. So how do you ensure core functionality remains stable in

6574
08:31:39.646 --> 08:31:44.705
the light of changes? The answer is tests. Tests are written and executed to assert

6575
08:31:44.705 --> 08:31:49.825
our expectations are matched. By committing tests to our codebase, we have a written

6576
08:31:49.825 --> 08:31:54.640
copy of our expectations as they were at some time in the past. So having tests

6577
08:31:54.640 --> 08:31:57.810
reduces the chances of introducing breaking changes.

6578
08:31:57.810 --> 08:32:02.450
If you need to change a small function somewhere so that it returns a tupon rather

6579
08:32:02.450 --> 08:32:06.920
than a single object, how sure are you that that function wasn't used elsewhere

6580
08:32:06.920 --> 08:32:11.561
where it required the return value to be a single object? Without tests, you would

6581
08:32:11.561 --> 08:32:16.314
not be informed until this function was called. This is costly. Automated tests help

6582
08:32:16.314 --> 08:32:20.671
in preventing this. Tests are rarely exhaustive though. A function that tests

6583
08:32:20.671 --> 08:32:25.368
whether a certain day is a holiday would need to test every day of every year to be

6584
08:32:25.368 --> 08:32:26.160
sure it works.

6585
08:32:26.160 --> 08:32:31.117
You would need a lookup table of thousands of records clearly this is insane instead

6586
08:32:31.117 --> 08:32:36.073
we choose to test only a few samples therefore tests don't assert correctness in all

6587
08:32:36.073 --> 08:32:40.794
cases but they raise confidence that our expectations are met in a few cases and

6588
08:32:40.794 --> 08:32:45.692
that already is a very good reason to have them another great argument for the need

6589
08:32:45.692 --> 08:32:50.294
of tests is that they are the most up-to-date form of documentation oftentimes

6590
08:32:50.294 --> 08:32:54.838
people write documents describing the functionality of some piece of software

6591
08:32:54.838 --> 08:32:55.310
manually

6592
08:32:55.310 --> 08:32:59.503
That's fine as long as the description remains high level. Details about certain

6593
08:32:59.503 --> 08:33:03.801
functions should be avoided, because they have a tendency to grow out of sync with

6594
08:33:03.801 --> 08:33:08.204
what is actually running. A well-written test can clarify what a piece of code does,

6595
08:33:08.204 --> 08:33:12.450
even if it treats that piece as a black box. There are more reasons to test code,

6596
08:33:12.450 --> 08:33:16.853
but these three reasons alone should provide sufficient incentive to have them. Now,

6597
08:33:16.853 --> 08:33:21.099
sometimes people avoid writing tests because it takes time they believe is better

6598
08:33:21.099 --> 08:33:23.300
spent on adding more features to software.

6599
08:33:23.300 --> 08:33:28.320
While it is true that testing takes time, it is an investment that pays back as your

6600
08:33:28.320 --> 08:33:33.340
project gets bigger, especially when tests aren't duplicated at different places and

6601
08:33:33.340 --> 08:33:38.181
tests non-trivial parts. A useful concept to keep in mind when designing tests is

6602
08:33:38.181 --> 08:33:43.141
the test pyramid. It tells you where to invest your efforts. Testing pieces of code

6603
08:33:43.141 --> 08:33:48.041
that do not rely on integration with external components are unit tests. These can

6604
08:33:48.041 --> 08:33:50.850
run fast and their development effort is cheap.

6605
08:33:50.850 --> 08:33:55.888
An example of this is a data cleaning function that transforms all variations of yes

6606
08:33:55.888 --> 08:34:00.866
and no strings to proper booleans. Because these tests run so fast, you should have

6607
08:34:00.866 --> 08:34:05.845
many of them. Interactions with file systems and databases are integration tests or

6608
08:34:05.845 --> 08:34:10.583
service tests as they integrate different services or components. They run more

6609
08:34:10.583 --> 08:34:15.381
slowly and their setup also takes more effort. You can have less of them because

6610
08:34:15.381 --> 08:34:18.260
your unit tests should already cover some parts.

6611
08:34:18.260 --> 08:34:23.327
Like the conversion to booleans before inserting records into a database. The same

6612
08:34:23.327 --> 08:34:28.333
goes for end-to-end tests, which often mimic the experience a user would get when

6613
08:34:28.333 --> 08:34:33.338
interacting through a user interface. They're even more costly as they run slowly

6614
08:34:33.338 --> 08:34:37.911
and are often difficult to write robustly, but are close to the end-user's

6615
08:34:37.911 --> 08:34:42.855
experience. Okay, let's see. Welcome back. In the previous lesson, we've covered

