09:57:41.414 --> 09:57:46.186
organizations. The attribute function gives some extra information to that

7575
09:57:46.186 --> 09:57:50.893
affiliation. For instance, somebody might act as a chairman for a certain

7576
09:57:50.893 --> 09:57:55.665
third-party organization. So the best idea at the moment is to store these

7577
09:57:55.665 --> 09:58:00.630
affiliations in their own table. It connects professors with their respective

7578
09:58:00.630 --> 09:58:03.790
organizations where they have a certain function.

7579
09:58:03.790 --> 09:58:09.146
The first thing you need to do now is to create four empty tables for professors

7580
09:58:09.146 --> 09:58:14.636
universities organizations and affiliations this is quite easy with SQL you'll use

7581
09:58:14.636 --> 09:58:20.193
the create table command for that at the minimum this command requires a table name

7582
09:58:20.193 --> 09:58:25.750
and one or more columns with the respective data types for example you could create

7583
09:58:25.750 --> 09:58:28.830
a weather table with three aptly named columns

7584
09:58:28.830 --> 09:58:33.892
After each column name, you must specify the data type. There are many different

7585
09:58:33.892 --> 09:58:39.144
types, and you will discover some in the remainder of this course. For example, you

7586
09:58:39.144 --> 09:58:43.763
could specify a text column, a numeric column, and a column that requires

7587
09:58:43.763 --> 09:58:48.952
fixed-length character strings with five characters each. These data types will be

7588
09:58:48.952 --> 09:58:54.014
explained in more detail in the next chapter. For now, you will first create the

7589
09:58:54.014 --> 09:58:58.190
four tables and then migrate data from the original table to them.

7590
09:58:58.190 --> 09:59:03.442
Well done so far. You now have a database consisting of five different tables. Now

7591
09:59:03.442 --> 09:59:08.437
it's time to migrate the data. Here's the current entity relationship diagram,

7592
09:59:08.437 --> 09:59:13.816
showing the five tables. At this moment, only the university professor's table holds

7593
09:59:13.816 --> 09:59:18.748
data. The other four, shown in red, are still empty. In the remainder of this

7594
09:59:18.748 --> 09:59:23.935
chapter, you'll migrate data from the green part of this diagram to the red part,

7595
09:59:23.935 --> 09:59:27.970
moving the respective entity types to their appropriate tables.

7596
09:59:27.970 --> 09:59:34.846
In the end, you'll be able to delete the University Professors table. One advantage

7597
09:59:34.846 --> 09:59:41.805
of splitting up University Professors into several tables is the reduced redundancy.

7598
09:59:41.805 --> 09:59:48.515
As of now, University Professors holds 1377 entries. However, there are only 1287

7599
09:59:48.515 --> 09:59:52.160
distinct organizations, as this query shows.

7600
09:59:52.160 --> 09:59:57.210
Therefore, you only need to store 1,287 distinct organizations in the new

7601
09:59:57.210 --> 10:00:02.953
organizations table. In order to copy data from an existing table to a new one, you

7602
10:00:02.953 --> 10:00:08.695
can use the insert into select distinct pattern. After insert into, you specify the

7603
10:00:08.695 --> 10:00:14.299
name of the target table, organizations in this case. Then you select the columns

7604
10:00:14.299 --> 10:00:19.764
that should be copied over from the source table, university professors in this

7605
10:00:19.764 --> 10:00:20.110
case.

7606
10:00:20.110 --> 10:00:25.992
You use the distinct keyword to only copy over distinct organizations. As the output

7607
10:00:25.992 --> 10:00:31.595
shows, only 1,287 records are inserted into the organizations table. If you just

7608
10:00:31.595 --> 10:00:37.197
used insert into select without the distinct keyword, duplicate records would be

7609
10:00:37.197 --> 10:00:42.800
copied over as well. In the following exercises, you'll migrate your data to the

7610
10:00:42.800 --> 10:00:43.920
four new tables.

7611
10:00:43.920 --> 10:00:48.998
By the way, this is the normal use case for insert into, where you insert values

7612
10:00:48.998 --> 10:00:54.266
manually. Insert into is followed by the table name and an optional list of columns

7613
10:00:54.266 --> 10:00:59.343
which should be filled with data. Then follows the values keyword and the actual

7614
10:00:59.343 --> 10:01:04.421
values you want to insert. Before you start migrating the table, you need to fix

7615
10:01:04.421 --> 10:01:09.054
some stuff. In the last lesson, I created the affiliations table for you.

7616
10:01:09.054 --> 10:01:13.180
Unfortunately, I made a mistake in this process. Can you spot it?

7617
10:01:13.180 --> 10:01:18.461
The way the organization column is spelled is not consistent with the American style

7618
10:01:18.461 --> 10:01:23.741
spelling of this table, using an S instead of a set. In the first exercise after the

7619
10:01:23.741 --> 10:01:28.896
video, you'll correct this with the known alter table syntax. You do this with the

7620
10:01:28.896 --> 10:01:33.799
rename column command by specifying the old column name first and then the new

7621
10:01:33.799 --> 10:01:39.017
column name. That is rename column old name to new name. Also, the university short

7622
10:01:39.017 --> 10:01:43.040
name column is not even needed here. So I want you to delete it.

7623
10:01:43.040 --> 10:01:47.871
The syntax for this is again very simple. You use a drop column command followed by

7624
10:01:47.871 --> 10:01:52.469
the name of the column. Dropping columns is straightforward when the tables are

7625
10:01:52.469 --> 10:01:57.242
still empty, so it's not too late to fix this error. But why is it an error in the

7626
10:01:57.242 --> 10:01:57.940
first place?

7627
10:01:57.940 --> 10:02:03.826
Well, I queried the university professors table and saw that there are 551 unique

7628
10:02:03.826 --> 10:02:09.858
combinations of first names, last names and associated universities. I then queried

7629
10:02:09.858 --> 10:02:15.672
the table again and only looked for unique combinations of first and last names.

7630
10:02:15.672 --> 10:02:21.776
Turns out, this is also 551 records. This means that the columns first name and last

7631
10:02:21.776 --> 10:02:24.320
name uniquely identify a professor.

7632
10:02:24.320 --> 10:02:29.522
So the university short name column is not needed in order to reference a professor

7633
10:02:29.522 --> 10:02:34.723
in the affiliations table. You can remove it and this will reduce the redundancy in

7634
10:02:34.723 --> 10:02:39.925
your database again. In other words the columns first name, last name, function and

7635
10:02:39.925 --> 10:02:44.876
organization are enough to store the affiliation a professor has with a certain

7636
10:02:44.876 --> 10:02:49.890
organization. Time to prepare the database for data migration. After this you'll

7637
10:02:49.890 --> 10:02:54.960
So far you've learned how to set up a simple database that consists of multiple

7638
10:02:54.960 --> 10:03:00.286
tables. Apart from storing different entity types, such as professors, in different

7639
10:03:00.286 --> 10:03:05.484
tables, you haven't made much use of database features. In the end, the idea of a

7640
10:03:05.484 --> 10:03:10.617
database is to push data into a certain structure, a predefined model, where you

7641
10:03:10.617 --> 10:03:13.890
enforce data types, relationships, and other rules.

7642
10:03:13.890 --> 10:03:19.077
Generally, these rules are called integrity constraints, although different names

7643
10:03:19.077 --> 10:03:24.073
exist. Integrity constraints can roughly be divided into three types. The most

7644
10:03:24.073 --> 10:03:29.452
simple ones are probably the so-called attribute constraints. For example, a certain

7645
10:03:29.452 --> 10:03:34.640
attribute represented through a database column could have the integer data type,

7646
10:03:34.640 --> 10:03:39.827
allowing only for integers to be stored in this column. They'll be the subject of

7647
10:03:39.827 --> 10:03:40.660
this chapter.

7648
10:03:40.660 --> 10:03:45.918
Secondly, there are so-called key constraints. Primary keys, for example, uniquely

7649
10:03:45.918 --> 10:03:51.112
identify each record or row of a database table. They'll be discussed in the next

7650
10:03:51.112 --> 10:03:56.306
chapter. Lastly, there are referential integrity constraints. In short, they glue

7651
10:03:56.306 --> 10:04:01.564
different database tables together. You'll learn about them in the last chapter of

7652
10:04:01.564 --> 10:04:05.540
this course. So why should you know about constraints? Well...

7653
10:04:05.540 --> 10:04:10.939
They press the data into a certain form. With good constraints in place, people who

7654
10:04:10.939 --> 10:04:16.208
type in birthdates, for example, have to enter them in always the same form. Data

7655
10:04:16.208 --> 10:04:21.282
entered by humans is often very tedious to preprocess. So constraints give you

7656
10:04:21.282 --> 10:04:26.551
consistency, meaning that the row in a certain table has exactly the same form as

7657
10:04:26.551 --> 10:04:31.755
the next row, and so forth. All in all, they help to solve a lot of data quality

7658
10:04:31.755 --> 10:04:32.210
issues.

7659
10:04:32.210 --> 10:04:37.371
While enforcing constraints on human entered data is difficult and tedious, database

7660
10:04:37.371 --> 10:04:42.408
management systems can be a great help. In the next chapters and exercises, you'll

7661
10:04:42.408 --> 10:04:47.077
explore how. You'll start with attribute constraints in this chapter. In its

7662
10:04:47.077 --> 10:04:52.115
simplest form, attribute constraints are data types that can be specified for each

7663
10:04:52.115 --> 10:04:56.784
column of a table. Here you see the beginning of a list of all data types in

7664
10:04:56.784 --> 10:04:57.460
PostgreSQL.

7665
10:04:57.460 --> 10:05:02.511
There are basic data types for numbers, such as BigInt, or strings of characters,

7666
10:05:02.511 --> 10:05:07.438
such as character varying. There are also more high-level data types like CIDR,

7667
10:05:07.438 --> 10:05:12.302
which can be used for IP addresses. Implementing such a type on a column would

7668
10:05:12.302 --> 10:05:17.478
disallow anything that doesn't fit the structure of an IP. Data types also restrict

7669
10:05:17.478 --> 10:05:20.160
possible SQL operations on the stored data.

7670
10:05:20.160 --> 10:05:25.782
For example, it is impossible to calculate the product from an integer and the text

7671
10:05:25.782 --> 10:05:28.220
column as shown here in the example.

7672
10:05:28.220 --> 10:05:33.512
The text column WinSpeed may store numbers, but PostgreSQL doesn't know how to use

7673
10:05:33.512 --> 10:05:38.932
text in a calculation. The solution for this is type casts, that is, on-the-fly type

7674
10:05:38.932 --> 10:05:44.030
conversion. In this case, you can use the cast function, followed by the column

7675
10:05:44.030 --> 10:05:49.387
name, the ask keyword, and the desired data type. And PostgreSQL will turn WinSpeed

7676
10:05:49.387 --> 10:05:54.420
into an integer right before the calculation. Okay, let's look into this first

7677
10:05:54.420 --> 10:05:59.248
Working with data types is straightforward in a database management system like

7678
10:05:59.248 --> 10:06:04.260
PostgreSQL. As said before, data types are attribute constraints and are therefore

7679
10:06:04.260 --> 10:06:09.027
implemented for single columns of a table. They define the so-called domain of

7680
10:06:09.027 --> 10:06:13.672
values in a column. That means what form these values can take and what not.

7681
10:06:13.672 --> 10:06:18.500
Therefore, they also define what operations are possible with the values in the

7682
10:06:18.500 --> 10:06:21.250
column, as you saw in the previous exercises.

7683
10:06:21.250 --> 10:06:26.567
Of course through this consistent storage is enforced so a street number will always

7684
10:06:26.567 --> 10:06:31.504
be an actual number and a postal code will always have no more than six digits

7685
10:06:31.504 --> 10:06:36.820
according to your conventions this greatly helps with data quality here are the most

7686
10:06:36.820 --> 10:06:42.074
common types in postgres SQL note that these types are specific to postgres SQL but

7687
10:06:42.074 --> 10:06:47.327
appear in many other database management systems as well and they mostly conform to

7688
10:06:47.327 --> 10:06:48.340
the SQL standard

7689
10:06:48.340 --> 10:06:53.376
The text type allows character strings of any length, while the wachar and char

7690
10:06:53.376 --> 10:06:58.731
types specify a maximum number of characters, or a character string of fixed length,

7691
10:06:58.731 --> 10:07:03.894
respectively. You'll use these two for your database. The boolean type allows for

7692
10:07:03.894 --> 10:07:08.930
two boolean values, for example true and false, or one or zero, and for a third

7693
10:07:08.930 --> 10:07:14.158
unknown value, expressed through null. Then there are various formats for date and

7694
10:07:14.158 --> 10:07:17.090
time calculation, also with time zone support.

7695
10:07:17.090 --> 10:07:22.139
Numeric is a general type for any sort of numbers with arbitrary precision while

7696
10:07:22.139 --> 10:07:27.441
integer allows only whole numbers in a certain range if that range is not enough for

7697
10:07:27.441 --> 10:07:32.680
your numbers there's also big int for larger numbers here's an example of how types

7698
10:07:32.680 --> 10:07:37.982
are specified upon table creation let's say the social security number ssn should be

7699
10:07:37.982 --> 10:07:41.390
stored as an integer as it only contains whole numbers

7700
10:07:41.390 --> 10:07:46.675
The name may be a string with a maximum of 64 characters, which might or might not

7701
10:07:46.675 --> 10:07:52.025
be enough. The date of birth, DOB, is naturally stored as a date, while the average

7702
10:07:52.025 --> 10:07:56.988
grade is a numeric value with a precision of 3 and a scale of 2, meaning that

7703
10:07:56.988 --> 10:08:01.886
numbers with a total of 3 digits and 2 digits after the fractional point are

7704
10:08:01.886 --> 10:08:07.236
allowed. Lastly, the information whether the tuition of the student was paid is, of

7705
10:08:07.236 --> 10:08:10.910
course, a boolean one, as it can be either true or false.

7706
10:08:10.910 --> 10:08:16.005
Altering types after table creation is also straightforward. Just use the shown

7707
10:08:16.005 --> 10:08:20.907
alter table alter column statement. In this case, the maximum name length is

7708
10:08:20.907 --> 10:08:26.260
extended to 128 characters. Sometimes it may be necessary to truncate column values

7709
10:08:26.260 --> 10:08:31.678
or transform them in any other way, so they fit with the new data type. Then you can

7710
10:08:31.678 --> 10:08:36.838
use the using keyword and specify a transformation that should happen before the

7711
10:08:36.838 --> 10:08:37.870
type is altered.

7712
10:08:37.870 --> 10:08:42.682
Let's say you'd want to turn the average grade column into an integer type. Normally

7713
10:08:42.682 --> 10:08:47.436
PostgreSQL would just keep the part of the number before the fractional point. With

7714
10:08:47.436 --> 10:08:51.961
using, you can tell it to round the number to the nearest integer, for example.

7715
10:08:51.961 --> 10:08:56.715
Let's apply this to your database and add the proper types to your database. In the

7716
10:08:56.715 --> 10:09:01.527
last part of this chapter, you'll get to know two special attribute constraints, the

7717
10:09:01.527 --> 10:09:03.360
not null and unique constraints.

7718
10:09:03.360 --> 10:09:08.466
As the name already says, the not null constraint disallows any null values on a

7719
10:09:08.466 --> 10:09:13.701
given column. This must hold true for the existing state of the database, but also

7720
10:09:13.701 --> 10:09:18.807
for any future state. Therefore, you can only specify a not null constraint on a

7721
10:09:18.807 --> 10:09:23.913
column that doesn't hold any null values yet. And it won't be possible to insert

7722
10:09:23.913 --> 10:09:28.765
null values in the future. Before I go on explaining how to specify not null

7723
10:09:28.765 --> 10:09:32.020
constraints, I want you to think about null values.

7724
10:09:32.020 --> 10:09:36.658
What do they actually mean to you? There's no clear definition. Null can mean a

7725
10:09:36.658 --> 10:09:41.472
couple of things. For example, that the value is unknown or does not exist at all.

7726
10:09:41.472 --> 10:09:46.404
It can also be possible that the value does not apply to the column. Let's look into

7727
10:09:46.404 --> 10:09:51.041
an example. Let's say we define a table students. The first two columns for the

7728
10:09:51.041 --> 10:09:55.738
social security number and the last name cannot be null, which makes sense. This

7729
10:09:55.738 --> 10:09:58.380
should be known and applied to every student.

7730
10:09:58.380 --> 10:10:03.871
The home phone and office phone columns, though, should allow for null values, which

7731
10:10:03.871 --> 10:10:09.296
is the default, by the way. Why? First of all, these numbers can be unknown for any

7732
10:10:09.296 --> 10:10:14.459
reason or simply not exist because a student might not have a phone. Also, some

7733
10:10:14.459 --> 10:10:19.885
values just don't apply. Some students might not have an office, so they don't have

7734
10:10:19.885 --> 10:10:25.114
an office phone and so forth. So, one important takeaway is that two null values

7735
10:10:25.114 --> 10:10:27.140
must not have the same meaning.

7736
10:10:27.140 --> 10:10:32.493
This also means that comparing null with null always results in a false value.

7737
10:10:32.493 --> 10:10:38.257
You've just seen how to add a not null constraint to certain columns when creating a

7738
10:10:38.257 --> 10:10:43.678
table. Just add not null after the respective columns. But you can also add and

7739
10:10:43.678 --> 10:10:48.756
remove not null constraints to and from existing tables. To add a not null

7740
10:10:48.756 --> 10:10:54.520
constraint to an existing table, you can use the alter column set not null syntax as

7741
10:10:54.520 --> 10:10:56.030
shown here. Similarly,

7742
10:10:56.030 --> 10:11:01.711
To remove a not null constraint, you can use alter column drop not null. The unique

7743
10:11:01.711 --> 10:11:07.391
constraint on a column makes sure there are no duplicates in a column. So any given

7744
10:11:07.391 --> 10:11:13.140
value in a column can only exist once. This, for example, makes sense for university

7745
10:11:13.140 --> 10:11:18.889
short names, as storing universities more than once leads to unnecessary redundancy.

7746
10:11:18.889 --> 10:11:24.159
However, it doesn't make sense for university cities, as two universities can

7747
10:11:24.159 --> 10:11:25.870
coexist in the same city.

7748
10:11:25.870 --> 10:11:30.767
Just as with the not null constraint you can only add a unique constraint if the

7749
10:11:30.767 --> 10:11:35.848
column doesn't hold any duplicates before you apply it here's how to create columns

7750
10:11:35.848 --> 10:11:40.622
with unique constraints just add the unique keyword after the respective table

7751
10:11:40.622 --> 10:11:44.540
column you can also add a unique constraint to an existing table

7752
10:11:44.540 --> 10:11:49.948
for that you have to use the add constraint syntax this is different from adding a

7753
10:11:49.948 --> 10:11:55.422
not null constraint however it's a pattern that frequently occurs you'll see plenty

7754
10:11:55.422 --> 10:12:00.961
of other examples of add constraint in the remainder of this course okay time now to

7755
10:12:00.961 --> 10:12:06.237
apply the not null and unique constraints to some of the columns of your current

7756
10:12:06.237 --> 10:12:10.590
university affiliations welcome back let's discuss key constraints

7757
10:12:10.590 --> 10:12:15.052
They are a very important concept in database systems, so we'll spend a whole

7758
10:12:15.052 --> 10:12:19.919
chapter on them. Let's have a look at your current database model first. In the last

7759
10:12:19.919 --> 10:12:24.554
chapter, you specified attribute constraints, first and foremost data types. You

7760
10:12:24.554 --> 10:12:29.422
also set not null and unique constraints on certain attributes. This didn't actually

7761
10:12:29.422 --> 10:12:34.115
change the structure of the model, so it still looks the same. By the end of this

7762
10:12:34.115 --> 10:12:37.070
chapter, the database will look slightly different.

7763
10:12:37.070 --> 10:12:42.271
You'll add so-called primary keys to the three different tables you'll name them id

7764
10:12:42.271 --> 10:12:47.347
in the entity relationship diagram keys are denoted by underlined attribute names

7765
10:12:47.347 --> 10:12:52.360
notice that you'll add a whole new attribute to the professor's table and you'll

7766
10:12:52.360 --> 10:12:57.436
modify existing columns of the organizations and universities tables before we go

7767
10:12:57.436 --> 10:13:02.261
into the nitty-gritty of what a primary key actually is let's look at keys in

7768
10:13:02.261 --> 10:13:02.700
general

7769
10:13:02.700 --> 10:13:08.295
Typically a database table has an attribute or a combination of multiple attributes

7770
10:13:08.295 --> 10:13:13.755
whose values are unique across the whole table. Such attributes identify a record

7771
10:13:13.755 --> 10:13:19.417
uniquely. Normally a table as a whole only contains unique records, meaning that the

7772
10:13:19.417 --> 10:13:22.720
combination of all attributes is a key in itself.

7773
10:13:22.720 --> 10:13:28.178
However, it's not called a key, but a super key. If attributes from that combination

7774
10:13:28.178 --> 10:13:33.506
can be removed and the attributes still uniquely identify records. If all possible

7775
10:13:33.506 --> 10:13:38.965
attributes have been removed, but the records are still uniquely identifiable by the

7776
10:13:38.965 --> 10:13:44.358
remaining attributes, we speak of a minimal super key. This is the actual key. So a

7777
10:13:44.358 --> 10:13:49.816
key is always minimal. Let's look at an example. Here's an example that I found in a

7778
10:13:49.816 --> 10:13:52.350
textbook on database system. Obviously,

7779
10:13:52.350 --> 10:13:57.502
the table shows six different cars, so the combination of all attributes is a

7780
10:13:57.502 --> 10:14:02.854
superkey. If we remove the year attribute from the superkey, the six records are

7781
10:14:02.854 --> 10:14:08.006
still unique, so it's still a superkey. Actually, there are a lot of possible

7782
10:14:08.006 --> 10:14:13.626
superkeys in this example. However, there are only four minimal superkeys, and these

7783
10:14:13.626 --> 10:14:19.245
are license number, serial number, and model, as well as the combination of make and

7784
10:14:19.245 --> 10:14:19.580
year.

7785
10:14:19.580 --> 10:14:25.406
Remember that superkeys are minimal if no attributes can be removed without losing

7786
10:14:25.406 --> 10:14:31.089
the uniqueness property. This is trivial for K1 to K3, as they only consist of a

