10090
13:13:34.117 --> 13:13:38.880
formats are limited depending on the language used. In addition to the issues with

10091
13:13:38.880 --> 13:13:43.470
CSV files in general, Spark has some specific problems processing CSV data. CSV

10092
13:13:43.470 --> 13:13:48.350
files are quite slow to import and parse. The files cannot be shared between workers

10093
13:13:48.350 --> 13:13:49.860
during the import process.

10094
13:13:49.860 --> 13:13:54.162
If no schema is defined, all data must be read before a schema can be inferred.

10095
13:13:54.162 --> 13:13:58.464
Spark has a feature known as predicate pushdown. Basically, this is the idea of

10096
13:13:58.464 --> 13:14:03.038
ordering tasks to do the least amount of work. Filtering data prior to processing is

10097
13:14:03.038 --> 13:14:07.612
one of the primary optimizations of predicate pushdown. This drastically reduces the

10098
13:14:07.612 --> 13:14:12.077
amount of information that must be processed in large datasets. Unfortunately, you

10099
13:14:12.077 --> 13:14:14.800
cannot filter the CSV data via predicate pushdown.

10100
13:14:14.800 --> 13:14:19.617
Finally, Spark processes are often multi-step and may utilize an intermediate file

10101
13:14:19.617 --> 13:14:23.905
representation. These representations allow data to be used later without

10102
13:14:23.905 --> 13:14:28.605
regenerating the data from source. Using CSV would instead require a significant

10103
13:14:28.605 --> 13:14:32.952
amount of extra work defining schemas, encoding formats, etc. Parquet is a

10104
13:14:32.952 --> 13:14:37.769
compressed columnar data format developed for use in any Hadoop-based system. This

10105
13:14:37.769 --> 13:14:39.120
includes Spark, Hadoop,

10106
13:14:39.120 --> 13:14:43.570
Apache Impala, and so forth. The Parquet format is structured with data accessible

10107
13:14:43.570 --> 13:14:47.966
in chunks, allowing efficient read-write operations without processing the entire

10108
13:14:47.966 --> 13:14:52.253
file. This structured format supports Spark's predicate pushdown functionality,

10109
13:14:52.253 --> 13:14:56.757
providing significant performance improvement. Finally, Parquet files automatically

10110
13:14:56.757 --> 13:15:00.664
include schema information and handle data encoding. This is perfect for

10111
13:15:00.664 --> 13:15:05.060
intermediary or on-disk representation of processed data. Note that Parquet files

10112
13:15:05.060 --> 13:15:08.750
are a binary file format and can only be used with the proper tools.

10113
13:15:08.750 --> 13:15:13.475
This is in contrast to CSV files, which can be edited with any text editor.

10114
13:15:13.475 --> 13:15:18.704
Interacting with Parquet files is very straightforward. To read a Parquet file into

10115
13:15:18.704 --> 13:15:23.869
a dataframe, you have two options. The first is using the Spark.read.format method

10116
13:15:23.869 --> 13:15:28.846
we've seen previously. The dataframe, df equals Spark.read.format, Parquet, dot

10117
13:15:28.846 --> 13:15:33.571
load, filename, dot Parquet. The second option is the shortcut version. The

10118
13:15:33.571 --> 13:15:37.540
dataframe, df equals Spark.read.Parquet, filename, dot Parquet.

10119
13:15:37.540 --> 13:15:42.832
Typically, the shortcut version is the easiest to use, but you can use them

10120
13:15:42.832 --> 13:15:47.277
interchangeably. Writing Parquet files is similar, using either

10121
13:15:47.277 --> 13:15:53.204
df.write.format.Parquet.save.filename.Parquet, or df.write.Parquet.filename.Parquet.

10122
13:15:53.204 --> 13:15:58.990
The long-form versions of each permit extra option flags, such as when overwriting

10123
13:15:58.990 --> 13:16:04.000
an existing Parquet file. Parquet files have various uses within Spark.

10124
13:16:04.000 --> 13:16:08.759
We've discussed using them as an intermediate data format, but they are also perfect

10125
13:16:08.759 --> 13:16:13.291
for performing SQL operations. To perform a SQL query against a Parquet file, we

10126
13:16:13.291 --> 13:16:17.880
first need to create a data frame via the Spark.read.Parquet method. Once we have

10127
13:16:17.880 --> 13:16:22.639
the data frame, we can use the create or replace temp view method to add an alias of

10128
13:16:22.639 --> 13:16:27.285
the Parquet data as a SQL table. Finally, we run our query using normal SQL syntax

10129
13:16:27.285 --> 13:16:31.590
and the Spark.SQL method. In this case, we're looking for all flights with a

10130
13:16:31.590 --> 13:16:33.120
duration under 100 minutes.

10131
13:16:33.120 --> 13:16:36.889
Because we're using Parquet as the backing store, we get all the performance

10132
13:16:36.889 --> 13:16:41.055
benefits we've discussed previously, primarily defined schemas and the available use

10133
13:16:41.055 --> 13:16:45.122
of predicate pushdown. You've seen a bit about what a Parquet file is and why we'd

10134
13:16:45.122 --> 13:16:48.941
want to use them. Now let's practice this working. Welcome back. In the first

10135
13:16:48.941 --> 13:16:52.661
chapter, we've spent some time discussing the basics of Spark data and file

10136
13:16:52.661 --> 13:16:56.827
handling. Let's now take a look at how to use Spark column operations to clean data.

10137
13:16:56.827 --> 13:17:00.944
Before we discuss manipulating data frames in depth, let's talk about some of their

10138
13:17:00.944 --> 13:17:01.390
features.

10139
13:17:01.390 --> 13:17:05.492
Data frames are made up of rows and columns and are generally analogous to a

10140
13:17:05.492 --> 13:17:10.025
database table. Data frames are immutable. Any change to the structure or content of

10141
13:17:10.025 --> 13:17:14.235
the data creates a new data frame. Data frames are modified through the use of

10142
13:17:14.235 --> 13:17:18.769
transformations. An example is the filter command to only return rows where the name

10143
13:17:18.769 --> 13:17:23.194
starts with the letter M. Another operation is select, in this case returning only

10144
13:17:23.194 --> 13:17:27.674
the name and position fields. There are many different transformations for use on a

10145
13:17:27.674 --> 13:17:30.750
data frame. They vary depending on what you'd like to do.

10146
13:17:30.750 --> 13:17:35.773
Some common transformations include the filter clause, which includes only rows that

10147
13:17:35.773 --> 13:17:40.496
satisfy the requirement defined in the argument. This is analogous to the where

10148
13:17:40.496 --> 13:17:45.101
clause in SQL. Spark includes a where alias you can use in place of filter if

10149
13:17:45.101 --> 13:17:49.824
desired. This call returns only rows where the vote occurred after January 1st,

10150
13:17:49.824 --> 13:17:54.309
2019. Another common option is the select method, which returns the columns

10151
13:17:54.309 --> 13:17:59.152
requested from the data frame. The with column method creates a new column in the

10152
13:17:59.152 --> 13:17:59.810
data frame.

10153
13:17:59.810 --> 13:18:04.469
The first argument is the name of the column and the second is the command to create

10154
13:18:04.469 --> 13:18:09.073
it. In this case, we create a column called year with just the year information. We

10155
13:18:09.073 --> 13:18:13.566
can also use the drop method to remove a column from a data frame. Among the most

10156
13:18:13.566 --> 13:18:18.059
common operations used when cleaning a data frame, filtering lets us use only the

10157
13:18:18.059 --> 13:18:22.663
data matching our desired result. We can use filter for many tasks such as removing

10158
13:18:22.663 --> 13:18:27.100
null values, removing odd entries, anything that doesn't fit our desired format,

10159
13:18:27.100 --> 13:18:31.632
We can also split a data frame containing combined data, such as a syslog file. As

10160
13:18:31.632 --> 13:18:35.888
mentioned previously, use the filter method to return only rows that meet the

10161
13:18:35.888 --> 13:18:40.365
specified criteria. The contains function takes a string argument that the column

10162
13:18:40.365 --> 13:18:44.842
must have to return true. You can negate these results using the tilde character.

10163
13:18:44.842 --> 13:18:48.932
Some of the most common operations used in data cleaning are modifying and

10164
13:18:48.932 --> 13:18:52.801
converting strings. You will typically apply these to each column as a

10165
13:18:52.801 --> 13:18:53.630
transformation.

10166
13:18:53.630 --> 13:18:58.649
Many of these functions are in the Spark.SQL.functions library. For brevity, we'll

10167
13:18:58.649 --> 13:19:03.546
import it as the alias f. We use the with column function to create a new column

10168
13:19:03.546 --> 13:19:08.627
called upper using PySpark.SQL.functions.upper on the name column. The upper column

10169
13:19:08.627 --> 13:19:13.524
will contain uppercase versions of all names. We can create intermediary columns

10170
13:19:13.524 --> 13:19:18.360
that are only for processing. This is useful to clarify complex transformations

10171
13:19:18.360 --> 13:19:19.890
requiring multiple steps.

10172
13:19:19.890 --> 13:19:24.217
In this instance, we call the split function with the name of the column and the

10173
13:19:24.217 --> 13:19:28.761
space character to split on. This returns a list of words in a column called splits.

10174
13:19:28.761 --> 13:19:32.980
A very common operation is converting string data to a different type, such as

10175
13:19:32.980 --> 13:19:37.361
converting a string column to an integer. We use the cast function to perform the

10176
13:19:37.361 --> 13:19:41.742
conversion to an integer type. While performing data cleaning with Spark, you may

10177
13:19:41.742 --> 13:19:46.069
need to interact with array type columns. These are analogous to lists in normal

10178
13:19:46.069 --> 13:19:48.990
Python environments. One function we will use is size.

10179
13:19:48.990 --> 13:19:53.283
Which returns the number of items present in the specified array type argument.

10180
13:19:53.283 --> 13:19:57.412
Another commonly used function for array types is getItem. It takes an index

10181
13:19:57.412 --> 13:20:01.813
argument and returns the item present at that index in the list column. Spark has

10182
13:20:01.813 --> 13:20:06.052
many more transformations and utility functions available. When using Spark in

10183
13:20:06.052 --> 13:20:10.453
production, make sure to reference the documentation for available options. We've

10184
13:20:10.453 --> 13:20:14.691
discussed some of the common operations used on Spark DataFrame columns. Let's

10185
13:20:14.691 --> 13:20:15.180
practice.

10186
13:20:15.180 --> 13:20:19.272
We've looked at some of the power available when using Spark's functions to filter

10187
13:20:19.272 --> 13:20:23.364
and modify our data frames. Let's spend some time with some more advanced options.

10188
13:20:23.364 --> 13:20:27.455
The data frame transformations we've covered thus far are blanket transformations,

10189
13:20:27.455 --> 13:20:31.597
meaning they're applied regardless of the data. Often, you'll want to conditionally

10190
13:20:31.597 --> 13:20:35.789
change some aspect of the contents. Spark provides some built-in conditional clauses

10191
13:20:35.789 --> 13:20:39.531
which act similar to an if-then-else statement in a traditional programming

10192
13:20:39.531 --> 13:20:40.130
environment.

10193
13:20:40.130 --> 13:20:44.848
While it is possible to perform a traditional if-then-else style statement in Spark,

10194
13:20:44.848 --> 13:20:49.510
it can lead to serious performance degradation as each row of a data frame would be

10195
13:20:49.510 --> 13:20:54.172
evaluated independently. Using the optimized built-in conditionals alleviates this.

10196
13:20:54.172 --> 13:20:58.328
There are two components to the conditional clauses, when and the optional

10197
13:20:58.328 --> 13:21:02.934
otherwise. Let's look at them in more depth. The when clause is a method available

10198
13:21:02.934 --> 13:21:07.090
from the PySpark.SQL.Functions library that is looking for two components.

10199
13:21:07.090 --> 13:21:12.205
The if condition, and what to do if it evaluates the true. This is best seen from an

10200
13:21:12.205 --> 13:21:17.320
example. Consider a data frame with the name and age columns. We can actually add an

10201
13:21:17.320 --> 13:21:22.009
extra argument to our select method when using the when clause. We select the

10202
13:21:22.009 --> 13:21:26.393
df.name and df.age as usual. For the third argument, we'll define a when

10203
13:21:26.393 --> 13:21:31.143
conditional. If the age column is 18 or up, we'll add the string adult. If the

10204
13:21:31.143 --> 13:21:33.700
clause doesn't match, nothing is returned.

10205
13:21:33.700 --> 13:21:38.289
Note that our return dataframe contains an unnamed column we didn't define using

10206
13:21:38.289 --> 13:21:42.648
with column. The select function can create columns dynamically based on the

10207
13:21:42.648 --> 13:21:47.295
arguments provided. Let's look at some more examples. You can chain multiple when

10208
13:21:47.295 --> 13:21:52.113
statements together, similar to an if-else-if structure. In this case, we define two

10209
13:21:52.113 --> 13:21:56.702
when clauses and return adult or minor based on the age column. You can chain as

10210
13:21:56.702 --> 13:22:01.520
many when clauses together as required. In addition to when is the otherwise clause.

10211
13:22:01.520 --> 13:22:06.024
Otherwise is analogous to the else statement. It takes a single argument, which is

10212
13:22:06.024 --> 13:22:10.583
what to return, in case the when clause or clauses do not evaluate as true. In this

10213
13:22:10.583 --> 13:22:15.088
example, we return adult when the age column is 18 or higher. Otherwise, we return

10214
13:22:15.088 --> 13:22:19.647
minor. The resulting data frame is the same, but the method is different. While you

10215
13:22:19.647 --> 13:22:23.931
can have multiple when statements chained together, you can only have a single

10216
13:22:23.931 --> 13:22:28.546
otherwise per when chain. Let's try a couple of examples of using when and otherwise

10217
13:22:28.546 --> 13:22:29.040
to model.

10218
13:22:29.040 --> 13:22:33.532
We've looked at the built-in functions in Spark and have had great results using

10219
13:22:33.532 --> 13:22:38.248
these, but let's consider what you would do if you needed to apply some custom logic

10220
13:22:38.248 --> 13:22:42.964
to your data cleaning processes. A user-defined function, or UDF, is a Python method

10221
13:22:42.964 --> 13:22:47.680
that the user writes to perform a specific bit of logic. Once written, the method is

10222
13:22:47.680 --> 13:22:52.341
called via the PySpark.SQL.functions.udf method. The result is stored as a variable

10223
13:22:52.341 --> 13:22:56.720
and can be called as a normal Spark function. Let's look at a couple examples.

10224
13:22:56.720 --> 13:23:00.970
Here is a fairly trivial example to illustrate how a UDF is defined. First, we

10225
13:23:00.970 --> 13:23:05.329
define a Python function. We'll call our function reverseString with an argument

10226
13:23:05.329 --> 13:23:09.906
called Meister. We'll use some Python shorthand to reverse the string and return it.

10227
13:23:09.906 --> 13:23:14.319
Don't worry about understanding how the return statement works, only that it will

10228
13:23:14.319 --> 13:23:18.896
reverse the lettering of whatever is fed into it. For example, help becomes P-L-E-H.

10229
13:23:18.896 --> 13:23:23.200
The next step is to wrap the function and store it in a variable for later use.

10230
13:23:23.200 --> 13:23:28.435
We'll use the PySpark.SQL.functions.udf method. It takes two arguments, the name of

10231
13:23:28.435 --> 13:23:33.732
the method you just defined, and the Spark data type it will return. This can be any

10232
13:23:33.732 --> 13:23:38.967
of the options in PySpark.SQL.types, and can even be a more complex type, including

10233
13:23:38.967 --> 13:23:43.949
a fully defined schema object. Most often, you'll return either a simple object

10234
13:23:43.949 --> 13:23:49.184
type, or perhaps an array type. We'll call UDF with our new method name and use the

10235
13:23:49.184 --> 13:23:52.400
string type, then store this as UDF reverse string.

10236
13:23:52.400 --> 13:23:56.789
Finally, we use our new UDF to add a column to the userDF dataframe within the

10237
13:23:56.789 --> 13:24:01.460
withColumn method. Note that we pass the column we're interested in as the argument

10238
13:24:01.460 --> 13:24:05.905
to UDF reverseString. The UDF function is called for each row of the dataframe.

10239
13:24:05.905 --> 13:24:10.464
Under the hood, the UDF function takes the value stored for the specified column,

10240
13:24:10.464 --> 13:24:15.191
per row, and passes it to the Python method. The result is fed back to the resulting

10241
13:24:15.191 --> 13:24:19.524
dataframe. Another quick example is using a function that does not require an

10242
13:24:19.524 --> 13:24:20.030
argument.

10243
13:24:20.030 --> 13:24:24.613
We're defining our sorting cap function to return one of the letters g, h, r, or s

10244
13:24:24.613 --> 13:24:29.309
at random. We still create our udfwrap function and define the return type as string

10245
13:24:29.309 --> 13:24:33.892
type. The primary difference is calling the function, this time without passing in

10246
13:24:33.892 --> 13:24:38.364
an argument as it is not required. As always, the best way to learn is practice.

10247
13:24:38.364 --> 13:24:43.059
Let's create some music. Welcome back to our discussion about modifying and cleaning

10248
13:24:43.059 --> 13:24:43.730
data frames.

10249
13:24:43.730 --> 13:24:47.803
We've discussed various transformations and methods to modify our data, but we

10250
13:24:47.803 --> 13:24:52.189
haven't covered much about how Spark actually processes the data. Let's look at that

10251
13:24:52.189 --> 13:24:56.471
now. Spark breaks data frames into partitions, or chunks of data. These partitions

10252
13:24:56.471 --> 13:25:00.805
can be automatically defined, enlarged, shrunk, and can differ greatly based on the

10253
13:25:00.805 --> 13:25:05.191
type of Spark cluster being used. The size of the partition does vary, but generally

10254
13:25:05.191 --> 13:25:09.107
try to keep your partition sizes equal. We'll discuss more about optimizing

10255
13:25:09.107 --> 13:25:11.300
partitioning and cluster details later on.

10256
13:25:11.300 --> 13:25:15.808
For now, let's assume that each partition is handled independently. This is part of

10257
13:25:15.808 --> 13:25:20.261
what provides the performance levels and horizontal scaling ability in Spark. If a

10258
13:25:20.261 --> 13:25:24.769
Spark node doesn't need to compete for resources nor consult with other Spark nodes

10259
13:25:24.769 --> 13:25:29.168
for answers, it can reliably schedule the processing for the best performance. In

10260
13:25:29.168 --> 13:25:33.730
Spark, any transformation operation is lazy. It's more like a recipe than a command.

10261
13:25:33.730 --> 13:25:38.020
It defines what should be done with a data frame rather than actually doing it.

10262
13:25:38.020 --> 13:25:42.381
Most operations at Spark are actually transformations, including with column,

10263
13:25:42.381 --> 13:25:46.685
select, filter, and so forth. The set of transformations you define are only

10264
13:25:46.685 --> 13:25:51.442
executed when you run a Spark action. This includes count, write, etc. Anything that

10265
13:25:51.442 --> 13:25:55.860
requires the transformations to be run to properly obtain an answer. Spark can

10266
13:25:55.860 --> 13:26:00.447
reorder transformations for the best performance. Usually, this isn't noticeable,

10267
13:26:00.447 --> 13:26:05.035
but can occasionally cause unexpected behavior, such as IDs not being added until

10268
13:26:05.035 --> 13:26:07.470
after other transformations have completed.

10269
13:26:07.470 --> 13:26:11.724
This doesn't actually cause a problem, but the data can look unusual if you don't

10270
13:26:11.724 --> 13:26:16.083
know what to expect. Relational databases tend to have a field used to identify the

10271
13:26:16.083 --> 13:26:19.864
row, whether it is for an actual relationship reference or just for data

10272
13:26:19.864 --> 13:26:23.961
identification. These IDs are typically an integer that increases in value, is

10273
13:26:23.961 --> 13:26:28.320
sequential, and most importantly, unique. The problem with these IDs is they're not

10274
13:26:28.320 --> 13:26:32.679
very parallel in nature. Given that the values are given out sequentially, if there

10275
13:26:32.679 --> 13:26:36.880
are multiple workers, they must all refer to a common source for the next entry.

10276
13:26:36.880 --> 13:26:41.693
This is okay in a single server environment, but in a distributed platform such as

10277
13:26:41.693 --> 13:26:46.506
Spark, it creates some undue bottlenecks. Let's take a look at how to generate IDs

10278
13:26:46.506 --> 13:26:51.437
in Spark. Spark has a built-in function called monotonically increasing ID, designed

10279
13:26:51.437 --> 13:26:56.191
to provide an integer ID that increases in value and is unique. These IDs are not

10280
13:26:56.191 --> 13:27:01.122
necessarily sequential. There can be gaps, often quite large, between values. Unlike

10281
13:27:01.122 --> 13:27:04.350
a normal relational ID, Spark's is completely parallel.

10282
13:27:04.350 --> 13:27:09.162
Each partition is allocated up to 8 billion IDs that can be assigned. Notice that

10283
13:27:09.162 --> 13:27:13.915
the ID fields in the sample table are integers, increasing in value, but are not

10284
13:27:13.915 --> 13:27:18.847
sequential. It's a little out of scope, but the IDs are a 64-bit number effectively

10285
13:27:18.847 --> 13:27:23.837
split into groups based on the Spark partition. Each group contains 8.4 billion IDs,

10286
13:27:23.837 --> 13:27:28.709
and there are 2.1 billion possible groups, none of which overlap. There's a lot of

10287
13:27:28.709 --> 13:27:32.690
nuance to how partitions and the monotonically increasing IDs work.

10288
13:27:32.690 --> 13:27:37.007
Remembering that Spark is lazy, it often helps in troubleshooting what can happen.

10289
13:27:37.007 --> 13:27:41.271
Operations are often out of order, especially if joins are involved. It's best to

10290
13:27:41.271 --> 13:27:45.693
test your transformations. We've discussed a lot of detail in this lesson. Let's now

10291
13:27:45.693 --> 13:27:49.905
take a look at a few exercises that will help solidify. Now that we've discussed

10292
13:27:49.905 --> 13:27:54.222
some data cleaning tasks using Spark, let's look at how to improve the performance

10293
13:27:54.222 --> 13:27:58.644
of running those tasks using caching. Caching in Spark refers to storing the results

10294
13:27:58.644 --> 13:28:02.540
of a data frame in memory or on disk of the processing nodes in a cluster.

10295
13:28:02.540 --> 13:28:06.754
Caching improves the speed for subsequent transformations or actions, as the data

10296
13:28:06.754 --> 13:28:11.072
likely no longer needs to be retrieved from the original data source. Using caching

10297
13:28:11.072 --> 13:28:15.286
reduces the resource utilization of the cluster. There is less need to access the

10298
13:28:15.286 --> 13:28:19.292
storage, networking, and CPU of the Spark nodes as the data is likely already

10299
13:28:19.292 --> 13:28:23.662
present. There are a few disadvantages of caching you should be aware of. Very large

10300
13:28:23.662 --> 13:28:28.032
datasets may not fit in the memory reserved for cached data frames. Depending on the

10301
13:28:28.032 --> 13:28:32.350
later transformations requested, the cache may not do anything to help performance.

10302
13:28:32.350 --> 13:28:36.903
If a dataset does not stay cached in memory, it may be persisted to disk. Depending

10303
13:28:36.903 --> 13:28:41.347
on the disk configuration of a Spark cluster, this may not be a large performance

10304
13:28:41.347 --> 13:28:45.736
improvement. If you're reading from a local network resource and have slow local

10305
13:28:45.736 --> 13:28:50.345
disk I.O., it may be better to avoid caching the objects. Finally, the lifetime of a

10306
13:28:50.345 --> 13:28:54.679
cached object is not guaranteed. Spark handles regenerating data frames for you

10307
13:28:54.679 --> 13:28:58.903
automatically, but this can cause delays in processing. Caching is incredibly

10308
13:28:58.903 --> 13:29:02.030
useful, but only if you plan to use the data frame again.

10309
13:29:02.030 --> 13:29:06.732
If you only need it for a single task, it's not worth caching. The best way to gauge

10310
13:29:06.732 --> 13:29:11.267
performance with caching is to test various configurations. Try caching your data

10311
13:29:11.267 --> 13:29:15.633
frames at various points in the processing cycle and check if it improves your

10312
13:29:15.633 --> 13:29:20.112
processing time. Try to cache in memory or fast NVMe or SSD storage. While still

10313
13:29:20.112 --> 13:29:24.366
slower than main memory, modern SSD-based storage is drastically faster than

10314
13:29:24.366 --> 13:29:25.150
spinning disk.

10315
13:29:25.150 --> 13:29:28.985
Local spinning hard drives can still be useful if you are processing large

10316
13:29:28.985 --> 13:29:33.182
dataframes that require a lot of steps to degenerate or must be accessed over the

10317
13:29:33.182 --> 13:29:37.224
internet. Testing this is crucial. If normal caching doesn't seem to work, try

10318
13:29:37.224 --> 13:29:41.421
creating intermediate Parquet representations like we did in Chapter 1. These can

10319
13:29:41.421 --> 13:29:45.774
provide a checkpoint in case a job fails mid-task and can still be used with caching

10320
13:29:45.774 --> 13:29:50.023
to further improve performance. Finally, you can manually stop caching a dataframe

10321
13:29:50.023 --> 13:29:54.220
when you're finished with it. This frees up cache resources for other dataframes.

10322
13:29:54.220 --> 13:29:58.845
Implementing caching in Spark is simple. The primary way is to call the function

10323
13:29:58.845 --> 13:30:03.586
cache on a DataFrame object prior to a given action. It requires no arguments. One

10324
13:30:03.586 --> 13:30:08.154
example is creating a DataFrame from some original CSV data. Prior to running a

10325
13:30:08.154 --> 13:30:12.952
count on the data, we call cache to tell Spark to store it in cache. Another option

10326
13:30:12.952 --> 13:30:17.578
is to call cache separately. Here we create an ID in one transformation. Then we

10327
13:30:17.578 --> 13:30:22.203
call cache on the DataFrame. When we call the show action, the VoterDF DataFrame

10328
13:30:22.203 --> 13:30:23.070
will be cached.

10329
13:30:23.070 --> 13:30:27.398
If you're following closely, this means that cache is a Spark transformation.

10330
13:30:27.398 --> 13:30:31.895
Nothing is actually cached until an action is called. A couple other options are

10331
13:30:31.895 --> 13:30:36.616
available with caching in Spark. To check if a dataframe is cached, use the isCached

10332
13:30:36.616 --> 13:30:40.944
boolean property, which returns true, as in this case, or false. To uncache a

10333
13:30:40.944 --> 13:30:45.441
dataframe, we call unpersist with no arguments. This removes the object from the

10334
13:30:45.441 --> 13:30:50.106
cache. We've discussed caching in depth. We've just finished working with improving

10335
13:30:50.106 --> 13:30:51.680
import performance in Spark.

10336
13:30:51.680 --> 13:30:56.107
Let's take a look at cluster configurations. Spark has many available configuration

10337
13:30:56.107 --> 13:31:00.427
settings controlling all aspects of the installation. These configurations can be

10338
13:31:00.427 --> 13:31:04.747
modified to best match the specific needs for the cluster. The configurations are

10339
13:31:04.747 --> 13:31:09.227
available in the configuration files via the Spark web interface and via the runtime

10340
13:31:09.227 --> 13:31:13.600
code. Our test cluster is only accessible via command shell, so we'll use the last

10341
13:31:13.600 --> 13:31:17.920
option. To read a configuration setting, call Spark.conf.get with the name of the

10342
13:31:17.920 --> 13:31:19.200
setting as the argument.

10343
13:31:19.200 --> 13:31:24.089
To write a configuration setting, call Spark.conf.set with the name of the setting

10344
13:31:24.089 --> 13:31:29.097
and the actual value as the function arguments. Spark deployments can vary depending

10345
13:31:29.097 --> 13:31:33.688
on the exact needs of the users. One component of a deployment is the cluster

10346
13:31:33.688 --> 13:31:38.339
management mechanism. Spark clusters can be single node clusters deploying all

10347
13:31:38.339 --> 13:31:43.347
components on a single system, physical, VM, or container. Stand-alone clusters with

10348
13:31:43.347 --> 13:31:46.030
dedicated machines as the driver and workers.

10349
13:31:46.030 --> 13:31:49.820
Or managed clusters, meaning that the cluster components are handled by a

10350
13:31:49.820 --> 13:31:53.922
third-party cluster manager such as Yarn, Mesos, or Kubernetes. In this course,

10351
13:31:53.922 --> 13:31:58.232
we're using a single node cluster. Your production environment can vary wildly, but

10352
13:31:58.232 --> 13:32:02.541
we'll discuss standalone clusters as the concepts flow across all management types.

10353
13:32:02.541 --> 13:32:06.851
If you recall, there is one driver per sport cluster. The driver is responsible for

10354
13:32:06.851 --> 13:32:11.005
several things, including the following. Handling task assignment to the various

10355
13:32:11.005 --> 13:32:12.770
nodes or processes in the cluster.

10356
13:32:12.770 --> 13:32:16.960
The driver monitors the state of all processes and tasks and handles any task

10357
13:32:16.960 --> 13:32:21.314
retries. The driver is also responsible for consolidating results from the other

10358
13:32:21.314 --> 13:32:25.831
processes in the cluster. The driver handles any access to shared data and verifies

10359
13:32:25.831 --> 13:32:29.913
each worker process has the necessary resources, code, data, etc. Given the

10360
13:32:29.913 --> 13:32:34.267
importance of the driver, it is often worth increasing the specifications of the

10361
13:32:34.267 --> 13:32:38.784
node compared to the other systems. Doubling the memory compared to the other nodes

10362
13:32:38.784 --> 13:32:39.600
is recommended.

10363
13:32:39.600 --> 13:32:44.045
This is useful for task monitoring and data consolidation tasks. As with all Spark

10364
13:32:44.045 --> 13:32:48.598
systems, fast local storage is useful for running Spark in an ideal setup. The Spark

10365
13:32:48.598 --> 13:32:53.043
worker handles running tasks assigned by the driver and communicates those results

10366
13:32:53.043 --> 13:32:57.542
back to the driver. Ideally, the worker has a copy of all code, data, and access to

10367
13:32:57.542 --> 13:33:01.770
the necessary resources required to complete a given task. If any of these are

10368
13:33:01.770 --> 13:33:06.215
unavailable, the worker must pause to obtain the resources. When sizing a cluster,

10369
13:33:06.215 --> 13:33:07.950
there are a few recommendations.

10370
13:33:07.950 --> 13:33:10.000
Depending on the type of task,

10371
13:33:10.000 --> 13:33:14.135
worker nodes is often better than larger nodes. This can be especially obvious

10372
13:33:14.135 --> 13:33:18.482
during import and export operations as there are more machines available to do the

10373
13:33:18.482 --> 13:33:22.829
work. As with everything in Spark, test various configurations to find the correct

10374
13:33:22.829 --> 13:33:26.858
balance for your workload. Assuming a cloud environment, 16 worker nodes may

10375
13:33:26.858 --> 13:33:31.205
complete a job in an hour and cost $50 in resources. An eight worker configuration

10376
13:33:31.205 --> 13:33:35.340
might take one and a quarter hours, but don't cost only half as much. Finally,

10377
13:33:35.340 --> 13:33:39.558
Workers can make use of fast local storage, SSD or NVMe for caching, intermediate

10378
13:33:39.558 --> 13:33:43.515
files, etc. Now that we've discussed cluster sizing and configuration, let's

10379
13:33:43.515 --> 13:33:47.629
practice working with them. We've discussed Spark clusters and improving import

10380
13:33:47.629 --> 13:33:52.003
performance. Let's look at how to improve the performance of Spark tasks in general.

10381
13:33:52.003 --> 13:33:56.273
To understand performance applications of Spark, you must be able to see what it's

10382
13:33:56.273 --> 13:34:00.647
doing under the hood. The easiest way to do this is to use the explain function on a

10383
13:34:00.647 --> 13:34:01.220
data frame.

10384
13:34:01.220 --> 13:34:05.449
This example is taken from an earlier exercise, simply requesting a single column

10385
13:34:05.449 --> 13:34:09.730
and running distinct against it. The result is the estimated plan that will be run

10386
13:34:09.730 --> 13:34:14.064
to generate the results from the data frame. Don't worry about the specifics of the

10387
13:34:14.064 --> 13:34:18.449
plan yet, just remember how to view it if needed. Spark distributes data amongst the

10388
13:34:18.449 --> 13:34:22.731
various nodes in the cluster. A side effect of this is what is known as shuffling.

10389
13:34:22.731 --> 13:34:26.908
Shuffling is moving of data fragments to various workers as required to complete

10390
13:34:26.908 --> 13:34:30.980
certain tasks. Shuffling is useful and hides overall complexity from the user.

10391
13:34:30.980 --> 13:34:35.089
The user doesn't have to know which nodes have what data. That being said, it can be

10392
13:34:35.089 --> 13:34:39.150
slow to complete the necessary transfers, especially if a few nodes require all the

10393
13:34:39.150 --> 13:34:43.063
data. Shuffling lowers the overall throughput of the cluster as the workers must

10394
13:34:43.063 --> 13:34:46.977
spend time waiting for the data to transfer. This limits the amount of available

10395
13:34:46.977 --> 13:34:50.744
workers for the remaining tasks in the system. Shuffling is often a necessary

10396
13:34:50.744 --> 13:34:54.608
component but it's helpful to try to minimize it as much as possible. It can be

10397
13:34:54.608 --> 13:34:58.620
tricky to remove shuffling operations entirely but there are a few things that can

10398
13:34:58.620 --> 13:34:59.060
limit it.

10399
13:34:59.060 --> 13:35:02.869
The dataframe repartition function takes a single argument of the number of

10400
13:35:02.869 --> 13:35:07.135
partitions requested. We've used this in an earlier chapter to illustrate the effect

10401
13:35:07.135 --> 13:35:11.401
of partitions with a monotonically increasing ID function. Repartitioning requires a

10402
13:35:11.401 --> 13:35:15.667
full shuffle of data between nodes and processes, and it's quite costly. If you need

10403
13:35:15.667 --> 13:35:19.781
to reduce the number of partitions, use the coalesce function instead. It takes a

10404
13:35:19.781 --> 13:35:23.996
number of partitions smaller than the current one and consolidates the data without

10405
13:35:23.996 --> 13:35:25.520
requiring a full data shuffle.

10406
13:35:25.520 --> 13:35:29.496
Note, calling coalesce with a larger number of partitions does not actually do

10407
13:35:29.496 --> 13:35:33.574
anything. The join function is a great use of Spark and provides a lot of power.

10408
13:35:33.574 --> 13:35:37.449
Calling join indiscriminately can often cause shuffle operations, leading to

10409
13:35:37.449 --> 13:35:41.527
increased cluster load and slower processing times. To avoid some of the shuffle

10410
13:35:41.527 --> 13:35:45.758
operations when joining Spark data frames, you can use the broadcast function. I'll

10411
13:35:45.758 --> 13:35:49.836
talk about this more in a moment. Finally, an important note about data cleaning

10412
13:35:49.836 --> 13:35:52.640
operations is remembering to optimize for what matters.

10413
13:35:52.640 --> 13:35:56.672
The speed of your initial code may be perfectly acceptable and time may be better

10414
13:35:56.672 --> 13:36:00.754
spent elsewhere. Broadcasting and Spark is a method to provide a copy of an object

10415
13:36:00.754 --> 13:36:04.787
to each worker. When each worker has its own copy of the data, there is less need

10416
13:36:04.787 --> 13:36:08.819
for communication between nodes. This limits data shuffles and it's more likely a

10417
13:36:08.819 --> 13:36:12.901
node will fulfill tasks independently. Using broadcasting can drastically speed up

10418
13:36:12.901 --> 13:36:17.033
joint operations, especially if one of the data frames being joined is much smaller

10419
13:36:17.033 --> 13:36:17.780
than the other.

10420
13:36:17.780 --> 13:36:21.429
To implement broadcasting, you must import the broadcast function from

10421
13:36:21.429 --> 13:36:25.652
PySpark.SQL.functions. Once imported, simply call the broadcast function with the

10422
13:36:25.652 --> 13:36:30.031
name of the data frame you wish to broadcast. Note, broadcasting can slow operations

10423
13:36:30.031 --> 13:36:34.254
when using very small data frames, or if you broadcast the larger data frame in a

10424
13:36:34.254 --> 13:36:38.268
join. Spark will often optimize this for you, but as usual, run tests in your

10425
13:36:38.268 --> 13:36:42.595
environment for best performance. We've looked how to limit shuffling and implement

10426
13:36:42.595 --> 13:36:45.410
broadcasting for data frames. Let's practice utilizing

10427
13:36:45.410 --> 13:36:49.858
We've spent most of this course working with individual transformations and actions

10428
13:36:49.858 --> 13:36:54.252
to clean data in Spark. But data is rarely so simple that a couple transformations

10429
13:36:54.252 --> 13:36:58.700
or actions can prepare it for real analysis. Let's look now at data pipelines. Data

10430
13:36:58.700 --> 13:37:03.041
pipelines are simply the set of steps needed to move from an input data source or

10431
13:37:03.041 --> 13:37:07.328
sources and convert it to the desired output. A data pipeline can consist of any

10432
13:37:07.328 --> 13:37:11.776
number of steps or components and can span many systems. For our purposes, we'll be

10433
13:37:11.776 --> 13:37:13.920
setting up a data pipeline within Spark.

10434
13:37:13.920 --> 13:37:18.522
But realize that a full production data pipeline will likely communicate with many

10435
13:37:18.522 --> 13:37:23.124
systems. Much like Spark in general, a data pipeline typically consists of inputs,

10436
13:37:23.124 --> 13:37:27.389
transformations, and the outputs of those steps. In addition, there is often

10437
13:37:27.389 --> 13:37:32.103
validation and analysis steps before delivery of the data to the next user. An input

10438
13:37:32.103 --> 13:37:36.817
can be any of the data types we've looked at so far, including CSV, JSON, text, etc.

10439
13:37:36.817 --> 13:37:41.532
It could be from the local file system or from web services, APIs, databases, and so

10440
13:37:41.532 --> 13:37:41.700
on.

10441
13:37:41.700 --> 13:37:46.038
The basic idea is to read the data into a data frame as we've done previously. Once

10442
13:37:46.038 --> 13:37:50.271
we have the data in a data frame, we need to transform it in some fashion. You've

10443
13:37:50.271 --> 13:37:54.399
done the individual steps several times throughout this course. Adding columns,

10444
13:37:54.399 --> 13:37:58.684
filtering rows, performing calculations as needed. In our previous examples, we've

10445
13:37:58.684 --> 13:38:03.074
only done one or two of these steps at a time, but a pipeline can consist of as many

10446
13:38:03.074 --> 13:38:07.307
of these steps as needed so we can format the data into our desired output. After

10447
13:38:07.307 --> 13:38:11.540
we've defined our transformations, we need to output the data into a usable form.

10448
13:38:11.540 --> 13:38:15.997
You've already written files out to CSV or Parquet format, but it could include

10449
13:38:15.997 --> 13:38:20.567
multiple copies with various formats or instead write the output to a database, a

10450
13:38:20.567 --> 13:38:25.080
web service, etc. The last two steps vary greatly depending on your needs. We'll

10451
13:38:25.080 --> 13:38:29.819
discuss validation in a later lesson, but the idea is to run some form of testing on

10452
13:38:29.819 --> 13:38:34.163
the data to verify it is as expected. Analysis is often the final step before

10453
13:38:34.163 --> 13:38:38.789
handing the data off to the next user. This can include things such as row counts,

10454
13:38:38.789 --> 13:38:40.030
specific calculations,

10455
13:38:40.030 --> 13:38:44.357
or pretty much anything that makes it easier for the user to consume the dataset.

10456
13:38:44.357 --> 13:38:48.577
It's important to note that in Spark, a data pipeline is not a formally defined

10457
13:38:48.577 --> 13:38:52.850
object, but rather a concept. This is different than if you've used the pipeline

10458
13:38:52.850 --> 13:38:57.177
object in Spark.ml. If you haven't, don't worry, it's not needed for this course.

10459
13:38:57.177 --> 13:39:01.451
For our purposes, a Spark pipeline is all the normal code required to complete a

10460
13:39:01.451 --> 13:39:05.778
task. In this example, we're doing the various tasks required to define a schema,

10461
13:39:05.778 --> 13:39:09.410
read a data file, add an ID, then write out two separate data types.

10462
13:39:09.410 --> 13:39:13.662
The task could be much more complex, but the concept is usually the same. When you

10463
13:39:13.662 --> 13:39:17.914
look at the components, a data pipeline in Spark is fairly simple, but can be very

10464
13:39:17.914 --> 13:39:22.270
powerful. Let's start working on a more elaborate. We've worked with many aspects of

10465
13:39:22.270 --> 13:39:26.470
Spark when it comes to data cleaning operations. Let's look at how to use some of

10466
13:39:26.470 --> 13:39:30.515
the methods we've learned to parse unconventional data. When reading data into

10467
13:39:30.515 --> 13:39:34.767
Spark, you're rarely given a fully uniform file. Often there is content that needs

10468
13:39:34.767 --> 13:39:38.500
to be removed or reformatted. Some common issues include incorrect data,

10469
13:39:38.500 --> 13:39:42.841
consisting of empty rows, commented lines, headers, or even rows that don't match

10470
13:39:42.841 --> 13:39:47.128
the intended schema. Real-world data often includes nested structures, including

10471
13:39:47.128 --> 13:39:51.255
columns that use different delimiters. This could include the primary columns

10472
13:39:51.255 --> 13:39:55.756
separated via a comma, but including some components separated via a semicolon. Real

10473
13:39:55.756 --> 13:39:59.990
data often won't fit into a tabular format, sometimes consisting of a differing

10474
13:39:59.990 --> 13:40:04.063
number of columns per row. There are various ways to parse data in all these

10475
13:40:04.063 --> 13:40:07.600
situations. The way you choose will depend on your specific needs.

10476
13:40:07.600 --> 13:40:12.025
We are focusing on CSV data for this course, but the general scenarios described

10477
13:40:12.025 --> 13:40:16.506
apply to other formats as well. For this chapter, we're going to use the Stanford

10478
13:40:16.506 --> 13:40:20.709
ImageNet annotations, which focus on finding and identifying dogs in various

10479
13:40:20.709 --> 13:40:25.301
ImageNet images. The annotations provide a list of all identified dogs in an image,

10480
13:40:25.301 --> 13:40:29.670
including when multiple dogs are in the same image. Other metadata is included,

10481
13:40:29.670 --> 13:40:34.040
including the folder within the ImageNet dataset, the image dimensions, and the

10482
13:40:34.040 --> 13:40:36.640
bounding box or boxes of the dogs in the image.

10483
13:40:36.640 --> 13:40:41.292
In the example rows, we have the folder names, the image net image reference, width,

10484
13:40:41.292 --> 13:40:45.889
and height. Then there is the image data for the type of dog or dogs in each image.

10485
13:40:45.889 --> 13:40:50.486
Each breed column consists of the breed name and the bounding box in the image. The

10486
13:40:50.486 --> 13:40:55.138
first row contains one Newfoundland, but notice that the second row actually has two

10487
13:40:55.138 --> 13:40:59.624
bull mastiffs identified and an additional column defined. Spark's CSV parser can

10488
13:40:59.624 --> 13:41:02.670
handle many common data issues via optional parameters.

10489
13:41:02.670 --> 13:41:06.970
Blake lines are automatically removed, unless specifically instructed otherwise,

10490
13:41:06.970 --> 13:41:11.485
when using the CSV parsing. Comments can be removed with an optional named argument,

10491
13:41:11.485 --> 13:41:15.785
comment, and specifying the character that any comment line would be defined by.

10492
13:41:15.785 --> 13:41:19.924
Note that this handles lines that begin with a specific comment. Parsing more

10493
13:41:19.924 --> 13:41:24.331
complex comment usage requires more involved procedures. Header rows can be parsed

10494
13:41:24.331 --> 13:41:28.631
via an optional parameter named header and set to true or false. If no schema is

10495
13:41:28.631 --> 13:41:32.340
defined, column names will be initially set as defined by the header.

10496
13:41:32.340 --> 13:41:36.524
If a schema is defined, the row is not used as data, but the header names are

10497
13:41:36.524 --> 13:41:41.035
otherwise ignored. When importing CSV data into Spark, it will automatically create

10498
13:41:41.035 --> 13:41:45.545
data frame columns if it can. It will split a row of text from the CSV on a defined

10499
13:41:45.545 --> 13:41:49.838
separator argument named sep. If sep is not defined, it will default to using a

10500
13:41:49.838 --> 13:41:54.349
comma. The CSV parser will still succeed in parsing data if the separator character

10501
13:41:54.349 --> 13:41:58.859
is not within the string. It will store the entire row in a column named underscore

10502
13:41:58.859 --> 13:41:59.620
C0 by default.

10503
13:41:59.620 --> 13:42:03.767
Using this trick allows parsing of nested or complex data. We'll look at this more

10504
13:42:03.767 --> 13:42:07.762
later on. Let's practice working with this data and extending it. Welcome back.

10505
13:42:07.762 --> 13:42:11.808
Validation is one step of a data pipeline we haven't covered yet, but it is very

10506
13:42:11.808 --> 13:42:15.956
important in verifying the quality of the data we're delivering. Let's look at how

10507
13:42:15.956 --> 13:42:19.749
to implement validation steps in a data cleaning pipeline. In this context,

10508
13:42:19.749 --> 13:42:23.390
validation is verifying that a dataset complies with an expected format.

10509
13:42:23.390 --> 13:42:27.542
This can include verifying the number of rows and columns as is expected. For

10510
13:42:27.542 --> 13:42:31.749
example, is the row count within 2% of the previous month's row count? Another

10511
