14:12:34.923 --> 14:12:40.810
whereas the second did not print this is because the second log statement is at

10877
14:12:40.810 --> 14:12:44.610
debug level this time we set the log level to debug

10878
14:12:44.610 --> 14:12:49.566
This means that the second log statement will display. Due to the combination of

10879
14:12:49.566 --> 14:12:50.000
Spark's

10880
14:12:50.000 --> 14:12:55.323
lazy evaluation and distributed computation, debugging a complex application can be

10881
14:12:55.323 --> 14:13:00.582
challenging. This is because an erroneous action might not be triggered until well

10882
14:13:00.582 --> 14:13:05.713
downstream of where the action is coded. One way to simplify the debugging of an

10883
14:13:05.713 --> 14:13:11.036
application is to force actions to be triggered. However, a naive approach to doing

10884
14:13:11.036 --> 14:13:16.231
this can cause stealth loss of CPU. We'll use a simple timer to prove the stealth

10885
14:13:16.231 --> 14:13:18.860
loss of CPU. It prints a sequence number,

10886
14:13:18.860 --> 14:13:23.938
and the elapsed time. It then resets its timer. The timer provides a function

10887
14:13:23.938 --> 14:13:29.148
elapsed, which prints the elapsed time. Here is an example of using this simple

10888
14:13:29.148 --> 14:13:34.226
timer. Immediately after creating it, it shows an elapsed time of zero. After

10889
14:13:34.226 --> 14:13:39.436
performing an operation that takes two seconds, it shows an elapsed time of two

10890
14:13:39.436 --> 14:13:44.844
seconds. We can zero the timer using reset. For concreteness, here is the code for

10891
14:13:44.844 --> 14:13:47.020
the simple timer being used here.

10892
14:13:47.020 --> 14:13:52.690
Here is a naive approach to printing a debug statement that prints the size of a

10893
14:13:52.690 --> 14:13:58.572
data frame. Only if debug log level is enabled. Carefully inspect what is happening

10894
14:13:58.572 --> 14:14:04.313
here. The log level is set to info. The info log statement prints as desired. The

10895
14:14:04.313 --> 14:14:10.124
debug log statement does not print also as desired. However, note that the elapsed

10896
14:14:10.124 --> 14:14:14.660
time of the debug statement is 2.0 seconds. That is not desired.

10897
14:14:14.660 --> 14:14:20.226
This is because the debug log statement contains a data frame action, the df.count

10898
14:14:20.226 --> 14:14:25.657
operator. That action is being triggered even though the debug statement is not.

10899
14:14:25.657 --> 14:14:30.952
Though the debug statement is below the log level threshold, the expression is

10900
14:14:30.952 --> 14:14:36.450
always evaluated. As a result, the data frame action is being performed even when

10901
14:14:36.450 --> 14:14:38.690
the log message is not displayed.

10902
14:14:38.690 --> 14:14:43.845
For most applications, the cost of evaluating an expression passed into the logging

10903
14:14:43.845 --> 14:14:48.627
statement is insignificant. However, account operation on a data frame can be

10904
14:14:48.627 --> 14:14:53.719
