14:42:44.245 --> 14:42:49.630
it, we can use the upper function. Upper converts a given string to uppercase. In

11228
14:42:49.630 --> 14:42:54.816
the following code, we can see that the upper function wraps the result of the

11229
14:42:54.816 --> 14:43:00.068
replace function. This results in the following output. Let's have fun cleaning

11230
14:43:00.068 --> 14:43:05.387
messy strings. In this lesson, we will learn how to check the similarity between

11231
14:43:05.387 --> 14:43:07.780
strings using soundex and different.

11232
14:43:07.780 --> 14:43:13.843
As we said in previous lessons, it is common to find messy strings. Sometimes, these

11233
14:43:13.843 --> 14:43:19.617
strings are written differently, but their sounds are similar or almost similar.

11234
14:43:19.617 --> 14:43:25.464
With the use of the functions Soundex and Difference, SQL Server provides help to

11235
14:43:25.464 --> 14:43:31.527
detect those strings. Let's start with Soundex. Soundex is a phonetic algorithm that

11236
14:43:31.527 --> 14:43:36.940
returns a 4-character code used to evaluate the similarity between strings.

11237
14:43:36.940 --> 14:43:42.673
Based on their pronunciation. Although the algorithm is based on the English

11238
14:43:42.673 --> 14:43:49.010
language, it also works with many words in other languages. For example, if we apply

11239
14:43:49.010 --> 14:43:54.593
Soundex to the string Illinois, to this other string Illinois, and also to

11240
14:43:54.593 --> 14:44:00.703
California, we will get the following output. The first two strings have the same

11241
14:44:00.703 --> 14:44:06.210
code, which means they are similar to the Soundex algorithm. By contrast,

11242
14:44:06.210 --> 14:44:12.414
California has another code. This string is different from the others. Let's see how

11243
14:44:12.414 --> 14:44:18.618
it works with the word Illinois. First of all, SQL Server writes the first letter of

11244
14:44:18.618 --> 14:44:24.822
the word I in this example. Then, it replaces to zero all the vowels and the letters

11245
14:44:24.822 --> 14:44:30.952
H, W and W after the first letter. After that, the rest of the consonants after the

11246
14:44:30.952 --> 14:44:35.900
first letter are replaced following this table. In this example, L,

11247
14:44:35.900 --> 14:44:42.647
will be replaced by 4, n by 5, and s by 2. After that, SQL Server replaces the same

11248
14:44:42.647 --> 14:44:49.475
adjacent digits with just 1. Then, it removes all the zeros. Finally, if the letters

11249
14:44:49.475 --> 14:44:56.141
digit is the same as the first digit, it removes the first digit. It appends zeros

11250
14:44:56.141 --> 14:45:02.726
if the code contains less than 3 digits, and removes final digits if the code has

11251
14:45:02.726 --> 14:45:04.270
more than 3 digits.

11252
14:45:04.270 --> 14:45:09.928
All these final steps don't apply to our sample. Soundex is not 100% perfect,

11253
14:45:09.928 --> 14:45:16.100
although it is very useful we need to check the results, because some exceptions may

11254
14:45:16.100 --> 14:45:22.198
occur. Here is an example of two words, Arizona and Arkansas, that sound different,

11255
14:45:22.198 --> 14:45:26.460
but return the same code. Let's put Soundex into practice.

11256
14:45:26.460 --> 14:45:31.981
In the following code, we are getting all the values from the airport state column

11257
14:45:31.981 --> 14:45:37.367
that have the same Soundex code between them, but are written differently. To do

11258
14:45:37.367 --> 14:45:42.484
this, we are joining the airport table with itself. The output we get is the

11259
14:45:42.484 --> 14:45:47.668
following. We can see that Soundex detected different strings for California,

11260
14:45:47.668 --> 14:45:53.122
Illinois and Texas. Between the results, we have New Jersey and New York, because

11261
14:45:53.122 --> 14:45:55.680
Soundex only applies to the first war.

11262
14:45:55.680 --> 14:46:01.278
New in this example. Let's see how to avoid these results and check the strings

11263
14:46:01.278 --> 14:46:06.875
correctly. We included in the previous code the replace function to omit spaces

11264
14:46:06.875 --> 14:46:12.615
between words, like New York. Soundex didn't find any matches between states with

11265
14:46:12.615 --> 14:46:18.213
more than one word, like New York or New Jersey. Let's study now the difference

11266
14:46:18.213 --> 14:46:24.165
function. Difference compares two Soundex values and returns an integer from 0 to 4.

11267
14:46:24.165 --> 14:46:25.440
0 indicates little

11268
14:46:25.440 --> 14:46:30.940
or no similarity, while 4 represents very similar or identically matching. If we

11269
14:46:30.940 --> 14:46:36.715
apply difference to these strings, the result we get is 4, which means they are very

11270
14:46:36.715 --> 14:46:42.077
similar or identical. However, applying difference to these other strings will

11271
14:46:42.077 --> 14:46:47.715
output 1, which means low similarity. Similar to the Soundex example, we can check

11272
14:46:47.715 --> 14:46:52.665
similarities between states that have a difference of 4, but are written

11273
14:46:52.665 --> 14:46:53.490
differently.

11274
14:46:53.490 --> 14:46:59.294
Between the results, we can see that the pair of states, Massachusetts and Michigan,

11275
14:46:59.294 --> 14:47:04.822
are completely different, although they have a value of 4. So, as in the Soundex

11276
14:47:04.822 --> 14:47:10.557
function, we always need to review the results. Let's put Soundex and difference in

11277
14:47:10.557 --> 14:47:16.361
here. Congratulations on completing chapter 1. In this chapter, we will learn how to

11278
14:47:16.361 --> 14:47:21.060
deal with missing data, avoid duplicate data, and clean messy dates.

11279
14:47:21.060 --> 14:47:26.577
On some occasions, we can find that our data has missing values. In SQL Server,

11280
14:47:26.577 --> 14:47:32.304
missing values are represented by null. However, in other cases, it is also common

11281
14:47:32.304 --> 14:47:37.891
to find blank values. Missing data can happen for some reason. Sometimes, it can

11282
14:47:37.891 --> 14:47:43.618
happen intentionally. For example, a person who doesn't want to reveal the H, or a

11283
14:47:43.618 --> 14:47:47.040
person who doesn't know the answer to a question.

11284
14:47:47.040 --> 14:47:52.713
In other cases, an error could have occurred when inserting the data. And finally,

11285
14:47:52.713 --> 14:47:58.316
sometimes the data simply doesn't exist. For example, the number of children of a

11286
14:47:58.316 --> 14:48:03.988
person that doesn't have any children. We have different ways to deal with missing

11287
14:48:03.988 --> 14:48:09.730
values. First of all, it is recommended to investigate if we can find those missing

11288
14:48:09.730 --> 14:48:13.950
values. Sometimes we can't find them in the original dataset.

11289
14:48:13.950 --> 14:48:19.726
If we don't find the missing values, we will have the following options depending on

11290
14:48:19.726 --> 14:48:25.296
the business assumptions behind our data. We can leave the data as it is, knowing

11291
14:48:25.296 --> 14:48:31.072
that we have missing values in our dataset. Another option is to avoid the rows with

11292
14:48:31.072 --> 14:48:36.642
missing data by removing them from our analysis. And finally, we can fill missing

11293
14:48:36.642 --> 14:48:41.730
values with another value, such as a text, the average of the values, etc.

11294
14:48:41.730 --> 14:48:47.217
In the next slides, we will learn how to remove the rows with missing values and

11295
14:48:47.217 --> 14:48:52.499
fill null values with other values. Let's see how to remove the rows from the

11296
14:48:52.499 --> 14:48:58.260
airports table that have null values for the airport state column. In SQL Server, we

11297
14:48:58.260 --> 14:49:04.022
can use isNotNull to select the values that aren't null. Note that we didn't get any

11298
14:49:04.022 --> 14:49:07.520
row with a null value for the airport state column.

11299
14:49:07.520 --> 14:49:12.909
If we want to check which are the rows that have null values for the airport state

11300
14:49:12.909 --> 14:49:18.036
column, we can do it by using isNull. As we said previously, we can find blank

11301
14:49:18.036 --> 14:49:23.491
spaces instead of null values. If we want to remove the rows that have blank values

11302
14:49:23.491 --> 14:49:29.012
for the airport state column, we can use the comparison operator to exclude them. We

11303
14:49:29.012 --> 14:49:34.138
can substitute null values using the isNull function. IsNull replaces the null

11304
14:49:34.138 --> 14:49:36.110
values with a specified value.

11305
14:49:36.110 --> 14:49:41.263
Note that it is written together unlike the isNull predicate that we already

11306
14:49:41.263 --> 14:49:46.822
studied. In this example, we replaced the null values for the airport state column

11307
14:49:46.822 --> 14:49:52.110
with the unknown string. On some occasions, null values can be replaced with a

11308
14:49:52.110 --> 14:49:57.669
number, for example, the average of the values for that column as long as it makes

11309
14:49:57.669 --> 14:50:02.890
sense. To get the average of the cancelled column, we need to use the average

11310
14:50:02.890 --> 14:50:03.500
function.

11311
14:50:03.500 --> 14:50:09.528
If we use average in combination with the isNull function, we will get the average

11312
14:50:09.528 --> 14:50:15.556
value in case there is a null value. Finally, another way of replacing null values

11313
14:50:15.556 --> 14:50:20.923
can be performed using coalesce. Coalesce evaluates the arguments between

11314
14:50:20.923 --> 14:50:26.804
parentheses and returns the first argument that is not null. In this example, we

11315
14:50:26.804 --> 14:50:32.905
used coalesce to evaluate the values of airport state, airport city and the unknown

11316
14:50:32.905 --> 14:50:33.420
string.

11317
14:50:33.420 --> 14:50:39.267
For the first row, we can see that as the airport state is not null, we get the

11318
14:50:39.267 --> 14:50:44.966
value Utah. In the second row, as the airport state is null, Coales evaluates

11319
14:50:44.966 --> 14:50:51.183
airport city. Airport city is not null, so it returns Philadelphia. In the last row,

11320
14:50:51.183 --> 14:50:57.178
as both airport state and airport city are null, we get the string unknown. Let's

11321
14:50:57.178 --> 14:50:59.620
practice with it. In this lesson,

11322
14:50:59.620 --> 14:51:04.830
we will learn how to avoid duplicate data in our salt. Let's first explain what

11323
14:51:04.830 --> 14:51:10.369
duplicate data is. In our data, we can find rows with identical information. In this

11324
14:51:10.369 --> 14:51:15.908
example, we have two rows that have the same values in each column. We can also find

11325
14:51:15.908 --> 14:51:21.184
rows with some common information. For instance, two or more rows with identical

11326
14:51:21.184 --> 14:51:26.262
information, but with a different registration code. This example is the most

11327
14:51:26.262 --> 14:51:28.240
typical case of duplicate data

11328
14:51:28.240 --> 14:51:33.769
where all the important information is duplicated. But we can also have two or more

11329
14:51:33.769 --> 14:51:39.032
rows with only some common information and different values for the rest of the

11330
14:51:39.032 --> 14:51:44.095
columns. Duplicate data can interfere in our analysis, so it is essential to

11331
14:51:44.095 --> 14:51:49.691
identify it. To detect duplicate data, first of all, we need to define which columns

11332
14:51:49.691 --> 14:51:55.287
form a repeating group. A repeating group will depend on our dataset. For the flight

11333
14:51:55.287 --> 14:51:56.420
statistics table,

11334
14:51:56.420 --> 14:52:02.166
we can consider that the columns that form a repeating group are the airport code,

11335
14:52:02.166 --> 14:52:07.771
carrier code and registration date. The reason is that only once every month the

11336
14:52:07.771 --> 14:52:13.167
statistics of the flights for a particular airport and a specific carrier are

11337
14:52:13.167 --> 14:52:16.320
registered. Considering this repeating group,

11338
14:52:16.320 --> 14:52:22.193
we can see that we won't have problems to exclude from our results those rows that

11339
14:52:22.193 --> 14:52:28.138
having the same airport code, carrier code, and registration date, have a different

11340
14:52:28.138 --> 14:52:30.000
registration code, or even

11341
14:52:30.000 --> 14:52:35.892
different statistician name and surname. The critical information is still the same.

11342
14:52:35.892 --> 14:52:41.784
Obviously, we won't have any problem to exclude from our results duplicate rows that

11343
14:52:41.784 --> 14:52:47.535
have the same values for every column. But what happens if having the same airport

11344
14:52:47.535 --> 14:52:53.147
code, carrier code, and registration date, the data of the columns cancelled, on

11345
14:52:53.147 --> 14:52:58.828
time, delayed, or diverted are different? Which one should we maintain? We have a

11346
14:52:58.828 --> 14:52:59.740
problem here.

11347
14:52:59.740 --> 14:53:04.548
If we find this kind of duplicates, we will need to investigate which one is

11348
14:53:04.548 --> 14:53:09.798
correct. Once we have found the repeating group, we are going to find the duplicate

11349
14:53:09.798 --> 14:53:14.606
rows for that group. To do that, we will use the rowNumber function. For the

11350
14:53:14.606 --> 14:53:19.540
partitionBy argument, we will specify the columns we considered as a repeating

11351
14:53:19.540 --> 14:53:24.538
group. So, for each row, with common information in the columns considered as a

11352
14:53:24.538 --> 14:53:25.550
repeating group,

11353
14:53:25.550 --> 14:53:30.840
RowNumber will give a number which begins at 1 for the first row and provides a

11354
14:53:30.840 --> 14:53:36.197
sequential number for each row within the same partition or repeating group. The

11355
