The first parameter is the message text, no staff member with such ID. The second

11772
15:28:16.545 --> 15:28:22.591
one, 16, is the severity level. Finally, the last one is the state. If we execute

11773
15:28:22.591 --> 15:28:28.711
this example as there is no staff member whose ID is 15, we will get the following

11774
15:28:28.711 --> 15:28:34.980
output. If we don't specify an error number, the error number will always be 50,000.

11775
15:28:34.980 --> 15:28:40.842
Let's change the message text included on the previous raceError statement and add

11776
15:28:40.842 --> 15:28:46.704
some parameter placeholders. Instead of no staff member with such id, let's change

11777
15:28:46.704 --> 15:28:52.495
the words staff member by the percent symbol followed by the letter s. Let's also

11778
15:28:52.495 --> 15:28:58.429
include %d. %s is used for stream values and it will be replaced by the penultimate

11779
15:28:58.429 --> 15:29:01.360
parameter of this function, staff member.

11780
15:29:01.360 --> 15:29:07.043
%d is used for integer values and it will be replaced by the last parameter of this

11781
15:29:07.043 --> 15:29:12.588
function, 15. If we execute this example, we will get this output. If you want to

11782
15:29:12.588 --> 15:29:18.271
include the %character in your message, you will have to write it twice. Apart from

11783
15:29:18.271 --> 15:29:23.680
the letters s and d, the % symbol can be followed by other characters for other

11784
15:29:23.680 --> 15:29:29.020
purposes. You can deepen your knowledge by looking at Microsoft documentation.

11785
15:29:29.020 --> 15:29:34.560
In the raceError statement, we can also specify an error number instead of a message

11786
15:29:34.560 --> 15:29:40.033
string. This error number comes from sys.messages. We will learn more about the use

11787
15:29:40.033 --> 15:29:45.573
of sys.messages in the lesson dedicated to the formatMessage function. Let's include

11788
15:29:45.573 --> 15:29:50.783
the raceError statement inside a try-cut construct to see what happens. In this

11789
15:29:50.783 --> 15:29:54.740
example, the severity level of the raceError statement is 9.

11790
15:29:54.740 --> 15:29:59.628
If you remember from the previous chapter, severity levels lower than 11 are not

11791
15:29:59.628 --> 15:30:04.394
catchable by the catch block. So here, this error won't be caught by the catch

11792
15:30:04.394 --> 15:30:09.466
block. This will be the output. However, if we set up the severity level between 11

11793
15:30:09.466 --> 15:30:14.476
and 19, the error will be caught by the catch block. In this example, the severity

11794
15:30:14.476 --> 15:30:19.120
level is 16, so the output will be you are in the catch block. Now, you will

11795
15:30:19.120 --> 15:30:21.870
practice applying the raise errors technique.

11796
15:30:21.870 --> 15:30:26.533
Now that you have learned the raise error statement, let's discover the throw

11797
15:30:26.533 --> 15:30:31.499
statement. As we said in the previous lesson, Microsoft recommends using the throw

11798
15:30:31.499 --> 15:30:36.587
statement over the raise error statement. Let's see the throw syntax. It starts with

11799
15:30:36.587 --> 15:30:41.432
the word throw and can optionally be followed by a list of parameters. The first

11800
15:30:41.432 --> 15:30:46.337
parameter is the error number, the second parameter is the message, and the third

11801
15:30:46.337 --> 15:30:47.730
parameter is the state.

11802
15:30:47.730 --> 15:30:52.911
Unlike the eraseError statement, the throw statement allows re-throwing an original

11803
15:30:52.911 --> 15:30:58.030
error caught by a catch block. Let's see some examples. The throw statement can be

11804
15:30:58.030 --> 15:31:03.149
invoked without parameters within a catch block. In this example, we intentionally

11805
15:31:03.149 --> 15:31:08.143
created an error, dividing the price of the orders by zero. This error makes the

11806
15:31:08.143 --> 15:31:13.200
control pass to the catch block, invoking the throw statement without parameters.

11807
15:31:13.200 --> 15:31:18.468
The throw statement re-throws the original error caused in the try block and stops

11808
15:31:18.468 --> 15:31:23.672
the execution, so this selected statement, this line is executed, isn't executed.

11809
15:31:23.672 --> 15:31:28.876
The re-thrown error has the same error number, severity and state as the original

11810
15:31:28.876 --> 15:31:34.080
one. You have to be careful when using a statement before the throw statement. In

11811
15:31:34.080 --> 15:31:39.413
such a case, this statement must always end with a semicolon. Otherwise, SQL Server

11812
15:31:39.413 --> 15:31:41.340
will have unexpected behavior.

11813
15:31:41.340 --> 15:31:46.602
In this example, the SELECT statement that goes before the THROW statement doesn't

11814
15:31:46.602 --> 15:31:51.736
end with a semicolon. SQL Server thinks that the word THROW is an alias for this

11815
15:31:51.736 --> 15:31:57.063
SELECT statement. So, if we execute this example, we will get the following output.

11816
15:31:57.063 --> 15:32:00.400
We can see that the THROW statement wasn't executed.

11817
15:32:00.400 --> 15:32:05.508
Let's correct this script and add a semicolon after the SELECT statement before the

11818
15:32:05.508 --> 15:32:10.000
THROW statement. If we execute this code, we will see the text, this line

11819
15:32:10.000 --> 15:32:15.016
is executed with no column name, as it doesn't have an alias. Now the throw

11820
15:32:15.016 --> 15:32:20.031
statement is executed, so we get the information about the error. The throw

11821
15:32:20.031 --> 15:32:25.582
statement can also be used with parameters for the error number, message and state.

11822
15:32:25.582 --> 15:32:30.731
This syntax can be included within a catch block or outside of it. Here is an

11823
15:32:30.731 --> 15:32:36.282
example of the use of the throw statement. The first parameter, 52000, is the error

11824
15:32:36.282 --> 15:32:36.750
number.

11825
15:32:36.750 --> 15:32:42.364
This number can be greater than or equal to 50,000. The second parameter, this is an

11826
15:32:42.364 --> 15:32:47.845
example, is the message. Unlike the eraseError statement, the throw statement does

11827
15:32:47.845 --> 15:32:53.460
not allow the inclusion of parameter placeholders such as %d or %s. However, we will

11828
15:32:53.460 --> 15:32:58.940
learn how to do something similar in the next lesson. Finally, the third parameter

11829
15:32:58.940 --> 15:33:04.020
is the state, one in this example. This code generates the following output.

11830
15:33:04.020 --> 15:33:09.089
The throw statement doesn't allow the specification of the severity. SQL Server

11831
15:33:09.089 --> 15:33:14.350
always sets it to 16. You can include the throw statement with parameters within a

11832
15:33:14.350 --> 15:33:19.290
try block. In this example, as the recent any staff whose ID is 15, the throw

11833
15:33:19.290 --> 15:33:24.616
statement with parameters is invoked. As SQL Server always sets the severity of the

11834
15:33:24.616 --> 15:33:29.556
throw statement to 16, the control passes to the cat block, showing the error

11835
15:33:29.556 --> 15:33:33.470
message. Now, you will practice applying the throw statement.

11836
15:33:33.470 --> 15:33:38.463
In this lesson, we will learn how to customize error messages in the throw

11837
15:33:38.463 --> 15:33:43.928
statement. As we learned in a previous lesson, the throw statement does not allow

11838
15:33:43.928 --> 15:33:49.460
parameter placeholders such as %s or %d as the eraseError statement does. However,

11839
15:33:49.460 --> 15:33:55.128
there are two ways to customize our errors for the throw statement. The first one is

11840
15:33:55.128 --> 15:34:00.188
by using a variable and concatenate strings. The second one is by using the

11841
15:34:00.188 --> 15:34:01.740
formatMessage function.

11842
15:34:01.740 --> 15:34:06.768
Let's see how to use a variable to do it. In this example, we declared two

11843
15:34:06.768 --> 15:34:12.272
variables. Staff ID with a value of 500 and myMessage. With the use of the concat

11844
15:34:12.272 --> 15:34:17.776
function, we concatenate in the second variable, myMessage, the text. There is no

11845
15:34:17.776 --> 15:34:23.415
staff member for ID. With the value of the variable, staff ID, and finally with the

11846
15:34:23.415 --> 15:34:25.250
text, try with another one.

11847
15:34:25.250 --> 15:34:31.175
After that, the code takes, if it exists, any staff member whose ID has the value of

11848
15:34:31.175 --> 15:34:36.960
the variable staff ID. If the recent any, the throw statement is executed, passing

11849
15:34:36.960 --> 15:34:42.885
the variable myMessage as a message parameter. If we execute this code as the recent

11850
15:34:42.885 --> 15:34:48.810
any staff member with that staff ID, we have the following output. There is no staff

11851
15:34:48.810 --> 15:34:50.080
member for ID 500.

11852
15:34:50.080 --> 15:34:55.285
Try with another one. The second way of customizing an error message is by using the

11853
15:34:55.285 --> 15:35:00.428
format message function. This is the syntax. It starts with the word format message

11854
15:35:00.428 --> 15:35:05.633
followed by a message string or a message number. After that, you can optionally add

11855
15:35:05.633 --> 15:35:10.652
arguments to replace the parameter placeholders of the first parameter. Let's see

11856
15:35:10.652 --> 15:35:14.990
how to use the format message function with the message string option.

11857
15:35:14.990 --> 15:35:20.314
In this example, we assigned the result of the formatMessage function to the

11858
15:35:20.314 --> 15:35:26.128
variable myMessage. Note that in the first parameter of the formatMessage function,

11859
15:35:26.128 --> 15:35:32.012
we included the parameter placeholders %d and %s. The value of the variable staff ID

11860
15:35:32.012 --> 15:35:37.685
will replace %d, and the text tried with another one will replace %s. After that,

11861
15:35:37.685 --> 15:35:43.219
the code also checks if there is any staff member whose ID has the value of the

11862
15:35:43.219 --> 15:35:44.480
variable staff ID.

11863
15:35:44.480 --> 15:35:49.864
If the recent any, the throw statement is executed, passing the variable myMessage

11864
15:35:49.864 --> 15:35:55.183
as the message parameter. If we execute this code, as the recent any staff member

11865
15:35:55.183 --> 15:36:00.370
with that staff ID, we have the following output. Now, let's see how to use the

11866
15:36:00.370 --> 15:36:05.558
format message function with a message number. The possible message numbers are

11867
15:36:05.558 --> 15:36:10.548
cataloged in the sys.messages view. It contains a row for each message ID or

11868
15:36:10.548 --> 15:36:13.700
language ID of the error messages in the system.

11869
15:36:13.700 --> 15:36:20.057
We can choose any message ID or add a new message to this view to customize our

11870
15:36:20.057 --> 15:36:26.414
errors. To add a new message to sys.messages, we need to do it by executing the

11871
15:36:26.414 --> 15:36:32.931
spAddMessageStored procedure. The first three parameters of this stored procedure

11872
15:36:32.931 --> 15:36:39.369
are mandatory. The number of the message ID must be greater than 50,000. You can

11873
15:36:39.369 --> 15:36:43.070
optionally specify the language. If you don't,

11874
15:36:43.070 --> 15:36:49.137
it will be the default language of the session. In this course, we are not covering

11875
15:36:49.137 --> 15:36:55.276
the rest of the parameters. Let's add a new message to sys.messages by executing the

11876
15:36:55.276 --> 15:37:00.904
stored procedure spAddMessage. In this example, the message ID is 55,000, the

11877
15:37:00.904 --> 15:37:06.898
severity 16, the message text, there is no staff member for ID %d %s, and finally,

11878
15:37:06.898 --> 15:37:09.310
the language is American English.

11879
15:37:09.310 --> 15:37:15.090
Now that we have the message, we can use it in the format message function, passing

11880
15:37:15.090 --> 15:37:20.521
the number 55000 as the first parameter. In this lesson, you will learn what a

11881
15:37:20.521 --> 15:37:26.370
transaction is and how to combine transactions with error handling. We will practice

11882
15:37:26.370 --> 15:37:32.150
concepts related to transactions with a dataset based on bank account transactions.

11883
15:37:32.150 --> 15:37:37.790
The dataset contains information about customers, accounts, and transactions. So,

11884
15:37:37.790 --> 15:37:43.967
What is a transaction? A transaction is the execution of one or more statements such

11885
15:37:43.967 --> 15:37:49.777
that either all or none of these statements are executed. Let's see an example.

11886
15:37:49.777 --> 15:37:55.587
Imagine we want to transfer $100 from account A to account B. First, we have to

11887
15:37:55.587 --> 15:38:01.470
subtract $100 from account A and after that, add those $100 to account B. If the

11888
15:38:01.470 --> 15:38:06.030
second operation fails, we can't subtract $100 from account A.

11889
15:38:06.030 --> 15:38:11.459
These two operations must behave as an atomic operation. We execute both of them or

11890
15:38:11.459 --> 15:38:16.561
none of them. They need to be in the same transaction. SQL Server defines some

11891
15:38:16.561 --> 15:38:21.990
transaction statements. Begin tran or transaction is used to set the starting point

11892
15:38:21.990 --> 15:38:27.223
of a transaction. You can optionally add a name for the transaction and the with

11893
15:38:27.223 --> 15:38:31.540
mark option. These options won't be covered in this course. Commit

11894
15:38:31.540 --> 15:38:36.225
Optionally followed by tran or transaction marks the end of a successful

11895
15:38:36.225 --> 15:38:41.496
transaction. When executed, the effect of the transaction cannot be reversed. You

11896
15:38:41.496 --> 15:38:46.962
can optionally add the name of the transaction and set to on or off the with delayed

11897
15:38:46.962 --> 15:38:52.298
durability option. These options won't be covered in this course. Rollback tran or

11898
15:38:52.298 --> 15:38:57.699
transaction reverts a transaction to the beginning of it or a save point inside the

11899
15:38:57.699 --> 15:38:58.480
transaction.

11900
15:38:58.480 --> 15:39:04.800
After rollback tran or transaction, you can optionally add the transaction or the

11901
15:39:04.800 --> 15:39:11.276
save point name. We will define save points in the next lesson. Let's see all these

11902
15:39:11.276 --> 15:39:17.752
statements. In this example, we try to transfer $100 from account 1 with $24,400 to

11903
15:39:17.752 --> 15:39:24.073
account 5 with $35,300. We mark the start of the transaction with begin tran. The

11904
15:39:24.073 --> 15:39:28.130
first operation updates account 1, subtracting $100.

11905
15:39:28.130 --> 15:39:34.262
Then, we register that movement into the transactions table. After that, we add $100

11906
15:39:34.262 --> 15:39:40.175
to account 5 and then register the movement into the transactions table. Finally,

11907
15:39:40.175 --> 15:39:46.233
commit tran confirms the changes. By executing this code, we can see that account 1

11908
15:39:46.233 --> 15:39:52.146
decreases by $100 and account 2 increases by $100. Besides, two rows are inserted

11909
15:39:52.146 --> 15:39:54.190
into the transactions table.

11910
15:39:54.190 --> 15:39:59.346
Let's change the previous example using rollback tran instead of commit tran to

11911
15:39:59.346 --> 15:40:04.632
revert all the changes as if nothing had happened. Accounts 1 and 5 have the same

11912
15:40:04.632 --> 15:40:09.591
balance as they had before the execution of the script. Besides, no rows are

11913
15:40:09.591 --> 15:40:14.877
inserted into the transactions table. Let's surround the previous code with a try

11914
15:40:14.877 --> 15:40:20.163
catch. We start the transaction within the try block. If everything between begin

11915
15:40:20.163 --> 15:40:23.100
tran and commit tran executes without errors,

11916
15:40:23.100 --> 15:40:28.729
the changes will be confirmed. However, if there is an error, the catch block will

11917
15:40:28.729 --> 15:40:34.358
take the control and revert the changes with the rollback run statement. Executing

11918
15:40:34.358 --> 15:40:39.575
this code, as there are no errors, account 1 decreases by $100 and account 2

11919
15:40:39.575 --> 15:40:44.930
increases by $100. Besides, two rows are inserted into the transactions table.

11920
15:40:44.930 --> 15:40:50.257
Let's introduce an error in the previous script inserting $100 into the transactions

11921
15:40:50.257 --> 15:40:55.521
table for account 500, which doesn't exist. If we execute this code as there is one

11922
15:40:55.521 --> 15:41:00.277
error, the catch block will take the control, executing the rollback trance

11923
15:41:00.277 --> 15:41:05.604
statement. All the changes are reverted as if nothing had happened. Accounts 1 and 5

11924
15:41:05.604 --> 15:41:09.980
have the same balance as they had before the execution of the script.

11925
15:41:09.980 --> 15:41:15.867
Finally, let's see what happens if we don't explicitly specify a transaction. SQL

11926
15:41:15.867 --> 15:41:21.464
Server considers each statement as an individual transaction. The three first

11927
15:41:21.464 --> 15:41:27.569
statements are executed correctly, but the last statement fails, and this row is not

11928
15:41:27.569 --> 15:41:33.675
inserted. Account 1 decreases by $100 and account 2 increases by $100. However, only

11929
15:41:33.675 --> 15:41:39.780
one row is inserted into the transactions table, resulting in an inconsistent state.

11930
15:41:39.780 --> 15:41:45.285
In this lesson, you will understand TRAN COUNT and save points. TRAN COUNT returns

11931
15:41:45.285 --> 15:41:50.790
the number of BEGIN TRAN statements that are active in your current connection. It

11932
15:41:50.790 --> 15:41:56.363
returns a value greater than zero if you are in an open transaction and zero if you

11933
15:41:56.363 --> 15:42:01.801
are not. It can be modified by the BEGIN TRAN statement, which increases the TRAN

11934
15:42:01.801 --> 15:42:07.440
COUNT value by one. The COMMIT TRAN statement decreases the TRAN COUNT value by one.

11935
15:42:07.440 --> 15:42:13.028
The rollback tran statement decrements the tran count to zero except if there is a

11936
15:42:13.028 --> 15:42:18.412
save point. Let's see an example of tran count in a nested transaction. In this

11937
15:42:18.412 --> 15:42:24.068
example, we can see a nested transaction. There is a begin tran after another begin

11938
15:42:24.068 --> 15:42:29.656
tran before commit tran and rollback tran. Initially, the tran count value is zero

11939
15:42:29.656 --> 15:42:35.380
as there is no open transaction yet. After that, a begin tran statement is executed.

11940
15:42:35.380 --> 15:42:41.463
Incrementing the tranCount value by one. Then, a delete statement deletes every row

11941
15:42:41.463 --> 15:42:47.472
from the transactions table. After that, another transaction is open, incrementing

11942
15:42:47.472 --> 15:42:53.628
the value of tranCount by one. The next statement deletes every row from the account

11943
15:42:53.628 --> 15:42:59.124
table. Then, a commit tran statement is executed, decrementing the value of

11944
15:42:59.124 --> 15:43:00.370
tranCount by one.

11945
15:43:00.370 --> 15:43:05.846
If the value of tranCount is 1 before executing the commit tran statement, the

11946
15:43:05.846 --> 15:43:11.603
current transaction is closed and the changes are confirmed. However, if its value

11947
15:43:11.603 --> 15:43:17.149
is richer than 1, like in this example where it is 2, the commit tran statement

11948
15:43:17.149 --> 15:43:22.414
decrements tranCount by 1 but doesn't commit the deletion of the table. The

11949
15:43:22.414 --> 15:43:24.380
transaction is still active.

11950
15:43:24.380 --> 15:43:29.895
Finally, rollback tran decreases the value of tranCount to 0 and reverts all the

11951
15:43:29.895 --> 15:43:35.687
uncommitted statements, including the deletion of the account table. Let's check the

11952
15:43:35.687 --> 15:43:41.133
content of transactions and accounts. We see that rows of these tables were not

11953
15:43:41.133 --> 15:43:46.648
deleted. Let's change the previous example using commit tran instead of rollback

11954
15:43:46.648 --> 15:43:52.302
tran in the penultimate statement. The last commit tran statement will be executed

11955
15:43:52.302 --> 15:43:54.370
when the tranCount value is 1.

11956
15:43:54.370 --> 15:43:59.186
So it will commit the deletion of the tables. If we check the content of

11957
15:43:59.186 --> 15:44:04.805
transactions and accounts, we can see that the rows of these tables were deleted. We

11958
15:44:04.805 --> 15:44:10.290
can use TRANCOUNT in a try-cut construct to determine whether any transactions are

11959
15:44:10.290 --> 15:44:15.708
still open, by checking if the TRANCOUNT value is greater than zero. If there are

11960
15:44:15.708 --> 15:44:20.992
any open transactions, we can commit or roll back the changes. Let's study save

11961
15:44:20.992 --> 15:44:21.460
points.

11962
15:44:21.460 --> 15:44:27.323
Savepoints are markers we can set within a transaction. They allow us to roll back

11963
15:44:27.323 --> 15:44:32.900
to the different Savepoints we set. Savepoints start with the word Savetran or

11964
15:44:32.900 --> 15:44:38.691
transaction followed by the name. Let's see an example that uses Savepoints. This

11965
15:44:38.691 --> 15:44:44.626
code opens a new transaction and after that marks the Savepoint one. Then, the data

11966
15:44:44.626 --> 15:44:47.200
of a new customer is inserted. Next,

11967
15:44:47.200 --> 15:44:52.730
Another save point, save point 2, is marked and the data of another customer is

11968
15:44:52.730 --> 15:44:58.470
inserted. After that, rollback tran save point 2 rollbacks the changes between the

11969
15:44:58.470 --> 15:45:03.860
declaration of save point 2 and the rollback of save point 2. In other words,

11970
15:45:03.860 --> 15:45:09.600
rollback tran rollbacks the insertion of the customer, Zach Roberts. Rollback tran

11971
15:45:09.600 --> 15:45:15.410
save point 1 rollbacks the changes between save point 1 and save point 2. It is the

11972
15:45:15.410 --> 15:45:16.740
data of Mark Davis.

11973
15:45:16.740 --> 15:45:22.434
Then, a new save point, save point 3, is marked. Then, the code inserts the data of

11974
15:45:22.434 --> 15:45:28.197
another customer. Finally, the commit tran statement is executed. If we execute this

11975
15:45:28.197 --> 15:45:33.891
code, we can see that only the last insert statement took place, because save point

11976
15:45:33.891 --> 15:45:39.311
1 and save point 2 were rolled back. If we select the value of tran count after

11977
15:45:39.311 --> 15:45:44.868
every save point rolled back, we can see that these statements don't decrease the

11978
15:45:44.868 --> 15:45:46.240
value of tran count.

11979
15:45:46.240 --> 15:45:52.142
In this example, we see that the outputs of both are 1. In this lesson, you will

11980
15:45:52.142 --> 15:45:57.675
learn how to control errors in transactions with the use of ExactAboard and

11981
15:45:57.675 --> 15:46:03.061
ExactState. ExactAboard specifies whether the current transaction will be

11982
15:46:03.061 --> 15:46:09.258
automatically rolled back when an error occurs. ExactAboard can be set to ON or OFF.

11983
15:46:09.258 --> 15:46:11.250
OFF is the default setting.

11984
15:46:11.250 --> 15:46:17.053
If an error occurs under this setting, the transaction can automatically be rolled

11985
15:46:17.053 --> 15:46:22.502
back or not, depending on the error. If the transaction isn't rolled back, it

11986
15:46:22.502 --> 15:46:28.021
remains open. Setting exact abort to on ensures that if there is an error, the

11987
15:46:28.021 --> 15:46:33.966
transaction is rolled back and the execution is aborted. Let's see some examples. In

11988
15:46:33.966 --> 15:46:39.839
this example, we set exact abort to off. We insert the data of two customers within

11989
15:46:39.839 --> 15:46:40.830
a transaction.

11990
15:46:40.830 --> 15:46:46.283
Finally, we commit the transaction. If we execute this code, we get an error when

11991
15:46:46.283 --> 15:46:51.735
inserting the data of the second customer. That is because in the customers table

11992
15:46:51.735 --> 15:46:57.322
there is a constraint that checks that every email must be unique and Dylan's email

11993
15:46:57.322 --> 15:47:02.775
is already registered. But if we check the customers table, we see that the first

11994
15:47:02.775 --> 15:47:08.362
insert statement was committed. That's because we didn't roll back anything in case

11995
15:47:08.362 --> 15:47:09.170
of an error.

11996
15:47:09.170 --> 15:47:15.029
Let's change the previous example setting exact abort to on. Now, the second insert

11997
15:47:15.029 --> 15:47:20.676
statement also generates an error. The difference is that with this setting, the

11998
15:47:20.676 --> 15:47:26.535
transaction is rolled back, so the first insert statement doesn't take place. Let's

11999
15:47:26.535 --> 15:47:32.112
study the exact abort behavior with phrase error. In this example, we set exact

12000
15:47:32.112 --> 15:47:37.971
abort to on. After that, we open a transaction and insert the data of two customers

12001
15:47:37.971 --> 15:47:39.100
with new emails.

12002
15:47:39.100 --> 15:47:44.966
Between these insertions, we raise an error using raiseError. Finally, we commit the

12003
15:47:44.966 --> 15:47:50.484
transaction. If we run this code, we can see that raiseError produces an error.

12004
15:47:50.484 --> 15:47:56.141
However, the execution continues and the transaction remains open, so the data of

12005
15:47:56.141 --> 15:48:01.937
the second customer is also inserted. Checking the content of the customer's table,

12006
15:48:01.937 --> 15:48:05.150
we see that both insert statements took place.

12007
15:48:05.150 --> 15:48:11.069
Let's modify the previous example using throw instead of raiseError. This time, we

12008
15:48:11.069 --> 15:48:16.771
also get an error. However, none of the customer's data is inserted because the

12009
15:48:16.771 --> 15:48:22.835
transaction is automatically rolled back. If you remember from the previous chapter,

12010
15:48:22.835 --> 15:48:28.681
Microsoft suggests throw over raiseError, and this is one important reason. Next,

12011
15:48:28.681 --> 15:48:31.280
let's study the exactState function.

12012
15:48:31.280 --> 15:48:36.765
Doesn't take any parameters and returns 0 when there is no open transaction 1 when

12013
15:48:36.765 --> 15:48:42.383
the transaction is open and committable and minus 1 when the transaction is open but

12014
15:48:42.383 --> 15:48:47.533
uncommittable also known as doomed transaction when a transaction is open but

12015
15:48:47.533 --> 15:48:53.151
uncommittable you cannot commit the transaction or rollback to a save point the only

12016
15:48:53.151 --> 15:48:58.703
option you have is to rollback the full transaction you cannot make any changes but

12017
15:48:58.703 --> 15:48:59.840
you can read data

12018
15:48:59.840 --> 15:49:05.857
Let's see some examples. This code sets exacta board to off. Then, a transaction

12019
15:49:05.857 --> 15:49:11.875
starts within the try block and inserts the data of two customers. If everything

12020
15:49:11.875 --> 15:49:17.967
goes fine, the transaction is committed. Otherwise, the catch block takes control

12021
15:49:17.967 --> 15:49:24.060
and rollbacks if exact state is "-1", or commits if exact state is 1. Finally, we

12022
15:49:24.060 --> 15:49:25.940
select the error message.

12023
15:49:25.940 --> 15:49:31.459
Resetting of exact award makes that when the catch block takes the control, the

12024
15:49:31.459 --> 15:49:37.257
transaction state remains open and committable. Therefore, exact state is 1, so the

12025
15:49:37.257 --> 15:49:43.055
transaction is committed. As the only statement to be committed is the insertion of

12026
15:49:43.055 --> 15:49:48.923
the first customer's data, it is inserted. Let's change the previous example setting

12027
15:49:48.923 --> 15:49:50.180
exact award to on.

12028
15:49:50.180 --> 15:49:56.141
This will cause that when the catch block takes the control, the transaction remains

12029
15:49:56.141 --> 15:50:01.818
open but uncommittable, so exact state is minus 1. The only option we have is to

12030
15:50:01.818 --> 15:50:07.637
rollback. If we execute this code, we can see that the first customer's data isn't

12031
15:50:07.637 --> 15:50:13.385
inserted because the transaction was rolled back. Let's play with exact abort. In

12032
15:50:13.385 --> 15:50:18.707
this final chapter, we will study what concurrency is and how it can affect

12033
15:50:18.707 --> 15:50:19.630
transactions.

12034
15:50:19.630 --> 15:50:25.257
Concurrency happens when two or more transactions that read or change shared data

12035
15:50:25.257 --> 15:50:31.023
are executed at the same time. Depending on the purpose of the transactions, we may

12036
15:50:31.023 --> 15:50:36.650
want to isolate our transaction from other transactions. We can achieve different

12037
15:50:36.650 --> 15:50:42.486
levels of isolation using the transaction isolation levels that SQL Server provides.

12038
15:50:42.486 --> 15:50:48.183
These are read committed, which is the default level, read uncommitted, repeatable

12039
15:50:48.183 --> 15:50:48.530
read,

12040
15:50:48.530 --> 15:50:54.568
to realizable, and snapshot. This is the syntax for setting an isolation level. It

12041
15:50:54.568 --> 15:51:00.607
starts with setTransactionIsolationLevel followed by the specific isolation level.

12042
15:51:00.607 --> 15:51:06.571
If we want to know the current transaction isolation level of our session, we can

12043
15:51:06.571 --> 15:51:12.242
select the TransactionIsolationLevel column from sys.dmxsessions. This column

12044
15:51:12.242 --> 15:51:17.470
returns a different value depending on the transaction isolation level.

12045
15:51:17.470 --> 15:51:22.829
In this example, the current isolation level is Read Committed. In this lesson, we

12046
15:51:22.829 --> 15:51:28.318
will explain Read Uncommitted and the rest of the isolation levels will be explained

12047
15:51:28.318 --> 15:51:33.677
in the next lessons. Read Uncommitted is the least restrictive isolation level. It

12048
15:51:33.677 --> 15:51:39.101
allows you to read rows modified by another transaction which hasn't been committed

12049
15:51:39.101 --> 15:51:44.199
or rolled back yet. Under Read Uncommitted, it is possible to encounter dirty,

12050
15:51:44.199 --> 15:51:46.290
non-repeatable or phantom reads.

12051
15:51:46.290 --> 15:51:50.944
These are called concurrency phenomena because they occur when two or more

12052
15:51:50.944 --> 15:51:56.101
transactions are running concurrently, so data that might have been chained by one

12053
15:51:56.101 --> 15:52:00.000
transaction is read by another. To understand what dirty reads

12054
15:52:00.000 --> 15:52:05.580
Let's suppose that account 5 has $35,000. One person starts a transaction,

12055
15:52:05.580 --> 15:52:11.235
transaction 1, and updates the current balance of account 5 to $30,000. But

12056
15:52:11.235 --> 15:52:16.739
transaction 1 doesn't commit or roll back yet. After this update, another

12057
15:52:16.739 --> 15:52:22.846
transaction, transaction 2, selects the current balance of account 5 under return

12058
15:52:22.846 --> 15:52:23.600
committed.

12059
15:52:23.600 --> 15:52:29.570
As this isolation level allows to read uncommitted changes, transaction 2 sees that

12060
15:52:29.570 --> 15:52:35.467
the current balance of account 5 is $30,000, that is, a dirty read. After that, if

12061
15:52:35.467 --> 15:52:41.365
transaction 1 rollbacks, the current balance of account 5 doesn't change. However,

12062
15:52:41.365 --> 15:52:47.118
transaction 2 still thinks that the current balance is $30,000. Read uncommitted

12063
15:52:47.118 --> 15:52:49.420
also allows non-reputable reads.

12064
15:52:49.420 --> 15:52:55.375
Let's suppose that transaction 1 selects the current balance of account 5, which is

12065
15:52:55.375 --> 15:53:00.971
$35,000. After that, transaction 2 updates the current balance of account 5 to

12066
15:53:00.971 --> 15:53:06.997
$30,000 and commits. If transaction 1 wants to select the current balance of account

12067
15:53:06.997 --> 15:53:12.952
5 again, it will output $30,000, resulting in a different value from the first time

12068
15:53:12.952 --> 15:53:16.180
it selected the current balance of account 5.

12069
15:53:16.180 --> 15:53:22.166
This difference can cause problems if we make a business decision based on the first

12070
15:53:22.166 --> 15:53:27.653
result. Finally, let's explain phantom reads. Let's imagine one person starts

12071
15:53:27.653 --> 15:53:33.425
transaction 1 under written committed and selects accounts with a balance between

12072
15:53:33.425 --> 15:53:38.770
$45,000 and $50,000. There is only one account that matches that criterion.

12073
15:53:38.770 --> 15:53:45.288
After some seconds, another person opens transaction 2 and inserts one account which

12074
15:53:45.288 --> 15:53:51.728
fulfills the selection criteria of transaction 1, adding one account with a balance

12075
15:53:51.728 --> 15:53:58.246
of $45,000 and commits. After that, if transaction 1 wants to select accounts with a

12076
15:53:58.246 --> 15:54:04.764
balance between $45,000 and $50,000 again, it will get one additional account . This

12077
15:54:04.764 --> 15:54:07.790
result is different from the first one.

12078
15:54:07.790 --> 15:54:13.418
This difference can also cause problems if we make a business decision based on the

12079
15:54:13.418 --> 15:54:18.842
first result. Let's finish with the pros, cons and when to use read uncommitted.

12080
15:54:18.842 --> 15:54:24.538
Read uncommitted can return the results of a query faster than using other isolation

12081
15:54:24.538 --> 15:54:30.166
levels. As we will see, other isolation levels can block other transactions, so the

12082
15:54:30.166 --> 15:54:34.912
information may not be returned immediately. However, it allows dirty,

12083
15:54:34.912 --> 15:54:37.150
non-repeatable and phantom reads.

12084
15:54:37.150 --> 15:54:42.931
You can use read uncommitted when you don't want to be blocked by other transactions

12085
15:54:42.931 --> 15:54:48.575
but don't mind concurrency phenomena. You can also use it when you explicitly want

12086
15:54:48.575 --> 15:54:54.356
to watch uncommitted data. Let's practice! In this lesson, you will be introduced to

12087
15:54:54.356 --> 15:54:59.725
the read committed and repeatable read isolation levels. Read committed is the

12088
15:54:59.725 --> 15:55:02.340
default isolation level in SQL Server.

12089
15:55:02.340 --> 15:55:07.777
Under this isolation level, you can't read data modified by other transactions that

12090
15:55:07.777 --> 15:55:12.755
hasn't committed or rolled back yet. This is the syntax for setting the read

12091
15:55:12.755 --> 15:55:18.257
committed isolation level. If you remember from the previous lesson, we learned that

12092
15:55:18.257 --> 15:55:22.907
under the written committed isolation level, we could find dirty reads,

12093
15:55:22.907 --> 15:55:27.820
non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads. The read committed isolation level

12094
15:55:27.820 --> 15:55:34.086
is more restrictive than read uncommitted in the sense that read committed prevents

12095
15:55:34.086 --> 15:55:39.975
dirty reads. Let's demonstrate this behavior. Let's suppose that account 5 has

12096
15:55:39.975 --> 15:55:46.241
$35,000. One person starts transaction 1 and updates the current balance of account

12097
15:55:46.241 --> 15:55:52.508
5 to $30,000. But transaction 1 doesn't commit or roll back yet. After this update,

12098
15:55:52.508 --> 15:55:55.150
another transaction, transaction 2,

12099
15:55:55.150 --> 15:56:00.548
selects the current balance of account 5 under the read committed isolation level.

12100
15:56:00.548 --> 15:56:06.011
As this isolation level doesn't allow us to read uncommitted changes, transaction 2

12101
15:56:06.011 --> 15:56:11.474
is blocked and has to wait until transaction 1 commits or rollbacks, preventing the

12102
15:56:11.474 --> 15:56:16.609
dirty read. After that, when transaction 1 commits or rollbacks, transaction 2

12103
15:56:16.609 --> 15:56:19.900
immediately gets the output of the selected query.

12104
15:56:19.900 --> 15:56:25.307
Let's see how read committed behaves when two transactions select data but don't

12105
15:56:25.307 --> 15:56:30.645
change that data. In this example, transaction 1 starts and selects the current

12106
15:56:30.645 --> 15:56:36.187
balance of account 5 but doesn't commit or rollback yet. After that, transaction 2

12107
15:56:36.187 --> 15:56:41.796
selects the current balance of account 5, under the read committed isolation level.

12108
15:56:41.796 --> 15:56:47.135
Since transaction 1 just made a selection without updating or deleting data, it

12109
15:56:47.135 --> 15:56:48.960
didn't block transaction 2.

12110
15:56:48.960 --> 15:56:54.205
Transaction 2 can immediately get the result without waiting. As we have seen,

12111
15:56:54.205 --> 15:56:59.652
ReadCommitted prevents dirty reads. However, it allows non-repeatable and phantom

12112
15:56:59.652 --> 15:57:05.233
reads. You can also be blocked by another transaction. You can use ReadCommitted if

12113
15:57:05.233 --> 15:57:10.882
you want to ensure that you only read committed data, not non-repeatable and phantom

12114
15:57:10.882 --> 15:57:14.580
reads. Let's study the repeatable read isolation level.

12115
15:57:14.580 --> 15:57:19.937
As with read committed, you can't read uncommitted data from other transactions.

12116
15:57:19.937 --> 15:57:24.959
Besides, if a transaction reads some data under this isolation level, other

12117
15:57:24.959 --> 15:57:30.584
transactions cannot modify that data until the repeatable read transaction finishes.

12118
15:57:30.584 --> 15:57:36.143
Let's review the isolation levels we learned previously. Under the read uncommitted

12119
15:57:36.143 --> 15:57:40.830
level, dirty reads, non-repeatable reads and phantom reads can happen.

12120
15:57:40.830 --> 15:57:45.842
The read committed isolation level prevents dirty reads, but still can have

12121
15:57:45.842 --> 15:57:51.323
non-repeatable and phantom reads. With the repeatable read isolation level, we can

12122
15:57:51.323 --> 15:57:56.803
prevent dirty reads as read committed does, but we can also prevent non-repeatable

12123
15:57:56.803 --> 15:58:02.350
reads. Let's demonstrate how this isolation level can prevent non-repeatable reads.

12124
15:58:02.350 --> 15:58:08.369
In this example, transaction 1, under the repeatable rate isolation level, selects

12125
15:58:08.369 --> 15:58:14.168
the current balance of account 5, getting the result. After that, transaction 2

12126
15:58:14.168 --> 15:58:20.040
starts and tries to update the current balance of account 5 to $30,000. However,

12127
15:58:20.040 --> 15:58:26.059
transaction 2 is blocked and has to wait to perform the update until transaction 1

12128
15:58:26.059 --> 15:58:26.720
finishes.

12129
15:58:26.720 --> 15:58:32.442
Now, if transaction 1 fires the same SELECT statement again, it gets the same result

12130
15:58:32.442 --> 15:58:37.484
as the first SELECT statement, without getting a non-repeatable read. When

12131
15:58:37.484 --> 15:58:42.934
transaction 1 finishes, transaction 2 can finally perform the update. As we have

12132
15:58:42.934 --> 15:58:48.452
seen, repeatable read prevents other transactions from modifying the data you are

12133
15:58:48.452 --> 15:58:53.629
reading. This prevention results in not having non-repeatable reads. It also

12134
15:58:53.629 --> 15:58:55.060
prevents dirty reads.

12135
15:58:55.060 --> 15:59:00.542
However, it allows phantom reads. You can be blocked if you are trying to modify the

12136
15:59:00.542 --> 15:59:05.894
data that a repeatable read transaction is reading. You can use repeatable read if

12137
15:59:05.894 --> 15:59:11.376
you want to ensure you only read committed data and don't want other transactions to

12138
15:59:11.376 --> 15:59:16.728
modify what you are reading. You don't care if phantom reads occur. Let's put read

12139
15:59:16.728 --> 15:59:21.818
committed and repeatable read in this lesson. We will discuss the serializable

12140
15:59:21.818 --> 15:59:22.210
level.

12141
15:59:22.210 --> 15:59:27.796
Serializable is the most restrictive isolation level. Here, you can see the syntax

12142
15:59:27.796 --> 15:59:32.905
for setting Serializable. Serializable, similar to repeatable read and read

12143
15:59:32.905 --> 15:59:37.946
committed, prevents dirty reads. As repeatable read, Serializable prevents

12144
15:59:37.946 --> 15:59:43.532
non-repeatable reads and it also prevents fountain reads. We can log records under

12145
15:59:43.532 --> 15:59:48.300
Serializable using a query with a word clause based on an index range.

12146
15:59:48.300 --> 15:59:53.886
This query will lock those records so that no other transaction can insert or change

12147
15:59:53.886 --> 15:59:59.471
this data. However, if the query doesn't have a work clause based on an index range,

12148
15:59:59.471 --> 16:00:04.658
the query will lock the complete table. Let's look at both cases in action and

12149
16:00:04.658 --> 16:00:09.911
demonstrate how the serializable isolation level prevents fountain reads. Let's

12150
16:00:09.911 --> 16:00:12.770
start with a query based on an index range.

12151
16:00:12.770 --> 16:00:18.423
In this example, transaction 1, under the serializable isolation level, selects

12152
16:00:18.423 --> 16:00:23.862
those records from the customers table whose customer ID is between 1 and 3.

12153
16:00:23.862 --> 16:00:29.014
Customer ID is an index, so only these records will be locked. With this

12154
16:00:29.014 --> 16:00:34.882
configuration, no other transaction will be able to modify these records or insert

12155
16:00:34.882 --> 16:00:40.320
new records with a customer ID between 1 and 3 until transaction 1 finishes.

12156
16:00:40.320 --> 16:00:46.115
Note that this query returned only one customer. After this selection, transaction 2

12157
16:00:46.115 --> 16:00:51.772
starts. It tries to insert a new record into the customers table with the customer

12158
16:00:51.772 --> 16:00:57.567
ID equals to 2. As this customer ID is between 1 and 3, matching the requirements of

12159
16:00:57.567 --> 16:01:02.603
the select query of transaction 1, this operation will have to wait until

12160
16:01:02.603 --> 16:01:04.190
transaction 1 finishes.

12161
16:01:04.190 --> 16:01:09.959
If within transaction 1 we select the customers whose customer ID is between 1 and 3

12162
16:01:09.959 --> 16:01:15.590
again, we will get the same result we got the first time we selected the customers

12163
16:01:15.590 --> 16:01:21.290
with a customer ID between 1 and 3. We avoided the phantom record. When transaction

12164
16:01:21.290 --> 16:01:26.578
1 finishes, the insert statement is executed. If transaction 2 had inserted a

12165
16:01:26.578 --> 16:01:29.600
customer with the customer ID equals to 200,

12166
16:01:29.600 --> 16:01:34.845
it would have added the record instantly. That's because the customer ID doesn't

12167
16:01:34.845 --> 16:01:40.221
match the requirements of the select query of transaction 1. Let's show an example

12168
16:01:40.221 --> 16:01:45.466
where there is a query that is not based on an index range. This example shows a

12169
16:01:45.466 --> 16:01:50.908
transaction which selects, under the serializable isolation level, every field from

12170
16:01:50.908 --> 16:01:56.284
the customers table. As it selects without an index range, the complete table will

12171
16:01:56.284 --> 16:01:56.940
be locked.

12172
16:01:56.940 --> 16:02:02.260
After that, transaction2 tries to insert a customer. As the complete table is

12173
16:02:02.260 --> 16:02:08.063
locked, it will have to wait until transaction1 ends. If transaction1 fires the same

12174
16:02:08.063 --> 16:02:13.866
select query again, we will get the same result we got in the first select query. We

12175
16:02:13.866 --> 16:02:19.600
avoided the phantom record. Finally, transaction1 finishes and the insert statement

12176
16:02:19.600 --> 16:02:22.640
is executed. Let's summarize the realizable.

12177
16:02:22.640 --> 16:02:28.261
It prevents dirty, non-reputable, and phantom reads, giving good data consistency.

12178
16:02:28.261 --> 16:02:33.951
By contrast, you can be blocked by a surrealizable transaction. You can use it when

12179
16:02:33.951 --> 16:02:39.641
data consistency is a must. Now, it is your turn to read. In this final lesson, you

12180
16:02:39.641 --> 16:02:45.399
will study the snapshot isolation level, the read committed snapshot option, and the

12181
16:02:45.399 --> 16:02:46.770
with no lock option.

12182
16:02:46.770 --> 16:02:52.306
Under snapshot, every time we modify a row, SQL Server stores the previous version

12183
16:02:52.306 --> 16:02:57.505
of the committed row in a table called tempdb. You can only see the committed

12184
16:02:57.505 --> 16:03:03.041
changes that occurred before the start of the snapshot transaction and the changes

12185
16:03:03.041 --> 16:03:08.172
made by that transaction. Therefore, you can't see any changes made by other

12186
16:03:08.172 --> 16:03:12.020
transactions after the start of the snapshot transaction.

12187
16:03:12.020 --> 16:03:18.128
Snapshot transactions that read data don't block other transactions that write data,

12188
16:03:18.128 --> 16:03:24.091
and transactions that write data don't block snapshot transactions that read data.

12189
16:03:24.091 --> 16:03:30.053
Using Snapshot, we can have update conflicts if two transactions try to update the

12190
16:03:30.053 --> 16:03:35.507
same data at the same time. To use it, you must activate the Allow Snapshot

12191
16:03:35.507 --> 16:03:39.870
Isolation option. Here, you can see the syntax for Snapshot.

12192
16:03:39.870 --> 16:03:45.905
Similar to Surrealizable, Snapshot prevents dirty, non-repeatable and phantom reads,

12193
16:03:45.905 --> 16:03:51.868
with the difference that Snapshot doesn't block transactions as Surrealizable does.

12194
16:03:51.868 --> 16:03:57.759
Let's see how Snapshot behaves. In this example, transaction 1, under the Snapshot

12195
