12268
16:10:24.100 --> 16:10:29.566
Another way to classify triggers is based on their behavior. A trigger can behave

12269
16:10:29.566 --> 16:10:35.032
differently in relation to the statement that fires it, resulting in two types of

12270
16:10:35.032 --> 16:10:40.700
triggers. The first type, an after trigger, is used when you want to execute a piece

12271
16:10:40.700 --> 16:10:46.234
of code after the initial statement that fires the trigger. An example use of this

12272
16:10:46.234 --> 16:10:51.700
type of trigger is to rebuild an index after a large insert of data into a table.

12273
16:10:51.700 --> 16:10:56.895
Another example is using a trigger to send alerts when update statements are run

12274
16:10:56.895 --> 16:11:02.350
against the database. You will see more examples as you progress through the course.

12275
16:11:02.350 --> 16:11:07.740
The second trigger type is the instead of trigger. As its name suggests, an instead

12276
16:11:07.740 --> 16:11:12.870
of trigger will not perform the initial operation, but will execute custom code

12277
16:11:12.870 --> 16:11:13.390
instead.

12278
16:11:13.390 --> 16:11:18.940
Some examples of using this behavior are to prevent inserting data into tables,

12279
16:11:18.940 --> 16:11:24.702
prevent updates or deletions, or prevent tables from being dropped. You can notify

12280
16:11:24.702 --> 16:11:30.603
the database administrator of suspicious behavior while also preventing any changes.

12281
16:11:30.603 --> 16:11:36.154
As with the after triggers, there are more possible use cases than the examples

12282
16:11:36.154 --> 16:11:40.580
provided here. Now, let's see how we actually create a trigger.

12283
16:11:40.580 --> 16:11:45.578
Because a trigger is a SQL Server object, we add a new one by using the create

12284
16:11:45.578 --> 16:11:50.000
statement. In this case, we use the create trigger statement to add a

12285
16:11:50.000 --> 16:11:55.658
new DML trigger. After naming the trigger, we have to link it to an existing object.

12286
16:11:55.658 --> 16:12:00.912
In this example, it's the products table. The next part focuses on setting the

12287
16:12:00.912 --> 16:12:06.503
trigger behavior. We use an after trigger for this example. We also need to specify

12288
16:12:06.503 --> 16:12:11.757
the event that fires the trigger. It can be either an insert, update or delete

12289
16:12:11.757 --> 16:12:17.415
statement. Let's stick with an insert for now. The as keyword marks the beginning of

12290
16:12:17.415 --> 16:12:18.830
our trigger workflow.

12291
16:12:18.830 --> 16:12:24.526
The actions performed by the trigger are mentioned after this keyword. Here, another

12292
16:12:24.526 --> 16:12:30.018
example of DML trigger uses an instead of statement to prevent deletions from the

12293
16:12:30.018 --> 16:12:35.714
table. Any attempt to remove rows from the table will fail due to the use of instead

12294
16:12:35.714 --> 16:12:41.342
of. Understanding the difference between the after and instead of behaviors is very

12295
16:12:41.342 --> 16:12:44.190
important. When the after keyword is used,

12296
16:12:44.190 --> 16:12:49.126
the trigger will perform the defined actions only after the event that fires it

12297
16:12:49.126 --> 16:12:54.374
finishes. When instead of is used, the event that fired the trigger will not run and

12298
16:12:54.374 --> 16:12:59.622
only the trigger actions will be performed. Let's practice what we've learned with a

12299
16:12:59.622 --> 16:13:04.682
couple of examples. Now that you have a basic understanding of what triggers are,

12300
16:13:04.682 --> 16:13:09.493
let's find out more about the reasons for using them. Developers and database

12301
16:13:09.493 --> 16:13:10.430
administrators.

12302
16:13:10.430 --> 16:13:16.402
Can create and use triggers for a multitude of purposes. The main reason for using

12303
16:13:16.402 --> 16:13:22.156
triggers is to initiate actions when manipulating data, inserting, modifying or

12304
16:13:22.156 --> 16:13:28.128
deleting information. Sometimes the manipulation of data needs to be prevented and

12305
16:13:28.128 --> 16:13:33.809
this can also be done with the use of triggers. Another use case often seen in

12306
16:13:33.809 --> 16:13:39.417
practice is using triggers for tracking data changes and even database object

12307
16:13:39.417 --> 16:13:40.000
changes.

12308
16:13:40.000 --> 16:13:45.799
Database admins also use triggers to track user actions and to secure the database

12309
16:13:45.799 --> 16:13:51.386
by protecting it from unwanted changes. As mentioned earlier, it's important to

12310
16:13:51.386 --> 16:13:57.044
understand the difference between the after and instead of trigger types, so you

12311
16:13:57.044 --> 16:14:02.560
know when to use each. From a trigger definition perspective, the two types of

12312
16:14:02.560 --> 16:14:08.006
triggers look almost the same, but in practice, they will have very different

12313
16:14:08.006 --> 16:14:09.420
outcomes when fired.

12314
16:14:09.420 --> 16:14:15.094
The outcome of the trigger execution is highly dependent on the keyword you choose,

12315
16:14:15.094 --> 16:14:20.837
after or instead of. The after trigger adds new statements to the initial one, while

12316
16:14:20.837 --> 16:14:25.896
the instead of trigger prevents the initial statement from executing. This

12317
16:14:25.896 --> 16:14:31.365
difference will influence the use cases for each type of trigger. One example of

12318
16:14:31.365 --> 16:14:36.971
using an after trigger is for a large insert of data into a sales table. Once that

12319
16:14:36.971 --> 16:14:38.270
data gets inserted,

12320
16:14:38.270 --> 16:14:43.426
A cleansing step should run to remove or repair any unwanted information. The

12321
16:14:43.426 --> 16:14:48.917
cleansing step will be started by the trigger. When the cleansing step finishes, a

12322
16:14:48.917 --> 16:14:54.141
report with the results will be generated. This report should be analyzed by a

12323
16:14:54.141 --> 16:14:59.565
database administrator, so the trigger will then send an email to the responsible

12324
16:14:59.565 --> 16:15:02.980
people. This is all part of the trigger definition.

12325
16:15:02.980 --> 16:15:08.337
Now, let's look at an example where using an instead of trigger is appropriate.

12326
16:15:08.337 --> 16:15:13.898
Imagine you have a table containing light bulb information and stock details for a

12327
16:15:13.898 --> 16:15:19.459
sales platform. The power column values have changed for some models and an update

12328
16:15:19.459 --> 16:15:24.748
is initiated to change the information in the table. There are still some bulb

12329
16:15:24.748 --> 16:15:30.037
models in stock that have the old power values, however, and they shouldn't be

12330
16:15:30.037 --> 16:15:30.580
updated.

12331
16:15:30.580 --> 16:15:36.059
An instead of trigger can help you deal with this more complex situation. The

12332
16:15:36.059 --> 16:15:41.751
correct approach is to update the characteristics only for the models that don't

12333
16:15:41.751 --> 16:15:47.514
have the old version in stock. The new models need to be in the table too, but as

12334
16:15:47.514 --> 16:15:53.490
new rows instead of updated ones. The only limit on the use cases of DML triggers is

