12340
16:16:16.073 --> 16:16:17.600
solution in SQL Server.

12341
16:16:17.600 --> 16:16:23.400
In this lesson, we will compare triggers with other objects and take a look at the

12342
16:16:23.400 --> 16:16:28.988
appropriate situations for using them. The first comparison of triggers is with

12343
16:16:28.988 --> 16:16:34.859
regular stored procedures. The trigger is a stored procedure as well, but a special

12344
16:16:34.859 --> 16:16:40.801
kind. What makes it so special? A trigger is fired when a defined database or server

12345
16:16:40.801 --> 16:16:45.540
event occurs. Regular stored procedures run when called explicitly.

12346
16:16:45.540 --> 16:16:51.706
A trigger runs only when the event that fires it is starting. Triggers don't allow

12347
16:16:51.706 --> 16:16:57.873
input parameters or transaction statements, like begin transaction or commit. They

12348
16:16:57.873 --> 16:17:04.189
also cannot return output values. On the other hand, regular stored procedures allow

12349
16:17:04.189 --> 16:17:10.130
input parameters, transaction statements, and the possibility to return output.

12350
16:17:10.130 --> 16:17:16.135
These considerations impact the real-life use cases for triggers and regular stored

12351
16:17:16.135 --> 16:17:22.212
procedures. We use triggers for audit and to enforce integrity in most cases, and we

12352
16:17:22.212 --> 16:17:27.855
use regular stored procedures for more general tasks. The second comparison of

12353
16:17:27.855 --> 16:17:33.498
triggers is with computed columns. Computed columns are a good way to automate

12354
16:17:33.498 --> 16:17:37.260
calculation of the values contained by some columns.

12355
16:17:37.260 --> 16:17:42.845
