12359
16:17:58.986 --> 16:18:03.140
a calculated column, it will be part of the table definition.

12360
16:18:03.140 --> 16:18:08.911
In this example, the column total amount is a computed column. Its value comes from

12361
16:18:08.911 --> 16:18:14.473
the multiplication of the quantity and price columns from the same table. But if

12362
16:18:14.473 --> 16:18:20.035
those two columns were not part of the same table, we wouldn't be able to do the

12363
16:18:20.035 --> 16:18:25.597
calculation using a computed column. In that case, we could replace the computed

12364
16:18:25.597 --> 16:18:28.030
column with its trigger definition.

12365
16:18:28.030 --> 16:18:33.753
Here, the price column is not part of the sales without price table. The trigger

12366
16:18:33.753 --> 16:18:39.620
determines the total amount by using the price column from another table, products

12367
16:18:39.620 --> 16:18:45.630
in this example. Now that you've seen how triggers can substitute for other database

12368
16:18:45.630 --> 16:18:51.568
objects, let's see how to use them in practice. After triggers can be used for both

12369
16:18:51.568 --> 16:18:54.000
DML statements and DDL statements.

12370
16:18:54.000 --> 16:19:00.580
However, this lesson will focus on DML after triggers. An after trigger is used for

12371
16:19:00.580 --> 16:19:06.923
