12409
16:22:06.360 --> 16:22:11.600
deleted table will store the values of the modified rows for update statements.

12410
16:22:11.600 --> 16:22:17.067
Or the values of the removed rows for delete statement the deleted table is empty

12411
16:22:17.067 --> 16:22:22.534
for insert statement the deleted table holds the information we need for the rows

12412
16:22:22.534 --> 16:22:27.934
that will be removed from products we get the relevant information directly from

12413
16:22:27.934 --> 16:22:33.536
this table now let's create the example trigger and see how it performs in contrast

12414
16:22:33.536 --> 16:22:39.070
with after triggers instead of triggers can only be used for dml statement not ddl

12415
16:22:39.070 --> 16:22:44.681
This is because they were designed to work with DML statements . An instead of

12416
16:22:44.681 --> 16:22:50.652
trigger will perform an additional set of actions when fired, in place of the event

12417
16:22:50.652 --> 16:22:56.694
that fired the trigger. That event is not run when using an instead of trigger. This

12418
16:22:56.694 --> 16:23:02.737
is the main difference between the two trigger types. In terms of the DML statements

12419
16:23:02.737 --> 16:23:08.420
that are able to fire an instead of trigger, we have the same list . As before,

12420
16:23:08.420 --> 16:23:13.799
Let's explore the steps we would perform to create an instead-of-trigger. For this

12421
16:23:13.799 --> 16:23:19.310
example, we will use the orders table that holds details of the orders placed by the

12422
16:23:19.310 --> 16:23:24.821
fresh fruit delivery company's customers. Following the same steps for our analysis,

12423
16:23:24.821 --> 16:23:30.332
we know that our target table is the orders table. The trigger we will create should

12424
16:23:30.332 --> 16:23:35.514
prevent updates to existing entries in this table. This will ensure that placed

12425
16:23:35.514 --> 16:23:37.220
orders cannot be modified.

12426
16:23:37.220 --> 16:23:42.585
This is a rule enforced by the company. This means that the trigger will fire as a

12427
16:23:42.585 --> 16:23:47.819
response to update statements. The chosen name for the trigger is Prevent Orders

12428
16:23:47.819 --> 16:23:53.315
Update. Having an informative name is important when creating triggers. It's good if

12429
16:23:53.315 --> 16:23:58.614
the name can set some expectations about what the trigger is intended to achieve.

12430
16:23:58.614 --> 16:24:04.110
Now that we have gathered all the needed details, we can start creating the trigger.

12431
16:24:04.110 --> 16:24:09.601
The trigger definition is quite straightforward. Do not allow row updates on the

12432
16:24:09.601 --> 16:24:15.091
orders table. You should already be familiar with the create trigger syntax from

12433
16:24:15.091 --> 16:24:20.650
past examples. Here we use prevent orders update for the trigger name. As we know

12434
16:24:20.650 --> 16:24:26.416
from the preceding analysis, the trigger will be attached to the orders table and it

12435
16:24:26.416 --> 16:24:29.710
will be executed when update statements are run.

12436
16:24:29.710 --> 16:24:34.976
We also want to inform the end user about the rule we set in place through the

12437
16:24:34.976 --> 16:24:40.444
trigger. So we're going to use the raiseError syntax to throw an error message as

12438
16:24:40.444 --> 16:24:46.047
output. The numbers after the error message represent the severity and the state of

12439
16:24:46.047 --> 16:24:51.650
the thrown error. The severity of the error in this example is 16. This is the most

12440
16:24:51.650 --> 16:24:55.700
common value. It means we are throwing a medium level error.

12441
16:24:55.700 --> 16:25:01.640
The state parameter is used to identify the error statement in the SQL code if it is

12442
16:25:01.640 --> 16:25:07.581
used multiple times. We will be using the value 1 because the error is used only one

12443
16:25:07.581 --> 16:25:13.168
time and can be easily identified in the SQL code. A further explanation of the

12444
16:25:13.168 --> 16:25:17.340
raiseError function is outside of the scope of this course.

12445
16:25:17.340 --> 16:25:22.870
Don't you think it would be a good idea to actually create a trigger and see it in

12446
16:25:22.870 --> 16:25:28.333
practice? So far, we've only talked about DML triggers. But as mentioned earlier,

12447
16:25:28.333 --> 16:25:33.796
triggers also come in data definition language, DDL, flavors. In this lesson, you

12448
16:25:33.796 --> 16:25:39.259
are going to learn how to create DDL triggers and how they are different from DML

12449
16:25:39.259 --> 16:25:44.857
triggers. The simplest way to illustrate the specific properties of DDL triggers is

12450
16:25:44.857 --> 16:25:47.150
to compare them with DML triggers.

12451
16:25:47.150 --> 16:25:54.251
The biggest difference is indicated by the names of the trigger types. DML triggers

12452
16:25:54.251 --> 16:26:01.266
respond to events associated with DML statements . On the other hand, DDL triggers

12453
16:26:01.266 --> 16:26:08.195
respond to events associated with DDL statements . While DML triggers can be used

12454
16:26:08.195 --> 16:26:15.296
with both the after and instead of keywords, the DDL triggers accept only the after

12455
16:26:15.296 --> 16:26:15.980
keyword.

12456
16:26:15.980 --> 16:26:21.505
DML triggers are attached to tables or views, while DDL triggers are created at the

12457
16:26:21.505 --> 16:26:26.963
database or server level. Furthermore, we can make use of the inserted and deleted

12458
16:26:26.963 --> 16:26:32.422
special tables when working with DML triggers, but there are no special tables for

12459
16:26:32.422 --> 16:26:37.947
use with DDL triggers. That said, you can use your own defined tables to keep track

12460
16:26:37.947 --> 16:26:43.538
of changes when using DDL triggers. When dealing with DDL triggers, you'll often see

12461
16:26:43.538 --> 16:26:44.670
syntax like this.

12462
16:26:44.670 --> 16:26:50.119
You might expect the after keyword to come before create table, but the for keyword

12463
16:26:50.119 --> 16:26:55.568
is used instead. That's because for SQL Server triggers, the for and after keywords

12464
16:26:55.568 --> 16:27:01.083
have the same results. To minimize the potential for confusion, people often use the

12465
16:27:01.083 --> 16:27:06.532
for keyword for DDL triggers and the after keyword for DML triggers. We'll stick to

12466
16:27:06.532 --> 16:27:12.047
that convention here, but be aware that both versions are correct and will behave in

12467
16:27:12.047 --> 16:27:12.900
the same way.

12468
16:27:12.900 --> 16:27:18.521
Let's do a quick analysis of an example DDL trigger. We'll create a trigger that

12469
16:27:18.521 --> 16:27:24.352
will log information about table changes in a database. The trigger will be created

12470
16:27:24.352 --> 16:27:30.254
at the database level. The changes will be written to a dedicated log table and will

12471
16:27:30.254 --> 16:27:36.015
include details about the creation, alteration and deletion of tables. The trigger

12472
16:27:36.015 --> 16:27:39.950
name will be track table changes. Creating a DDL trigger

12473
16:27:39.950 --> 16:27:45.496
is not much different from creating a DML trigger. We start with the create trigger

12474
16:27:45.496 --> 16:27:51.108
syntax, only this time we attach the trigger at the database level. As mentioned, we

12475
16:27:51.108 --> 16:27:56.120
use the for keyword for DDL triggers. After the keyword, we include the DDL

12476
16:27:56.120 --> 16:28:01.198
statements that will fire the trigger. Note that although the DDL statements

12477
16:28:01.198 --> 16:28:06.744
included in the trigger definition resemble the actual DDL syntax, an underscore is

12478
16:28:06.744 --> 16:28:09.550
used between the words instead of a space.

12479
16:28:09.550 --> 16:28:14.784
The statement that is going to perform the data insert comes next. This statement

12480
16:28:14.784 --> 16:28:20.148
will log the information from the event data function, as well as details about the

12481
16:28:20.148 --> 16:28:25.576
user performing the change. The event data function actually holds information about

12482
16:28:25.576 --> 16:28:30.552
the event that runs and fires the trigger. Pretty cool, right? The instead of

12483
16:28:30.552 --> 16:28:35.722
statement cannot be used to prevent the triggering action from happening for DDL

12484
16:28:35.722 --> 16:28:39.470
triggers. So are DDL triggers used only as after triggers?

12485
16:28:39.470 --> 16:28:44.838
The answer is no. You can define a trigger to roll back the statements that fired

12486
16:28:44.838 --> 16:28:50.339
it. In this example, we don't want the users to remove tables from the database, so

12487
16:28:50.339 --> 16:28:55.840
we set drop table as the event firing the trigger. When the trigger fires, we throw

12488
16:28:55.840 --> 16:29:01.275
an error, but also roll back the initial operation, so the table deletion does not

12489
16:29:01.275 --> 16:29:06.444
take place. We now have a database trigger that prevents the firing event from

12490
16:29:06.444 --> 16:29:08.300
happening. It's that simple.

12491
16:29:08.300 --> 16:29:14.138
How about we put those examples? In this lesson, you will learn about a special type

12492
16:29:14.138 --> 16:29:19.837
of trigger called the logon trigger. Like other triggers, logon triggers perform a

12493
16:29:19.837 --> 16:29:25.536
set of actions when fired. Their defining characteristic is that they are fired by

12494
16:29:25.536 --> 16:29:31.235
logon events. A logon event occurs when a user logs on and creates a connection to

12495
16:29:31.235 --> 16:29:32.000
SQL Server.

12496
16:29:32.000 --> 16:29:37.418
The trigger is fired after the authentication phase, meaning after the username and

12497
16:29:37.418 --> 16:29:42.248
password are checked, but before the user session is established, when the

12498
16:29:42.248 --> 16:29:47.731
information from SQL Server becomes available for queries. This is important to know

12499
16:29:47.731 --> 16:29:53.149
when using these triggers to audit and control server sessions. A logon trigger can

12500
16:29:53.149 --> 16:29:58.110
only be attached at the server level and the firing event can only be logon.

12501
16:29:58.110 --> 16:30:03.621
When you create a logon trigger, you define the set of actions to be performed by

12502
16:30:03.621 --> 16:30:09.267
the trigger when it's fired. We will just audit the logon sessions in this example,

12503
16:30:09.267 --> 16:30:14.574
and we'll use logon audit as the trigger name. Now let's see how to create the

12504
16:30:14.574 --> 16:30:20.085
trigger. We start with the same syntax we used for the other triggers and pass in

12505
16:30:20.085 --> 16:30:25.595
the name we chose. Logon triggers are attached at the server level, so we use the

12506
16:30:25.595 --> 16:30:26.820
all server syntax.

12507
16:30:26.820 --> 16:30:32.831
When the event that fires the trigger starts, the trigger will be executed under the

12508
16:30:32.831 --> 16:30:38.555
same credentials as the firing event. Regular users don't usually have access to

12509
16:30:38.555 --> 16:30:44.351
sensitive information like logon details, so we instruct the trigger to run under

12510
16:30:44.351 --> 16:30:50.218
the SE account. This is a built-in administrator account that has full permissions

12511
16:30:50.218 --> 16:30:51.220
on the server.

12512
16:30:51.220 --> 16:30:56.846
Running it under this account ensures that the trigger will be able to extract

12513
16:30:56.846 --> 16:31:02.905
information about the logon details. This information will be stored into the server

12514
16:31:02.905 --> 16:31:08.819
logon log table. To summarize, we are creating a trigger called logon audit at the

12515
16:31:08.819 --> 16:31:14.589
server level. To avoid permission issues, the trigger will be executed as the SA

12516
16:31:14.589 --> 16:31:17.330
account with administrator privileges.

12517
16:31:17.330 --> 16:31:22.070
The trigger will be fired for logon events and it will store details about the user

12518
16:31:22.070 --> 16:31:26.752
connecting to SQL Server into the server logon log table. You'll find dealing with

12519
16:31:26.752 --> 16:31:31.549
logon triggers much easier with some hands-on experience. Nothing is perfect in real

12520
16:31:31.549 --> 16:31:36.117
life, including triggers. Let's recap the advantages of triggers and learn about

12521
16:31:36.117 --> 16:31:40.000
their most common limitations. Triggers provide numerous advantages.

12522
16:31:40.000 --> 16:31:45.598
Most common limitation. Triggers provide numerous advantages. As we've seen, they

12523
16:31:45.598 --> 16:31:51.057
can be used successfully for database integrity purposes. Business rules can be

12524
16:31:51.057 --> 16:31:56.516
enforced and stored directly in the database when using triggers. This makes it

12525
16:31:56.516 --> 16:32:02.321
simpler to change or update the applications that are using the database because the

12526
16:32:02.321 --> 16:32:05.500
business logic is kept in the database itself.

12527
16:32:05.500 --> 16:32:10.991
Triggers give you control over which statements are allowed in a database, a good

12528
16:32:10.991 --> 16:32:16.415
feature when permission schemes don't offer you enough flexibility. Triggers can

12529
16:32:16.415 --> 16:32:22.109
help you implement complex business logic fired by a single event, and they are also

12530
16:32:22.109 --> 16:32:27.532
used to audit the database for changes or user activity. Triggers also have some

12531
16:32:27.532 --> 16:32:31.600
disadvantages. First, they are difficult to view and detect.

12532
16:32:31.600 --> 16:32:36.751
It can be hard for an administrator to have a clear overview of the existing

12533
16:32:36.751 --> 16:32:42.038
triggers in a database and their behavior. This means triggers are not easy to

12534
16:32:42.038 --> 16:32:47.664
manage in a centralized manner. Triggers are also invisible to client applications.

12535
16:32:47.664 --> 16:32:53.086
When debugging code, triggers are difficult to trace in most situations. And for

12536
16:32:53.086 --> 16:32:58.306
some triggers, their complex code can make it hard to follow their logic when

12537
16:32:58.306 --> 16:32:59.390
troubleshooting.

12538
16:32:59.390 --> 16:33:04.551
Triggers can also affect server performance when they are overused or poorly

12539
16:33:04.551 --> 16:33:10.119
designed. Before learning some best practices to overcome their limitations, let's

12540
16:33:10.119 --> 16:33:15.619
see how to get a good overview of all your triggers. Triggers can be difficult to

12541
16:33:15.619 --> 16:33:21.323
manage when they are undocumented, when they have complex logic in their design, and

12542
16:33:21.323 --> 16:33:25.940
because they can be created on many levels, server, database, table.

12543
16:33:25.940 --> 16:33:31.988
Luckily, SQL Server offers system views that gather all the information about

12544
16:33:31.988 --> 16:33:38.586
triggers in one place. For example, this is the statement you need to run to get all

12545
16:33:38.586 --> 16:33:45.105
the information about server level triggers. A similar view will get you details on

12546
16:33:45.105 --> 16:33:50.996
both database level triggers and table triggers. The type of trigger can be

12547
16:33:50.996 --> 16:33:54.530
determined from the parent class desk column.

12548
16:33:54.530 --> 16:33:59.790
If you only want to look at a couple of triggers, using the graphical interface of

12549
16:33:59.790 --> 16:34:04.922
SQL Server Management Studio is a good solution. Just right-click on the trigger

12550
16:34:04.922 --> 16:34:10.246
name and script the trigger definition. A smarter approach is needed, however, when

12551
16:34:10.246 --> 16:34:15.506
you want to view the definitions for lots of triggers in the database. If you turn

12552
16:34:15.506 --> 16:34:20.573
your attention to SQL System Views again, you'll find there are several ways to

12553
16:34:20.573 --> 16:34:23.460
extract trigger definitions. SQL System Views

12554
16:34:23.460 --> 16:34:28.770
are like virtual tables in the database, helping you to reach information that

12555
16:34:28.770 --> 16:34:34.421
cannot be reached otherwise. This example is based on the SQL module's system view.

12556
16:34:34.421 --> 16:34:40.140
It extracts the definition of a trigger based on its ID, but you don't actually need

12557
16:34:40.140 --> 16:34:45.791
to know the ID. You can use the function objectID to get the trigger's ID using its

12558
16:34:45.791 --> 16:34:50.080
name. This example returns the definition for only one trigger.

12559
16:34:50.080 --> 16:34:55.729
But the code can be adjusted to output more than one result you can also get the

12560
16:34:55.729 --> 16:35:01.660
definition of a trigger using the object definition function you need to pass in the

12561
16:35:01.660 --> 16:35:07.591
id of the trigger as the function parameter to get the id use the object id function

12562
16:35:07.591 --> 16:35:13.311
first passing it the trigger name the last option you can use is the sp help text

12563
16:35:13.311 --> 16:35:16.700
procedure which uses a parameter called obg name

12564
16:35:16.700 --> 16:35:22.241
You can pass the name of the trigger directly to this parameter to get the trigger

12565
16:35:22.241 --> 16:35:27.781
definition. In practice, the use of the sp-help text procedure is not that common,

12566
16:35:27.781 --> 16:35:33.389
mostly because procedures cannot be used inside of select syntax. Use of the object

12567
16:35:33.389 --> 16:35:38.862
definition function or the sys.SQL modules view is more widespread. Which one you

12568
16:35:38.862 --> 16:35:41.700
choose is a matter of personal preference.

12569
16:35:41.700 --> 16:35:47.221
We've seen a couple of helpful hints on how to discover and analyze existing

12570
16:35:47.221 --> 16:35:52.959
triggers in a database. But the following tips should also always be taken into

12571
16:35:52.959 --> 16:35:58.843
account. Make sure your database design is well documented so that it's clear for

12572
16:35:58.843 --> 16:36:04.872
anybody working with it. Keep your trigger design simple. Avoid complex logic where

12573
16:36:04.872 --> 16:36:09.230
possible. Avoid overusing triggers when they are not needed.

12574
16:36:09.230 --> 16:36:15.396
In this lesson, we are going to see the most popular use cases for after triggers in

12575
16:36:15.396 --> 16:36:21.561
real-world scenarios. A common use for after triggers is to store historical data in

12576
16:36:21.561 --> 16:36:27.653
other tables. In practice, this usually means having a history of changes performed

12577
16:36:27.653 --> 16:36:33.525
on a table. For example, here we have the customers table containing information

12578
16:36:33.525 --> 16:36:35.360
about existing customers.

12579
16:36:35.360 --> 16:36:40.954
The customer's details may change over time and the information in the table will

12580
16:36:40.954 --> 16:36:46.411
need to be updated. It is considered a good practice to keep an overview of the

12581
16:36:46.411 --> 16:36:51.522
changes for the most important tables in your database. To start with, the

12582
16:36:51.522 --> 16:36:57.254
customer's history table holds exactly the same details as customer, but it keeps a

12583
16:36:57.254 --> 16:37:02.642
record of any changes that are made. Suppose the phone number for the customer

12584
16:37:02.642 --> 16:37:04.990
eFruits changes. After the change,

12585
16:37:04.990 --> 16:37:10.459
the tables will hold the following details for this customer. The customer's table

12586
16:37:10.459 --> 16:37:15.795
always shows the current information. The customer's history table shows all the

12587
16:37:15.795 --> 16:37:21.264
changes that have occurred for the customer along with the change date. The result

12588
16:37:21.264 --> 16:37:26.734
you saw previously is obtained using a trigger. The trigger will be fired for both

12589
16:37:26.734 --> 16:37:32.203
insert and update statements. You can include both statements, comma separated, as

12590
16:37:32.203 --> 16:37:33.470
the firing actions.

12591
16:37:33.470 --> 16:37:38.821
This will ensure that when new rows are added to the customer's table, they are

12592
16:37:38.821 --> 16:37:44.376
copied to the customer's history table. And any changes to the details of existing

12593
16:37:44.376 --> 16:37:50.066
customers will also end up in the history table. The insert statements will be taken

12594
16:37:50.066 --> 16:37:55.689
care of using the inserted special table. Another major use of after triggers is to

12595
16:37:55.689 --> 16:38:01.040
audit changes occurring in a database. Auditing means tracking any changes that

12596
16:38:01.040 --> 16:38:03.140
occur within the defined scope.

12597
16:38:03.140 --> 16:38:08.787
In this example, the scope of the audit is comprised of very important tables from

12598
16:38:08.787 --> 16:38:14.366
the database. A trigger will be created on the orders table. It will fire for any

12599
16:38:14.366 --> 16:38:20.013
DML statement. Inside the trigger, we will declare two Boolean variables that will

12600
16:38:20.013 --> 16:38:25.317
check the special tables inserted and deleted. When one of the special tables

12601
16:38:25.317 --> 16:38:29.380
contains data, the associated variable will be set to true.

12602
16:38:29.380 --> 16:38:34.849
The combination of values will tell us if the operation is an insert, update or

12603
16:38:34.849 --> 16:38:40.525
delete. A table called tables audit will be used to track the changes. The trigger

12604
16:38:40.525 --> 16:38:45.993
will insert into that table information about the rows being modified, the user

12605
16:38:45.993 --> 16:38:51.600
making the change and the date and time of the change. A simple and effective use

12606
16:38:51.600 --> 16:38:55.200
case of triggers is to have them send notifications.

12607
16:38:55.200 --> 16:39:00.918
Most of the notifications will be about events happening in the database and will be

12608
16:39:00.918 --> 16:39:06.431
sent to interested users. For example, the sales department must be notified when

12609
16:39:06.431 --> 16:39:11.741
new orders are placed. A trigger attached to the orders table will execute the

12610
16:39:11.741 --> 16:39:17.390
procedure that sends an email when insert statements are executed. Now it's time to

12611
16:39:17.390 --> 16:39:22.768
practice. If after triggers are used mostly for auditing and logs, the story is

12612
16:39:22.768 --> 16:39:25.150
different with instead of triggers.

12613
16:39:25.150 --> 16:39:30.506
We are going to learn the details in this lesson. The use cases for these triggers

12614
16:39:30.506 --> 16:39:35.994
are suggested by their name, instead of. These kinds of triggers focus on preventing

12615
16:39:35.994 --> 16:39:41.481
certain operations from happening in your database. If you can control the permitted

12616
16:39:41.481 --> 16:39:46.837
statements inside your database and set some rules, you can enforce data integrity

12617
16:39:46.837 --> 16:39:52.063
through triggers as well. In this example, updates to the products table are not

12618
16:39:52.063 --> 16:39:54.480
permitted for regular database users.

12619
16:39:54.480 --> 16:39:59.829
When a non-administrator runs an update statement, the trigger will raise an error

12620
16:39:59.829 --> 16:40:05.243
using the raiseError function. An error message will be included to inform the user

12621
16:40:05.243 --> 16:40:10.331
that they aren't allowed to make any changes to this table. The restriction is

12622
16:40:10.331 --> 16:40:15.484
applied because the table contains information about stock. An incorrect update

12623
16:40:15.484 --> 16:40:20.702
statement could wreak havoc by considering the real stock numbers. In the second

12624
16:40:20.702 --> 16:40:24.420
example, the trigger doesn't just raise an error message,

12625
16:40:24.420 --> 16:40:29.165
to prevent the deletion of a customer. An alert destined for the database

12626
16:40:29.165 --> 16:40:34.040
administrator is also sent. When a user tries to remove a customer from the

12627
16:40:34.040 --> 16:40:39.305
customers table, an email will be sent to the database administrator. The removal

12628
16:40:39.305 --> 16:40:44.375
action is, of course, denied with an error message. The body of the message is

12629
16:40:44.375 --> 16:40:49.575
stored in the email body text variable and will include the name of the user who

12630
16:40:49.575 --> 16:40:51.460
attempted to make the change.

12631
16:40:51.460 --> 16:40:57.084
After raising the error, the trigger executes the send notification procedure to

12632
16:40:57.084 --> 16:41:02.849
send the email alert to the database administrator. Instead of triggers should not

12633
16:41:02.849 --> 16:41:08.543
always be considered as objects that deny operations on the database. They can be

12634
16:41:08.543 --> 16:41:14.238
used to decide whether or not some operations should succeed. In this example, we

12635
16:41:14.238 --> 16:41:17.120
create a new trigger on the orders table.

12636
16:41:17.120 --> 16:41:22.585
It makes no sense for an order to be placed if there is insufficient stock of the

12637
16:41:22.585 --> 16:41:28.049
product and instead of trigger can check whether there is sufficient stock for an

12638
16:41:28.049 --> 16:41:33.717
order through an if statement the process used to decide what the trigger will do is

12639
16:41:33.717 --> 16:41:39.249
called conditional logic it gets this name because operations will be performed or

12640
16:41:39.249 --> 16:41:44.376
not based on logical conditions like the if statement in this example the if

12641
16:41:44.376 --> 16:41:46.670
statement checks for the condition

12642
16:41:46.670 --> 16:41:52.266
where the order quantity is higher than the existing stock level. If the condition

12643
16:41:52.266 --> 16:41:57.862
is true, an error will be raised and the order will be denied. If the condition is

12644
16:41:57.862 --> 16:42:03.457
false, indicating that there is sufficient stock, the initial insert operation can

12645
16:42:03.457 --> 16:42:08.507
only be executed if stated explicitly in the trigger code. Here's what the

12646
16:42:08.507 --> 16:42:14.171
conditional logic used in the trigger looks like. First, the insert operation fires

12647
16:42:14.171 --> 16:42:14.990
the trigger.

12648
16:42:14.990 --> 16:42:20.714
The trigger then verifies whether there is sufficient stock of the product included

12649
16:42:20.714 --> 16:42:26.369
in the insert operation. If the condition is true, the order is placed by adding a

12650
16:42:26.369 --> 16:42:31.817
new row in the orders table. If it's false, the trigger throws an error and the

12651
16:42:31.817 --> 16:42:37.541
insert operation is dropped. Ready to practice with some triggers? Now it's time to

12652
16:42:37.541 --> 16:42:40.300
look at some use cases for DDL triggers.

12653
16:42:40.300 --> 16:42:45.567
This lesson will only give you an overview of DDL triggers at the database and

12654
16:42:45.567 --> 16:42:50.970
server level. The topic is more complex than what you will be learning here, but

12655
16:42:50.970 --> 16:42:56.574
this lesson will provide you with the fundamentals to get you started. DDL triggers

12656
16:42:56.574 --> 16:43:02.044
can be created at the database level or at the server level. Where the trigger is

12657
16:43:02.044 --> 16:43:07.311
created will influence the types of statements able to fire it. For example, a

12658
16:43:07.311 --> 16:43:09.810
trigger created at the database level

12659
16:43:09.810 --> 16:43:15.448
can respond to statements related to tables, view interactions and index management,

12660
16:43:15.448 --> 16:43:21.086
as well as more specific statements to do with permissions management or statistics.

12661
16:43:21.086 --> 16:43:26.388
At the server level, we can use triggers for database management and to control

12662
16:43:26.388 --> 16:43:32.026
server permissions and the use of credentials. Don't worry about remembering exactly

12663
16:43:32.026 --> 16:43:37.060
which DDL statements can be used to fire database or server level triggers.

12664
16:43:37.060 --> 16:43:43.051
You can always check the online documentation from Microsoft for a full list of DDL

12665
16:43:43.051 --> 16:43:49.042
events. So what exactly can we do with database level triggers? Well, we can keep a

12666
16:43:49.042 --> 16:43:54.816
trace of any activity happening at the database level. This is called a database

12667
16:43:54.816 --> 16:44:00.591
audit. We are going to use a group event to fire our database audit trigger, DDL

12668
16:44:00.591 --> 16:44:01.890
table view events.

12669
16:44:01.890 --> 16:44:07.305
This DML event includes any operations dealing with tables, views, indexes or

12670
16:44:07.305 --> 16:44:13.002
statistics. The advantage of using a group event is that you can specify a single

12671
16:44:13.002 --> 16:44:18.909
event to cover all the cases that should fire the trigger. In this case, more than a

12672
16:44:18.909 --> 16:44:24.676
dozen statements. We are going to insert details about the operations performed in

12673
16:44:24.676 --> 16:44:27.700
the database into the database audit table.

12674
16:44:27.700 --> 16:44:33.803
The details of the operations will be extracted using the eventData function. This

12675
16:44:33.803 --> 16:44:39.906
function returns information about an operation in XML format. In order to extract

12676
16:44:39.906 --> 16:44:45.861
specific information in clear text, we call the value function. For example, the

12677
16:44:45.861 --> 16:44:52.113
first call of the eventData and value functions will get the event type from the XML

12678
16:44:52.113 --> 16:44:55.760
and convert it to the SQL and Varchart data type.

12679
16:44:55.760 --> 16:45:01.039
The same logic is applied for the other columns. Here's what the database audit

12680
16:45:01.039 --> 16:45:06.251
table looks like after using the trigger. All the user actions are kept in the

12681
16:45:06.251 --> 16:45:11.463
table. Any breaking changes can then be traced back to the responsible person.

12682
16:45:11.463 --> 16:45:16.875
Remember, it is not possible to use instead of for DDL triggers. But that doesn't

12683
16:45:16.875 --> 16:45:21.820
mean you cannot prevent operations from happening when using DDL triggers.

12684
16:45:21.820 --> 16:45:27.955
DDL triggers are used in the real world to prevent database or server changes that

12685
16:45:27.955 --> 16:45:34.164
could lead to data loss. The trigger in this example prevents database deletion. As

12686
16:45:34.164 --> 16:45:40.448
you can see, it uses the rollback statement to prevent drop operations on databases.

12687
16:45:40.448 --> 16:45:46.508
The statement that fired the trigger was prevented without the use of instead of.

12688
16:45:46.508 --> 16:45:51.820
The best way to understand use cases for DDL triggers is by practicing.

12689
16:45:51.820 --> 16:45:57.161
You've learned a lot about creating various types of triggers and had some practice.

12690
16:45:57.161 --> 16:46:02.501
But what happens when you don't want a trigger anymore? Or when you decide to change

12691
16:46:02.501 --> 16:46:07.778
the trigger actions? You'll learn how it's done in this lesson. It's easy to delete

12692
16:46:07.778 --> 16:46:12.928
a trigger in SQL Server. The syntax is straightforward. Drop trigger, followed by

12693
16:46:12.928 --> 16:46:18.141
the trigger name. This syntax is applicable when you're removing triggers attached

12694
16:46:18.141 --> 16:46:20.430
to tables or views. In this example,

12695
16:46:20.430 --> 16:46:25.984
the trigger prevent new discounts will be deleted. If you want to remove a database

12696
16:46:25.984 --> 16:46:31.204
level trigger, you have to make that explicit in the syntax. You will use drop

12697
16:46:31.204 --> 16:46:36.625
trigger with the trigger name as you did before, but this time the syntax will be

12698
16:46:36.625 --> 16:46:42.179
expanded with the on database statement. This will inform SQL Server to look at the

12699
16:46:42.179 --> 16:46:47.533
database level for the trigger to be deleted. In the second example, the trigger

12700
16:46:47.533 --> 16:46:49.340
prevent views modifications

12701
16:46:49.340 --> 16:46:55.040
will be deleted at the database level. A slightly different syntax should be used if

12702
16:46:55.040 --> 16:47:00.672
you want to delete triggers at the server level. As before, you'll use drop trigger

12703
16:47:00.672 --> 16:47:06.304
and the trigger name, but then you'll instruct SQL Server to search for the trigger

12704
16:47:06.304 --> 16:47:11.733
on all server. In this case, the trigger Disallow Linked Servers will be deleted

12705
16:47:11.733 --> 16:47:17.297
from the server. A deleted trigger can never be used again unless you recreate the

12706
16:47:17.297 --> 16:47:17.840
trigger.

12707
16:47:17.840 --> 16:47:23.992
Deleting triggers is okay when you no longer need them, but what if you just want to

12708
16:47:23.992 --> 16:47:30.144
avoid them for a brief period? Luckily, SQL Server offers the possibility to disable

12709
16:47:30.144 --> 16:47:36.002
triggers. A disabled trigger still exists as a SQL Server object, but as long as

12710
16:47:36.002 --> 16:47:41.568
it's disabled, it will not be fired. The syntax to disable a trigger is also

12711
16:47:41.568 --> 16:47:42.740
straightforward.

12712
16:47:42.740 --> 16:47:48.516
But note that when disabling, you need to specify the object the trigger is attached

12713
16:47:48.516 --> 16:47:54.224
to, even if it is a table. To switch off a table level trigger, use disable trigger

12714
16:47:54.224 --> 16:47:59.863
and the trigger name and specify the name of the table, for example, on discounts.

12715
16:47:59.863 --> 16:48:05.158
Replace that with the onDatabase statement for database level triggers or the

12716
16:48:05.158 --> 16:48:09.490
onAllServer statement for triggers created at the server level.

12717
16:48:09.490 --> 16:48:14.842
When you want a disabled trigger to start working again, you simply re-enable it.

12718
16:48:14.842 --> 16:48:20.193
This is accomplished by using the enabled trigger syntax, followed by the trigger

12719
16:48:20.193 --> 16:48:25.743
name and the scope of the trigger. Specify the name of the table or view the trigger

12720
16:48:25.743 --> 16:48:30.831
is attached to or used on database or on all server. Now, all the triggers we

12721
16:48:30.831 --> 16:48:36.249
disabled previously will be working again. There will be situations where you need

12722
16:48:36.249 --> 16:48:37.570
to modify a trigger.

12723
16:48:37.570 --> 16:48:43.180
It might be malfunctioning, or you might want to add new functionality. Whatever the

12724
16:48:43.180 --> 16:48:48.455
reason, at some point you're likely to end up having to change triggers, either

12725
16:48:48.455 --> 16:48:53.798
during development or later. In this example, a simple trigger is created on the

12726
16:48:53.798 --> 16:48:59.274
discounts table to prevent any deletions. You run the code and create the trigger,

12727
16:48:59.274 --> 16:49:04.549
but you notice you forgot to include the word remove between to and data in the

12728
16:49:04.549 --> 16:49:06.820
print statement. To fix the error,

12729
16:49:06.820 --> 16:49:11.972
Simply drop the existing trigger, then fix your code and run it again. This can

12730
16:49:11.972 --> 16:49:17.384
become a hassle in the development phase when you want to test the trigger and make

12731
16:49:17.384 --> 16:49:22.470
changes immediately. To avoid this create and drop flow, you can use the ALTER

12732
16:49:22.470 --> 16:49:27.948
statement to modify the definition of an existing trigger. Simply replace the create

12733
16:49:27.948 --> 16:49:31.730
keyword with ALTER while keeping everything else the same.

12734
16:49:31.730 --> 16:49:36.913
Except for the changes you wish to make, of course. This example shows how you can

12735
16:49:36.913 --> 16:49:42.159
add the word remove to the message the trigger prints using alter. Now it's time to

12736
16:49:42.159 --> 16:49:47.342
practice deleting and alter. As you saw in previous lessons, you can end up having

12737
16:49:47.342 --> 16:49:52.335
lots of triggers in your database. One of the main challenges is the ability to

12738
16:49:52.335 --> 16:49:57.582
manage all these triggers and have a good overview. You will learn how it's done in

12739
16:49:57.582 --> 16:49:58.340
this lesson.

12740
16:49:58.340 --> 16:50:03.612
All the necessary information about existing triggers can be extracted from SQL

12741
16:50:03.612 --> 16:50:08.950
Server system tables or views. These objects are used to store information about

12742
16:50:08.950 --> 16:50:14.489
everything that is happening in SQL Server. We will first turn our attention to the

12743
16:50:14.489 --> 16:50:19.627
sys.triggers view and explore the information it can provide us. Sys.triggers

12744
16:50:19.627 --> 16:50:24.965
contains 13 columns but we will only look at the most important. The name column

12745
16:50:24.965 --> 16:50:26.700
contains the trigger name.

12746
16:50:26.700 --> 16:50:32.311
Given when the trigger is created. The object ID column contains a unique identifier

12747
16:50:32.311 --> 16:50:37.855
for the trigger object. There are a few double columns where one column contains an

12748
16:50:37.855 --> 16:50:43.065
integer code and the other column holds the text explanation. For example, the

12749
16:50:43.065 --> 16:50:48.409
parent class column contains an integer representing the trigger type. If it's a

12750
16:50:48.409 --> 16:50:54.020
table trigger, the value will be 1, and for a database trigger, the value will be 0.

12751
16:50:54.020 --> 16:50:59.621
The column Parent Class Desk gives a textual description of the trigger type. The

12752
16:50:59.621 --> 16:51:05.360
Parent ID column will tell you the ID of the object the trigger is attached to. The

12753
16:51:05.360 --> 16:51:10.961
Create Date column stores the trigger creation date, while the Modify Date column

12754
16:51:10.961 --> 16:51:16.492
tells you when the trigger was last modified. If the trigger is disabled, the Is

12755
16:51:16.492 --> 16:51:21.540
Disabled column will have the value 1, otherwise it will be 0. Similarly,

12756
16:51:21.540 --> 16:51:27.693
The value in the is instead of trigger column will be 1 for instead of and 0 for

12757
16:51:27.693 --> 16:51:30.000
after triggers. The sys.server

12758
16:51:30.000 --> 16:51:35.507
triggers view holds information about server level triggers. It has exactly the same

12759
16:51:35.507 --> 16:51:40.622
structure as sys.triggers and the columns hold similar information. What about

12760
16:51:40.622 --> 16:51:45.736
identifying the events that will fire a trigger? This information is stored in

12761
16:51:45.736 --> 16:51:51.046
sys.trigger events. Among the most useful columns in this view is object ID which

12762
16:51:51.046 --> 16:51:56.357
identifies the trigger. The columns type and type desk will tell you which events

12763
16:51:56.357 --> 16:51:59.570
will fire a trigger. The columns event group type

12764
16:51:59.570 --> 16:52:04.941
and event group type desk will point you to any group events that will fire the

12765
16:52:04.941 --> 16:52:10.652
trigger trigger group events are special events that are used to fire a trigger they

12766
16:52:10.652 --> 16:52:16.362
can contain multiple regular events the advantage is that you do not need to specify

12767
16:52:16.362 --> 16:52:21.869
the events individually for example the group ddl table view events contains more

12768
16:52:21.869 --> 16:52:27.580
than a dozen events related to table and view interaction like create drop and alter

12769
16:52:27.580 --> 16:52:33.176
A similar view can be used to extract information about the server level triggers.

12770
16:52:33.176 --> 16:52:38.839
The view is called sys.ServerTriggerEvents. As mentioned before, you do not need to

12771
16:52:38.839 --> 16:52:44.571
memorize all the existing events that can be used to fire triggers. The full list is

12772
16:52:44.571 --> 16:52:50.303
accessible in the sys.TriggerEventTypes view, which shows the event types as numbers

12773
16:52:50.303 --> 16:52:55.831
and as text. It will also show the parent type when the event is part of a larger

12774
16:52:55.831 --> 16:52:56.240
group.

12775
16:52:56.240 --> 16:53:02.878
In this example, create table, alter table and drop table have the value 1 0 0 1 8

12776
16:53:02.878 --> 16:53:09.677
in the parent type column. In the last row shown here, you can see that this type is

12777
16:53:09.677 --> 16:53:16.395
actually the DDL table events group. In practice, all of this information has to be

12778
16:53:16.395 --> 16:53:18.500
packed into a useful form.

12779
16:53:18.500 --> 16:53:23.687
For example, if you want to see a list of triggers along with their firing events

12780
16:53:23.687 --> 16:53:28.937
and the objects they're attached to, you need to combine information from multiple

12781
16:53:28.937 --> 16:53:33.996
views. You can get the trigger name and type from sys.triggers. If you join the

12782
16:53:33.996 --> 16:53:39.310
output with sys.trigger events based on the object ID, you can determine the firing

12783
16:53:39.310 --> 16:53:44.561
events for each trigger. If you further join the parent ID of the trigger with the

12784
16:53:44.561 --> 16:53:46.290
object ID from sys.objects,

12785
16:53:46.290 --> 16:53:51.405
you can extract the name and type of the object the trigger is attached to. The

12786
16:53:51.405 --> 16:53:56.520
second join is chosen to be a left join, because database level triggers do not

12787
16:53:56.520 --> 16:54:01.894
appear as attached to an object. A left join will still keep the results in case of

12788
16:54:01.894 --> 16:54:07.074
non-matching join parameters. If we'd used an inner join, the results would have

12789
16:54:07.074 --> 16:54:12.188
been filtered only for matching rows, and the database triggers would have been

12790
16:54:12.188 --> 16:54:15.620
filtered out from the list. This is the query result.

12791
16:54:15.620 --> 16:54:20.707
There are no values for attached to and object type for database level triggers,

12792
16:54:20.707 --> 16:54:25.604
because they are not attached to any kind of object. They simply exist on the

12793
16:54:25.604 --> 16:54:30.373
database. This script is just one of many you can use in the real world. An

12794
16:54:30.373 --> 16:54:35.269
essential thing to remember is that the views seen in this lesson are usually

12795
16:54:35.269 --> 16:54:40.102
combined to get more detailed results, not used in isolation. Let's practice

12796
16:54:40.102 --> 16:54:45.380
triggers. In this lesson, you will learn how to troubleshoot and optimize triggers.

12797
16:54:45.380 --> 16:54:50.951
You will learn how to keep a history of trigger runs and how to search for triggers

12798
16:54:50.951 --> 16:54:56.254
causing issues. One important factor when troubleshooting triggers is to have a

12799
16:54:56.254 --> 16:55:01.826
history of their execution. This allows you to associate the timing of trigger runs

12800
16:55:01.826 --> 16:55:07.397
with issues caused by triggers. SQL Server provides information on the execution of

12801
16:55:07.397 --> 16:55:12.499
triggers that are currently stored in memory. The information can be seen in

12802
16:55:12.499 --> 16:55:14.110
sys.dmxec trigger stats.

12803
16:55:14.110 --> 16:55:19.447
Unfortunately, when a trigger is removed from the memory, the information about that

12804
16:55:19.447 --> 16:55:24.402
trigger is removed from the view as well. This leaves you with a major problem

12805
16:55:24.402 --> 16:55:29.548
regarding tracking trigger execution history. There is no other option to see the

12806
16:55:29.548 --> 16:55:33.360
history of trigger runs unless you create a custom solution.

12807
16:55:33.360 --> 16:55:38.687
For example, suppose we want to create a permanent record of executions of the

12808
16:55:38.687 --> 16:55:44.288
trigger prevent orders update, designed to prevent any updates to the order table.

12809
16:55:44.288 --> 16:55:49.751
We can use the trigger audit table to store information on trigger execution. To

12810
16:55:49.751 --> 16:55:54.874
enable this, we will update the trigger definition to specify that, when it

12811
16:55:54.874 --> 16:56:00.474
executes, the trigger name and the current date and time will be inserted into the

12812
16:56:00.474 --> 16:56:01.840
trigger audit table.

12813
16:56:01.840 --> 16:56:07.588
Will enhance the trigger definition with the new code using the ALTER statement as

12814
16:56:07.588 --> 16:56:13.195
shown here. An attempt to update the quantity for the order with number 784 will

12815
16:56:13.195 --> 16:56:18.943
result in the PREVENT ORDERS UPDATE trigger being fired. The trigger will throw an

12816
16:56:18.943 --> 16:56:24.620
error message saying that updates are not permitted on the ORDERS table. However,

12817
16:56:24.620 --> 16:56:29.874
With our change to the trigger definition in place, it will also insert information

12818
16:56:29.874 --> 16:56:35.065
about the trigger execution into the trigger audit table. If we check the contents

12819
16:56:35.065 --> 16:56:40.382
of that table, we will see the information about this trigger execution. We now have

12820
16:56:40.382 --> 16:56:44.940
a permanent record that we can use to track the history of trigger runs.

12821
16:56:44.940 --> 16:56:50.055
A solution like this will allow you to associate the timings of trigger executions

12822
16:56:50.055 --> 16:56:55.232
with issues potentially caused by the trigger in future investigations. If the date

12823
16:56:55.232 --> 16:56:59.910
and time of the issue are similar to the date and time when the trigger was

12824
16:56:59.910 --> 16:57:04.962
executed, you can assume the trigger might be causing the problem and investigate

12825
16:57:04.962 --> 16:57:10.077
further. Sometimes you may have issues with certain tables and suspect triggers to

12826
16:57:10.077 --> 16:57:11.200
be the root cause.

12827
16:57:11.200 --> 16:57:16.508
But how can you investigate them if no documentation is available? In this example,

12828
16:57:16.508 --> 16:57:21.817
we want to find out what triggers are attached to the products table. Starting from

12829
16:57:21.817 --> 16:57:27.125
the table name, we can query the sys.objects view to find the table ID of products.

12830
16:57:27.125 --> 16:57:32.497
We can then use this ID to determine what triggers are attached to the table and get

12831
16:57:32.497 --> 16:57:37.549
some information about them. We enhance the script further by joining the first

12832
16:57:37.549 --> 16:57:39.660
query with the sys.triggers view.

12833
16:57:39.660 --> 16:57:45.011
This will help us to find the triggers attached to the products table. The join is

12834
16:57:45.011 --> 16:57:50.296
made by matching the parent ID column of the trigger with the object ID column of

12835
16:57:50.296 --> 16:57:55.777
the products table. We add the following information to our script. The trigger name

12836
16:57:55.777 --> 16:58:01.258
from the name column, whether it's disabled, the isDisabled column, and whether it's

12837
16:58:01.258 --> 16:58:06.674
