13048
17:13:47.291 --> 17:13:51.584
this one does. I'm pretty confident no single player had a thousand or more

13049
17:13:51.584 --> 17:13:56.163
offensive rebounds in a season. We need to use having because the filter is on a

13050
17:13:56.163 --> 17:14:00.627
grouped numeric aggregate. Can we substitute where for having and use the same

13051
17:14:00.627 --> 17:14:05.435
filter condition? No, merely doing this will raise an error when we run the query. A

13052
17:14:05.435 --> 17:14:09.842
numeric column that is using a having filter condition must be enclosed in an

13053
17:14:09.842 --> 17:14:10.930
aggregate function.

13054
17:14:10.930 --> 17:14:15.735
Also, note the position of having in this query. It's always after group by. When

13055
17:14:15.735 --> 17:14:20.540
applied correctly, as in this example, we enclose the filtering column in the sum

13056
17:14:20.540 --> 17:14:25.464
aggregate function. One team meets that condition, incidentally the team I support,

13057
17:14:25.464 --> 17:14:30.329
go OKC. Let's summarize what we've learned. Do not use having to filter individual

13058
17:14:30.329 --> 17:14:35.015
or ungrouped rows. Use where to filter individual rows and having for a numeric

13059
17:14:35.015 --> 17:14:38.990
filter ungrouped rows. We can only apply having to a numeric column

13060
17:14:38.990 --> 17:14:44.042
in an aggregate function filter if we're creating a query we may want to interrogate

13061
17:14:44.042 --> 17:14:48.914
the data first what does it look like how many columns are there what columns are

13062
17:14:48.914 --> 17:14:53.425
required and what columns can we join on previously we said that any syntax

13063
17:14:53.425 --> 17:14:58.417
processed after select is mainly concerned with actions on the final data extracted

13064
17:14:58.417 --> 17:15:03.409
most of these actions are commonly used to interrogate the data before working on a

13065
17:15:03.409 --> 17:15:03.710
query

13066
17:15:03.710 --> 17:15:08.479
we also said that these processes tend to be quite expensive as they can potentially

13067
17:15:08.479 --> 17:15:13.078
tie up database processing resources and therefore likely to increase the time it

13068
17:15:13.078 --> 17:15:17.790
takes a query to run in this lesson we'll look at some ways to interrogate data and

13069
17:15:17.790 --> 17:15:22.275
the effects these may have on performance one of the first things we learn when

13070
17:15:22.275 --> 17:15:26.987
taking a course in SQL is select star from a table select star returns all rows and

13071
17:15:26.987 --> 17:15:31.699
all columns from the table this is great for data interrogation and getting to know

13072
17:15:31.699 --> 17:15:32.210
your data

13073
17:15:32.210 --> 17:15:37.189
but potentially bad for performance, especially if the table has millions of rows

13074
17:15:37.189 --> 17:15:42.291
and many columns. We could be waiting for a while. Our query should only select the

13075
17:15:42.291 --> 17:15:47.270
columns we need. Another problem with select star it is that in joins, it returns

13076
17:15:47.270 --> 17:15:52.188
duplicates of the joining columns. In this example, the player name column is in

13077
17:15:52.188 --> 17:15:57.351
both the players and player stats tables. We need to explicitly state the columns to

13078
17:15:57.351 --> 17:16:01.470
be returned and from what tables. This is where aliasing is useful.

13079
17:16:01.470 --> 17:16:06.314
Is there a way to restrict the number of rows returned but still provide us enough

13080
17:16:06.314 --> 17:16:11.158
to interrogate the data adequately yes we can use the top function although top is

13081
17:16:11.158 --> 17:16:15.944
one of the last syntaxes to be processed in a query we use it at the beginning of

13082
17:16:15.944 --> 17:16:20.611
the query just after select top can be useful to limit the exact number of rows

13083
17:16:20.611 --> 17:16:25.573
returned or used in conjunction with percent a proportion of the rows to be returned

13084
17:16:25.573 --> 17:16:30.240
because top only processes rows we still need to specify the columns we require

13085
17:16:30.240 --> 17:16:35.589
either using star for all columns or explicitly identifying them after top. Select

13086
17:16:35.589 --> 17:16:40.874
top 5 will return the first 5 rows and select top 1% will return the first 10,000

13087
17:16:40.874 --> 17:16:46.158
rows and a million row table. What if we want to check the last rows of the table

13088
17:16:46.158 --> 17:16:51.507
instead? Top is only for the top of the dataset, right? Technically yes, but there

13089
17:16:51.507 --> 17:16:56.400
is no top or bottom. By using order by and experimenting with ASC and DESC,

13090
17:16:56.400 --> 17:17:01.451
We can view a subset of data sets sorted by any column in the SELECT statement

13091
17:17:01.451 --> 17:17:06.436
ascending or descending. Autobuy is supported by most major database vendors.

13092
17:17:06.436 --> 17:17:11.616
However, when interrogating data by limiting rows returned, be aware that TOP is

13093
17:17:11.616 --> 17:17:16.926
specific to Microsoft SQL Server. In other courses, we may have been introduced to

13094
17:17:16.926 --> 17:17:22.365
LIMIT or ROW NUM. These perform the same function but are specific to PostgreSQL and

13095
17:17:22.365 --> 17:17:23.660
Oracle respectively.

13096
17:17:23.660 --> 17:17:28.391
Also, their position in the query is quite different. Use Autobi with caution. It

13097
17:17:28.391 --> 17:17:32.946
works well for interrogating data but slows performance in a query. Often data

13098
17:17:32.946 --> 17:17:37.561
scientists will only use SQL to extract data from a database and use some other

13099
17:17:37.561 --> 17:17:42.116
program like R or Python to perform analytics on the data output. If a dataset

13100
17:17:42.116 --> 17:17:46.905
requires sorting before analysis, these programs are a lot more efficient at this.

13101
17:17:46.905 --> 17:17:51.753
Unless there's a good reason to sort the data in a query or you want to interrogate

13102
17:17:51.753 --> 17:17:53.330
the data, leave Autobi out.

13103
17:17:53.330 --> 17:17:57.858
A common problem that SQL practitioners come across is duplicate rows and the

13104
17:17:57.858 --> 17:18:02.621
results of a query. Duplicate rows can be the result of a poor database design, a

13105
17:18:02.621 --> 17:18:07.207
poorly designed query, or both. In any case, we may need a mechanism to remove

13106
17:18:07.207 --> 17:18:11.676
duplicates. In this lesson, we'll look at the roles of distinct and union in

13107
17:18:11.676 --> 17:18:16.557
removing duplicates and their potential effects on performance. If we want a simple

13108
17:18:16.557 --> 17:18:20.849
query on the player stats table that returns the name of each player that

13109
17:18:20.849 --> 17:18:23.260
participated in the 2017-2018 NBA season,

13110
17:18:23.260 --> 17:18:27.968
there are 605 rows returned. But there's a problem. Team is also a column in the

13111
17:18:27.968 --> 17:18:32.677
table, and players could have played for multiple teams that season. So a simple

13112
17:18:32.677 --> 17:18:37.621
query produces duplicate player name rows. We can remove duplicate rows by enclosing

13113
17:18:37.621 --> 17:18:42.447
the player name column in the distinct clause after select. But could have we just

13114
17:18:42.447 --> 17:18:47.332
used group by instead? Yes, and there'd be not much difference in the time it takes

13115
17:18:47.332 --> 17:18:52.041
for either query to run. If we want to remove duplicates and return unique rows,

13116
17:18:52.041 --> 17:18:53.100
then use distinct.

13117
17:18:53.100 --> 17:18:57.838
However, if we're going to apply aggregate function, for example we want the count

13118
17:18:57.838 --> 17:19:02.519
of teams each player is played for, we'll need to use group by. Often there is an

13119
17:19:02.519 --> 17:19:07.200
easier way to get the results we want. In this case we wanted to know the players

13120
17:19:07.200 --> 17:19:11.881
that participated in the 2017-2018 NBA season. By looking at the database, we see

13121
17:19:11.881 --> 17:19:16.619
that we could have simply queried the players table, where player name is a unique

13122
17:19:16.619 --> 17:19:21.358
key, so there are no duplicate rows. We can also produce duplicates when we append

13123
17:19:21.358 --> 17:19:22.340
queries together.

13124
17:19:22.340 --> 17:19:27.277
To form one data set. Appending is different from a join because when we append we

13125
17:19:27.277 --> 17:19:32.033
are combining the query rows rather than joining on key columns. Appending rows

13126
17:19:32.033 --> 17:19:37.030
together may create the issue of duplicates as we can see in this example. The rows

13127
17:19:37.030 --> 17:19:42.087
for orange and strawberry duplicate when we append. In SQL we use the union operator

13128
17:19:42.087 --> 17:19:46.964
for appending. Union will do the work of checking and removing duplicate rows for

13129
17:19:46.964 --> 17:19:51.600
us. In some cases we may require the return results to contain duplicate rows

13130
17:19:51.600 --> 17:19:56.471
or we may know that there will be no duplicate rows when we append queries. In this

13131
17:19:56.471 --> 17:20:01.400
case, we can use UnionAll, which returns all rows regardless if there are duplicates

13132
17:20:01.400 --> 17:20:06.036
or not. Interrogating our data before creating your query will give you an idea

13133
17:20:06.036 --> 17:20:10.907
whether Union or UnionAll is the appropriate operator to use for appending queries.

13134
17:20:10.907 --> 17:20:15.601
Using distinct and union in queries to remove duplicate rows should be used with

13135
17:20:15.601 --> 17:20:18.770
caution. Depending on the design of the database, both

13136
17:20:18.770 --> 17:20:23.105
Both may use an internal sort mechanism to order and check for duplicates. This

13137
17:20:23.105 --> 17:20:27.165
sorting has the potential to increase the time it takes for a query to run

13138
17:20:27.165 --> 17:20:31.719
significantly. When using distinct, check if there is an alternative way to achieve

13139
17:20:31.719 --> 17:20:35.998
the results we want. For example, there may be another table with the columnar

13140
17:20:35.998 --> 17:20:40.607
require as a unique key, or use groupby if the query is using an aggregate function.

13141
17:20:40.607 --> 17:20:45.216
When appending data, we need to ask ourselves if duplicates are okay, or whether the

13142
17:20:45.216 --> 17:20:47.740
appending queries will not produce duplicates.

13143
17:20:47.740 --> 17:20:52.110
In these cases, consider using Union All, which does not make use of the internal

13144
17:20:52.110 --> 17:20:56.480
sort. We'll look at this internal sorting and how it affects performance later in

13145
17:20:56.480 --> 17:21:00.743
this course. This is practice managing. Welcome to chapter three. A subquery is

13146
17:21:00.743 --> 17:21:04.951
another query within a query. Subqueries are used to retrieve information from

13147
17:21:04.951 --> 17:21:09.267
another table or query that is separate to the main query. In this lesson, we'll

13148
17:21:09.267 --> 17:21:13.476
examine the different types of subqueries and their potential impacts on query

13149
17:21:13.476 --> 17:21:15.580
performance. So how do subqueries look?

13150
17:21:15.580 --> 17:21:20.401
A subquery generally has its own SELECT statement and returns results just like a

13151
17:21:20.401 --> 17:21:24.983
regular query. However, results from a subquery are used by an outer query. A

13152
17:21:24.983 --> 17:21:29.982
subquery is processed first before returning the results for processing by the outer

13153
17:21:29.982 --> 17:21:34.744
query. Therefore, a query that contains a subquery runs from the inside out. SQL

13154
17:21:34.744 --> 17:21:39.445
practitioners will often indent subqueries to identify them from outer or other

13155
17:21:39.445 --> 17:21:44.147
queries. In the following three examples, the subquery is in red text and outer

13156
17:21:44.147 --> 17:21:44.980
query in blue.

13157
17:21:44.980 --> 17:21:50.046
The most common places to use a subquery are, first with from, the subquery acts as

13158
17:21:50.046 --> 17:21:54.807
a virtual table or data source. In this example, the subquery calculates a new

13159
17:21:54.807 --> 17:21:59.812
column from the orders table, numDays, which is a difference in days from ordering

13160
17:21:59.812 --> 17:22:04.940
to shipping. The new column is then used by the outer query to filter for numDays of

13161
17:22:04.940 --> 17:22:09.884
35 or more. The results of this query show seven customers had to wait 35 days or

13162
17:22:09.884 --> 17:22:12.020
more for their order to be shipped.

13163
17:22:12.020 --> 17:22:16.449
Second, with where, to return a filter condition to the outer query. In this

13164
17:22:16.449 --> 17:22:21.111
example, the subquery returns customer IDs from the orders table where a freight

13165
17:22:21.111 --> 17:22:25.831
weight is greater than 800 kilograms. The customer IDs are then used to filter on

13166
17:22:25.831 --> 17:22:30.260
the customers table in the outer query. The results of this query show three

13167
17:22:30.260 --> 17:22:35.038
customers having made orders with a freight weight of more than 800 kilograms. And

13168
17:22:35.038 --> 17:22:39.467
third, with select, to derive a new column. In this example, the subquery is

13169
17:22:39.467 --> 17:22:41.740
calculating the average freight weight,

13170
17:22:41.740 --> 17:22:46.899
for each customer from the orders table. The new column, AVG Freight, is added to

13171
17:22:46.899 --> 17:22:52.122
the select statement of the outer query. The results return the AVG Freight column

13172
17:22:52.122 --> 17:22:57.409
with the customer ID and company name from the customers table. There are two types

13173
17:22:57.409 --> 17:23:02.760
of subqueries, uncorrelated and correlated. Uncorrelated subqueries do not contain a

13174
17:23:02.760 --> 17:23:08.110
reference to the outer query and therefore can run independently of the outer query.

13175
17:23:08.110 --> 17:23:12.732
On the other hand, correlated subqueries include a reference to the outer query and

13176
17:23:12.732 --> 17:23:17.299
therefore cannot run independently of the outer query. Uncorrelated subqueries are

13177
17:23:17.299 --> 17:23:21.921
commonly found with WHERE and FROM and correlated subqueries with WHERE and SELECT.

13178
17:23:21.921 --> 17:23:26.488
Correlated subqueries can be quite inefficient because the subqueries executes for

13179
17:23:26.488 --> 17:23:31.055
each row in the outer query. Compare this to the uncorrelated subqueries where the

13180
17:23:31.055 --> 17:23:35.287
subqueries executes only once and returns the results to the outer query for

13181
17:23:35.287 --> 17:23:35.900
processing.

13182
17:23:35.900 --> 17:23:40.581
Often the results from a correlated subquery can be replicated using an inner join.

13183
17:23:40.581 --> 17:23:45.150
Because the correlated subquery must execute for each row in the outer query, the

13184
17:23:45.150 --> 17:23:49.719
query may take some time to complete, especially if the outer query contains many

13185
17:23:49.719 --> 17:23:54.400
rows. In this case, consider using an inner join, which only makes one pass through

13186
17:23:54.400 --> 17:23:58.912
the data. In this example, we replace the correlated subquery with an inner join

13187
17:23:58.912 --> 17:24:03.142
between the customers and orders table. Let's practice our understanding of

13188
17:24:03.142 --> 17:24:03.650
subquery.

13189
17:24:03.650 --> 17:24:08.543
A common question that data scientists ask when analyzing SQL databases is whether

13190
17:24:08.543 --> 17:24:13.496
the data in one table is present or absent in a related table. Fortunately, this is

13191
17:24:13.496 --> 17:24:18.449
an easy question to answer using SQL. However, depending on the requirements, there

13192
17:24:18.449 --> 17:24:23.402
may be more than one method, and some methods may be more efficient than others. In

13193
17:24:23.402 --> 17:24:28.116
this lesson, we'll discuss what we mean by is data present or absent in related

13194
17:24:28.116 --> 17:24:32.830
tables, and we'll look at using intersect and accept to help us determine this.

13195
17:24:32.830 --> 17:24:37.621
First, what do we mean by data is present or absent in related tables? We'll use

13196
17:24:37.621 --> 17:24:42.293
Venn diagrams to demonstrate this. Here, the overlapping circles represent the

13197
17:24:42.293 --> 17:24:47.144
outputs of queries on two tables. Where the outputs intersect is where we'll find

13198
17:24:47.144 --> 17:24:52.056
the same data present in both tables. If we take a portion in the left output that

13199
17:24:52.056 --> 17:24:57.027
does not intersect the right output, then we say that the data in the table that we

13200
17:24:57.027 --> 17:25:00.800
query on the left is absent in the table we query on the right.

13201
17:25:00.800 --> 17:25:05.211
Let's apply this concept to our customers orders database. Any customers placing

13202
17:25:05.211 --> 17:25:09.732
orders will have their customer information in the customers table and their order

13203
17:25:09.732 --> 17:25:14.253
information in the orders table. We say these tables are related because they both

13204
17:25:14.253 --> 17:25:18.554
contain customer ID. Customers that have not placed any orders will have their

13205
17:25:18.554 --> 17:25:23.130
customer information in the customers table, but not in the orders table. If we are

13206
17:25:23.130 --> 17:25:26.990
working on a sales project, we may ask who is ordering and who is not.

13207
17:25:26.990 --> 17:25:32.319
We can find out which customers have placed orders by using the intersect operator.

13208
17:25:32.319 --> 17:25:37.584
We select customer ID from the customers table, the left query, and intersect, and

13209
17:25:37.584 --> 17:25:42.721
then select customer ID from the orders table, the right query. To check for the

13210
17:25:42.721 --> 17:25:48.114
absence of customers in the orders table that are present in the customers table, we

13211
17:25:48.114 --> 17:25:52.801
substitute intersect with accept. Intersect and accept are great for data

13212
17:25:52.801 --> 17:25:53.700
interrogation.

13213
17:25:53.700 --> 17:25:58.575
One feature they provide is removing duplicates, meaning that the results will only

13214
17:25:58.575 --> 17:26:03.273
contain unique values of the data that is present or absent. One disadvantage of

13215
17:26:03.273 --> 17:26:07.972
intersect and accept is the number and order of columns in the select statements

13216
17:26:07.972 --> 17:26:12.494
must be the same between queries. We'll discuss this disadvantage in upcoming

13217
17:26:12.494 --> 17:26:17.311
lessons. Let's practice intersect. In this chapter, we've already looked at how we

13218
17:26:17.311 --> 17:26:22.244
can use intersect and accept to determine if data in one table was present or absent

13219
17:26:22.244 --> 17:26:23.360
in a related table.

13220
17:26:23.360 --> 17:26:28.276
In this lesson we'll examine alternative methods, exists, not exists, in and not in.

13221
17:26:28.276 --> 17:26:32.957
Let's start with exists. Exists filters the outer query when there is a match of

13222
17:26:32.957 --> 17:26:37.639
data between the outer query and a subquery. Exists evaluates for either true or

13223
17:26:37.639 --> 17:26:42.320
false on a match, therefore we can specify anything in the select statement of a

13224
17:26:42.320 --> 17:26:47.177
subquery. In this example, if customer ID from the customers table matches customer

13225
17:26:47.177 --> 17:26:48.640
ID from the orders table,

13226
17:26:48.640 --> 17:26:53.135
A 1 is returned, indicating it is true and the customers table is filtered on the

13227
17:26:53.135 --> 17:26:57.520
match. Next, in. In works in a similar way with subqueries. However, we need to

13228
17:26:57.520 --> 17:27:02.181
specify the columns to match on in the where filter condition of the outer query and

13229
17:27:02.181 --> 17:27:06.788
the select statement of the subquery. Here we're looking for a match of customer ID

13230
17:27:06.788 --> 17:27:11.338
from the customers table with customer ID from the subquery, which in this case is

13231
17:27:11.338 --> 17:27:15.612
just the orders table. In here two examples of exists and in, the results are

13232
17:27:15.612 --> 17:27:18.220
identical. Is one method better than the other?

13233
17:27:18.220 --> 17:27:22.966
Well, it depends. The subquery used with exists looks like a correlated subquery,

13234
17:27:22.966 --> 17:27:27.712
which we said previously can be inefficient. Technically, yes, it is a correlated

13235
17:27:27.712 --> 17:27:32.400
subquery. However, exists will stop searching the subquery when the condition is

13236
17:27:32.400 --> 17:27:37.029
true, whereas in with a subquery will collect all the results from the subquery

13237
17:27:37.029 --> 17:27:41.775
before further processing, which is potentially slower. For this reason, consider

13238
17:27:41.775 --> 17:27:46.697
using exists instead of in with a subquery. Not exists and not in are used to do the

13239
17:27:46.697 --> 17:27:48.220
opposite of exists and in.

13240
17:27:48.220 --> 17:27:53.391
In a query substitute exists and in with not exists and not in respectively to check

13241
17:27:53.391 --> 17:27:58.377
for the presence of data on one table that is absent in another this example uses

13242
17:27:58.377 --> 17:28:03.547
not exists to return customer details from the customers table where the customer is

13243
17:28:03.547 --> 17:28:08.657
absent from the orders table and this example uses not in to perform the same check

13244
17:28:08.657 --> 17:28:13.273
here we need to specify the column we want to filter on in the where filter

13245
17:28:13.273 --> 17:28:15.120
condition one major issue with

13246
17:28:15.120 --> 17:28:20.282
using not in is the way it handles null values. If the columns in the subquery being

13247
17:28:20.282 --> 17:28:25.136
evaluated for a non-match contain null values, no results are returned. In this

13248
17:28:25.136 --> 17:28:30.052
example, we want to know which nation capitals from the nations table are absent

13249
17:28:30.052 --> 17:28:34.783
from the nearest pop column in the earthquakes table. Using not in returns no

13250
17:28:34.783 --> 17:28:39.822
results. Does this mean every capital city has been near to a recorded earthquake?

13251
17:28:39.822 --> 17:28:43.570
No, it's because the nearest pop column contains null values.

13252
17:28:43.570 --> 17:28:48.422
To handle null values the subquery needs an IsNotNull filter. Here we filter the

13253
17:28:48.422 --> 17:28:53.212
subquery to remove null values. Because of this issue we should always consider

13254
17:28:53.212 --> 17:28:57.761
using NotExists instead of NotIn with the subquery. An advantage of Exists,

13255
17:28:57.761 --> 17:29:02.794
NotExistsIn and NotIn over other methods is the results can contain any column from

13256
17:29:02.794 --> 17:29:07.646
the outer query and in any order. Compare this to Intersect and Accept where the

13257
17:29:07.646 --> 17:29:12.255
number and order of columns in the SELECT statement must be the same between

13258
17:29:12.255 --> 17:29:12.740
queries.

13259
17:29:12.740 --> 17:29:17.075
As previously discussed, one disadvantage of not in is the way it handles null

13260
17:29:17.075 --> 17:29:21.577
values in the subquery. Let's practice these. Another way to determine if data in

13261
17:29:21.577 --> 17:29:26.024
one table is present or absent in a related table is with joins. In this lesson,

13262
17:29:26.024 --> 17:29:30.470
we'll discuss the inner join and the exclusive left outer join. We'll finish off

13263
17:29:30.470 --> 17:29:35.083
with a review of the different methods presented in this chapter and the advantages

13264
17:29:35.083 --> 17:29:39.530
and disadvantages of each one. When joining queries with an inner join, the left

13265
17:29:39.530 --> 17:29:42.420
query joins the right query through related columns.

13266
17:29:42.420 --> 17:29:46.984
An inner join will match values and the related columns and return the results of

13267
17:29:46.984 --> 17:29:51.716
the match. Therefore, this is a check for the presence of data in related tables. In

13268
17:29:51.716 --> 17:29:56.336
this example, the customers and orders tables both contain the column customer ID.

13269
17:29:56.336 --> 17:30:01.013
This is the column which relates these tables. To check for the presence of data in

13270
17:30:01.013 --> 17:30:05.689
one table that is absent in another table with a join, we can use an exclusive left

13271
17:30:05.689 --> 17:30:10.140
outer join. An inclusive left outer join returns all the rows in the left query

13272
17:30:10.140 --> 17:30:15.007
whereas an exclusive left outer join returns only rows in the left query that are

13273
17:30:15.007 --> 17:30:19.994
not present in the right query. We can demonstrate this concept with Venn diagrams.

13274
17:30:19.994 --> 17:30:24.981
The highlighted areas represent the results of the join between queries. To make an

13275
17:30:24.981 --> 17:30:29.848
exclusive left outer join, we add isNull to a where filter condition of the right

13276
17:30:29.848 --> 17:30:34.775
query to restrict it to rows that do not match. This example returns customer data

13277
17:30:34.775 --> 17:30:38.500
from the customers table that is absent from the orders table.

13278
17:30:38.500 --> 17:30:43.224
To end this chapter, let's review the different methods to determine if data in one

13279
17:30:43.224 --> 17:30:47.891
table is present or absent in a related table and the advantages and disadvantages

13280
17:30:47.891 --> 17:30:52.387
of each one. First, intersect and accept. Intersect checks for the presence and

13281
17:30:52.387 --> 17:30:56.428
accept for the absence. The advantages are that they are great for data

13282
17:30:56.428 --> 17:31:01.096
interrogation and they remove duplicates from the returned results. A disadvantage

13283
17:31:01.096 --> 17:31:05.649
is that the number and order of columns in the select statement must be the same

13284
17:31:05.649 --> 17:31:08.210
between queries. Next, exists and not exists.

13285
17:31:08.210 --> 17:31:10.000
Both of these operators use a subquery

13286
17:31:10.000 --> 17:31:15.076
in the where filter condition. Exist checks for the presence and not exists for the

13287
17:31:15.076 --> 17:31:19.784
absence. One advantage is that the subquery will stop searching as soon as it

13288
17:31:19.784 --> 17:31:24.921
evaluates to true, which is a match for exists and non-match for not exists. Another

13289
17:31:24.921 --> 17:31:29.752
advantage is the results can contain any column from the outer query and in any

13290
17:31:29.752 --> 17:31:34.522
order, which is an advantage over intersect and accept. Restricting results to

13291
17:31:34.522 --> 17:31:39.170
columns from the outer query only is also a disadvantage compared to a join.

13292
17:31:39.170 --> 17:31:43.921
Next in and not in. In checks for the presence and not in for the absence. An

13293
17:31:43.921 --> 17:31:48.918
advantage is the results can contain any column from the outer query. Like exists

13294
17:31:48.918 --> 17:31:54.040
and not exists, this is an advantage over intersect and accept. Like exists and not

13295
17:31:54.040 --> 17:31:59.099
exists, restricting results to columns from the outer query only is a disadvantage

13296
17:31:59.099 --> 17:32:04.158
compared to a join. Another disadvantage is no results returned with not in if the

13297
17:32:04.158 --> 17:32:07.490
column being evaluated in the subquery contains nulls.

13298
17:32:07.490 --> 17:32:12.205
And finally, the inner join exclusive left outer join. Inner join checks for the

13299
17:32:12.205 --> 17:32:16.979
presence and exclusive left outer join for the absence. One advantage of joins is

13300
17:32:16.979 --> 17:32:21.518
that the results can contain any column from all joined queries in any order.

13301
17:32:21.518 --> 17:32:26.410
Compare this to exists, not exists, in and not in, where the results are restricted

13302
17:32:26.410 --> 17:32:31.302
to columns from the outer query only. A possible disadvantage of the exclusive left

13303
17:32:31.302 --> 17:32:35.310
outer join is the requirement to add is null where filter condition.

13304
17:32:35.310 --> 17:32:40.842
In a large complex query, this could be easily missed. Let's practice. In this final

13305
17:32:40.842 --> 17:32:46.111
chapter, we'll introduce some tools and commands available in SQL Server to help

13306
17:32:46.111 --> 17:32:51.380
analyze and tune query performance. Most SQL Server practitioners use SQL Server

13307
17:32:51.380 --> 17:32:56.715
Management Studio, or SSMS for short, for creating and editing queries. The tools

13308
17:32:56.715 --> 17:33:00.930
and commands discussed in this chapter are accessed within SSMS,

13309
17:33:00.930 --> 17:33:05.878
The SSMS graphics are from 2017 version and may vary slightly from other releases.

13310
17:33:05.878 --> 17:33:10.946
Throughout this course we've heard terms like increase the time it takes for a query

13311
17:33:10.946 --> 17:33:15.894
to run. In SQL Server, there are many tools and commands available to quantify and

13312
17:33:15.894 --> 17:33:20.782
measure performance. One way to measure query time is with the command statistics

13313
17:33:20.782 --> 17:33:25.790
time, which reports the milliseconds required to pass, compile and execute a query.

13314
17:33:25.790 --> 17:33:30.999
In SSMS, if settings are set to Results to Grid, the statistics are displayed in the

13315
17:33:30.999 --> 17:33:35.837
Messages tab to the right of the Results tab. The output contains several time

13316
17:33:35.837 --> 17:33:40.984
statistics, however, we'll only discuss CPU time and elapsed time under the heading

13317
17:33:40.984 --> 17:33:45.945
SQL Server Execution Times. CPU time is the actual time taken up by the database

13318
17:33:45.945 --> 17:33:50.969
server processors to process the query, and elapsed time is the total duration of

13319
17:33:50.969 --> 17:33:55.310
the query from execution to returning the complete results back to us.

13320
17:33:55.310 --> 17:34:00.313
Let's look at statistics time and action. In this example, our query returns region,

13321
17:34:00.313 --> 17:34:05.018
country and capital, where the 2017 population of a capital city is more than 1

13322
17:34:05.018 --> 17:34:09.961
million. This query uses two subqueries in a where filter condition, first to check

13323
17:34:09.961 --> 17:34:14.606
for the presence of the capital city in the cities table, and second to return

13324
17:34:14.606 --> 17:34:19.609
cities with populations of more than 1 million. To get the time statistics, we first

13325
17:34:19.609 --> 17:34:24.195
need to run set statistics time on before executing our query. Looking at the

13326
17:34:24.195 --> 17:34:24.850
statistics,

13327
17:34:24.850 --> 17:34:29.758
output, both CPU and elapsed time are close to 400 milliseconds. We can rewrite the

13328
17:34:29.758 --> 17:34:34.608
query and replace the two subqueries in the where filter condition with exists and

13329
17:34:34.608 --> 17:34:39.575
one subquery. Because statistics time is already on, we do not need to rerun it. The

13330
17:34:39.575 --> 17:34:44.365
output shows a significant improvement in time. The database processes spent less

13331
17:34:44.365 --> 17:34:48.623
than one millisecond on the query, and total execution time was only two

13332
17:34:48.623 --> 17:34:52.940
milliseconds. Once we've completed our assessment of the time statistics,

13333
17:34:52.940 --> 17:34:57.813
we turn it off by running set statistics time off. When we compare our two examples,

13334
17:34:57.813 --> 17:35:02.396
the first query was slower than the second because of the two subqueries in the

13335
17:35:02.396 --> 17:35:06.920
where filter condition. In the first query, each subquery must collect all the

13336
17:35:06.920 --> 17:35:11.793
results first before further processing, whereas the second query uses exists, which

13337
17:35:11.793 --> 17:35:15.970
stops searching the subquery when there is a match, much more efficient.

13338
17:35:15.970 --> 17:35:20.665
When analyzing query time statistics, the elapsed time may vary because it is

13339
17:35:20.665 --> 17:35:25.542
sensitive to several factors, including load on the server and network bandwidth

13340
17:35:25.542 --> 17:35:30.602
between the database server and a query editing application like SSMS. The CPU time

13341
17:35:30.602 --> 17:35:35.724
is less sensitive and will generally show little variation. One issue with using CPU

13342
17:35:35.724 --> 17:35:40.479
time is when the server processes are running in parallel. In this case, it is

13343
17:35:40.479 --> 17:35:44.320
possible that CPU time is multiplied by the number of processes

13344
17:35:44.320 --> 17:35:49.063
making it appear significantly higher than the elapsed time, and therefore making it

13345
17:35:49.063 --> 17:35:53.693
less useful for query performance tuning. In most cases, the desired outcome is to

13346
17:35:53.693 --> 17:35:58.436
tune a query to return the results to us as fast as possible. Therefore, the elapsed

13347
17:35:58.436 --> 17:36:03.179
time is the best measure to use. Because of variability, particularly in the elapsed

13348
17:36:03.179 --> 17:36:07.753
time, it's good practice to execute a query multiple times and take an average of

13349
17:36:07.753 --> 17:36:12.383
time measurements. We saw that with statistics time, we could get a measure of the

13350
17:36:12.383 --> 17:36:14.190
total time a query takes to run.

13351
17:36:14.190 --> 17:36:19.073
Another way we can review query performance is by examining the amount of disk or

13352
17:36:19.073 --> 17:36:23.956
memory activity generated by the query. We can do this by using the statistics IO

13353
17:36:23.956 --> 17:36:29.020
command. Before we start, we need a brief understanding of how data is stored in the

13354
17:36:29.020 --> 17:36:33.842
database table. All data, either a memory or on disk is stored in eight kilobyte

13355
17:36:33.842 --> 17:36:38.665
size pages. Depending on the size of each row of data, a single page could store

13356
17:36:38.665 --> 17:36:41.920
many rows or just one value could span multiple pages.

13357
17:36:41.920 --> 17:36:46.941
A page can only belong to one table. If tables are small, each table would require

13358
17:36:46.941 --> 17:36:52.023
its own page. SQL Server will do most of its work from pages cached in memory. If a

13359
17:36:52.023 --> 17:36:56.798
query requests a page that is not in memory, it is read from the disk into the

13360
17:36:56.798 --> 17:37:01.819
memory cache. This is an example of how we might imagine the first four data pages

13361
17:37:01.819 --> 17:37:06.840
of our customer table is stored. Like statistics times, statistics.io is initiated

13362
17:37:06.840 --> 17:37:09.840
before running a query with set statistics.io on.

13363
17:37:09.840 --> 17:37:14.775
The statistics are displayed in SSMS in the messages tab to the right of the results

13364
17:37:14.775 --> 17:37:19.592
tab. All the statistics reported by Statistics.io are useful for query performance

13365
17:37:19.592 --> 17:37:24.469
tuning. However, as an introduction to Statistics.io in this lesson, we'll focus on

13366
17:37:24.469 --> 17:37:29.404
the logical reads measure and how it will be used to tune query performance. Logical

13367
17:37:29.404 --> 17:37:33.869
reads are reported for each table. They are a measure of the number of eight

13368
17:37:33.869 --> 17:37:38.510
kilobyte pages read from memory to process and return the results of our query.

13369
17:37:38.510 --> 17:37:43.339
In general, the more pages that need to be read, the slower our query will run.

13370
17:37:43.339 --> 17:37:48.475
Let's look at an example of the logical reads measure using Statistics.io. Our query

13371
17:37:48.475 --> 17:37:53.365
returns the maximum earthquake magnitude where a country capital was the closest

13372
17:37:53.365 --> 17:37:58.378
city to an earthquake of magnitude 7.5 or more. This query uses three sub queries.

13373
17:37:58.378 --> 17:38:03.146
The first is a correlated sub query in the select statement to get the maximum

13374
17:38:03.146 --> 17:38:04.430
magnitude earthquake.

13375
17:38:04.430 --> 17:38:09.094
The second is another correlated subquery to check for the presence of country

13376
17:38:09.094 --> 17:38:13.879
capitals in the Earthquakes table, and the third subquery applies a filter to be

13377
17:38:13.879 --> 17:38:18.304
passed back to the Nations table. To get the statistics to display, we run

13378
17:38:18.304 --> 17:38:23.088
setStatistics.io on before executing our query. The results are reported against

13379
17:38:23.088 --> 17:38:27.992
each table that participates in the query. The output shows 54 pages read from the

13380
17:38:27.992 --> 17:38:31.460
Earthquakes table and 3 pages read from the Nations table.

13381
17:38:31.460 --> 17:38:36.006
We can rewrite the query this time using an inner join between the Nations and

13382
17:38:36.006 --> 17:38:40.902
Earthquakes tables. Because Statistics.io is already on, we do not need to rerun it.

13383
17:38:40.902 --> 17:38:45.506
The output shows the same amount of page reads from the Nations table, 3, but 3

13384
17:38:45.506 --> 17:38:50.286
times fewer page reads from the Earthquakes table, only 18. Once we have completed

13385
17:38:50.286 --> 17:38:55.065
our assessment of the statistics, we turn it off by running Set Statistics.io off.

13386
17:38:55.065 --> 17:38:59.844
Using an inner join on the Earthquakes table in the second query, the entire table

13387
17:38:59.844 --> 17:39:00.660
was read once.

13388
17:39:00.660 --> 17:39:05.591
When the query was processed, 18 pages. Multiply that by the number of subqueries

13389
17:39:05.591 --> 17:39:10.217
accessing the earthquakes table in the first query and this increases to 54.

13390
17:39:10.217 --> 17:39:15.208
Therefore we may expect the first query to run slower than the second because more

13391
17:39:15.208 --> 17:39:20.139
pages are accessed. We can confirm this by running the statistics time command on

13392
17:39:20.139 --> 17:39:25.131
each query. 29 milliseconds for the first query and 3 milliseconds for the second.

13393
17:39:25.131 --> 17:39:30.183
When it comes to query performance tuning, adding an index to a table can be a game

13394
17:39:30.183 --> 17:39:30.670
changer.

13395
17:39:30.670 --> 17:39:35.328
In this lesson we'll examine a brief overview of indexes and how they can be used to

13396
17:39:35.328 --> 17:39:39.876
improve query performance. An index is a structure added to a table to improve the

13397
17:39:39.876 --> 17:39:44.423
speed of accessing data. Indexes are used to locate data quickly without having to

13398
17:39:44.423 --> 17:39:48.638
scan or search every row in a table. This makes them particularly useful for

13399
17:39:48.638 --> 17:39:53.019
improving performance of queries with filter conditions. Indexes are applied to

13400
17:39:53.019 --> 17:39:57.344
table columns and they can be added at any time. Adding an index to a table is

13401
17:39:57.344 --> 17:39:59.840
typically done by the database administrator.

13402
17:39:59.840 --> 17:40:04.907
SQL Server supports several different index types. The two most common table indexes

13403
17:40:04.907 --> 17:40:09.371
are clustered and non-clustered. A good analogy for a clustered index is a

13404
17:40:09.371 --> 17:40:14.318
dictionary where words are stored alphabetically. With a clustered index, the data

13405
17:40:14.318 --> 17:40:19.324
in the data pages are stored and ordered by the columns with the index. Because the

13406
17:40:19.324 --> 17:40:23.969
data is ordered one way, a table can only have one clustered index. Clustered

13407
17:40:23.969 --> 17:40:29.036
indexes reduce the number of data page reads by a query, which helps speed up search

13408
17:40:29.036 --> 17:40:29.700
operations.

13409
17:40:29.700 --> 17:40:34.465
For a non-clustered index, a good analogy is a textbook with an index at the back.

13410
17:40:34.465 --> 17:40:39.229
Data in the book is unordered and the index at the back indicates the page numbers

13411
17:40:39.229 --> 17:40:43.994
containing a search condition. Like a textbook, a table with a non-clustered index

13412
17:40:43.994 --> 17:40:48.585
allows data to be unordered in the table data pages. Another layer in the index

13413
17:40:48.585 --> 17:40:53.465
structure contains ordered pointers to the data pages. A table can contain more than

13414
17:40:53.465 --> 17:40:54.860
one non-clustered index.

13415
17:40:54.860 --> 17:40:59.435
Non-clustered index is commonly used to improve table insert and update operations.

13416
17:40:59.435 --> 17:41:03.734
Let's look at how a clustered index can improve performance. A clustered index

13417
17:41:03.734 --> 17:41:08.363
creates what is called a B-tree structure on a table. We can think of a B-tree as an

13418
17:41:08.363 --> 17:41:12.993
upside-down tree where the trunk is called the root node, branches the branch nodes,

13419
17:41:12.993 --> 17:41:17.403
and leaves the page nodes. The root node contains ordered pointers to the branch

13420
17:41:17.403 --> 17:41:20.930
nodes, which in turn contain ordered pointers to the page nodes.

13421
17:41:20.930 --> 17:41:25.996
The page node level contains all the 8KB data pages from the table with the data

13422
17:41:25.996 --> 17:41:30.999
physically ordered by the columns with the clustered index. Without a clustered

13423
17:41:30.999 --> 17:41:36.255
index, there is no guarantee the data is ordered in the table data pages. Using our

13424
17:41:36.255 --> 17:41:41.574
customers table as an example, if we queried for all rows where customer ID is equal

13425
17:41:41.574 --> 17:41:46.640
to PARIS, without an index, all pages in the table would be scanned for a match.

13426
17:41:46.640 --> 17:41:52.620
If our customers table has a clustered index on customer ID, then the search would

13427
17:41:52.620 --> 17:41:58.600
enter the B tree at pointers nearest to our match. In this case, between OLDWO and

13428
17:41:58.600 --> 17:42:04.580
WOLZA. The ordered pointers at each branch node would in turn direct the search to

13429
17:42:04.580 --> 17:42:10.705
the page or pages containing the customer ID we're searching for. Let's look at this

13430
17:42:10.705 --> 17:42:14.060
in action. We'll query the player stats table.

13431
17:42:14.060 --> 17:42:18.496
For all rows with a team is Oklahoma City, team code OKC. We'll turn on

13432
17:42:18.496 --> 17:42:23.307
Statistics.io so we can determine the number of page reads used by our query.

13433
17:42:23.307 --> 17:42:27.930
Without an index, the whole table is scanned for rows that meet the filter

13434
17:42:27.930 --> 17:42:33.053
condition. Logical reads indicates 12 pages read. When we add a clustered index to

13435
17:42:33.053 --> 17:42:38.114
the team column, the number of page reads drops to 2. In general, the fewer pages

13436
17:42:38.114 --> 17:42:41.300
read to complete the query, the faster it will run.

13437
17:42:41.300 --> 17:42:46.372
In this final lesson, we'll introduce SQL execution plans and some basic examples of

13438
17:42:46.372 --> 17:42:51.443
what they can tell us. When a query is submitted to a database engine, after passing

13439
17:42:51.443 --> 17:42:56.455
all the syntax, table and data checks, it is passed to an optimization phase, which

13440
17:42:56.455 --> 17:43:01.466
evaluates several execution plans to determine which one will return results at the

13441
17:43:01.466 --> 17:43:06.357
lowest cost. Among other parameters, costs include processor usage, memory usage,

13442
17:43:06.357 --> 17:43:10.100
and data pages read. Once the best execution plan is selected,

13443
17:43:10.100 --> 17:43:14.928
it is passed to the execution engine to process the query. Reading execution plans

13444
17:43:14.928 --> 17:43:19.873
is useful. Among other important information, they can tell us if indexes were used,

13445
17:43:19.873 --> 17:43:24.642
the types of joins used, if and where filter conditions, sorting and aggregations

13446
17:43:24.642 --> 17:43:29.352
occurred, and their relative costs. Any issue with a query should be immediately

13447
17:43:29.352 --> 17:43:34.238
apparent in an execution plan, which makes it an excellent tool for troubleshooting

13448
17:43:34.238 --> 17:43:39.066
query performance. This image shows how we can view an estimated execution plan in

13449
17:43:39.066 --> 17:43:39.360
SSMS.

13450
17:43:39.360 --> 17:43:44.652
We highlight the query and select the display estimated execution plan icon from the

13451
17:43:44.652 --> 17:43:49.880
menu toolbar a graphical execution plan for the query is displayed in the execution

13452
17:43:49.880 --> 17:43:55.172
plan tab each icon in an execution plan represents an operator that you that is used

13453
17:43:55.172 --> 17:44:00.401
to perform a specific task hovering over an operator provides a detailed statistics

13454
17:44:00.401 --> 17:44:05.188
of the task used in the query execution plans are read from right to left as

13455
17:44:05.188 --> 17:44:08.590
indicated by the direction of arrows between operators

13456
17:44:08.590 --> 17:44:13.384
the width of each arrow reflects how much data was passed from one operator to the

13457
17:44:13.384 --> 17:44:17.827
next let's take a look at some information execution plans can provide us an

13458
17:44:17.827 --> 17:44:22.504
execution plan can tell us if an index is accessed while processing the query in

13459
17:44:22.504 --> 17:44:27.298
this example we have two versions of the customers table from the customers orders

13460
17:44:27.298 --> 17:44:32.151
database customers ci has a clustered index on the customer id column and customers

13461
17:44:32.151 --> 17:44:35.600
does not we'll query both tables for a customer id of paris

13462
17:44:35.600 --> 17:44:40.390
the execution plan for the query on the customers table shows a table scan operator

13463
17:44:40.390 --> 17:44:45.008
this operator indicates the entire table was scanned for the filter condition in

13464
17:44:45.008 --> 17:44:49.856
contrast the execution plan for the query on the customer ci table shows a clustered

13465
17:44:49.856 --> 17:44:54.646
index seek operator this operator indicates an index was used to go directly to the

13466
17:44:54.646 --> 17:44:59.206
data pages that meet the filter condition in an earlier chapter we said that we

13467
17:44:59.206 --> 17:45:02.380
could append queries together using union and union all

13468
17:45:02.380 --> 17:45:07.052
We noted that union removes duplicates and unionall returns everything including

13469
17:45:07.052 --> 17:45:11.783
duplicates. To check for and removes duplicates requires a sort operation. We can

13470
17:45:11.783 --> 17:45:16.572
see this by comparing two queries with union and unionall. The execution plan with

13471
17:45:16.572 --> 17:45:21.302
union uses a sort operator internally to check and remove duplicates and unionall

13472
17:45:21.302 --> 17:45:26.208
does not. Be aware of the sort operator and execution plans because it can be costly

13473
17:45:26.208 --> 17:45:31.114
on a query. Examining an execution plan could tell us particular syntax or operators

13474
17:45:31.114 --> 17:45:32.340
that are unnecessary.

13475
17:45:32.340 --> 17:45:36.899
Often queries using different methods to return the same results will produce the

13476
17:45:36.899 --> 17:45:41.401
same execution plan in an earlier chapter we compared in and exists operators in

13477
17:45:41.401 --> 17:45:45.847
this example we have two simple queries one using in the other using exists the

13478
17:45:45.847 --> 17:45:50.575
optimizer has decided to use the same execution plan for both this is not always the

13479
17:45:50.575 --> 17:45:55.302
case and it does not mean we should let the optimizer do the crew performance tuning

13480
17:45:55.302 --> 17:45:59.917
for us if time is important we should always ask ourselves if there is a better or

13481
17:45:59.917 --> 17:46:00.480
faster way

13482
17:46:00.480 --> 17:46:05.074
to return the results we require we'll complete this chapter with some final notes

13483
17:46:05.074 --> 17:46:09.612
on tools and commands that are available with SQL Server to help analyze and tune

13484
17:46:09.612 --> 17:46:13.645
query performance in SQL Server time statistics are commonly reported in

13485
17:46:13.645 --> 17:46:18.127
milliseconds in the examples and exercises presented in this chapter we compared

13486
17:46:18.127 --> 17:46:22.721
queries and said that based on the differences in time one would likely run slower

13487
17:46:22.721 --> 17:46:27.259
or faster than the other in reality the time differences reported in the examples

13488
17:46:27.259 --> 17:46:29.220
and exercises would be unnoticeable

13489
17:46:29.220 --> 17:46:33.879
in the real world it would not be uncommon to work with large complex queries that

13490
17:46:33.879 --> 17:46:38.368
run for 10 minutes one hour or more this is where utilizing time statistics for

13491
17:46:38.368 --> 17:46:42.914
query tuning performance is invaluable this chapter provided a brief overview of

13492
17:46:42.914 --> 17:46:47.573
query statistics indexes and execution plans all of these are advanced topics with

13493
17:46:47.573 --> 17:46:52.289
many entire books and websites devoted to each of these and other query performance

13494
17:46:52.289 --> 17:46:57.062
tuning tools and commands the use of SQL Server tools and commands discussed in this

13495
17:46:57.062 --> 17:46:57.460
chapter

13496
17:46:57.460 --> 17:47:02.065
may require elevated levels of database permissions. We recommend communicating with

13497
17:47:02.065 --> 17:47:06.561
your database administrator about your requirements before starting a project that

13498
17:47:06.561 --> 17:47:11.111
may require analyzing query performance. When trying to tune query performance with

13499
17:47:11.111 --> 17:47:15.661
any of the tools and commands available in SQL Server, don't just rely on one. Each

13500
17:47:15.661 --> 17:47:20.102
one can provide a different insight and often they complement one another. Thanks

13501
17:47:20.102 --> 17:47:24.543
for taking this course. We hope you enjoyed it and we look forward to seeing you.

13502
17:47:24.543 --> 17:47:26.900
Welcome to my intro to MongoDB with Python.

13503
17:47:26.900 --> 17:47:32.057
MongoDB is a tool that helps you explore data without requiring it to have a strict,

13504
17:47:32.057 --> 17:47:36.969
known structure. Because of this, you can handle diverse data together and unify

13505
17:47:36.969 --> 17:47:42.004
analytics. You can also keep improving and fix issues as your requirements evolve.

13506
17:47:42.004 --> 17:47:47.099
Most application programming interfaces, or APIs, on the web today expose a certain

13507
17:47:47.099 --> 17:47:52.134
data format. If you can express your data in this format, then you can get started

13508
17:47:52.134 --> 17:47:54.160
with MongoDB. Here's what I mean.

13509
17:47:54.160 --> 17:48:00.461
JavaScript is the language of web browsers. JavaScript Object Notation, or JSON, is

13510
17:48:00.461 --> 17:48:06.762
a common way that web services and client code pass data. JSON is also the basis of

13511
17:48:06.762 --> 17:48:13.063
MongoDB's data format. So what is JSON? JSON has two collection structures. Objects

13512
17:48:13.063 --> 17:48:19.440
map string keys to values and arrays order values. Values, in turn, are one of a few

13513
17:48:19.440 --> 17:48:21.490
things. Values are strings.

13514
17:48:21.490 --> 17:48:27.817
Numbers, the value true, the value false, the value null, or another object or

13515
17:48:27.817 --> 17:48:34.632
array. That's it. These JSON data types have equivalents in Python. JSON objects are

13516
17:48:34.632 --> 17:48:41.202
like Python dictionaries with string type keys. Arrays are like Python lists. And

13517
17:48:41.202 --> 17:48:48.017
the values I mentioned also map to Python. For example, null in JSON maps to none in

13518
17:48:48.017 --> 17:48:48.990
Python. Now,

13519
17:48:48.990 --> 17:48:54.462
How are these JSON Python types expressed in MongoDB? A database maps names to

13520
17:48:54.462 --> 17:49:00.355
collections. You can access collections by name the same way you would access values

13521
17:49:00.355 --> 17:49:06.247
in a Python dictionary. A collection, in turn, is like a list of dictionaries called

13522
17:49:06.247 --> 17:49:11.719
documents by MongoDB. When a dictionary is a value within a document, that's a

13523
17:49:11.719 --> 17:49:16.700
sub-document. Values in a document can be any of the types I mentioned.

13524
17:49:16.700 --> 17:49:22.278
MongoDB also supports some types native to Python but not to JSON. Two examples are

13525
17:49:22.278 --> 17:49:27.722
dates and regular expressions. Let's make concrete how JSON maps to Python and in

13526
17:49:27.722 --> 17:49:33.233
turn to MongoDB. Here is how I accessed the Nobel Prize API and collected its data

13527
17:49:33.233 --> 17:49:38.811
into a Mongo database for you. First, I import the requests library, which will get

13528
17:49:38.811 --> 17:49:43.650
the data from the API. I also import the MongoClient class from PyMongo.

13529
17:49:43.650 --> 17:49:48.912
PyMongo is the official Python driver for MongoDB. Then I connect to my local

13530
17:49:48.912 --> 17:49:54.243
database server. I say that I want a database with the name Nobel, and MongoDB

13531
17:49:54.243 --> 17:49:59.984
creates it. Finally, I gather JSON responses for the prize and laureate endpoints. I

13532
17:49:59.984 --> 17:50:05.519
insert them into the prizes and laureates collections, which MongoDB also creates

13533
17:50:05.519 --> 17:50:10.030
for me. Now, let's go over how to count documents in a collection.

13534
17:50:10.030 --> 17:50:15.357
And how to find one to inspect. First, a note on accessing databases and collections

13535
17:50:15.357 --> 17:50:20.239
from a client object. One way is square bracket notation, as if a client is a

13536
17:50:20.239 --> 17:50:25.376
dictionary of databases with database names as keys. A database in turn is like a

13537
17:50:25.376 --> 17:50:30.322
dictionary of collections with collection names as keys. Another way to access

13538
17:50:30.322 --> 17:50:35.395
things is dot notation. Databases are attributes of a client and collections are

13539
17:50:35.395 --> 17:50:36.980
attributes of a database.

13540
17:50:36.980 --> 17:50:41.573
To count documents, use the countDocumentsCollection method. Pass a filter document

13541
17:50:41.573 --> 17:50:46.111
to filter to limit what you count. In this case, I want an unfiltered total count,

13542
17:50:46.111 --> 17:50:50.537
so I pass an empty document as the filter. Finally, you can fetch a document and

13543
17:50:50.537 --> 17:50:55.130
infer the schema of the raw JSON data given by the Nobel Prize API. Use the findOne

13544
17:50:55.130 --> 17:50:59.502
method, again with no filter, to grab a document from the collection. Now let's

13545
17:50:59.502 --> 17:51:00.000
practice.

13546
17:51:00.000 --> 17:51:04.878
To grab a document from the collection. Now, let's practice. You'll access databases

13547
17:51:04.878 --> 17:51:09.756
and collections from a connected client. You'll count documents and you'll... In the

13548
17:51:09.756 --> 17:51:14.633
last lesson, we learned about databases, collections, and documents. We also learned

13549
17:51:14.633 --> 17:51:19.395
how data structures in MongoDB relate to those of JSON and Python. In this lesson,

13550
17:51:19.395 --> 17:51:24.215
we will learn how to query a collection to find documents of interest. Here we have

13551
17:51:24.215 --> 17:51:27.060
an example document in the Laureate's collection.

13552
17:51:27.060 --> 17:51:32.112
Finding documents in MongoDB reminds me of the work of this particular laureate. The

13553
17:51:32.112 --> 17:51:36.562
remarkable rays named after him are today called X-rays. To find documents

13554
17:51:36.562 --> 17:51:41.253
satisfying some criteria, we express those criteria as a document. This filter

13555
17:51:41.253 --> 17:51:46.124
document mirrors the structure of documents to match in the collection. Imagine a

13556
17:51:46.124 --> 17:51:50.995
filter document as an X-ray image like the one on the right. This image is one of

13557
17:51:50.995 --> 17:51:53.220
the first X-ray images ever recorded.

13558
17:51:53.220 --> 17:51:58.614
Imagine you use it to filter by eye a collection of color photos of people's hands.

13559
17:51:58.614 --> 17:52:03.943
You might keep ones with five fingers and a ring on the finger. Here's another way

13560
17:52:03.943 --> 17:52:09.402
to think about filter documents. Hold a pair of pants up to a collection of mammals,

13561
17:52:09.402 --> 17:52:14.730
one at a time. The pants fit a part of the structure of human beings, but not that

13562
17:52:14.730 --> 17:52:16.420
of a walrus. Sorry, Wally.

13563
17:52:16.420 --> 17:52:21.982
Filter documents can be small. For example, there are 48 laureate documents with a

13564
17:52:21.982 --> 17:52:27.409
value for the field gender equal to female. We can do the same for other fields,

13565
17:52:27.409 --> 17:52:32.971
like country of death and city of birth. Also, we can merge criteria into a single

13566
17:52:32.971 --> 17:52:38.669
filter document. This filter document will have the same form as matching documents.

13567
17:52:38.669 --> 17:52:44.231
In this case, the filter matches Marie Curie. She discovered the element polonium,

13568
17:52:44.231 --> 17:52:46.130
named after her native land.

13569
17:52:46.130 --> 17:52:51.199
Poland was under Russian rule at the time. She hoped that naming an element after it

13570
17:52:51.199 --> 17:52:56.147
would publicize its lack of independence. Many immigrants won Nobel Prizes, but in

13571
17:52:56.147 --> 17:53:00.974
what proportion? Later exercises in the course will explore this question. We've

13572
17:53:00.974 --> 17:53:05.741
seen filters that match exact values in a document. What about satisfying other

13573
17:53:05.741 --> 17:53:10.628
constraints? Query operators are like different ways to input values on a website

13574
17:53:10.628 --> 17:53:10.930
form.

13575
17:53:10.930 --> 17:53:16.140
Some values you select from options, like countries. Some you select with a range

13576
17:53:16.140 --> 17:53:21.350
slider. All operators on fields wrap around their corresponding values. You might

13577
17:53:21.350 --> 17:53:26.368
operate a drop-down menu to select a country and a slider to pick a value in a

13578
17:53:26.368 --> 17:53:31.449
range. Query operators in MongoDB work the same way. You place an operator in a

13579
17:53:31.449 --> 17:53:35.630
filter document to wrap around a field and its acceptable values.

13580
17:53:35.630 --> 17:53:41.237
For example, let's find documents where the field diedCountry is either France or

13581
17:53:41.237 --> 17:53:46.913
USA. We use the in operator to wrap around acceptable values. Operators in MongoDB

13582
17:53:46.913 --> 17:53:52.589
have a dollar sign prefix. Another example, to find documents where diedCountry is

13583
17:53:52.589 --> 17:53:57.988
not equal to a certain string, we can use the not equal or ne operator. We can

13584
17:53:57.988 --> 17:54:00.480
compose query operators for a field.

13585
17:54:00.480 --> 17:54:05.727
For example, here we query for documents with diedCountry greater than, or GT, the

13586
17:54:05.727 --> 17:54:11.103
string Belgium. At the same time, we query for diedCountry less than or equal to, or

13587
17:54:11.103 --> 17:54:16.286
LTE, the string USA. How rude that MongoDB considers some countries to be greater

13588
17:54:16.286 --> 17:54:21.661
than or less than others. Actually, this highlights MongoDB's loose requirements for

13589
17:54:21.661 --> 17:54:26.652
comparisons. Comparison operators order values in lexicographic order, meaning

13590
17:54:26.652 --> 17:54:29.340
alphabetical order for non-numeric values.

13591
17:54:29.340 --> 17:54:34.313
This behavior is something to keep in mind when working with raw, unprocessed data.

13592
17:54:34.313 --> 17:54:39.106
Let's practice constructing filter documents, including those with query. In the

13593
17:54:39.106 --> 17:54:43.780
last lesson, we learned how to construct and compose filter documents. We also

13594
17:54:43.780 --> 17:54:48.573
learned about query operators to express criteria other than simple equality. In

13595
17:54:48.573 --> 17:54:53.487
this lesson, we are going to learn how to query arrays and sub-documents using dot

13596
17:54:53.487 --> 17:54:58.220
notation. Dot notation is how MongoDB allows us to query document substructure.

13597
17:54:58.220 --> 17:55:03.609
Let's use the find one method to retrieve one of my favorite laureates. Walter Cohn

13598
17:55:03.609 --> 17:55:08.803
co-developed an important technique for computational chemistry. Notice that the

13599
17:55:08.803 --> 17:55:13.868
prizes field is an array. In this case, the array has one element with data on

13600
17:55:13.868 --> 17:55:19.192
Cohn's two-way share of the 1998 prize in chemistry. Note also that a laureate may

13601
17:55:19.192 --> 17:55:23.867
have many affiliations for a prize. The affiliations field of each prize

13602
17:55:23.867 --> 17:55:25.490
sub-document is an array.

13603
17:55:25.490 --> 17:55:30.537
To fit text on this slide, I use parentheses to form multi-line strings and I show

13604
17:55:30.537 --> 17:55:35.585
only part of the document. MongoDB allows you to query document substructure using

13605
17:55:35.585 --> 17:55:40.632
dot notation. Here's a count of laureates. We reach into the prizes array to query

13606
17:55:40.632 --> 17:55:45.618
on the affiliations field across prizes. From there, we reach again, this time to

13607
17:55:45.618 --> 17:55:50.604
query on the name field across affiliations. We count laureates with at least one

13608
17:55:50.604 --> 17:55:52.820
prize affiliation name as specified.

13609
17:55:52.820 --> 17:55:57.790
The dot notation gives a full path to a field from the document root. I'm curious

13610
17:55:57.790 --> 17:56:02.821
how many laureates had an affiliation in Berkeley, California when they received a

13611
17:56:02.821 --> 17:56:07.975
prize. Here's my query. Go Bears! MongoDB allows you to specify and enforce a schema

13612
17:56:07.975 --> 17:56:13.067
for a collection, but this is not required. For example, fields do not need to have

13613
17:56:13.067 --> 17:56:17.731
the same type of value across documents in a collection. In the case of this

13614
17:56:17.731 --> 17:56:21.780
laureate, there is an empty affiliation associated with his prize.

13615
17:56:21.780 --> 17:56:26.946
Another accommodation in MongoDB is that of field presence. Even root level fields

13616
17:56:26.946 --> 17:56:32.111
don't need to be present in all documents. In this document, for example, the born

13617
17:56:32.111 --> 17:56:37.277
country field is absent. Using the exists operator, we can query for the existence

13618
17:56:37.277 --> 17:56:42.506
or non-existence of fields. Here we see that many laureates documents do not have a

13619
17:56:42.506 --> 17:56:47.419
born country field. Do all laureates have a prizes field? With the help of the

13620
17:56:47.419 --> 17:56:50.380
exists operator, we see that, in fact, they do.

13621
17:56:50.380 --> 17:56:55.460
But are any of those prizes fields empty arrays? I hope not. We can check using dot

13622
17:56:55.460 --> 17:57:00.540
notation to access array elements. This borrows from JavaScript syntax. Here we see

13623
17:57:00.540 --> 17:57:05.497
a filter document for the criteria that a value exists for the field 0 within the

13624
17:57:05.497 --> 17:57:10.455
prizes field. You can reference an array element by its numerical index using dot

13625
17:57:10.455 --> 17:57:15.596
notation. Thus, this expression counts documents that have a non-empty prizes array.

13626
17:57:15.596 --> 17:57:20.370
We see, to our relief, that all laureate documents contain at least one prize.

13627
17:57:20.370 --> 17:57:24.994
Are there laureates with more than one prize? Yes! We see that Marie Curie is in

13628
17:57:24.994 --> 17:57:29.850
this group, along with a few other people you may recognize. We've learned about dot

13629
17:57:29.850 --> 17:57:34.647
notation to query array and sub-document fields. We've also learned about the exist

13630
17:57:34.647 --> 17:57:39.272
operator to check for the presence of fields. Now let's explore. In this lesson,

13631
17:57:39.272 --> 17:57:44.127
we'll learn to use the distinct method on MongoCollection. Using this method, we can

13632
17:57:44.127 --> 17:57:48.000
collect the set of values assigned to a field across all documents.

13633
17:57:48.000 --> 17:57:52.887
We found an exceptional laureate in the last chapter. This laureate has received

13634
17:57:52.887 --> 17:57:57.896
three Nobel Prizes, more than any other laureate. Here we can see that this is the

13635
17:57:57.896 --> 17:58:02.966
International Committee of the Red Cross. You may not have known that organizations

13636
17:58:02.966 --> 17:58:08.036
can win Nobel Prizes, but they have. For example, 23 employees of Lawrence Berkeley

13637
17:58:08.036 --> 17:58:12.984
Lab shared the Nobel Peace Prize in 2007. They were part of the Intergovernmental

13638
17:58:12.984 --> 17:58:14.450
Panel on Climate Change.

13639
17:58:14.450 --> 17:58:18.807
A future exercise will be about the proportion of Nobel Prizes awarded to

13640
17:58:18.807 --> 17:58:23.282
immigrants. Keep in mind that the idea of immigration doesn't apply to some

13641
17:58:23.282 --> 17:58:27.818
laureates. In this document, I see that the gender field has a value of org,

13642
17:58:27.818 --> 17:58:32.831
presumably short for organization. What are the values that this field stores across

13643
17:58:32.831 --> 17:58:37.844
documents in this collection? MongoDB provides a built-in collection method for such

13644
17:58:37.844 --> 17:58:42.320
aggregation. Here we call the distinct method on the laureate's collection.

13645
17:58:42.320 --> 17:58:47.308
We pass a single argument, gender. MongoDB collects the distinct values that this

13646
17:58:47.308 --> 17:58:52.481
field takes across the collection. We see that there are three and only three values

13647
17:58:52.481 --> 17:58:57.530
for the gender field across the collection. You may be wondering where this method

13648
17:58:57.530 --> 17:59:02.579
comes from or how you can define a similar operation yourself. The distinct method

13649
17:59:02.579 --> 17:59:07.567
is a convenience for a common aggregation. The countDocuments method we have been

13650
17:59:07.567 --> 17:59:10.400
using is a similar convenience. An aggregation

13651
17:59:10.400 --> 17:59:15.826
processes data across the collection and produces a computed result. In the last

13652
17:59:15.826 --> 17:59:21.320
chapter of this course, we'll learn how to create custom aggregations. You may be

13653
17:59:21.320 --> 17:59:26.407
wondering about the efficiency of aggregations in MongoDB. You can register

13654
17:59:26.407 --> 17:59:31.630
so-called indexes on fields for MongoDB to maintain. These indexes can ensure

13655
17:59:31.630 --> 17:59:37.123
efficient queries and aggregations. In some cases, a query might not even need to

13656
17:59:37.123 --> 17:59:38.480
run on a collection.

13657
17:59:38.480 --> 17:59:43.699
We will learn how to create indexes in the next chapter. But if we're not working

13658
17:59:43.699 --> 17:59:49.111
with a lot of data, indexes are generally not needed. The laureates collection we're

13659
17:59:49.111 --> 17:59:54.523
using in this course fits in memory. It weighs in at under a megabyte and has on the

13660
17:59:54.523 --> 17:59:59.420
order of a thousand documents or fewer. It doesn't matter much if you use an

13661
17:59:59.420 --> 18:00:04.381
inefficient algorithm to sort a list of a few hundred items. Likewise, a full

13662
18:00:04.381 --> 18:00:07.860
collection scan isn't a big deal for this aggregation.

13663
18:00:07.860 --> 18:00:13.093
You can use dot notation to specify fields embedded deeper than the root level of a

13664
18:00:13.093 --> 18:00:18.074
document. This applies in query methods like find and find1, and it applies for

13665
18:00:18.074 --> 18:00:23.370
aggregations as well. I notice here that each sub-document in the prizes array field

13666
18:00:23.370 --> 18:00:28.603
has a category field. The .2 in the filter denotes index 2 of an array field. Thus,

13667
18:00:28.603 --> 18:00:33.899
this is a laureate where a third element exists in the prizes array. Let's fetch the

13668
18:00:33.899 --> 18:00:35.790
distinct values of this field.

13669
18:00:35.790 --> 18:00:40.907
We see, as expected, that there is a value for each category of Nobel Prize. Let's

13670
18:00:40.907 --> 18:00:45.836
use the distinct method to answer some questions about our Nobel Prize. In this

13671
18:00:45.836 --> 18:00:50.952
lesson, we're going to dip our toes into the world of aggregation pipelines. We'll

13672
18:00:50.952 --> 18:00:55.882
use the filter parameter of the distinct method to match certain documents. The

13673
18:00:55.882 --> 18:01:00.000
method will fetch field values only from these matching documents.

13674
18:01:00.000 --> 18:01:05.524
Here I found a laureate document with a value of 4 for the share field in one of its

13675
18:01:05.524 --> 18:01:10.917
prizes sub-documents. Pierre Curie shared the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics with his

13676
18:01:10.917 --> 18:01:16.179
wife Marie. They also shared it with physicist Henri Becquerel. Marie and Pierre

13677
18:01:16.179 --> 18:01:21.703
each received a quarter share of the prize. Henri received the remaining half share.

13678
18:01:21.703 --> 18:01:26.767
The Nobel Prize API encodes prize share, in particular the denominator of the

13679
18:01:26.767 --> 18:01:28.740
fractional share, as a string.

13680
18:01:28.740 --> 18:01:34.292
Thus, the document records Pierre's quarter share as the string 4. Which Nobel Prize

13681
18:01:34.292 --> 18:01:39.844
categories other than physics have laureates with quarter shares? We know how to get

13682
18:01:39.844 --> 18:01:45.263
the distinct values of prize categories from the laureates collection. We pass the

13683
18:01:45.263 --> 18:01:50.815
dotted path prizes dot category to the distinct method. We also know how to find and

13684
18:01:50.815 --> 18:01:56.367
list all laureate documents satisfying some criteria using a filter document. Can we

13685
18:01:56.367 --> 18:01:58.350
compose these two ideas? Sure.

13686
18:01:58.350 --> 18:02:03.458
The distinct method takes an optional filter argument. You can think of this as a

13687
18:02:03.458 --> 18:02:08.691
two-stage pipeline. First, filter the collection for documents that match a filter.

13688
18:02:08.691 --> 18:02:13.988
Then, collect and return distinct values of a field for these documents. In the last

13689
18:02:13.988 --> 18:02:18.780
chapter of this course, we'll learn how to custom build such pipelines in an

13690
18:02:18.780 --> 18:02:23.888
explicit way. The Nobel Prize API serves its data in a denormalized way. Thus, we

13691
18:02:23.888 --> 18:02:27.230
can answer our question from a different perspective.

13692
18:02:27.230 --> 18:02:32.060
Here we ask the prizes collection to return distinct prize categories. Given a

13693
18:02:32.060 --> 18:02:36.951
filter on laureate shares, we return distinct values of the category field. The

13694
18:02:36.951 --> 18:02:42.028
result is the same as the call above using the laureates collection. Let's look at

13695
18:02:42.028 --> 18:02:46.858
one more example of pre-filtering distinct values. Which prize categories have

13696
18:02:46.858 --> 18:02:51.873
laureates who have won more than one prize? We'll start by counting laureates who

13697
18:02:51.873 --> 18:02:54.350
won at least two prizes. In other words,

13698
18:02:54.350 --> 18:02:59.519
those for which a second element exists in their document's prizes array. Next, we

13699
18:02:59.519 --> 18:03:04.562
pass this filter document as a second argument to the distinct method. Our first

13700
18:03:04.562 --> 18:03:09.542
argument is the dotted path to the prize category field. This returns all prize

13701
18:03:09.542 --> 18:03:14.395
category values with laureates who have won more than one prize. Here are the

13702
18:03:14.395 --> 18:03:19.375
corresponding prize categories for these six laureates. Notice that not all won

13703
18:03:19.375 --> 18:03:21.140
prizes in the same category.

13704
18:03:21.140 --> 18:03:26.060
Marie Curie, for instance, won prizes in both physics and chemistry. Linus Pauling

13705
18:03:26.060 --> 18:03:31.100
won prizes in both chemistry and peace. We'll learn in the next chapter how to fetch

13706
18:03:31.100 --> 18:03:35.960
only the document substructure we need. In this case, the MongoDB server returned

13707
18:03:35.960 --> 18:03:40.700
only enough to communicate prize categories. Now it's your turn. Let's practice

13708
18:03:40.700 --> 18:03:45.620
enumerating distinct field values of collections. In this lesson, we'll learn more

13709
18:03:45.620 --> 18:03:49.220
about how to query array fields and their structured values.

13710
18:03:49.220 --> 18:03:54.014
Here we see part of the laureates collection document for John Bardeen. He won two

13711
18:03:54.014 --> 18:03:58.341
prizes, both in physics. Each prizes array in a laureate document contains

13712
18:03:58.341 --> 18:04:03.018
sub-document. Each sub-document has a category field. We can use dot notation to

13713
18:04:03.018 --> 18:04:07.636
filter for and count laureates with a prize category equal to physics. Here's a

13714
18:04:07.636 --> 18:04:12.489
simpler example using a fictitious field. Let's imagine that laureate documents had

13715
18:04:12.489 --> 18:04:16.640
an extra field, nicknames. This field stores an array of string values.

13716
18:04:16.640 --> 18:04:21.670
Let's now find all laureates with a nickname of JB. We could use a filter document

13717
18:04:21.670 --> 18:04:26.517
like this. The filter matches all documents that have at least one value in the

13718
18:04:26.517 --> 18:04:31.547
nicknames array field equal to JB. This notation is familiar. If nicknames was not

13719
18:04:31.547 --> 18:04:36.394
an array, the filter would match for the field value being equal to JB. Because

13720
18:04:36.394 --> 18:04:41.485
nicknames is an array, the filter matches if any member of the array matches. Let's

13721
18:04:41.485 --> 18:04:44.430
go back to filtering on the real category field.

13722
18:04:44.430 --> 18:04:49.352
This field is within sub-documents of the top-level Prizes array field. As before,

13723
18:04:49.352 --> 18:04:54.274
we can wrap filter document values with operators. For example, here we filter for

13724
18:04:54.274 --> 18:04:59.316
laureates with a prize not in Physics. Note that these documents may contain a prize

13725
18:04:59.316 --> 18:05:03.878
sub-document with a category of Physics. They need only also contain a prize

13726
18:05:03.878 --> 18:05:07.360
sub-document with another category value. Another example.

13727
18:05:07.360 --> 18:05:12.866
Here we use the in operator to find laureates with at least one prize in these three

13728
18:05:12.866 --> 18:05:18.306
categories. And here we use the not in operator to find laureates with at least one

13729
18:05:18.306 --> 18:05:23.746
prize not in these three categories. But what if we want to filter on more than one

13730
18:05:23.746 --> 18:05:29.186
field within a prize sub-document? Let's try something like this to count laureates

13731
18:05:29.186 --> 18:05:33.840
who won unshared prizes in physics. Hmm, that's not quite what we want.

13732
18:05:33.840 --> 18:05:38.204
This filter matches prize subdocuments that have two and only two fields. No

13733
18:05:38.204 --> 18:05:42.453
laureates have a prize subdocument that looks exactly like this. All prize

13734
18:05:42.453 --> 18:05:47.161
subdocuments also have a year field, for instance. This next filter is better, but

13735
18:05:47.161 --> 18:05:51.869
it's not quite what we want. This filter matches laureate documents satisfying two

13736
18:05:51.869 --> 18:05:56.463
conditions. The first is that a prizes field has at least one subdocument with a

13737
18:05:56.463 --> 18:05:58.300
category field equal to physics.

13738
18:05:58.300 --> 18:06:03.647
The second is that a prizes field has at least one subdocument with a share field

13739
18:06:03.647 --> 18:06:09.060
equal to one. The prizes that match for a laureate could be different prizes. This

13740
18:06:09.060 --> 18:06:14.473
is where the element match, or elemmatch, operator comes in. Finally, we count all

13741
18:06:14.473 --> 18:06:19.754
laureates that have at least one unshared prize in physics. Within the elemmatch

13742
18:06:19.754 --> 18:06:25.300
operation, as with any operation, we can continue to drill down. Operations can nest

13743
18:06:25.300 --> 18:06:27.280
to make finer grained queries.

13744
18:06:27.280 --> 18:06:32.309
Here, we extend the last filter to include laureates only if they won a solo prize

13745
18:06:32.309 --> 18:06:37.216
in physics before 1945. Let's make sure you understand how to filter arrays with

13746
18:06:37.216 --> 18:06:42.367
elemmatch. We'll do this in concert with other operators, and we'll learn some Nobel

13747
18:06:42.367 --> 18:06:47.212
Prize statistics. We've seen how to construct filters comparing a field's value

13748
18:06:47.212 --> 18:06:52.364
exactly. For string-valued fields, we may want instead to match a field's value to a

13749
18:06:52.364 --> 18:06:54.940
pattern. We may want to match a substring.

13750
18:06:54.940 --> 18:06:59.459
We may want to constrain that substring to appear at the start or end of a field's

13751
18:06:59.459 --> 18:07:04.087
value, or we may want something more complex. Regular expressions are a powerful way

13752
18:07:04.087 --> 18:07:08.441
to express such filters. Let's see how MongoDB supports them. Let's look at the

13753
18:07:08.441 --> 18:07:12.794
laureate document for Marie Curie. Recall that she discovered a new element and

13754
18:07:12.794 --> 18:07:17.423
named it polonium. She did this to publicize her native land's lack of independence.

13755
18:07:17.423 --> 18:07:21.280
We see here that Poland is a substring of her document's born country.

13756
18:07:21.280 --> 18:07:26.196
How can we filter for values of born country that contain Poland as a substring? We

13757
18:07:26.196 --> 18:07:31.111
can use MongoDB's regular expression operator, regex. Here I use the regex operator

13758
18:07:31.111 --> 18:07:35.967
on the string Poland in a filter document. This expression gets distinct values of

13759
18:07:35.967 --> 18:07:40.409
born country that contain Poland as a substring. The results show that some

13760
18:07:40.409 --> 18:07:45.147
laureates were born in places that at the time were not part of Poland but today

13761
18:07:45.147 --> 18:07:50.003
are. Others were born in places that at the time were part of Poland but today are

13762
18:07:50.003 --> 18:07:50.240
not.

13763
18:07:50.240 --> 18:07:55.742
And finally, some were born in places that both at the time were and today are part

13764
18:07:55.742 --> 18:08:01.111
of Poland. We can use the regex operator together with the options operator. This

13765
18:08:01.111 --> 18:08:06.016
will change the conditions for matching. For example, the I option ensures

13766
18:08:06.016 --> 18:08:11.319
case-insensitive matching. The string passed to regex in the second statement is

13767
18:08:11.319 --> 18:08:16.821
Poland, all lowercase. The assertion here is true. Poland is always capitalized for

13768
18:08:16.821 --> 18:08:17.550
this field.

13769
18:08:17.550 --> 18:08:22.809
MongoDB also supports compiled regular expression objects. The PyMongo driver

13770
18:08:22.809 --> 18:08:28.410
includes a bthon package with a regex class which you can import and use as shown.

13771
18:08:28.410 --> 18:08:33.669
Finally, using native Python regular expression objects is possible. I do not

13772
18:08:33.669 --> 18:08:39.338
recommend this though. Use of the bthon regex class is more robust for MongoDB. The

13773
18:08:39.338 --> 18:08:44.871
syntax of regular expressions is rich. For the exercises though, you only need to

13774
18:08:44.871 --> 18:08:46.100
know a few tricks.

13775
18:08:46.100 --> 18:08:50.861
First, you need to know how to match the beginning or end of a field's value.

13776
18:08:50.861 --> 18:08:55.808
Second, you need to know how to escape a special character so that you match the

13777
18:08:55.808 --> 18:09:00.384
character itself. To match the beginning of a field's value, use the caret

13778
18:09:00.384 --> 18:09:05.146
character. Anchor it to the beginning of the string you passed to regex. This

13779
18:09:05.146 --> 18:09:10.278
expression returns distinct values of the borncountry field that start with Poland.

13780
18:09:10.278 --> 18:09:12.690
To escape a character, use a backslash.

13781
18:09:12.690 --> 18:09:17.967
A paren functions to capture groups in regular expressions. Because we want to match

13782
18:09:17.967 --> 18:09:23.180
a literal open paren and not use this function, we escape it with a backslash. This

13783
18:09:23.180 --> 18:09:27.954
expression returns born country values for countries that used to be Poland.

13784
18:09:27.954 --> 18:09:33.168
Finally, to match the end of a field's value, use the dollar sign. Anchor it to the

13785
18:09:33.168 --> 18:09:38.319
end of what you passed to regex. This expression returns all countries that became

13786
18:09:38.319 --> 18:09:41.460
Poland after a laureate's birth. What you see here

13787
18:09:41.460 --> 18:09:46.551
is all you need for the exercises. Use a caret to match the beginning of a field,

13788
18:09:46.551 --> 18:09:51.704
use a dollar sign to match the end, and a backslash to escape parentheses. We have

13789
18:09:51.704 --> 18:09:56.920
new tools to answer questions about string-valued fields in MongoDB collections. In

13790
18:09:56.920 --> 18:10:02.137
this chapter, we're going to learn about getting only what you need and fast. We'll

13791
18:10:02.137 --> 18:10:07.102
start with what MongoDB calls projection. The term projection is about reducing

13792
18:10:07.102 --> 18:10:09.490
multidimensional data. In cartography,

13793
18:10:09.490 --> 18:10:14.661
It's about getting what you need to make a reasonable image from a 3D Earth. You can

13794
18:10:14.661 --> 18:10:19.708
also think about asking a certain part of your data to project its voice, to speak

13795
18:10:19.708 --> 18:10:24.632
up. With a table of data, it's about selecting columns. With MongoDB, it's about

13796
18:10:24.632 --> 18:10:29.803
selecting substructure. In MongoDB, we fetch projections by specifying what document

13797
18:10:29.803 --> 18:10:34.850
fields interest us. We can do this by passing a dictionary as a second argument to

13798
18:10:34.850 --> 18:10:36.820
the find method of a collection.

13799
18:10:36.820 --> 18:10:40.935
For each field that we want to include in the projection, we give a value of 1.

13800
18:10:40.935 --> 18:10:45.207
Fields that we don't include in the dictionary are not included in the projection.

13801
18:10:45.207 --> 18:10:49.583
The exception is a document's underscore ID field. The underscore ID field is always

13802
18:10:49.583 --> 18:10:50.000
included

13803
18:10:50.000 --> 18:10:54.543
in a projection by default. We must assign it the value zero in the projection

13804
18:10:54.543 --> 18:10:59.435
dictionary to leave it out. Here I try to collect the prize affiliation data for all

13805
18:10:59.435 --> 18:11:04.269
laureates. My filter document is empty. What I get back is not the data itself, but

13806
18:11:04.269 --> 18:11:09.045
a so-called cursor, an iterable that I can fetch documents from, one at a time. In

13807
18:11:09.045 --> 18:11:13.937
Python, we can collect from an iterable into a list. We do this by passing it to the

13808
18:11:13.937 --> 18:11:18.130
list function. I don't want to print out hundreds of laureate documents.

13809
18:11:18.130 --> 18:11:22.232
Thus, I also use slicing syntax to get only the first three elements of the

13810
18:11:22.232 --> 18:11:26.608
resulting list. We can see that our projections contain only document data about

13811
18:11:26.608 --> 18:11:30.984
prize affiliations. We also retained the structure of that data. Remember how to

13812
18:11:30.984 --> 18:11:35.360
project fields this way. It's going to be very useful in the rest of the course.

13813
18:11:35.360 --> 18:11:39.517
What happens when you try to project out fields that are not present in some

13814
18:11:39.517 --> 18:11:44.002
documents? Rather than raise an error, MongoDB returns the documents without those

13815
18:11:44.002 --> 18:11:47.010
fields. This expression projects the borncountry field.

13816
18:11:47.010 --> 18:11:51.851
This field isn't present for organization laureates, though. Only the first name and

13817
18:11:51.851 --> 18:11:56.519
ID fields get returned. Notice that I formatted a projection as a list of fields.

13818
18:11:56.519 --> 18:12:01.302
When a projection doesn't involve excluding fields, the PyMongo driver accepts this

13819
18:12:01.302 --> 18:12:05.624
format. Also, it's okay if a projected field isn't in any of a collection's

13820
18:12:05.624 --> 18:12:10.464
documents. Here, because there is no favorite ice cream flavor field, the projection

13821
18:12:10.464 --> 18:12:15.247
returns only object IDs. We're going to learn about MongoDB's aggregation framework

13822
18:12:15.247 --> 18:12:16.400
in the next chapter.

13823
18:12:16.400 --> 18:12:21.111
But already we have a new tool to fetch less data, only what we need. For example,

13824
18:12:21.111 --> 18:12:25.822
let's count the total number of prize medals awarded, that is, the total number of

13825
18:12:25.822 --> 18:12:30.590
elements in prizes arrays across all laureates. We can iterate over a cursor of all

13826
18:12:30.590 --> 18:12:35.416
laureates with only the prizes fields projected out, and this way we avoid having to

13827
18:12:35.416 --> 18:12:39.840
download the other data in each laureate document. This can definitely affect

13828
18:12:39.840 --> 18:12:42.770
performance for very large collections. We can even

13829
18:12:42.770 --> 18:12:46.987
In this case, use a comprehension to reduce memory overhead in Python. We can

13830
18:12:46.987 --> 18:12:51.314
leverage Python's built-in tools for iterables and dictionaries. And we can use

13831
18:12:51.314 --> 18:12:55.641
projection to slim down these dictionaries to contain only what we need for our

13832
18:12:55.641 --> 18:13:00.187
analysis. Let's practice some projection. We've learned how to project out only the

13833
18:13:00.187 --> 18:13:04.788
fields we need from a query. There are other conditions we can give to MongoDB about

13834
18:13:04.788 --> 18:13:09.170
the data returned. In this lesson, we'll learn how to sort results on the server

13835
18:13:09.170 --> 18:13:13.961
before they get returned to us. First, it's useful to review how we can sort a list

13836
18:13:13.961 --> 18:13:18.695
of retrieved documents with only Python. This may be plenty performant, especially

13837
18:13:18.695 --> 18:13:23.428
for small datasets. After all, we can store a local cache of our data and try many

13838
18:13:23.428 --> 18:13:28.277
different operations on it. Let's get a list of all physics prize documents with the

13839
18:13:28.277 --> 18:13:32.607
year field projected. I'm not interested in printing out the full projected

13840
18:13:32.607 --> 18:13:37.225
documents with the document IDs, so I'll use a list comprehension to collect the

13841
18:13:37.225 --> 18:13:37.860
year values

13842
18:13:37.860 --> 18:13:43.131
slice out the first five values, and print them. It looks like the documents are in

13843
18:13:43.131 --> 18:13:48.403
reverse chronological order, but we get no guarantee of this. To sort the documents

13844
18:13:48.403 --> 18:13:53.738
in ascending order of year, I use the built-in Python sorted function. I also import

13845
18:13:53.738 --> 18:13:59.073
the item getter function from Python's standard library. Given a key, it fetches the

13846
18:13:59.073 --> 18:14:04.217
value for that key in a dictionary. To sort in reverse order, we can pass true as

13847
18:14:04.217 --> 18:14:07.520
the reverse keyword argument to the sorted function.

13848
18:14:07.520 --> 18:14:12.234
We can also ask Mongo to do simple sorting by field values on the server and yield

13849
18:14:12.234 --> 18:14:16.947
results in sorted order. Here we pass a sort argument to the find method, giving a

13850
18:14:16.947 --> 18:14:21.661
list of field direction pairs. In this case, we want to sort on the year field and

13851
18:14:21.661 --> 18:14:26.375
in the ascending direction. I don't need the documents beyond the print statement,

13852
18:14:26.375 --> 18:14:31.203
so I pass the cursor to the list comprehension. To sort by year in descending order,

13853
18:14:31.203 --> 18:14:34.480
we use negative 1 as the second element of the sort pair.

13854
18:14:34.480 --> 18:14:39.375
Why is a list passed to the sort keyword argument? This is because you can sort

13855
18:14:39.375 --> 18:14:44.331
first by one field and then by others. Let's see how. Let's sort prize documents

13856
18:14:44.331 --> 18:14:49.474
first by ascending year and then by descending category. To do this, we provide the

13857
18:14:49.474 --> 18:14:54.307
corresponding pairs in order as a list. Here, we also query for prizes awarded

13858
18:14:54.307 --> 18:14:59.511
between 1966 and 1970, exclusive. We project out only the data we need, the category

13859
18:14:59.511 --> 18:15:04.220
and year values. Notice that we could sort by fields that we do not project.

13860
18:15:04.220 --> 18:15:09.004
In this case, we happen to be also projecting the sort fields. For each projection

13861
18:15:09.004 --> 18:15:13.730
yielded by the cursor, we format a string with the year and category value. To do

13862
18:15:13.730 --> 18:15:18.339
this, we use Python's double-star dictionary unpacking syntax. The output shows

13863
18:15:18.339 --> 18:15:23.240
years increasing and, for each year, categories decreasing. In both cases, the order

13864
18:15:23.240 --> 18:15:28.024
is alphabetical because the fields are both string valued. For the four-digit year

13865
18:15:28.024 --> 18:15:31.700
strings, sorting produces the same result as numerical sorting.

13866
18:15:31.700 --> 18:15:37.098
We can see that there was no award for economics in 1967 or 1968, and there was no

13867
18:15:37.098 --> 18:15:42.364
award for peace in 1967. One last thing. The command line shell for MongoDB uses

13868
18:15:42.364 --> 18:15:47.697
JavaScript. You specify a sort using the form of a JavaScript object, which looks

13869
18:15:47.697 --> 18:15:53.160
like a Python dictionary. This works because JavaScript objects in the console keep

13870
18:15:53.160 --> 18:15:58.427
their key order as entered. In Python 3.6 and below, though, there is no similar

13871
18:15:58.427 --> 18:16:00.270
guarantee with dictionaries.

13872
18:16:00.270 --> 18:16:05.158
The order of keys may not be preserved as entered. This is why PyMongo requires a

13873
18:16:05.158 --> 18:16:10.047
list of tuples. Let's get some practice. It's time to speed up our queries. Enter

13874
18:16:10.047 --> 18:16:15.056
indexes. An index in MongoDB is like a book's index. Let's say I grab a textbook on

13875
18:16:15.056 --> 18:16:20.065
material science. I want information on eutectic temperatures. I could flip through

13876
18:16:20.065 --> 18:16:24.954
the book. I could also try narrowing down to a chapter from skimming the table of

13877
18:16:24.954 --> 18:16:27.730
contents. Instead, I go to the index and back.

13878
18:16:27.730 --> 18:16:33.092
I see an alphabetical ordering of terms with page numbers for each. I find eutectic

13879
18:16:33.092 --> 18:16:38.196
temperature, which directs me to page 314. I go there, and voila. With MongoDB,

13880
18:16:38.196 --> 18:16:43.623
imagine each collection as a book, each document as a page, and each field as a type

13881
18:16:43.623 --> 18:16:48.921
of content. Imagine an ordered index for temperatures. This means you can find all

13882
18:16:48.921 --> 18:16:54.025
pages that list temperatures in a range of interest. This is hard to do with an

13883
18:16:54.025 --> 18:16:54.800
actual book.

13884
18:16:54.800 --> 18:16:59.801
But MongoDB structures documents with fields, thus you can index fields using the

13885
18:16:59.801 --> 18:17:04.926
values of those fields. When are indexes useful? First, when you expect to get only

13886
18:17:04.926 --> 18:17:09.557
one or a few documents back. If your typical queries fetch most, if not all

13887
18:17:09.557 --> 18:17:14.496
